 "2026-10-09T05:26:22.336365+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1921", "title": "Federal Ministry of Trade and Investment Holds Top Management Meeting", "url": "https://son.gov.ng/2025/03/27/federal-ministry-of-trade-and-investment-holds-top-management-meeting/", "final_url": "https://son.gov.ng/2025/03/27/federal-ministry-of-trade-and-investment-holds-top-management-meeting/", "text": "FEDERAL MINISTRY OF INDUSTRY, TRADE AND INVESTMENT HOLDS TOP MANAGEMENT MEETING The Director General/Chief Executive, Standards Organisation of Nigeria. Dr. Ifeanyi Chukwunonso Okeke, and other Chief Executive Officers (CEOs) under the Federal Ministry of Industry, Trade and Investment participated in a top management meeting organised by the Honourable Minister, Federal Ministry of Industry, Trade and Investment. Jumoke Oduwole and the Minister for Industry, Senator John Owah Enoh on Tuesday in Abuja. The meeting was set to review the Ministry\u2019s performance for the 1st Quarter of the year 2025 in preparation for the submission to the Central Results Delivery Coordinator Unit (CRDCU). The meeting brought together the Ministry\u2019s Permanent Secretary, Ambassador Nuru Abba, CEOs of the 20 Agencies under the Ministry and Directors of the Ministry for strategic discussions. The highlight of the meeting was the presentation of a report on the 2024 FMITI deliverables performance; a report on the revised performance bond; a presentation of January and February Central Delivery Coordination Unit\u2019s monthly reports and the presentation of reports on the implementation of three (3) game changing projects.", "content_hash": "61dc35726ce93a6446016af86e18f3762153090f62f3c6c34d373d8d0220a808", "domain": "son.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:26:30.217885+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1922", "title": "Technical Committee of the Association of Food Beverage and Tobacco Employers Pays Courtesy Visit to SON", "url": "https://son.gov.ng/2025/04/08/technical-committee-of-the-association-of-food-beverage-and-tobacco-employers-pays-courtesy-visit-to-son/", "final_url": "https://son.gov.ng/2025/04/08/technical-committee-of-the-association-of-food-beverage-and-tobacco-employers-pays-courtesy-visit-to-son/", "text": "TECHNICAL COMMITTEE OF THE ASSOCIATION OF FOOD BEVERAGE AND TOBACCO EMPLOYERS PAYS COURTESY VISIT TO SON In a recent meeting at the Standards Organisation of Nigeria (SON) Operational Headquarters in Lekki, the Technical Committee of the Association of Food, Beverage, and Tobacco Manufacturers (AFBTE), under the leadership of its chairman Mr. Fred Chiazor, paid a courtesy visit to the Director General and management of SON to engage in discussions regarding crucial issues and strategies for the sustainable development of the nation. In his introductory remarks, the Director General, Dr. Ifeanyi Okeke, emphasized the significance of the meeting to address substantial concerns and exchange information on the latest developments in standardization activities in Nigeria. He highlighted that food safety is a matter of ethical importance, and it is SON\u2019s responsibility to safeguard public health in accordance with its mandate. The Director General expressed that the meeting offered a platform to review the progress made, identify areas in need of improvement as regulators, and brainstorm on how to capitalize on existing opportunities. He stressed the importance of providing appropriate guidance to ensure compliance, thereby addressing the challenges faced by manufacturers and key stakeholders in the nation\u2019s economic development in meeting regulatory standards. Dr. Okeke underscored the pivotal role of the food and beverage industry in ensuring economic stability and national food security. He emphasized the necessity of continually introducing initiatives to enhance the quality of manufactured goods, such as the introduction of the new MANCAP logo. This initiative aims to elevate the standard for manufacturers, thereby boosting the competitiveness of Nigerian products on the global stage. Similarly, the chairman of the association, Mr. Fred Chiazor, highlighted the significance of the visit to acquaint the new management and engage in discussions on critical issues concerning MANCAP inspections citing the need to reduce the number of inspections. More insights on expediting guidelines, as well as updates on the free trade zone, standards deliberations, and other pressing matters were discussed. The chairman also sought the support of SON to address the issue of some unauthorized Chinese companies who were reportedly importing and selling substandard msg in the northern part of the country illegally, thereby endangering the lives of its citizens. While the association raised several issues, the Director General has already implemented measures to address these pressing concerns. Regarding MANCAP and fast track, the DG mentioned that inspections are conducted on a quarterly basis and for certification, two inspections are carried out, with a 60-day timeline for the process. The management of AFBTE acknowledged that this administration\u2019s timing could not have been more opportune, while also assuring the DG of their willingness and support to collaborate in combating the influx and sale of substandard goods in the country. Concluding with a vote of thanks, the Director of Corporate Affairs, Mrs. Talatu Ethan, expressed gratitude to the delegates from AFBTE for taking time out of their busy schedules to visit SON. She also assured them that the DG and management of SON are always prepared to collaborate with them to ensure a better Nigeria, as the significance of collaboration cannot be overstated in pursuing a common goal.", "content_hash": "920976f55694ba549a868eddb2fd250c77f5c53b105cbfc215661f7ea3caee2b", "domain": "son.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:26:39.291194+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1923", "title": "download", "url": "https://budgetoffice.gov.ng/index.php/2026-2028-mtef-fsp/2026-2028-mtef-fsp/download", "final_url": "https://budgetoffice.gov.ng/index.php/2026-2028-mtef-fsp/2026-2028-mtef-fsp/download", "text": "i \n \n\n\nii \n \n \n \n \nTable of Contents \n1.0 INTRODUCTION ......................................................................................................... 1 \n2.0 ECONOMIC AND FISCAL DEVELOPMENTS ................................................................... 3 \n2.1 Global Economic Developments and Implications ................................ ........... 3 \n2.2 Domestic Economic Environment ................................ ................................ ......... 4 \nGDP growth and Sectoral Performance .................................................................................................. 4 \n2.3 Unemployment and Underemployment ................................ ................................ .... 5 \n2.4 Monetary Sector ................................ ................................ ................................ ...... 6 \nInterest Rate ................................ ................................ ................................ ......................... 6 \nExchange Rates ......................................................................................................................................... 7 \nForeign Reserve ......................................................................................................................................... 7 \n2.5 Fiscal Sector ................................ ................................ ................................ ............. 8 \n2.6 External Sector ................................ ................................ ................................ ....... 11 \nCapital Importation ................................................................................................................................... 12 \n3.0 Review of 2024 Budget Performance ....................................................................... 15 \n3.1 Revenue Outturns ................................ ................................ ................................ ...... 15 \n3.2 Expenditure Outturns ................................ ................................ ............................ 18 \n4.0 Review of 2025 Budget Implementation .................................................................. 19 \n4.1 Revenue Outturns ................................ ................................ ................................ .. 19 \n4.2 Expenditure Outturns ................................ ................................ ................................ 21 \n5.0 Assumptions Underlying 2026 - 2028 Revenue Projections ....................................... 22 \n5.1 Economic Growth ................................ ................................ ................................ ...... 22 \n5.2 Inflation Rate ................................ ................................ ................................ ............. 22 \n5.3 Exchange Rate ................................ ................................ ................................ ........... 22 \n5.4 Oil Revenue Assumptions ................................ ................................ ........................... 23 \n5.4.1 Crude Oil Price Benchmark ........................................................................................................... 23 \n5.4.2 Crude Oil Production and Demand ............................................................................................... 23 \n5.5 Non-Oil Revenue Baseline Assumptions ................................ ................................ ...... 25 \n6.0 Medium-term Macroeconomic framework: parameters and targets for 2026 - 2028 28 \n6.1 Macroeconomic Projections ................................ ................................ ....................... 28 \n6.2 2025-2027 Medium-Term Fiscal Framework (MTFF) ................................ .......... 29 \n6.2.1 Federation Account Revenues ...................................................................................................... 29 \n6.2.1 FGN Revenue ................................................................................................................................ 30 \n6.2.2 FGN Expenditure ........................................................................................................................... 31 \n6.2.3 Aggregate Expenditure ................................................................................................................. 31 \n6.2.4 Fiscal Deficit and Deficit Financing ............................................................................................... 33 \n7.0 Medium Term Objectives, Policies and Strategies .................................................... 35 \n7.1 Macroeconomic Policy Objectives ................................ ................................ .............. 35 \n\niii \n \n7.2 Fiscal Policy Objectives and Strategies ................................ ................................ ....... 40 \n7.3 Monetary Policy Objectives, Strategy and Forecasts ................................ .................... 47 \n7.3.4.1 Assumptions and Method for Setting Inflation Targets ................................................................ 52 \n8.0 ANALYSIS & STATEMENT ON CONSOLIDATED DEBT & CONTINGENT LIABILITIES ....... 54 \n8.1 Nigeria\u2019s Current Debt Profile ................................ ................................ ..................... 54 \n8.1.1 Debt Stock ..................................................................................................................................... 54 \n8.1.2 Debt Management Strategy ......................................................................................................... 55 \n8.1.3 Nature and Fiscal Implications of Contingent Liabilities .............................................................. 56 \n9.0 RISKS TO THE MEDIUM-TERM OUTLOOK ................................................................. 58 \n9.1 Global Economic Trends & Geo-Political Tensions ................................ ........... 58 \n9.2 International Oil Market Developments ................................ ............................. 60 \n9.2.1 Oil Prices ..................................................................................................................................... 60 \n9.2.2 Oil Demand & Supply Risks ..................................................................................................... 60 \n9.3 Exchange Rate Risks ................................ ................................ ............................. 61 \n9.4 Risks to FGN Revenue Outlook ................................ ................................ ........... 61 \n9.5 Sensitivity of Budget aggregates to Macro-economic conditions ................ 62 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\niv \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nThe economic and fiscal estimates presented in the 2026 - 28 MTEF/FSP incorporate \nassumptions, narratives and judgments based on information available at the time of \npreparation. These estimates are subject to uncertainty. This MTEF/FSP provides details of \nthe historical performance of Budget forecasts for the macroeconomic aggregates of real \nand nominal GDP, as well as for estimates of government revenues & expenditure. The \nFiscal Strategy Paper also presents several economic forecasts / projections and key \nunderlying assumptions as well as Government\u2019s medium-term outlook. \n \n \n \n \nThis document is available at \u2013 www.budgetoffice.gov,ng \n \n\nv \n \nLIST OF TABLES \n2.1 Global Economic Growth (%), 2022 - 2025 4 \n3.1 Performance of Selected Indicators and Key Parameters, 2024 15 \n3.2 FY 2024 Revenue Performance 16 \n3.3 Federation Account Revenue and VAT Distributable FY 2024 17 \n3.4 FGN Revenue Profile FY 2024 (N\u2019Billion) 17 \n3.5 FGN Expenditure Performance FY 2024 (N\u2019Billion) 18 \n4.1 Revenue Performance (Jan - Jul) 2025 19 \n4.2 Federation Account Revenue and VAT Distributable, Jul 2025 20 \n4.3 FGN Revenue Performance (Jan - Jul) 2025 20 \n4.4 FGN Expenditure Performance (Jan - Jul) 2025 21 \n6.1 Key Parameters & Macroeconomic Projections (2026 - 2028) 28 \n6.2 Federation Account and VAT Revenues (2026 - 2028) 30 \n6.3 Overview of the Revenue Framework 30 \n6.4 Overview of the Expenditure Framework 32 \n6.5 Deficit, Financing and Critical Ratios 33 \n7.1 Selected Macroeconomic Projections for the 2026 - 2028 MTEF 49 \n8.1 Medium Term Debt Strategy 2020 \u2013 2024 Key Targets 55 \n8.2 FGN Contingent Liabilities (2024 - 2027) 56 \n9.1 Risks Likelihood, Impact and Mitigation Strategies 63 \nLIST OF FIGURES \n2.1 Real GDP Growth Rates 2023 \u2013 2026 3 \n2.2 Sectoral Quarterly GDP Growth Rates 2021Q1 \u2013 2024Q4 4 \n2.3 Non-oil Growth Rate 5 \n2.4 Unemployment Trend 5 \n2.5 Inflation Rate 2017 \u2013 2025 6 \n2.7 Money Market Indicators 7 \n2.8 Exchange Rates 7 \n2.9 Foreign Reserves 7 \n2.10 Government Revenues 8 \n2.11 Government Expenditure 8 \n2.12 Nigeria Public Debt Stock 10 \n2.13 FGN Debt Stock 11 \n2.14 Nigerian Imports 12 \n2.15 Nigerian Exports 13 \n2.16 Capital Importation 13 \n5.1 Crude Oil Price 23 \n5.2 Crude Oil Outputs 24 \n7.1 Inflation Scenario Analysis 50 \n7.2 Inflation and Change in Inflation Trend 51 \n8.1 Trend in Nigeria\u2019s Total Public Debt (2020 \u2013 2025 Q1) 54 \n8.2 FGN Total Debt Service Payments (2018 \u2013 2024) 54 \n9.2 Historical Dated Brent Oil Spot Price Average 60 \n \n\nvi \n \nLIST OF ACRONYMS \n \nACRONYM DESCRIPTION \n3MTT 3 Million Technical Talents \nAfCFTA Africa Continental Free Trade Agreement \nADF African Development Fund \nADR Average Duty Rate \nAKK Ajaokuta-Kaduna-Kano \nARMOR Accelerated Revenue Mobilisation Reforms \nBHCPF Basic Healthcare Provision Fund \nBOF Budget Office of the Federation \nBOT Balance of Trade \nBOP Balance of Payment \nCBN Central Bank of Nigeria \nCBDC Central Bank Digital Currency \nCEDF Creative Economy Development Fund \nCET Common External Tariff \nCIF Cost, Insurance and Freight \nCIT Companies Income Tax \nCRF Consolidated Revenue Fund \nDMO Debt Management Office \nDPR Department of Petroleum Resources \nDSSI Debt Service Suspension Initiative \nECA Excess Crude Account \nECD Early Childhood Development \nEIA Energy Information Administration \nETP Energy Transition Plan \nEU European Union \nEV Electric Vehicles \nFCDA Federal Capital Development Authority \nFCT Federal Capital Territory \nFDI Foreign Direct Investment \n\nvii \n \nFG Federal Government \nFGN Federal Government of Nigeria \nFMFBNP Federal Ministry of Finance, Budget and National Planning \nFRA Fiscal Responsibility Act \nFSP Fiscal Strategy Paper \nFX Foreign Exchange \nFY Financial Year \nGAVI Global Alliance for Vaccines \nGDP Gross Domestic Product \nGENCOs Electricity Generation Company \nGOEs Government Owned Enterprises \nHOPE Human Capital Opportunities for Prosperity and Equity \nIAT Import Adjustment Tax \nICT Information and Communication Technology \nI&E Import and Export \nIDA International Development Association \nIEA International Energy Agency \nIGR Internally Generated Revenues \nIMF International Monetary Fund \nIPPIS Integrated Personnel and Payroll Information System \nJET-P Just Energy Transition Partnership \nJV Joint Venture \nLEEP Labour Employment and Empowerment Programme \nLIC Low Income Countries \nMBPD Million Barrels Per Day \nMDAs Ministries, Departments and Agencies \nMPC Monetary Policy Committee \nMPR Monetary Policy Rate \nMSMEs Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises \nMTFF Medium-Term Fiscal Framework \nMTEF Medium-Term Expenditure Framework \nMTDS Medium-Term Debt Strategy \n\nviii \n \nMTNDP Medium Term National Development Plan \nNAFEX the Nigerian Autonomous Foreign Exchange \nNASS National Assembly \nNBET Nigerian Bulk Electricity Trading \nNCS Nigeria Customs Service \nNEDC North East Development Commission (NEDC). \nNEXIM Nigerian Export-Import Bank \nNIRP Nigeria Industrial Revolution Plan \nNNPC Nigerian National Petroleum Corporation \nNPA Nigeria Ports Authority \nNSSP National Social Safety Net Programme \nOAGF Office of the Accountant General of the Federation \nOECD Organisation for Economic Cooperation and Development \nOPEC Organisation of Petroleum Exporting Countries \nPCOA Put-Call Option Agreement \nPFM Public Finance Management \nPIGB Petroleum Industry Governance Bill \nPMS Premium Motor Spirit \nPPP Public Private Partnership \nPPT Petroleum Profit Tax \nPRG Partial Risk Guarantees \nPRMRC Presidential Revenue Monitoring & Reconciliation Committee \nPSRP Power Sector Recovery Program \nPSRO Power Sector Recovery Operations \nPV Photovoltaic \nSDG Sustainable Development Goals \nSIP Social Investment Programme \nSSI Safe School Initiative \nTE Tax Expenditure \nTES Tax Expenditure Statement \nTSA Treasury Single Account \nUK United Kingdom \n\nix \n \n \n \n. \nUSA United States of America \nUSD United States Dollar \nVAT Value Added Tax \nWEO World Economic Outlook \nYoY Year on Year \n\n1 \n \n1.0 INTRODUCTION \nSince the last Fiscal Strategy Paper (2025 - 27), economic developments have been in a \npositive direction, and the federal government remains committed to maintaining policy mix as \nit looks ahead to consolidate and advance inclusive economic growth. \n \nThe 2026 - 2028 Medium Term Expenditure Framework and Fiscal Strategy Paper \n(MTEF/FSP) is being developed at a time of cautious optimism for the Nigerian economy. It \nbuilds on recent gains made through bold structural reforms and policy recalibrations, while \nconfronting persistent vulnerabilities and uncertainties in the global and domestic \nenvironment. The period ahead presents Nigeria with both an opportunity and a responsibility \nto sustain macroeconomic stability, deepen structural transformation, and entrench inclusive \nand resilient growth across all sectors of the economy. \n \nOver the past two years, the Federal Government has made significant strides in stabilizing \nthe macroeconomic landscape. Real GDP growth improved to 3.4 percent in 2024, the \nstrongest pace since 2014 (outside the COVID-19 recovery period) driven by continued \nrecovery in the oil sector and robust growth in ICT and financial services. While inflation \nremained elevated, averaging above 25 percent in 2024, a tight monetary stance by the \nCentral Bank of Nigeria and complementary fiscal discipline have begun to restore confidence \nin economic management. The unification of the foreign exchange market, adoption of a \ntransparent FX trading platform, and sustained fiscal consolidation efforts have contributed to \na stronger external position, a firmer naira, and growing investor interest. \n \nThese reforms have not been without cost. The removal of fuel subsidies and other \nadjustments have placed short-term pressures on household welfare and business \noperations, but they are necessary and overdue steps to reposition the economy for long-term \ncompetitiveness. Following the enactment of the Nigerian Tax Acts with implementation \nexpected to commence in 2026, improved revenue outturns, relative expenditure discipline, \nand a narrowing fiscal deficit are expected positive signals of the early impact of these reforms. \n \nThe focus of the 2026 - 2028 MTEF/FSP is to consolidate these gains and accelerate progress \ntoward a more diversified and resilient economy. The assumptions underpinning the \nframework (Chapter 5) reflect moderate global growth, a stable but still volatile oil market, and \ndomestic reforms aimed at boosting productivity and investment. The medium-term \nmacroeconomic framework (Chapter 6) is designed to balance growth aspirations with fiscal \nprudence, setting realistic targets for revenue mobilisation, expenditure efficiency, and deficit \nreduction. \n \nKey to the medium-term strategy is the government\u2019s continued investment in physical and \nsocial infrastructure, including energy, transport, education, and health, as well as a renewed \nfocus on human capital development and job creation. Enhanced public financial management \nsystems, especially through digital transformation and automation, will underpin efforts to \nclose leakages, improve tax compliance, and ensure that every naira spent delivers value for \nmoney. \n \nThe Federal Government remains committed to maintaining debt sustainability. Chapter 8 \nprovides a comprehensive analysis of Nigeria\u2019s debt stock, contingent liabilities, and the \nevolving debt management strategy. Debt accumulation will remain guided by cost-risk trade-\noffs and targeted at high impact, growth enabling investments, consistent with Nigeria\u2019s fiscal \nresponsibility framework. \n \nThe risks to Nigeria\u2019s fiscal and economic outlook (Chapter 9) are not trivial. Geopolitical \ntensions, volatility in the international oil market, global inflation persistence, and domestic \n\n2 \n \nsecurity challenges continue to pose threats to stability. However, the government\u2019s strategy \nis to anticipate, mitigate, and respond to these risks with sound macroeconomic management \nand flexible policy instruments. \n \nThis MTEF/FSP thus represents more than a technical pre-budget statement. It is a \nconsolidation of the government\u2019s economic recovery efforts and a reaffirmation of its \ncommitment to inclusive, job rich growth. It lays the foundation for the 2026 Appropriation Bill \nand signals to the Nigerian people, development partners, and investors that Nigeria remains \non a steady path to economic transformation, backed by reform resolve and fiscal realism. \n \n\n3 \n \n2.0 ECONOMIC AND FISCAL DEVELOPMENTS \n2.1 Global Economic Developments and Implications \nGlobal economic growth is projected to moderate as temporary trade-related support \nmechanisms fade. Growth is forecast at 3.0% in 2025 and 3.1% in 2026, down from 3.3% in \n2024. While these projections exceed the IMF April 2025 reference forecast, they remain \nbelow the pre-pandemic historical average of 3.7%. \n \nAdvanced economies face significant headwinds, with growth projected at 1.5% in 2025 and \n1.6% in 2026. The euro area is expected to see modest acceleration from 1.0% in 2025 to \n1.2% in 2026, though these levels remain historically subdued. Regional performance varies \nconsiderably. Middle East and Central Asia show the strongest prospects, with growth \naccelerating from 3.4% in 2025 to 3.5% in 2026. Sub-Saharan Africa demonstrates resilience \nwith stable growth at 4.0% in 2025 before strengthening to 4.3% in 2026. Latin America and \nthe Caribbean face temporary deceleration to 2.2% in 2025 before recovering to 2.4% in 2026. \nEmerging and Developing Europe presents the most challenging outlook, with sluggish growth \nat 1.8% in 2025 improving modestly to 2.2% in 2026. \n \nThe IMF's upward revision reflects strong front-loading in international trade, lower worldwide \neffective tariff rates than initially assumed, and improved global financial conditions. This front-\nloading effect is expected to reverse in coming quarters, creating headwinds for 2026 activity. \nThe United States economy is projected to expand 1.9% in 2025, supported by tariff rates \nsettling below April 2025 announcements and accommodative financial conditions. Global \nheadline inflation is expected to decline to 4.2% in 2025 and 3.6% in 2026, with cross-country \nvariation. US inflation will likely remain above target while other major economies experience \nmore subdued price pressures. \n \nDownside risks include \ngeopolitical tensions disrupting \nsupply chains, fiscal \nimbalances raising long-term \ninterest rates, and continued \ntrade policy uncertainty \ndampening investment. \nConversely, breakthrough \ntrade negotiations could \nestablish predictable \nframeworks and reduce tariff \nbarriers. Energy commodity \nprices are expected to fall 7% \nin 2025, with oil price volatility \nduring June Iran-Israel \ntensions now receding. Natural \ngas prices remain contained \ndue to trade uncertainty and \nanticipated supply increases. \nSource: IMF-WEO July 2025 \n \n\n\n4 \n \nActual Estimate\nReal GDP Growth, Percent Change 2023 2024 2025 2026\nWorld Output 3.3 3.2 3.0 3.1\nAdvanced Economies 1.7 1.7 1.5 1.6\nUnited States 2.5 2.6 1.9 2.0\nEuro Area 0.5 0.9 1.0 1.2\nEmerging Market & Developing Countries\n4.4 4.3 4.1 4.0\nChina 5.2 5.0 4.8 4.2\nIndia 8.2 7.0 6.4 6.4\nBrazil 2.9 2.1 2.2 2.4\nSub \u2013 Saharan Africa 3.4 3.7 4.0 4.3\nNigeria 2.9 3.1 3.4 3.2\nSouth Africa 0.7 0.9 1.0 1.3\nLow- Income Developing Countries 3.9 4.4 4.4 5.0\nProjections\n \nSource: IMF-WEO July 2025 \n2.2 Domestic Economic Environment \nGDP growth and Sectoral Performance \n \nNigeria's economy demonstrated robust performance with real GDP growth of 4.6% year-on-\nyear in Q4 2024, delivering full-year growth of 3.4%\u2014the highest since 2014 excluding the \nCOVID-19 rebound period. This acceleration was driven by continued oil and gas sector \nrecovery and particularly strong services growth, notably in ICT and finance. Agricultural \nperformance remained constrained at 1.2% growth due to elevated input costs and security \nchallenges. \nReal GDP growth moderated to \n3.13% year-on-year in Q1 2025, \ndown from 3.76% in Q4 2024 but \nhigher than Q1 2024's 2.27%. This \nmarked the fifteenth consecutive \nquarter of positive growth despite \nmultiple headwinds. For 2025, real \nGDP growth is projected at 3.7%, \nequivalent to 1.6% in per capita \nterms. \nThe services sector continued as \nthe primary growth driver, \nexpanding 4.33% and contributing \n57.5% to aggregate GDP in Q1 \n2025. \n\n\n5 \n \n \nThe non-oil sector grew 3.19% in real \nterms, contributing 96.03% to GDP, \nslightly higher than Q1 2024's 95.98% \nshare. Oil sector performance was \nmixed, with real growth of 1.87% \nyear-on-year in Q1 2025, down from \n4.71% in Q1 2024. Despite this \ndeceleration, the sector's contribution \nto total GDP increased to 6.38% from \n3.97% in the corresponding 2024 \nperiod. Average daily crude oil \nproduction reached 1.47 million \nbarrels per day, exceeding both Q1 \n2024 (1.27 mbpd) and Q4 2024 (1.40 \nmbpd) levels. \n \n2.3 Unemployment and Underemployment \nLabour force participation declined to 77.3% in Q1 2024 from 79.5% in Q3 2023, with men \nand women showing similar participation rates of 77.5% and 77.1% respectively. Rural areas \nmaintained higher participation rates than urban centers. \n \nEmployment-to-population ratio stood at 73.2% in Q1 2024, with minimal gender disparity \n(72.4% for men, 72.3% for women). Rural areas significantly outperformed urban areas with \nemployment ratios of 78.9% versus 69.5%. Self-employment remains dominant, accounting \nfor 84% of employed Nigerians, with women showing higher self-employment rates (87.9%) \nthan men (79.9%). Rural self-employment rates (91.9%) substantially exceed urban rates \n(78.2%). \nThe unemployment rate increased to 5.3% \nin Q1 2024 from 5.0% in Q3 2023. Gender \ndisparities persist, with female \nunemployment (6.2%) exceeding male \nunemployment (4.3%). Urban \nunemployment (6.0%) remains higher than \nrural unemployment (4.3%). Youth \nunemployment reached 8.4%, highlighting \nthe need for targeted policy interventions. \nUnderemployment affected 10.9% of \nemployed Nigerians in Q1 2024, with youth \nunderemployment (ages 15-24) at 14.4%, \nindicating significant labour market \ninefficiencies requiring policy attention. \nSource: National Bureau of Statistics \n \n \n \n\n\n6 \n \n2.4 Monetary Sector \n \nGenerally, Inflationary \npressures persist in Nigeria \ndespite recent moderation. \nHeadline inflation declined to \n22.22% in June 2025 from \n22.97% in May 2025. Month-\non-month inflation increased \nslightly to 1.68% from May's \n1.53%, indicating continued \nprice pressures. Persistent \ninflation drivers include \nsecurity challenges in food-\nproducing regions, elevated \ntransportation costs from \nenergy price increases, \ndistribution bottlenecks, \nforeign exchange market \nadjustments, and electricity \ntariff increases. \nSource: National Bureau of Statistics, 2025 \nInterest Rate \nSource: Central Bank of Nigeria \n \nThe Central Bank of Nigeria maintained the Monetary Policy Rate at 27.5% for the third \nconsecutive meeting in July 2025, reflecting continued anti-inflation priorities. The Cash \nReserve Ratio was held at 50% to maintain tight liquidity conditions. Treasury bill rates \nremained elevated, declining marginally from 18.00% in May to 17.98% in June 2025. Fixed \ndeposit rates across various maturities ranged from 9.84% (one month) to 11.71% (six \nmonths), reflecting tight monetary conditions. \n \n\n\n7 \n \nExchange Rates \n \nThe Naira depreciated to N1,535 \nper US dollar in August 2025, \ncontinuing the currency's \nadjustment following the \nunification of exchange rate \nsegments in June 2023. The \nCentral Bank continues \nimplementing measures to \nenhance market efficiency and \nattract investment while \nmaintaining the market-\ndetermined exchange rate \nframework. \n \n \nForeign Reserve \nForeign exchange reserves position strengthened in the first half of 2025, with foreign \nexchange reserves rising to $40.1 billion in July 2025, a 6.0% increase from $37.05 billion in \nthe corresponding period of 2024. The reserve accumulation was supported by moderated \nimport pressures and improved foreign exchange inflows, marking a reversal from the \ndeclining trajectory observed in recent years. \n \nCapital flows exhibited \ndivergent patterns in Q1 \n2025. Foreign direct \ninvestment inflows \ntotaled $5.6 billion, \nreflecting enhanced \ninvestor sentiment \ntoward Nigeria's policy \nframework. Conversely, \nremittance flows \ndeclined moderately to \n$4.9 billion from $5.1 \nbillion in the previous \nquarter, suggesting \nsome weakening in \ndiaspora transfers. \nForeign portfolio \ninvestment surged to $5.2 billion, significantly exceeding prior-year levels, supported by \nimproved global risk appetite and strengthening domestic fundamentals. \n \nThe Central Bank of Nigeria has intensified efforts to bolster foreign exchange reserves \nthrough targeted policy measures aimed at attracting portfolio investments and increasing \nexport receipts. These initiatives are designed to reinforce external stability and support the \nongoing structural reform program, while maintaining exchange rate competitiveness in line \nwith macroeconomic objectives. \n \n \n\n\n8 \n \n2.5 Fiscal Sector \nGovernment Revenue \nFiscal position has been \nconstrained by persistently low \ngovernment revenues, limiting the \nauthorities' capacity to meet fiscal \nobligations and finance \ndevelopment priorities. In \nresponse, the government has \nundertaken comprehensive \nrevenue mobilization reforms \ntargeting both tax administration \nand oil sector challenges, \nincluding pipeline vandalism and \ncrude oil theft. The tax-to-GDP \nratio improved markedly to 10.9% \nfrom approximately 5-6% in \nprevious years, driven primarily by \nnon-oil revenue expansion and the incorporation of previously excluded federal and sub-\nnational revenue streams in official statistics. This adjustment provides a more accurate \nrepresentation of Nigeria's revenue performance, though the ratio remains below regional \nbenchmarks. \nThe authorities are implementing a multi-pronged reform strategy focused on enhancing \nrevenue collection efficiency. Key initiatives include process automation, strengthened fiscal \nprudence measures emphasizing value-for-money principles, and institutional reforms to \noptimize tax revenues and government enterprise remittances. Tax harmonization efforts aim \nto eliminate multiple taxation while improving voluntary compliance through streamlined \nprocedures. \nThese structural reforms are expected to yield further revenue gains as implementation \ndeepens. However, sustaining these improvements will require continued political \ncommitment to reform implementation and addressing underlying constraints in the oil sector \nthat have historically undermined fiscal performance. \nTotal revenues exhibited strong growth in 2024, increasing by 70% to N7.23 trillion from N4.25 \ntrillion in 2023. This improvement was broad-based, with both oil and non-oil revenues \ncontributing to the expansion. Oil revenues rose by 81.8% to N2.0 trillion, constituting 27.7% \nof total revenues, driven by increased crude oil production averaging 1.49 million barrels per \nday (mbpd) compared to 1.41 mbpd in 2023. Non-oil revenues grew by 65.3% to N5.22 trillion, \nrepresenting 72.3% of total collections and reflecting the impact of ongoing tax administration \nreforms. \nThe revenue gains are expected to strengthen fiscal capacity and support implementation of \nthe government's development agenda. However, despite improvements in non-oil revenue \nperformance, Nigeria's tax-to-GDP ratio remains below comparator countries with similar \neconomic fundamentals, indicating substantial scope for further revenue mobilization. \n\n\n9 \n \nTax system reforms are progressing, with implementation of the Presidential Tax Reform \nCommittee's recommendations underway. International recognition of these efforts is \nemerging, as evidenced by Nigeria's inclusion in the 2024 Tax Transparency in Africa Report, \nwhich noted progress in developing the country's Exchange of Information framework to \ncombat tax evasion through enhanced transparency mechanisms among participating \njurisdictions. \nWhile the revenue trajectory is encouraging, sustaining these gains will require continued \nstrengthening of tax administration capacity and addressing structural challenges in the oil \nsector. The authorities' commitment to comprehensive tax reform positions Nigeria to \ngradually close the revenue gap with regional peers. \n \nGovernment Expenditure \nGovernment expenditure remains a key driver of socioeconomic development. Government \nchannels spending to areas of \npriority with a view to enhancing \nand promoting inclusive growth. \nThe persistent growth in federal \nspending is attributable to the \ngovernment\u2019s investment in \ncritical infrastructures and social \nprotection schemes. \nExpansionary fiscal measures \nhave been employed to spur \neconomic activities by putting \nmore money into the hands of \nconsumers and businesses as a \nkey part of government\u2019s policy \nresponse to accelerate recovery in the wake of the last two successive economic recessions. \n \nEnsuring fiscal discipline and effective allocation of resources to the different sectors, as well \nas efficiency in MDAs service delivery, guarantees value for money for the government. FGN \nexpenditure rose by 13% from N4.95 trillion in 2023 to N5.60 trillion in Q4 2024. Recurrent \nspending increased by 26% to N4.21 trillion in Q4 2024 and accounted for 75.13% of total \noutlay. Capital spending dropped by 25% to N0.96 trillion and accounted for 17.1% of the \noverall spending. \nTo facilitate inclusive economic growth, the allocation of the capital outlay prioritizes projects \nin key infrastructure sectors like works and power, as well as in the area of human capital \ndevelopment, especially health and education. The government also invests appreciable \namount of its outlays to offer succour to the poor to cushion the effects of the unintended \nnegative but transient effects of reforms. Overall, the fiscal operations of the federal \ngovernment resulted in a deficit of N3.08 trillion in 2024. The fiscal deficit was mainly financed \nthrough domestic borrowing. A direct result of the inadequate revenues and persistent fiscal \ndeficit is the significant increase in public debt overtime. \n \n \n \n\n\n10 \n \nPublic Debt \nAs of December 31, 2024, the total public debt was US$94.2 billion, of which US$45.7 billion \nor 48.5% was external debt and US$48.4 billion or 51.4% domestic debt. The total debt \nincreased to US$97.2 billion at the end of the first quarter of 2025, with external debt at \nUS$45.9 billion and domestic debt US$51.2 billion. The FGN\u2019s share of the total public debt \nwas US$86.8 billion or 92.16% in 2024 and US$48.7 billion or 50.13% by the first quarter of \n2025. \nSource: Debt Management Office \nSustainable debt management is a key priority of the fiscal authorities in Nigeria. Borrowing \nactivities, debt servicing, and debt sustainability are managed and monitored by the Debt \nManagement Office (DMO) with the primary objective of ensuring that Nigeria's debt remains \nsustainable and does not pose a significant risk to the country's economy. \n \nThe external debts are mainly due to borrowing from the multilateral, bilateral, commercial, \nand syndicated sources. Over the years, the DMO has proactively developed more debt \ninstruments to enable the government further access the domestic debt market. This also \noffers different categories of retail, corpoarte and institutional investors, the opportinties to \ninvest in the sovereign debt instruments. While the FGN Bonds and the Nigerian Treasury \nBills are the dorminant domestic debt instruments, the Savings Bond, Green Bond, Sukuk, \nand the Promissiory Notes are also issued as part of the local debt instruments. \nWhile multilateral and bilateral loans are concessionary, the commercial and syndicated \nborrowings are on commercial terms. As at December 31, 2024, borrowings from the \nmultilateral window was US$600 million or 55.7% of the total external loans, while US$1.08 \nmillion or 4% was from bilateral sources. Borrowing on commercial terms through the \nEurobond was US$148 million or 13.8%, while the syndicated loans totalled US$280 million \nor 26% of the total external debt for 2024. Given that almost half of the borrowing are from \nconcessionary sources, it reduces the interest payment burden that would have been incurred \nfrom the commercial loans. \n \n\n\n11 \n \nThe FGN\u2019s debt profile \nincreased from N97.1 trillion as \nat December 31, 2023 to N86.8 \ntrillion as at the end of 2024. As \nof March 31, 2025, total public \ndebt stood at N149.3 trillion. \nThis figure includes domestic \ndebts of the Federal \nGovernment, States and the \nFederal Capital Territory \namounting to N51.2 trillion. \nExternal debt amounted to \nN45.9 trillion, including the \nobligations of state government \nand the FCT. \nSource: Debt Management Office \n \nThe sustainability of public debt remains a significant concern, as high debt servicing costs \nand limited fiscal space constrain the government's ability to invest in critical sectors such as \nhealthcare, education, and infrastructure. Total debt service cost in 2024 was N13.12 trillion. \nThis represents 46% of FGN\u2019s expenditure and 77.5% of revenues. Improved domestic \nresource mobilization will significantly reduce government\u2019s borrowing requirement. \n2.6 External Sector \nTrade \nForeign trade stood at N36.6 trillion in Q4 2024, total exports stood at N20 trillion, and total \nimports amounted to N16.6 trillion. Total imports increased by 13% compared to the value \nrecorded in the third quarter of 2024 (N14.67 trillion) but increased by 68.32% when compared \nto the value recorded in the corresponding quarter of 2023 (N21.75 trillion). However, by the \nend of Q1 2025, foreign trade stood at N36 trillion indicating a decline in trade activity. Total \nexports for the period amounted to N20.6 trillion while imports stood at N15.4 trillion. \nSource: National Bureau of Statistics \nCompared to Q1 2024, total trade increased by 15% in Q1 2025. Similarly, total imports \nincreased by 22% compared to the N12.6 trillion that was recorded in Q1 2024. Top five \ncountries of origin of imports to Nigeria in Q1 2024 were China, United States, Belgium, India \nand Netherlands. With total imports valued at N12.64 trillion representing 39.75% of trade \nbalance. China is the largest country of origin for Nigerian imports. The values of imports from \nthe top five countries amounted to N6.56 trillion representing a share of 51.86% of total \nimports. Premium Motor Spirit imports accounted for 20.84% (N2.63 trillion). Other imports \nincluded used Vehicles, and Automotive Gas oil (AGO). \n \nIn contrast, at the end of Q4 2023, the top five countries with imports into Nigeria included: \nSingapore (N5.09 trillion), China (N2.06 trillion), Belgium (N1.14 trillion), India (N908.59 \nbillion), and USA (N512.99 billion). However, in the first quarter of 2024, China ranked highest \namong the top trading partners on the import side, followed by India, United States of America, \nBelgium, and The Netherlands. The most traded commodities were Motor spirit ordinary, Gas \noil, Durum wheat (Not in seeds), Cane sugar meant for sugar refinery, and Other Liquefied \npetroleum gases and other gaseous hydrocarbons. \n \n\n\n12 \n \nTotal exports increased by 7.5% when compared to the amount recorded in the first quarter \nof 2025 (N20.6 \ntrillion). Total \nexports in Q1 \n2024 were \nvalued at \n\u20a619.17 trillion, \nreflecting a \n51.0% increase \ncompared to \n\u20a612.69 trillion in \nQ4 2023 and a \n195.5% rise \ncompared to \n\u20a66.49 trillion in \nQ1 2023. \n \nSource: National Bureau of Statistics \n \nThe top five export destinations in Q4, 2024 were Netherlands with N2.09 trillion representing \n10.44% of total exports, France with N1.91 trillion, Spain with N1.74 trillion, India with N1.6 \ntrillion and Indonesia with N1.41 trillion. Altogether, exports to the top five countries amounted \nto 43.67% of the total value of exports. In Q4 2023, the top five export destinations include \nNetherlands (N1.91 trillion), India (N1.10 trillion), Spain (N1.03 trillion), Canada (N907.64 \nbillion), and France (N799.77 billion). Exports to these top counties constitute about 45.3% of \ntotal exports value. While in Q1 2025, the top trading export partners were China, France, \nSpain, the Netherlands, India, United Arab Emirates and the United States of America. \n \nThe largest export product was \u2018Petroleum oils and oils obtained from bituminous minerals, \ncrude\u2019 valued at N13 trillion representing 62.89%. Others were Liquefied Natural Gas (LNG), \nliquefied\u2019 billion and Urea. However, in Q2 2024, the most exported commodities included \ncrude oil, liquefied natural gas, sesamum seeds, urea (whether or not in aqueous solution), \nand superior-quality cocoa beans. \n \n \nCapital Importation \nThere has been a steady decline in Nigeria\u2019s capital importation for four consecutive years, \nfrom an annual level of $23.99 billion in 2019 to a paltry $5.32 billion in 2022. Quarterly data \nalso showed capital importation declined by 51.5% from $2.19 billion recorded in Q4 2021 to \n$1.06 billion in Q4 2022. Trade credits, loans, currency deposits represent the largest \ncomponent of capital importation in 2022 accounting for 65.2% ($691.23 million) of total capital \nimported in Q4 2022. This was followed by Portfolio Investment with 26.9% ($285.26 million) \nand Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) with 7.94% ($84.23 million). \n \nHowever, in Q2 2024, total capital imports were US$2.60 billion, higher than US$1.03 billion \nrecorded in Q2 2023, indicating an increase of 152.81%. In comparison to the preceding \nquarter, capital importation declined by 22.85% from US$3.38 billion in Q1 2024. Compared \nto Q1 2023 (US$1.13 billion), capital imports increased by 199.1%. \n \nGovernment is implementing various policy reforms to attract capital inflows. These include \nthe ease of doing business initiatives, improvements in the regulatory environment, and efforts \n\n\n13 \n \nto diversify the economy away from oil dependency. These reforms aim to create a more \ninvestor-friendly climate and enhance the attractiveness of Nigeria as an investment \ndestination. \n \nSource National Bureau of Statistics \n \nForeign capital inflow to the domestic economy was adversely affected by the relative \nattractiveness of securities in the international financial market, particularly in the United \nStates. \nSource: National Bureau of Statistics \nIn Q1 2025, total capital importation into Nigeria stood at US$5.64 billion, higher than US$3.38 \nbillion recorded in Q1 2024, indicating an increase of 67.12 %. In comparison to the preceding \nquarter, capital importation increased by 10.86% from US$5.09 billion in Q4 2024. \nPortfolio Investment ranked top with US$5.20 billion, accounting for 92.25%, followed by Other \nInvestment with US$0.31 billion, accounting for 5.52%. Foreign Direct Investment recorded \nthe least with US$0.13 billion (2.24%) of total capital importation in Q1 2025. \nThe Banking sector recorded the highest inflow with US$3.13 billion, representing 55.44% of \ntotal capital imported in Q1 2025, followed by the Financing sector, valued at US$2.10 billion \n(37.18%), and Production/ Manufacturing sector with US$0.13 billion (2.30%). \n\n\n14 \n \nCapital Importation during the reference period originated largely from the United Kingdom \nwith US$3.68 billion, showing 65.26% of the total capital imported. This was followed by the \nRepublic of South Africa with US$0.50 billion (8.88%) and Mauritius with US$0.39 billion \n(6.99%). \n \n \n\n15 \n \n3.0 REVIEW OF 2024 BUDGET PERFORMANCE \nThe performance of key budget parameters in the 2024 fiscal year indicates a mixed picture \nof gradual economic recovery amid persistent structural and macroeconomic challenges. \nWhile the average crude oil price exceeded the budget benchmark, providing some cushion \nto oil revenue projections, actual oil production volumes fell significantly short of the budgeted \ntarget due to ongoing issues, including pipeline vandalism, oil theft, and underinvestment in \nupstream infrastructure. \nInflation remained above the projected levels, driven by exchange rate volatility, rising energy \nand food costs, and the continued effects of fuel subsidy removal. Real GDP growth also \nunderperformed relative to expectations, constrained by weak performance in critical sectors \nand limited fiscal space to stimulate growth. \nFurthermore, the 2024 fiscal year was marked by substantial depreciation of the naira, mainly \nresulting from the transition to a more market-determined exchange rate regime, external \nimbalances, and heightened demand pressures in the foreign exchange market. These \ndevelopments collectively posed significant risks to budget implementation, eroded \npurchasing power, and amplified the cost of servicing foreign-denominated obligations. \n Table 3.1: Performance of key 2024 Budget parameters \nDescription 2023 Actual 2024 Budget 2024 Actual\nOil Price Benchmark (US$/b) 82.99 77.96 80.76*\nOil Production (mbpd) 1.39 1.78 1.55+\nExchange Rate (N/$) 672.85 800.00 1,408.28^\nInflation (%) 28.92 21.40 33.18++\nGDP Growth Rate (%) 2.74 3.76 3.38**\n \n+ Average production in FY2024, exclusive of volumes for repayment of arrears. \n* Average price of Bonny Light crude as at December 2024 \n^ CBN exchange rate FY, 2024. \n++ NBS Inflation rate FY 2024, (28.92% as of December 2023); \n ** NBS FY 2024 GDP growth rate (based on the rebased GDP). \nSource: BOF, NBS, CBN, NNPC, OPEC, PRM&RC \n3.1 Revenue Outturns \n3.1.1 Oil Revenue \nIn 2024, gross oil and gas revenue amounted to N15.07 trillion, falling short of the N20.00 \ntrillion projected in the Federal Government\u2019s revenue framework and translating to a \nperformance rate of 75.3%. The shortfall reflects persistent challenges in the oil sector, \nincluding production disruptions, underinvestment in upstream operations, and oil theft, which \nlimited Nigeria\u2019s ability to meet production targets despite relatively high international crude \nprices during the year. \n \nAfter accounting for statutory deductions such as the cost of collection and other first-line \ncharges, the net oil and gas revenue transferred to the Federation Account was N12.92 trillion. \nThis represents a shortfall of N4.06 trillion, or 23.9%, when compared to the approved target. \nThis revenue underperformance not only reduced the fiscal space available for critical \ngovernment expenditure but also heightened reliance on non-oil revenue and debt to bridge \nthe fiscal gap. The outcome highlights Nigeria\u2019s continued vulnerability to volatility in the oil \n\n16 \n \nsector, underscoring the urgency of diversifying government revenue sources and \nimplementing deeper reforms to enhance efficiency in the oil and gas value chain. \nTable 3.2 2024 Revenue Performance \n3.1.2 Non-Oil Revenue \nIn contrast to the oil sector, non-\noil revenue significantly \noutperformed expectations in \n2024, both at the gross and net \nlevels. While the budget \nprojected N10.81 trillion in gross \nnon-oil taxes, actual collections \nreached N17.65 trillion, \nexceeding the target by 63.3%. \n \nThe major drivers of this \nperformance were Corporate \nIncome Tax (CIT) and Value-\nAdded Tax (VAT). CIT collections \nstood at N5.80 trillion, which \nrepresents 178.1% of the \nprojected figure, while VAT \ncollections totalled N6.72 trillion, representing 170.0% of the target. This remarkable growth \nreflects improved compliance, tighter enforcement in tax collection, and the broadening of the \ntax base despite economic headwinds. \n \nOn the other hand, Customs revenue underperformed slightly. Collections amounted to N3.36 \ntrillion, representing 98% of the target, with the shortfall attributable primarily to weaker import \nvolumes caused by high inflation and foreign exchange challenges. \n \nOverall, the strong performance of non-oil revenue provided a critical buffer against the \nunderperformance of oil receipts, helping to moderate fiscal pressures. It underscores the \nincreasing importance of Nigeria\u2019s non-oil sector in fiscal sustainability and highlights the \npotential benefits of deepening ongoing revenue mobilization reforms, especially in tax \nadministration and trade facilitation. \n3.1.3 Federation Revenue Distributable \nIn 2024, total accretions to the Federation Account stood at N22.98 trillion for the Main Pool \nand N6.27 trillion for the VAT Pool. Relative to projections, the Main Pool fell short by N961.55 \nbillion, while the VAT Pool exceeded its target by N2.61 trillion, reflecting stronger-than-\nexpected performance in VAT collections. \nIn addition, N208.15 billion was realized from the Electronic Money Transfer Levy (EMTL), \nwhich was distributed among the three tiers of government in line with the revenue allocation \nframework. \n2024 Budget Actuals \n(Jan-Dec)\nBillions of \nNaira %\nA OIL REVENUE\na Gross Oil and Gas Revenue 19,996.33 15,066.95 (4,929.38) -24.7%\nb Deductions* 3,013.03 2,143.44 (869.59) -28.9%\nc Net Oil and Gas Revenue 16,983.31 12,923.51 (4,059.79) -23.9%\nB NON-OIL REVENUE\na Non-Oil Taxes 10,808.05 17,647.56 6,839.51 63.3%\ni Corporate Tax 3,256.77 5,798.84 2,542.07 78.1%\nii VAT 3,954.12 6,721.36 2,767.24 70.0%\niii Customs 3,422.05 3,355.16 (66.89) -2.0%\niv Electronic Money Transfer \nLevy 175.11 219.11 44.00 25.1%\nv Exchange Gain 1,553.10\nb Deductions 735.84 1,083.65 347.81 47.3%\nc Net Non-Oil Taxes 10,072.21 16,563.91 6,491.70 64.5%\nd Other Revenues** 776.19 23.47 (752.72) -97.0%\ne Other deductions 120.84 118.29\nf Net Non-Oil Revenue 10,727.56 16,469.10 5,741.54 53.5%\nSource: BOF, OAGF, PRMRC\nFiscal Items\nVariance\nBillions of Naira\n* This include 13% derivation and transfer to Nigeria Police Trust Fund\n** This include revenue from Soild Minerals after derivation and NLNG Dividend\n\n17 \n \nFrom the Main Pool, the Federal \nGovernment received N12.07 trillion, while \nthe States and Local Governments received \nN6.12 trillion and N4.72 trillion, respectively. \nDistributions from the VAT Pool amounted to \nN940.87 billion for the Federal Government, \nN3.14 trillion for the States, and N2.20 trillion \nfor Local Governments. Proceeds from the \nEMTL were also shared, with the Federal \nGovernment receiving N31.22 billion, the \nStates N104.08 billion, and Local \nGovernments N72.85 billion. \nThese allocations were critical in sustaining \nsubnational fiscal operations during the year, \nespecially for States and Local Governments \nthat remain heavily dependent on statutory transfers. The strong VAT performance and steady \nEMTL inflows helped to offset the shortfall in Main Pool revenue, underscoring the growing \nrole of non-oil sources in maintaining fiscal stability across the federation. \n \n3.1.4 FGN Revenue \nTable 3.4 FGN 2024 Revenue Profile (N Billion) \nThe performance of the Federal \nGovernment of Nigeria (FGN) \nrevenue in 2024 was marginally \nbelow budget expectations, \nachieving N20.98 trillion, or \napproximately 81.1% of the \nannual target. Oil revenue \namounted to N6.26 trillion, \nrepresenting 76.6% of its \nprojected level, reflecting \nongoing production challenges \ndespite favourable crude oil \nprices. \nNon-oil tax revenue, however, \nsignificantly outperformed \nprojections, reaching N5.01 \ntrillion, 41.4% above target. \nWithin this category, Company \nIncome Tax (CIT) collections \ntotalled N2.61 trillion (176.9% of \ntarget), while Value Added Tax \n(VAT) receipts stood at N868.86 billion (169.4% of target). \nTable 3.3 Federation Account Revenue and VAT Distributable, 2024 (N Billion)\n2024 Budget Actuals \n(Jan-Dec) Variance\nBillions of \nNaira\nA Net Federation Account \nDistributable (Main Pool) 23,873.53 22,911.98 (961.55)\ni FGN's Share (52.68%) 12,576.58 12,070.03 (506.55)\nii States' Share (26.72%) 6,379.01 6,122.08 (256.93)\niii LG's Share (20.60%) 4,917.95 4,719.87 (198.08)\nB Net VAT Pool Account \nDistributable 3,663.10 6,272.48 2,609.38 \niv FGN's Share (15%) 549.46 940.87 391.41 \nv States' Share (50%) 1,831.55 3,136.24 1,304.69 \nvi Local Govt.'s Share (35%) 1,282.08 2,195.37 913.28 \nC Electronic Money Transfer \nLevy (EMTL) Distributable 174.24 208.15 33.92 \nvii FGN's Share (15%) 26.14 31.22 5.09 \nviii States' Share (50%) 87.12 104.08 16.96 \nix Local Govt.'s Share (35%) 60.98 72.85 11.87 \nSource: BOF, OAGF, PRMRC\nFiscal Items\nBillions of Naira\nFiscal Items 2024\nBudget Actual\nBillions of\nNaira %\nS/N AGGREGATE FEDERAL\nGOVERNMENT REVENUE 25,876.60 20,980.07 (1,793.56) 81.08%\nFEDERAL RETAINED REVENUES (excl.\nGOEs) 23,015.52 19,879.76 (1,047.09) 86.38%\nA\n1 Oil Revenue 8,176.28 6,260.82 (1,915.46) 76.57%\n2 Share of Dividend (NLNG, BOI, DBN,\nBOA, GB) 357.92 0.00 (357.92) 0.00%\n3 Minerals & Mining Revenue 4.56 11.33 6.77 248.37%\n4 Non-Oil Revenue: 3,545.59 5,011.25 1,442.96 141.34%\ni CIT 1,472.58 2,605.39 1,132.81 176.93%\nii VAT 512.83 868.86 356.03 169.42%\niii Customs Revenues 1,287.53 1,361.15 73.62 105.72%\niv Federation Account Levies 248.26 144.63 (103.64) 58.26%\nv Share of Electronic Money Transfer Levy 24.39 31.22 8.31 128.01%\nvi Share of Oil Price Royalty 24.17 (24.17) 0.00%\nB 7,489.95 9,696.67 5,258.09 129.46%\n1 FGN Independent Revenue 2,691.78 3,675.88 984.11 136.56%\n2 FGN Drawdowns from Special\nAccounts/Levies 300.00 1,177.50 877.50 392.50%\n3 Signature Bonus / Renewals / Early\nRenewals 251.46 369.58 118.12 146.97%\n4 Domestic Recoveries + Assets + Fines* 519.16 519.16 \n5 Grants and Donor Funding 685.63 1,206.95 2,557.03 176.04%\n6 Education Tax (TETFUND) 700.00 1,637.85 937.85 233.98%\n7 GOEs Retained Revenue 2,861.08 1,100.30 (746.47) 38.46%\n8 Other Revenue - Copyright Levy 9.45 10.79 \nC Additional Revenue (Windfalll tax, \nExchange rate differential, etc) 6,278.13 0.00 (6,223.13) 0.00%\nVariance\nBillions of Naira\nSource: BOF, OAGF\n\n18 \n \nRevenue from the Nigerian Customs Service amounted to N1.51 trillion, comprising N1.36 \ntrillion from import duties, excise duties, and fees, and N144.63 billion from special levies. The \nFGN\u2019s share of the Electronic Money Transfer Levy (EMTL) was N31.2 billion. \nOther notable revenue sources included independent revenues, which totalled N3.68 trillion; \na drawdown of N1.18 trillion from Special Accounts; a Signature Bonus of N369.58 billion; and \nEducation Tax receipts amounting to N1.64 trillion. Government-Owned Enterprises (GOEs) \ncontributed N1.10 trillion in retained revenue, while grants and aid inflows reached N1.21 \ntrillion. \n3.2 Expenditure Outturns \nTable 3.5 FGN 2024 Expenditure Performance \nThe approved expenditure for \nthe 2024 fiscal year was N35.06 \ntrillion, while actual spending \namounted to N34.49 trillion, \nrepresenting 98.4% of the \nbudgeted amount. \n \nRecurrent expenditure totalled \nN21.16 trillion, exceeding the \nbudget estimate of N19.54 \ntrillion by N1.62 trillion. Within \nthis, personnel costs and \npensions accounted for N5.46 \ntrillion. Debt service obligations \nreached N12.63 trillion, \nsurpassing the budget provision \nby N4.36 trillion. This overrun \nwas primarily driven by \nincreased interest payments \nresulting from the significant \ndepreciation of the naira. Debt \nservicing accounted for 36.6% \nof aggregate expenditure and \n60.2% of total revenue, underscoring the significant fiscal pressures resulting from debt \nobligations. \n \nCapital expenditure stood at N11.59 trillion, equivalent to 84.2% of the budgeted allocation. \nThis outcome was partly influenced by exchange rate movements affecting the naira value of \ngrants and donor-funded projects. Of the actual capital expenditure, MDAs incurred N8.05 \ntrillion in capital spending (out of which N2.23 trillion was financed in 2025), representing \n71.7% of the target as of June 2025. \n \nIt is important to note that the execution of the capital component of the 2024 budget remains \nongoing, following the National Assembly\u2019s approval to extend its implementation period until \nDecember 2025. \n \n2024\nBudget Actual\nBillions \nof\nNaira\n%\nS/N AGGREGATE FGN EXPENDITURE 35,055.54 34,494.25 (561.29) 98.40%\nFGN EXPENDITURE (excl. GOEs and\nProject-tied Loans) 32,123.10 32,801.74 678.64 102.11%\nA Statutory Transfers 1,742.79 1,742.79 0.00 100.00%\nB Recurrent Expenditure 19,539.47 21,162.29 1,622.82 108.31%\n1 Non-Debt Recurrent Expenditure 11,268.51 8,531.41 (2,737.10) 75.71%\nNon-Debt Recurrent Expenditure\n(excl. GOEs) 10,208.90 7,193.89 (3,015.01) 70.47%\ni Personnel Costs (MDAs) 4,792.05 4,792.05 0.0 100.00%\nii Personnel Costs (GOEs) 608.41 666.27 57.86 109.51%\niii Pensions & Gratuities including Service\nwide pension 673.13 448.83 (224.30) 66.68%\niv Overheads (MDAs) 673.16 671.15 (2.01) 99.70%\nv Overheads (GOEs) 451.20 671.25 220.05 148.77%\nvi Other Service Wide Votes 3,770.55 1,167.72 (2,602.83) 30.97%\nvii Presidential Amnesty 65.00 65.00 0 100.00%\niv TETFUND - Recurrent 35.00 49.14 14.14 140.40%\nv Special Intervention Programme 200.00 0.00 (200.00) 0.00%\n2 Debt Service 8,270.96 12,630.88 4,359.92 152.71%\ni Domestic Debt 5,299.70 5,704.05 404.35 107.63%\nii Foreign Debt 2,747.60 6,657.92 3,910.32 242.32%\niii Sinking Fund 223.66 265.86 42.20 118.87%\niv Interest on FGN Bonds for Securitized\nWays & Means 3.05 3.05\nC Aggregate Capital Expenditure 13,773.28 11,589.17 (2,184.11) 84.14%\ni Capital Expenditure (MDAs + Others) 11,214.82 5,814.41\nii Capital Dev. Fund (Main)-2024 in 2025 2,231.45\niii GOEs Capital Expenditure 820.91 354.99 (465.92) 43.24%\niv Grants and Donor Funding 685.63 1,206.95 521.32 176.04%\nv Multi-lateral/Bilateral Project-tied loans 1,051.91 1,981.36 929.45 188.36%\nSource: BOF, OAGF\nFiscal Items\nVariance\nBillions of Naira\n(3,168.96) 71.74%\n\n19 \n \n4.0 REVIEW OF 2025 BUDGET IMPLEMENTATION \nThe FY 2025 Budget Implementation Review indicates measured progress in both revenue \nmobilization and expenditure management. While actual collections fell slightly short of \nprorated targets, the overall fiscal trajectory remains broadly on track. Notably, key non-oil \nrevenue streams showed mixed performance, with some underperforming relative to \nprojections, while others, such as CIT and VAT, exceeded expectations. \nOn the expenditure side, releases have been cautiously managed in line with revenue outturns \nand the extended implementation of the FY 2024 Budget. This prudent approach reflects the \ngovernment\u2019s effort to balance fiscal discipline with the need to sustain essential spending \ncommitments. \nOngoing reforms in tax administration, expenditure control, and revenue diversification are \ngradually strengthening the government\u2019s capacity to mobilize resources, thereby laying the \ngroundwork for improved fiscal stability and medium-term growth. However, persistent oil \nproduction shortfalls continue to pose the single most significant risk to revenue performance, \nunderscoring the urgency of accelerating both oil sector reforms and non-oil revenue \nexpansion. \n4.1 Revenue Outturns \n4.1.1 Oil Revenue \n \nGross oil and gas revenue for \n2025 was projected at N51.04 \ntrillion. By July 2025, only N11.17 \ntrillion had been realized against \nthe prorated target of N29.78 \ntrillion, representing a \nperformance rate of just 37.5%. \n After statutory deductions, \nincluding the 13% derivation for \noil-producing states and other \nfirst-line charges, net inflows to \nthe Federation Account stood at \nN9.61 trillion, which is N15.78 \ntrillion (62.2%) below the half-\nyear target. \nNon-oil revenue also underperformed relative to projections, though with mixed outcomes \nacross specific tax heads. Corporate Income Tax (CIT) totalled N5.86 trillion, slightly above \nthe prorated projection of N5.44 trillion, indicating an overperformance of 7.6%. Value-Added \nTax (VAT) performance was stronger, with collections of N4.82 trillion, surpassing the half-\nyear target by N439.22 billion, or 10%. The Electronic Money Transfer Levy (EMTL) was \nN88.73 billion above target, a 66.1% outperformance, reflecting the rising adoption of \nelectronic transactions. \nAs of June 2025, the net non-oil revenue (including receipts from Solid Minerals) was N12.14 \ntrillion, representing a shortfall of N1.81 trillion, or 13%, against the prorated target. \nTable 4.1 2025 Revenue Performance (Jan\u2013July)\n2025 Budget Prorata Actuals \n(Jan-July)\nBillions of \nNaira %\nA OIL REVENUE 7\na Gross Oil and Gas Revenue 51,043.26 29,775.24 11,169.63 (18,605.61) -62.5%\nb Deductions* 7,516.74 4,384.77 1,563.46 (2,821.30) -64.3%\nc Net Oil and Gas Revenue 43,526.52 25,390.47 9,606.16 (15,784.31) -62.2%\nB NON-OIL REVENUE\na Non-Oil Taxes 24,217.13 14,126.66 13,340.28 (786.38) -5.6%\ni Corporate Tax 9,329.76 5,442.36 5,857.96 415.60 7.6%\nii VAT 7,501.60 4,375.93 4,815.15 439.22 10.0%\niii Customs 7,155.77 4,174.20 2,369.38 (1,804.82) -43.2%\niv Electronic Money Transfer \nLevy 230.00 134.17 222.90 88.73 66.1%\nv Exchange Gain 74.89\nb Deductions 1,549.96 904.14 1,065.49 161.35 17.8%\nc Net Non-Oil Taxes 22,667.17 13,222.51 12,274.78 (947.73) -7.2%\nd Other Revenues** 1,544.44 900.93 33.26 (867.66) -96.3%\ne Other deductions 303.92 177.29 171.62\nf Net Non-Oil Revenue 23,907.69 13,946.15 12,136.43 (1,809.72) -13.0%\nFiscal Items\nVariance\nBillions of Naira\n* This include 13% derivation and transfer to Nigeria Police Trust Fund\n** This include revenue from Soild Minerals after derivation and NLNG Dividend\n\n20 \n \nOverall, while VAT and EMTL provided some relief, their overperformance was insufficient to \noffset the deep shortfall in oil revenues and weaker-than-expected CIT collections. The mid-\nyear outcome highlights Nigeria\u2019s continued fiscal vulnerability to oil sector underperformance, \neven as non-oil revenue sources gradually increase their contribution to the Federation \nAccount. \n \n4.1.2 Federation and VAT Pool Accounts Distributable \nTotal distributable revenue from \nthe Federation Account \u2013 Main \nPool amounted to N17.03 trillion. \nOf this, the Federal Government \nreceived N8.97 trillion, while the \nStates and Local Governments \nreceived N4.55 trillion and N3.51 \ntrillion, respectively, in line with the \nexisting revenue allocation \nformula. \nFrom the VAT Pool Account, the \nFederal Government received \nN675.11 billion, the States N2.25 \ntrillion, and the Local Governments \nN1.58 trillion. In addition, N211.75 \nbillion was distributed from the \nElectronic Money Transfer Levy \n(EMTL) Account across the three tiers of government. \nThe combination of Main Pool, VAT, and EMTL inflows provided a measure of fiscal stability, \nhelping to cushion the impact of oil revenue shortfalls recorded during the period. \n \n4.1.3 FGN Revenue \nTable 4.3 2025 FGN Revenue Profile (Jan\u2013July) N Billion \nAs of July 2025, the Federal \nGovernment of Nigeria (FGN) \nrealized N13.67 trillion in \nretained revenue, representing \n66.8% of the full-year target of \nN23.85 trillion. \nThe FGN\u2019s share of oil revenue \nstood at N4.64 trillion, \nachieving only 37.8% of its \nprorated target, reflecting \npersistent production and \nmarket challenges in the oil \nsector. In contrast, non-oil tax \nrevenues performed relatively \nstrongly, totalling N4.27 trillion, \nor 86.3% of the half-year target. \n2025 Budget Prorata Actuals \n(Jan-July) Variance\nBillions of \nNaira\nA Net Federation Account \nDistributable (Main Pool) 60,255.88 35,149.26 17,030.12 (18,119.14)\ni FGN's Share (52.68%) 31,742.80 18,516.63 8,971.47 (9,545.16)\nii States' Share (26.72%) 16,100.37 9,391.88 4,550.45 (4,841.43)\niii LG's Share (20.60%) 12,412.71 7,240.75 3,508.20 (3,732.54)\nB Net VAT Pool Account \nDistributable 6,949.48 4,053.86 4,500.72 446.85 \niv FGN's Share (15%) 1,042.42 608.08 675.11 67.03 \nv States' Share (50%) 3,474.74 2,026.93 2,250.36 223.43 \nvi Local Govt.'s Share (35%) 2,432.32 1,418.85 1,575.25 156.40 \nC Electronic Money Transfer \nLevy (EMTL) Distributable 228.85 133.50 211.75 78.26 \nvii FGN's Share (15%) 34.33 20.02 31.76 11.74 \nviii States' Share (50%) 114.43 66.75 105.88 39.13 \nix Local Govt.'s Share (35%) 80.10 46.72 74.11 27.39 \nSource: BOF, OAGF, PRMRC\nTable 4.2 Federation Account Revenue and VAT Distributable, 2025 (N \nBillion)\nFiscal Items\nBillions of Naira\n2025 Budget Pro Rata \n(Jan - July)\nActual \n(Jan - July)\nBillions of \nNaira\n% \nShortfall\nS/N\nAGGREGATE FEDERAL GOVERNMENT \nREVENUE 40,889.74 23,852.35 13,665.56 (10186.78) -42.7%\nFEDERAL RETAINED REVENUES (excl. GOEs) 38,019.47 22,178.02 12,362.60 (9815.43) -44.3%\nA\n1 Oil Revenue 21,004.81 12,252.81 4,635.69 (7617.11) -62.2%\n2 Share of Dividend (NLNG, BOI, DBN, BOA,\nGB) 734.93 428.71 104.64 (324.07) -75.6%\n3 Minerals & Mining Revenue 15.49 9.03 16.05 7.02 77.7%\n4 Non-Oil Revenue: 8,481.36 4,947.46 4,270.60 (676.86) -13.7%\ni CIT 4,262.50 2,486.46 2,544.50 58.04 2.3%\nii VAT 972.93 567.54 630.10 62.56 11.0%\niii Customs Revenues 2,781.34 1622.45 988.29 (634.16) -39.1%\niv Federation Account Levies 430.14 250.91 75.08 (175.83) -70.1%\nv NPTF Levy 2.43 1.41 0.87 (0.54) -38.5%\nvi Share of Electronic Money Transfer Levy 32.04 18.69 31.76 13.07 70.0%\nvii Share of Oil Price Royalty 386.57 225.50 0.00 (225.50) -100.0%\nB\n1 FGN Independent Revenue 5,261.70 3,069.32 1,499.31 (1570.01) -51.2%\n2 FGN Drawdowns from Special\nAccounts/Levies 300.00 175.00 - (175.00) -100.0%\n3 Signature Bonus / Renewals / Early Renewals 0.00 0.00 436.82 436.82\n4 Domestic Recoveries + Assets + Fines* 82.70 48.24 0.00 (48.24) -100.0%\n5 Grants and Donor Funding 761.91 444.44 608.95 164.51 37.0%\n6 Education Tax (TETFUND) 990.00 577.50 790.53 213.03 36.9%\n7 GOEs Retained Revenue 2,870.27 1,674.33 1,302.97 (371.36) -22.2%\nVariance\nBillions of Naira\n\n21 \n \nN2.54 trillion was collected as CIT, representing 2.3% over the prorated target. VAT \ncollections amounted to N630.10 billion, exceeding the target by 11.0%. Customs revenue \nrecorded N1.06 trillion against a prorated projection of N1.87 trillion, translating to 56.8% \nperformance. Other revenues contributed N4.64 trillion, including N1.50 trillion from FGN \nIndependent Revenue. \nOverall, while non-oil revenue taxes, particularly VAT and CIT, showed resilience and in some \ncases surpassed expectations, the weaker performance of oil revenues and customs duties \ncontinued to weigh heavily on the Federal Government\u2019s fiscal position, underscoring the need \nto strengthen non-oil revenue mobilization and improve efficiency in revenue collection. \n4.2 Expenditure Outturns \nTable 4.4 2025 FGN Expenditure Performance (Jan-July) \nFor the 2025 fiscal year, \naggregate expenditure is \nprojected at N54.99 trillion. \nBy the end of July, the pro-\nrata spending target was \nN32.08 trillion; however, \nactual spending amounted \nto N20.40 trillion, indicating \nsignificant \nunderperformance. \nOf the actual spending, \nN9.81 trillion was applied to \ndebt service and N4.51 \ntrillion to personnel costs, \nincluding pensions. Debt \nservicing alone exceeded its \npro-rata target by 17.5%, \nwith both domestic and \nexternal debt obligations \nsurpassing their respective \nbenchmarks by 10.9% and \n28.7%. This trend reflects rising debt pressures and the increasing share of government \nresources allocated to interest payments. \nCapital expenditure implementation was notably weak. Only N834.80 billion had been \nreleased to Ministries, Departments, and Agencies (MDAs) out of the pro-rata capital budget \nof N10.81 trillion, indicating less than 10% performance at the review period. The low capital \nexpenditure is mainly due to the effort to meet the 2024 capital budget, which was extended \nto December 2025. Overall, the total capital expenditure reached N3.60 trillion as of July 2025, \nrepresenting a shortfall of 73.7% of the target for the first seven months. \n2025 Budget Pro Rata \n(Jan - July)\nActual \n(Jan - July)\nBillions of \nNaira\n% \nShortfall\nS/N AGGREGATE FGN EXPENDITURE 54,990.17 32,077.60 20,395.51 11,682.08 36.4%\nFGN EXPENDITURE (excl. GOEs and Project-\ntied Loans) 48,895.36 28,522.29 17,126.92 11,395.37 40.0%\nA Statutory Transfers 3,645.76 2,126.69 1,117.32 1,009.37 47.5%\nB Recurrent Expenditure 27,905.15 16,278.01 15,679.60 598.40 3.7%\n1 Non-Debt Recurrent Expenditure 13,588.01 7,926.34 5,865.51 2,060.83 26.0%\nNon-Debt Recurrent Expenditure (excl. GOEs) 11,678.39 6,812.40 4,751.57 2,060.83 30.3%\ni Personnel Costs (MDAs) 7,595.67 4,430.81 3,913.00 517.81 11.7%\nii Personnel Costs (GOEs) 1,017.41 593.49 593.49 - 0.0%\niii Pensions & Gratuities including Service wide \npension 1,444.02 842.34 445.67 396.68 47.1%\niv Overheads (MDAs) 1,216.34 709.53 249.82 459.71 64.8%\nv Overheads (GOEs) 892.20 520.45 520.45 - 0.0%\nvi Other Service Wide Votes 1,057.87 617.09 56.73 560.36 90.8%\nvii Presidential Amnesty 115.00 67.08 62.65 4.44 6.6%\nviii TETFUND - Recurrent 49.50 28.88 23.72 5.16 17.9%\nix Special Intervention Programme 200.00 116.67 - 116.67 100.0%\n2 Debt Service 14,317.14 8,351.67 9,814.09 (1,462.42) -17.5%\ni Domestic Debt 7,191.20 4,194.86 4,651.87 (457.00) -10.9%\nii Foreign Debt 6,748.65 3,936.71 5,065.53 (1,128.82) -28.7%\niii Sinking Fund 377.30 220.09 96.70 123.39 56.1%\niv Interest on FGN Bonds for Securitized Ways & \nMeans - - \nC Aggregate Capital Expenditure 23,439.25 13,672.90 3,598.59 10,074.31 73.7%\ni Capital Expenditure (MDAs + Others) 18,532.16 10,810.42 834.80 9,975.62 92.3%\nii GOEs Capital Expenditure 820.91 478.86 478.86 - 0.0%\niii Grants and Donor Funding 721.91 421.11 609.13 (188.02) -44.6%\niv Multi-lateral/Bilateral Project-tied loans 3,364.28 1,962.50 1,675.79 286.71 14.6%\nFiscal Items\nVariance\nBillions of Naira\n\n22 \n \n5.0 ASSUMPTIONS UNDERLYING 2026 - 2028 REVENUE PROJECTIONS \n5.1 Economic Growth \nThe Nigerian economy is projected to grow in real terms by 4.7% in 2026, up from the 3.38% \nrecorded post-rebasing in 2024 and 4.6% estimated for 2025. Through increased \nproductivity and strategic investments, real GDP is expected to accelerate to 6% by 2027, \nreaching 7.9 in 2028 and over 9% by 2030. This growth trajectory aligns with the Renewed \nHope Agenda\u2019s eight Priority Areas, and positions Nigeria to achieve the $1 trillion economy \ntarget. The projected real GDP growth is expected to be driven by strong services, a rebound \nin agriculture, and improved industrial activity amid a more stable domestic environment, \nfavourable global economic conditions and the present Administration's overall development \npolicy thrust. In dollar terms, the country\u2019s GDP is expected to increase from US$348.4 billion \nin 2023 to US$363.8 billion, projected for 2026. \n5.2 Inflation Rate \nInflation is projected to decline remarkably in the medium term, from an average of 16.5% in \n2026 to 13.0% in 2027, and 9.0% in 2028 \u00b12.0. As of July 2025, inflation rate stood at 21.9% \nbut averaged 23.2% from January to July. Key drivers supporting the price outlook include: \n\u2022 Foreign exchange stability, stemming from ongoing Central Bank reform measures. \n\u2022 Tight monetary stance, reflecting the Central Bank's firm commitment to price stability. \n\u2022 Enhanced fiscal-monetary policy coordination. \n\u2022 Improved fiscal spending allocation. \n\u2022 Easing supply pressures and increased agricultural production. \n\u2022 Falling transportation costs. \n\u2022 Transition to inflation-targeting framework and ongoing fiscal reforms. \n\u2022 Use of public borrowings for productive supply-side investments. \n\u2022 Election-Related Spending in 2026. \n5.3 Exchange Rate \nThe exchange rate exerts significant influence on economic projections in Nigeria as it impacts \noil revenues, non-oil foreign trade, and foreign currency expenditures. The exchange rate also \naffects foreign currency-denominated expenditures like Foreign Missions, Immigration \nattach\u00e9, Defence Missions, and counterpart funding for GAVI \u2013 the Vaccine Alliance. \nThe CBN expects the Naira exchange rate to be generally stable over the medium term, \noscillating from an average of N1,500 per US$ in 2025 to N1,512 per US$ in 2026, and \nappreciating to N1,432.15 per US$ in 2027. This expectation is based on more efficient foreign \nexchange market, appropriate monetary policy stance, and improved foreign exchange supply \ndue to sustained rebound of economic activities, growing non-oil exports, stronger remittances \ninflow, and increased domiciliary account conversions. Current efforts towards effective oil \nsector management are expected to increase oil production and significantly contribute to the \nachievement of the medium-term target. Measures are in place to prevent speculative \nactivity and the central bank has embedded best practices into the market\u2019s \noperational framework, including foreign exchange code of conduct and revised foreign \n\n23 \n \nexchange manual. \n5.4 Oil Revenue Assumptions \n5.4.1 Crude Oil Price Benchmark \nThe assumptions about the prices of key commodities, \nsuch as oil, gas, and minerals, will impact export \nrevenues. In consultation with relevant stakeholders, a \nbenchmark crude oil price of US$64.90 per barrel has \nbeen proposed for 2026. The estimate is about 14% lower \nthan the US$75.00 per barrel in 2025. The baseline \nforecast prices used are the latest year-to-date averages \nbased on the submission from the Nigerian Upstream \nPetroleum Regulatory Commission (NUPRC). The \nestimation took account of crude oil market fundamentals, \nmarket sentiments and global economic outlook. To \nensure budget realism, the budget crude oil benchmark \nprice was set considering forecasts. \nAs Figure 5.1 shows, Bonny light crude oil price has declined from an average of US$84.35 \nper barrel in 2024 to an average of US$71.35 per barrel between January and September \n2025. The World Bank projects the price of Brent crude oil to average US$64 per barrel in \n2025, trending afterwards to an average of US$60 per barrel in 2026 assuming there are no \nprotracted trade disruptions in energy commodities and considering the trade-off the Eight \ncore OPEC+ producers face between maintaining market share and accepting a lower oil \nprice. \n5.4.2 Crude Oil Production and Demand \nOPEC expects global oil demand to increase by 4.8 mbpd over the medium-term \nperiod, rising from 105.0 mbpd in 2025 to 109.8 mbpd by 2028. This represents an \naverage annual increase of 1.6 mbpd. This growth pattern is expected to be driven largely by \nstrong and persistent crude oil demand growth in non-OECD countries. Key factors underlying \nthe OECD demand include rising population growth, urbanization and continued robust \neconomic growth. \nDespite current uncertainties surrounding the global economy and energy landscape, \nOPEC projects global oil supply to continually expand to meet rising oil demand. \nMarket fundamentals are projected to remain supportive, as OPEC and its partners in \nthe Declaration of Cooperation, and industry stakeholders work together to maintain market \nstability. \nIn the medium term, global liquids supply is expected to increase by 11.2 mbpd from 102.4 \nmbpd in 2024 to 113.6 mbpd in 2030. Global liquids supply growth in the medium term \nis expected to remain dominated by US liquids production, particularly by the ongoing \nrise in tight oil. Continued efficiency gains in production techniques, as well as \nsustained investment, are expected to result in continued liquids production. Several \nestablished producers, including Canada, Qatar and Norway, are also expected to \ncontribute additional medium-term supplies alongside newer \u2018frontier\u2019 areas, including \nBrazilian ultra-deepwater and Argentinian tight oil. Additionally, barrels are anticipated \n0\n20\n40\n60\n80\n100\nJan-24\nMar-24\nMay-24\nJul-24\nSep-24\nNov-24\nJan-25\nMar-25\nMay-25\nJul-25\nSep-25\nFigure 5.1: Bonny Light \nCrude Oil Price (US$/Barrel)\n\n24 \n \nfrom newcomers - Senegal, Suriname, Uganda and Namibia. \nRecent forecasts suggest that global crude liquids supply is likely to outstrip demand in the \nmedium term. Energy Information Administration (EIA) forecasts that growing global oil supply \nand the transition away from peak summer seasonal demand will lead to significant growth in \nglobal oil inventories, causing crude oil prices to fall in the coming months. Brent crude oil spot \nprice is expected to fall to an average of $62 per barrel in the fourth quarter of 2025 and $52 \nper barrel in the first half of 2026. Inventory builds are forecasted to average 2.6 million barrels \nper day in the fourth quarter of 2025 and to remain elevated through 2026, putting significant \ndownward pressure on oil prices. \nIn Nigeria, Hydrocarbon is currently extracted from 323 developed fields located in onshore \nand offshore terrains. These fields, which either contain Crude Oil, Condensates or Natural \nGas reservoirs, are connected to 265 production processing stations, after which the stabilised \nOil and Gas are exported via 31 export terminals. The onshore processing infrastructures are \nlinked to 8 crude oil/condensates and NGLs export terminals through pipelines that span 5,284 \nkm. Some of these delivery pipelines connected to the five onshore export terminals are \nutilised by both the asset operators and third-party oil producers, for transportation, storage \nand lifting of Crude Oil blends through export or delivery to domestic refineries. \nThe significance of crude oil and gas production in the Nigerian economy has reduced \nsignificantly in recent times. The contribution of the sector to the real GDP declined from 8.78% \nin 2019 to 5.7% in 2023, and 4.05% in the second quarter of 2025. Nevertheless, Oil and Gas \nremains a key sector in the economy. \nFrom 1.81 million barrels a day (mbpd) in 2016, average crude oil and condensate production \n(net of incremental production for Repayment Arrears) peaked at 2.09 mbpd in 2018 but \ndeclined thereafter to 1.7 mbpd in 2020 and 1.28 mbpd in 2022. As Figure 5.2 shows, actual \ndaily crude oil production has trended \nbelow budget projections since 2013. The \ndivergence reflects production challenges \nsuch as crude oil theft, shutdown of flow \nstations for pipeline leakage repairs, \nterminal maintenance, and flare \nmanagement. \nHowever, Oil and gas production has \nimproved recently. Data from the Nigerian \nUpstream Regulatory Commission \n(NUPRC) shows that a total of 454.28 \nmillion barrels of crude oil and condensate \nwas produced between January and \nSeptember 2025. This represents an \naverage of 1.66 mbpd as against the output benchmark of 2.12 million barrels per day. \nAverage crude oil production in September 2025 was 1.58 million barrels per day, comprising \nof both Crude oil (1.39 mbpd) and condensate (0.19 mbpd). This represents 93% of OPEC \nquota (1.5 mbpd). The recent improvement in crude oil production is a direct result of strategic \ninterventions aimed at enhanced security measures and improved operational efficiencies \nacross the upstream value chain. Current efforts aim at providing enabling environment that \nwill attract fresh investments and encourage existing investors to scale up. The target is to \nincrease crude production to 2.1 mbpd by 2026. \n0.00\n0.50\n1.00\n1.50\n2.00\n2.50\n3.00Million Barrels Per Day\nFigure 5.2: Actual and Benchmark Crude Oil \nOutput in Nigeria\nOil Output Output Benchmark\n\n25 \n \nNigeria\u2019s crude oil production capacity in 2026 is estimated at 2.25mbpd. Following \nconsultations with stakeholders, crude oil production is projected to increase to 1.84 million \nbarrels per day in 2026, down from the 2025 projection of 2.12mbpd. Of the 1.84 mbpd, 1.5 \nmbpd represents the OPEC production quota, while 340 kbpd is the production of \nCondensates. The target is to increase crude oil production to over 2 million barrels per day, \nsustained through 2027 and attain 3 mbpd by 2030. Gas production is expected to grow to \n10bcf per day by 2027 and 12bcf by 2030. The optimistic crude oil production outlook reflects \nthe ambitious post-PIA aspirations of the sector and expectations of significant improvements \nin domestic crude oil production following the effective implementation of sectoral reforms. \nTo boost crude oil production in the medium term, key reforms are being implemented to \naddress the challenges of oil theft, pipeline vandalism, infrastructure deficiencies, and \ninvestment shortages. The NNPC Command and Control Centre has been upgraded to \nmonitor oil and gas installations and improve coordination between security forces and \noperators. In addition, there is increasing investment and localization in onshore and shallow \nwater. Interest in offshore exploration is being stimulated through improved investment \nenvironment. Already, over $8 billion in new investments have been committed to oil and gas \nprojects. \nThese measures are expected to enhance production capacity, optimize resource \nmanagement, and attract investment to the oil sector. Security will be further enhanced in oil \nproduction areas to protect oil installations and curb illegal activities. Advanced technologies \nand better surveillance systems will be employed to monitor oil flows and detect sabotage in \nreal-time. The new NNPC management is focusing on improving the business structure, \nconducting independent value assessments to inform data-driven decisions, enforcing a \nrobust performance management framework, and increasing transparency. \n5.5 Non-Oil Revenue Baseline Assumptions \nThe assumptions underlying the non-oil revenue forecasts for the period 2026-2028 are \noutlined below. In view of the low revenue from crude oil, Government will implement various \nreform measures to further broaden the revenue base, modernize and further improve tax \nadministration, and enhance non-oil revenue collections. Efforts will be geared towards \nensuring better compliance and closing loopholes. This involves bringing more businesses \nand individuals into the tax net, particularly in the informal sector, which has historically been \nunder-taxed. \nThe medium-term non-oil revenue forecasts are based on effective implementation of these \nreform efforts in order to enhance the contribution of non-oil revenue sources to funding the \nFGN budget. The medium-term non-oil revenue estimates were estimated based on \nanticipated growth in the different tax bases, the effective tax rate, and the projected tax \ncollection efficiency. Tax rates are assumed to remain the same in 2026. Tax revenue \nprojections are based on forecast crude oil production, benchmark crude oil price, and \nexchange rate. Technical costs (T1 and T2) are based on 3-year average (2022-2024). \nHistorical tax revenue collection for three years was used to arrive at the forecast values for \nthe period. \nCustoms Collections: Import Duties, Excise, Fees and Special Levies \nImport duty projections are based on the cost, insurance and freight (CIF) value of imports, \napplicable tariffs, and a projected efficiency factor. The growth of the nominal tax base is \n\n26 \n \nassumed to be driven by tax elasticity in the medium term. Other considerations include the \nforeign exchange rate, effective implementation of extant tax laws, and the implementation of \nthe Common External Tariff (CET) 2022-2026. The exchange rate is expected to vary \nminimally in 2026 within the average of the current rate in 2025. \nRevenue collections by the Nigeria Customs Service is expected to be driven by improved \nefficiency, enhanced port operations, reduction of manual/paper\u2010based processing, and \nimprovements in cargo clearance times. Recent reforms include speedier inspection, the \nintroduction of fast-track lanes for compliant importers, and streamlining processes in major \nports like Apapa and Tin Can. \nMore importantly, the effective implementation of the National Single Window project and \nlarge-scale digital overhaul of customs processing, surveillance, and payments under the E-\nCustoms Modernisation Project are expected to significantly facilitate transparent trade, and \nenhance customs revenue and efficiency. In addition, anti-smuggling efforts are being \nintensified, recovery from undervalued or under-assessed imports has increased and revenue \nleakages are being identified and acted upon. \nCompanies Income Tax (CIT) \nThe CIT projections are based on estimated nominal GDP, Companies\u2019 Profitability Ratio, and \nfurther improvement in collection efficiency. The Gross Operating Surplus of firms used for \nCIT forecast is projected to average \u20a638.92 trillion in 2026, \u20a645.77 trillion in 2027, and \u20a651.26 \ntrillion in 2028, after adjustments for informal sector firms and enterprises. Estimates were \nderived taking into consideration significant growth of domestic economic activities as well as \nthe consolidation of ongoing reforms and effective implementation of the Renewed Hope \nDevelopment Plan 2026-2030. Other important assumptions include significant improvement \nin the Nigerian business and investment environment, expected formalisation of businesses \narising from the ongoing tax reforms and successful broadening of the tax net. \nUnder the Nigerian Tax Act (NTA) 2025, Education Tax, Nigerian Police Trust Fund Levy, \nNational Agency for Science and Engineering Infrastructure Levy and the National Information \nTechnology Development Fund Levy have been replaced with Development Levy charged at \n4% of the company\u2019s assessable profit. Similarly, Capital Gains Tax has been discontinued, \nwith gains from the disposal of chargeable assets now subsumed under CIT. \nValue Added Tax (VAT) \nThe Value Added Tax collection in the medium term was projected using estimated aggregate \nnominal consumption, taking into account vatable items and collection efficiency. VATable \nconsumption expenditure is projected to increase from an average average of \u20a6446,309.37 \ntrillion in 2026, \u20a6525,936.08 trillion in 2027, and \u20a6642,245.15 trillion in 2028, after accounting \nfor exemptions, zero-rated items, and companies below the \u20a650 million turnover threshold. \nThe Nigerian Tax (2025) allows taxpayers to claim input VAT on services and fixed assets, \npreviously disallowed. This will likely increase VAT refund claims and slow the growth of Non-\nimport VAT revenue from 2026 onwards. Like the CIT, more VAT payers are expected to be \nbrought into the tax net with the effective implementation of reform measures. The increase in \nfirm turnover threshold from N25 million to N50 million and the reduced FGN share from 15% \nto 10% may further reduce the growth of VAT collections. \nThe VAT projections over the medium-term are based on holding the rate at 7.5%. Raising \nthe VAT rate however remains a medium-term policy option. In the medium term, Government \nwill further intensify efforts aimed at improving VAT coverage and collection efficiency. Wider \n\n27 \n \ncoverage and improved collection efficiency will be achieved through nationwide VAT \nregistration and monitoring, and deployment of ICT (auto-collect) platforms in more sectors of \nthe economy. \nTax refund projections were made based on the number and amount of refund applications \nreceived and processed in the last 2 years, macroeconomic factors including exchange rate \nand inflation, taxpayers who are perpetually in refund position by virtue of legislations and \nincreased taxpayers\u2019 awareness with regards to tax refunds \nStamp Duty \nUnder the Nigeria Tax Act (2025), the Electronic Money Transfer Levy (EMTL) has been \nrenamed Stamp Duty, with liability shifting from the receiver to the sender. This change, along \nwith the removal of the 1% duty on government contracts, may reduce overall stamp duty \ncollections going forward. Compliance with the approved Regulations governing the \nadministration of Stamp Duties will be enforced to ensure full collections over the medium \nterm. Stamp duty is estimated based on the duties payable on chargeable instruments \nincluding tax stamps, electronic tagging, electronic receipts, and certificate issuance. \nFGN Independent Revenue \nThe Independent revenue of the Federal Government was estimated taking into consideration \nrecent efforts aimed at addressing revenue leakages, excessive spending and weak \naccountability of Government-Owned Enterprises (GOEs). The estimation of Operating \nSurpluses (the main component of FGN Independent Revenues) is based on strict and \neffective implementation of the various measures introduced to ensure that GOEs operate in \na more fiscally responsible manner. Additional measures and tighter performance \nmanagement framework will be introduced to ensure greater fiscal discipline among the GOEs \nand substantially improve remittances in the short to medium term. Accordingly, independent \nrevenue collections are expected to be considerably higher than projections in the medium \nterm. \n \n \n \n \n \n\n28 \n \n6.0 MEDIUM-TERM MACROECONOMIC FRAMEWORK: PARAMETERS AND \nTARGETS FOR 2026 - 2028 \n6.1 Macroeconomic Projections \nThe medium-term outlook points to a steady improvement in key macroeconomic indicators, \nreflecting a gradual but sustained rebound of the Nigerian economy. This trajectory is \nunderpinned by the government\u2019s ongoing reforms aimed at strengthening fiscal sustainability, \nenhancing productivity, and stimulating inclusive growth across all sectors. While the \ninternational oil market is expected to remain relatively stable, the oil price benchmark is \nprojected to moderate slightly in response to global supply adjustments and the dynamics of \nthe energy transition. The exchange rate is projected to average around \u20a61,442 per US dollar \nover the medium term, reflecting the anticipated effects of continued foreign exchange market \nreforms, improved external reserves, and stronger non-oil export performance. \nTable 6.1: Key Parameters and other Macroeconomic Projections \n \nSource: Ministry of Budget & Economic Planning; NUPRC; BOF; NBS \nThe projected economic growth rate under the 2026\u20132028 Medium-Term Expenditure \nFramework (MTEF) indicates a steady and sustained expansion of the Nigerian economy over \nthe medium term. Real Gross Domestic Product (GDP) is expected to grow by 4.7% in 2026, \naccelerating to 6.2% in 2027 and reaching 7.9% by 2028. This positive outlook reflects the \nanticipated impact of ongoing structural reforms and increased capital investments in key \ngrowth-enabling sectors. \nGrowth will be primarily driven by enhanced infrastructure spending, expansion in agriculture, \nand investments in social services aimed at improving human capital outcomes. While the \neconomy remains largely consumption-driven, the bulk of real GDP growth during the period \nis expected to emanate from the non-oil sector, particularly telecommunications, crop \nproduction, trade, and services, reflecting sustained diversification efforts. The implementation \nof the Renewed Hope Infrastructure Development Fund (RHIDF) is projected to catalyse \n\n\n29 \n \npublic and private sector investments, enhancing productivity, job creation, and overall \ncompetitiveness. \nThe oil sector is also expected to see a boost as investments increase, following more assets \nswitching into the new fiscal terms and fields\u2019 re-entry programmes begin to yield results. The \nre-entry of key players and new bid rounds is boosting technical competence, capital injection, \nand renewed confidence in our upstream environment. These developments materially \nexpand our capacity to ramp up production to the target levels of 2.06 mbpd, 2.10 mbpd, and \n2.14 mbpd over the medium term. Despite these target levels, the medium-term fiscal \nframework is prepared on conservative estimates of 1.84 mbpd, 1.85 mbpd, and 1.89 mbpd \nfor 2026, 2027, and 2028, respectively. Any revenue above the benchmark oil output will be \nescrowed to strengthen fiscal discipline, improve cash management, and build stabilisation \nbuffers. \nNominal consumption expenditure is projected to rise from \u20a6446.31 trillion in 2026 to \u20a6525.94 \ntrillion in 2027 and \u20a6642.25 trillion in 2028, driven by expected increases in disposable income \nfollowing the implementation of a new national minimum wage, expanded social protection \nprogrammes, and targeted cash transfers to vulnerable households. These factors, combined \nwith exchange rate adjustments and energy reforms, are expected to exert moderate upward \npressure on prices. Consequently, the headline inflation rate is projected to average 16.5% in \n2026, 13.0% in 2027, and 9.0% in 2028, as supply-side interventions take effect. \nImports of goods are expected to follow a similar upward trend, reflecting higher domestic \ndemand for capital and intermediate goods. Import values are projected at \u20a682.31 trillion in \n2026, increasing to \u20a695.65 trillion in 2027 and \u20a6111.27 trillion in 2028, in line with anticipated \ngrowth in productive activities and infrastructure investments. \n6.2 2025-2027 Medium-Term Fiscal Framework (MTFF) \n6.2.1 Federation Account Revenues \nIn the medium term, Federation Account revenues are projected to moderate, reflecting \nadjustments to key assumptions in the oil sector. Both crude oil production and the benchmark \noil price have been revised downward to align with prevailing sector realities and the ongoing \nvolatility in global energy markets driven by geopolitical tensions. While non-oil tax revenues \nare expected to record steady growth, these gains will be insufficient to offset the anticipated \nshortfall in oil-related inflows fully. \nFor the 2026 fiscal year, total net accruals to the Federation Account are projected at \u20a650.74 \ntrillion. Of this amount, \u20a641.06 trillion is expected to flow into the Federation Account Main \nPool, while \u20a69.23 trillion and \u20a6456.07 billion are projected to accrue to the VAT Pool and \nStamp Duty Pool, respectively. \nOil revenues are estimated at \u20a625.24 trillion, accounting for about 49.7% of total Federation \nAccount receipts and 61.5% of Main Pool inflows. Other revenue components in the Main Pool \ninclude Corporate Tax (\u20a610.49 trillion), Customs Revenues (\u20a64.30 trillion), Special Levies \n(\u20a6847.03 billion), NLNG Dividend (\u20a6114.31 billion), and Solid Minerals (\u20a674.36 billion). \nFrom the Federation Account Main Pool, the Federal Government is projected to receive \n\u20a621.63 trillion, while the State and Local Governments are expected to receive \u20a610.97 trillion \n\n30 \n \nand \u20a68.46 trillion, respectively. \nFor the VAT Pool, revenue allocations are projected at \u20a6922.53 billion for the Federal \nGovernment, \u20a65.07 trillion for the States, and \u20a63.23 trillion for Local Governments. From \nStamp Duty collections, the Federal Government is expected to receive \u20a645.61 billion, while \nthe States and Local Governments are projected to receive \u20a6250.84 billion and \u20a6159.62 \nbillion, respectively. \nOverall, the revenue outlook reflects the government\u2019s commitment to maintaining fiscal \nrealism, improving the efficiency of non-oil revenue mobilization, and reducing the economy\u2019s \nvulnerability to oil market fluctuations. The medium-term strategy will therefore continue to \nprioritize diversification, tax administration reforms, and sustainable expenditure planning to \nstrengthen fiscal resilience and support inclusive economic growth. \nTable 6.2: Federation Account Revenue \u2013 Main Pool, VAT and Stamp Duty (2026 \u2013 2028) \n2025 NASS \nApproval \n2026 Budget \nProposal 2027 2028\n60,558,646,846,741 46,093,028,808,310 50,721,322,825,731 55,975,104,704,265 \n302,768,234,234 230,465,144,042 253,606,614,129 279,875,523,521 \n1,200,000,000,000 1,200,000,000,000 1,200,000,000,000 \n3,600,000,000,000 3,600,000,000,000 3,600,000,000,000 \n60,255,878,612,507 41,062,563,664,268 45,667,716,211,602 50,895,229,180,744 \n43,308,886,646,186 25,236,385,045,244 26,127,971,127,009 26,159,061,935,839 \n31,928,259,692 74,364,759,025 84,210,943,495 28,686,907,119 \n1,499,819,220,000 114,310,494,260 109,428,570,880 106,729,471,381 \n8,788,655,389,463 10,494,086,430,555 13,475,263,395,808 17,713,894,230,997 \n5,734,712,665,432 4,296,386,988,878 4,650,757,622,191 5,477,398,916,173 \n886,876,431,735 847,029,946,305 1,220,084,552,220 1,409,457,719,236 \n5,000,000,000 - - \n6,949,482,718,752 9,225,348,497,036 10,871,256,491,624 13,275,399,959,126 \n230,000,000,000 477,460,318,813 607,017,886,495 787,738,873,568 \n1,150,000,000 2,291,809,530 2,913,685,855 3,781,146,593 \n228,850,000,000 456,070,096,530 579,823,485,180 752,448,172,032 \n67,434,211,331,260 50,743,982,257,834 57,118,796,188,406 64,923,077,311,902 \n60,255,878,612,507 41,062,563,664,268 45,667,716,211,602 50,895,229,180,744 \n31,742,796,853,069 21,631,758,538,336 24,057,752,900,272 26,811,606,732,416 \n16,100,370,765,262 10,971,917,011,092 12,202,413,771,740 13,599,205,237,095 \n12,412,710,994,177 8,458,888,114,839 9,407,549,539,590 10,484,417,211,233 \n6,949,482,718,752 9,225,348,497,036 10,871,256,491,624 13,275,399,959,126 \n1,042,422,407,813 922,534,849,704 1,087,125,649,162 1,327,539,995,913\n3,474,741,359,376 5,073,941,673,370 5,979,191,070,393 7,301,469,977,519\n2,432,318,951,563 3,228,871,973,963 3,804,939,772,068 4,646,389,985,694\n228,850,000,000 456,070,096,530 579,823,485,180 752,448,172,032\n34,327,500,000 45,607,009,653 57,982,348,518 75,244,817,203\n114,425,000,000 250,838,553,091 318,902,916,849 413,846,494,618\n80,097,500,000 159,624,533,785 202,938,219,813 263,356,860,211Local Govt.'s Share (35%)\nStates' Share of VAT Pool Account (50%, now 55% based on the NTA)\nLocal Govt.'s Share of VAT Pool Account (35%)\nStamp Duty (formerly Electronic Money Transfer Levy )\nFGN's' Share (15%, now 10%)\nStates' Share (50%, now 55%)\nFGN's Share of Main Pool Account (52.68%)\nStates' Share of Main Pool Account (26.72%)\nLocal Govt.'s Share of Main Pool Account (20.60%)\nVAT Pool\nFGN's' Share of VAT Pool Account (15%, now 10% based on the NTA)\nTransfer to Police Trust Fund (0.5% of Fed. Acct.) from Stamp Duty\nNet Stamp Duty after transfer to NPTF\nDISTRIBUTION\nDistribution - Federation Account (Main Pool, VAT Pool and EMTL)\nMain Pool\nNPTF Levy\nNET FEDERATION ACCOUNT (VAT POOL)\nNet Federation Account - VAT Pool (after CoC and Transfers to NEDC \nand NPTF)\nFEDERATION ACCOUNT (Stamp Duty)\nNet Oil Revenue after Costs, Deductions & Derivation\nNet Solid Minerals Revenue after Derivation\nNLNG Dividend\nNet Corporate Tax Distributable\nNet Customs Revenue Distributable\nNet Special Levies Distributable\nNET FEDERATION ACCOUNT (MAIN POOL)\nTransfer to Police Trust Fund (0.5% of Fed. Acct.)\nTransfer to NBET (Electricity Subsidy)\nOther FAAC Deductions\nNET FEDERATION ACCOUNT - MAIN POOL (after 0.5% transfer to \nPolice Trust Fund)\nFISCAL ITEMS\n \n \n6.2.1 FGN Revenue \nThe total revenue of the Federal Government of Nigeria (FGN) for the 2026 fiscal year is \nprojected at \u20a634.33 trillion, representing a decrease of \u20a66.55 trillion or 16.0% over the 2025 \nBudget estimate. This projection reflects a deliberate balance between realistic oil sector \nexpectations and strengthened non-oil revenue mobilization efforts. \nOf the total projected revenue, \u20a612.24 trillion (35.6%) is expected to be derived from oil-related \nsources, while \u20a622.09 trillion (64.4%) will be generated from non-oil sources, underscoring \nthe government\u2019s ongoing efforts to diversify its fiscal base and reduce dependence on oil \nrevenues. \nThe FGN\u2019s share of non-oil tax revenues is projected at \u20a68.87 trillion in 2026, compared to \n\n31 \n \n\u20a68.45 trillion in 2025, reflecting continued improvements in tax administration and compliance. \nRevenue from minerals and mining is expected to rise modestly to \u20a636.07 billion, up from \n\u20a615.49 billion projected for 2025, driven by reforms in the solid minerals sector aimed at \nformalizing artisanal operations and improving royalty collections. \nIndependent revenues from government-owned enterprises and agencies are projected at \n\u20a64.31 trillion, compared to \u20a65.26 trillion in 2025. Although this reflects a moderation from the \n2025 projection, enhanced remittance enforcement and stronger performance monitoring \nframeworks are expected to deliver a more robust outcome. In addition, Grants and Donor-\nfunded Projects are expected to contribute \u20a61.48 trillion (including $532.68 million consistent \nwith the proposed US Government financial assistance in the health sector). \nThe 2026 projections also recognize anticipated dividends from government equity holdings \nin institutions such as the Bank of Industry (BOI), Development Bank of Nigeria (DBN), Galaxy \nBackbone, and Nigeria LNG Limited (NLNG), estimated at \u20a662.37 billion. This represents a \nsignificant reduction of about 90.9% relative to the 2025 estimate, reflecting more conservative \nassumptions and the evolving profitability outlook of these entities. \nOther revenues, including the FGN\u2019s share of Oil Price Royalty, Development Levy, \nDrawdowns from Special Accounts, and Recoveries, are projected at \u20a62.29 trillion in 2026. \nOverall, the 2026 revenue framework demonstrates the FGN\u2019s commitment to fiscal \nconsolidation, enhanced non-oil revenue generation, and strategic realignment of fiscal \nassumptions in line with emerging macroeconomic conditions and reform priorities. \nTable 6.3: Overview of the Revenue Framework \n2025 NASS \nApproval \n2026 Budget \nProposal 2027 2028\n38,019,466,563,962 29,353,982,247,203 31,534,865,004,135 34,903,734,800,493 \na Share of Oil Revenue 21,004,810,023,400 12,239,646,746,943 12,672,065,996,599 12,687,145,038,882\nb Dividends 734,934,080,860 62,367,261,396 60,678,793,557 55,316,420,300\nc Share of Minerals & Mining 15,485,205,951 36,066,908,127 40,842,307,595 13,913,149,953\nd Share of Non-Oil Taxes 8,449,321,156,641 8,875,737,921,968 10,904,837,110,384 13,789,920,164,484 \nShare of CIT 4,262,497,863,889 5,089,631,918,819 6,535,502,746,967 8,591,238,702,034\nShare of VAT 972,927,580,625 1,291,548,789,585 1,521,975,908,827 1,858,555,994,278\nShare of Customs 2,781,335,642,734 2,083,747,689,606 2,255,617,446,763 2,656,538,474,344\nShare of Federation Acct. Levies 430,135,069,392 410,809,523,958 591,741,007,827 683,586,993,829\ne 2,425,000,000 - -\nf Share of Stamp Duty (formerly Electronic Money Transfer Levy) 32,039,000,000 63,849,813,514 81,175,287,925 105,342,744,085\ng Share of Oil Price Royalty 386,570,896,585 70,258,842,452 82,654,249,319 83,037,559,901\nh Revenue from GOEs 4,069,565,246,556 9,400,398,417,348 12,387,974,225,724 13,743,354,845,582\ni GOEs Operating Surplus (80% of which is captured in Independent \nRevenue)\n(1,199,292,499,539) (4,419,520,452,217) (6,855,109,695,273) (7,562,060,498,138)\nj Independent Revenue 5,261,696,898,168 4,307,333,503,722 3,761,746,489,132 4,382,252,586,410 \nk Draw-down from Special Levies Accounts 300,000,000,000 300,000,000,000 300,000,000,000 300,000,000,000 \nl Signature Bonus - - - - \nm Recoveries + Assets + Fines 82,704,244,858 22,679,465,942 29,341,530,863 37,973,860,287 \nn Grants and Donor Funding 761,905,057,500 1,476,523,974,240 1,186,576,403,843 314,909,973,780 \no Education Tax (TETFUND) 990,000,000,000 - - - \np Development Levy 1,899,517,808,898 2,414,946,834,919 3,133,923,302,411 \n40,889,739,310,980 34,334,860,212,335 37,067,729,534,586 41,085,029,147,937 \nFISCAL ITEMS\nAMOUNT AVAILABLE FOR FGN BUDGET (excluding GOEs retained \nrevenue)\nNPTF Levy\nAMOUNT AVAILABLE FOR FGN BUDGET (including GOEs)\n \n \n6.2.2 FGN Expenditure \n6.2.3 Aggregate Expenditure \nThe FGN has proposed an aggregate expenditure of \u20a654.46 trillion for the 2026 fiscal year. \nThis figure includes the projected spending of Government-Owned Enterprises (GOEs) \namounting to \u20a64.98 trillion, and \u20a61.48 trillion for grants and donor-funded projects. The \n\n32 \n \nproposed aggregate expenditure represents a decrease of \u20a6531.20 trillion, or 1.0%, less than \nthe \u20a654.99 trillion estimated in the 2025 Budget. \nThe 2026 expenditure framework provides for statutory transfers of \u20a63.15 trillion, debt service \nof \u20a615.91 trillion (including \u20a6388.54 billion earmarked for the Sinking Fund to retire maturing \nbonds issued to local contractors and creditors), and non-debt recurrent expenditure of \u20a615.27 \ntrillion. \nPersonnel and pension costs are projected at \u20a610.76 trillion, which includes \u20a61.02 trillion for \nGOEs. This represents an increase of \u20a6768.55 billion, or 8.9%, compared to the 2025 \nprovision, reflecting adjustments for wage reviews, new recruitments in critical sectors, and \npension obligations. \nIn line with the government\u2019s commitment to strengthening health services, \u20a6237.03 billion \nhas been allocated as a first-line statutory transfer to the Basic Health Care Provision Fund \n(BHCPF) to improve access to quality primary healthcare services. In addition, \u20a6250.76 billion \nhas been set aside under the Service-Wide Votes to support GAVI/Routine Immunisation and \nthe Malaria Vaccination of Infants initiative. \nThe aggregate capital expenditure in the 2026 Budget is projected at \u20a622.37 trillion, \nrepresenting 41% of total expenditure. This is, however, 14.6% lower than the capital \nallocation in the 2025 Budget, reflecting a more conservative approach to capital planning and \nthe focus on ongoing projects. The capital budget components include: \u20a68.68 trillion for MDAs, \nreflecting the rollover of about 70% of the 2025 capital budget; \u20a63.15 trillion for capital \nsupplementation; \u20a62.24 trillion for the capital component of statutory transfers; \u20a614 billion for \nthe Family Home Fund; \u20a62.96 trillion for capital projects of GOEs; \u20a61.48 trillion for donor and \ngrant-funded expenditures; \u20a61.80 trillion as the capital component of the Development Levy; \nand \u20a62.05 trillion for project-tied multilateral and bilateral loans. \nOverall, the 2026 expenditure framework underscores the FGN\u2019s continued focus on fiscal \nconsolidation, human capital development, and strategic capital investments to accelerate \ninclusive growth while maintaining fiscal sustainability. \n \n\n33 \n \nTable 6.4: Overview of the Expenditure Framework \n2025 NASS \nApproval \n2026 Budget \nProposal 2027 2028\nSTATUTORY TRANSFER 3,645,761,358,925 3,152,684,631,399 3,196,239,315,577 3,416,850,431,633 \nDEBT SERVICE 13,939,843,871,543 15,520,821,315,539 19,498,180,259,381 19,498,180,259,381 \nSINKING FUND 377,298,818,005 388,540,316,118 296,205,220,177 296,205,220,177 \nRECURRENT (NON-DEBT) 13,588,009,682,673 15,265,878,585,384 15,627,312,164,713 15,975,335,385,569 \na Personnel Costs (MDAs) 7,595,671,610,471 8,364,216,859,307 8,364,216,859,307 8,364,216,859,307 \nb Personnel Costs (GOEs) 1,017,413,229,133 1,017,413,229,133 1,220,895,874,960 1,465,075,049,952 \nc Overheads (MDAs) 1,216,336,322,080 1,293,242,333,125 1,357,904,449,781 1,425,799,672,270 \nd Overheads (GOEs) 892,204,551,288 1,002,204,551,288 1,002,204,551,288 1,002,204,551,288 \ne Pensions, Gratuities & Retirees Benefits 1,444,017,441,338 1,376,500,075,792 1,444,017,441,338 1,444,017,441,338 \nf Other Service Wide Votes (including GAVI/Immunization) 1,057,866,528,363 1,852,325,646,294 1,852,325,646,294 1,852,325,646,294 \ng Presidential Amnesty Programme 115,000,000,000 65,000,000,000 65,000,000,000 65,000,000,000 \nh TETFUND - Recurrent 49,500,000,000 - - - \ni Development Levy - Recurrent 94,975,890,445 120,747,341,746 156,696,165,121 \n200,000,000,000 200,000,000,000 200,000,000,000 200,000,000,000 \nAGGREGATE CAPITAL EXPENDITURE 26,192,058,757,219 22,372,203,271,592 23,281,625,381,690 21,264,854,266,958 \na Capital Supplementation 5,372,767,602,157 3,151,879,854,091 3,151,879,854,091 3,151,879,854,091 \nb Capital Expenditure in Statutory Transfers 2,752,807,132,967 2,241,159,084,524 2,281,659,780,534 2,481,715,550,309 \nc Special Intervention Programme (Capital) - Family Home Fund 7,000,000,000 14,000,000,000 14,000,000,000 14,000,000,000 \nd Amount Available for MDAs Capital Expenditure 12,391,318,233,730 8,673,922,763,611 9,975,011,178,153 11,471,262,854,876 \ne GOEs Capital Expenditure 820,908,398,828 2,961,260,184,710 3,203,334,580,141 3,274,430,878,484 \nf TETFUND Capital Expenditure 374,500,000,000 - - - \ng Grants and Donor Funded Projects 721,905,057,500 1,476,523,974,240 1,186,576,403,843 314,909,973,780 \nh Multi-lateral / Bi-lateral Project-tied Loans 3,364,281,435,452 2,048,915,491,962 1,174,964,091,755 556,655,155,419 \ni FGN Share of Oil Price Royalty Transferred to NSIA 386,570,896,585 - - - \nj Development Levy - Capital 1,804,541,918,453 2,294,199,493,173 2,977,227,137,291 \nCapital Expenditure (Exclusive of Transfers) 23,439,251,624,252 20,131,044,187,068 20,999,965,601,156 18,783,138,716,649 \nTOTAL FGN BUDGET (Excluding GOEs & Project-tied Loans) 48,895,357,740,697 47,429,175,578,414 53,016,503,462,860 51,671,344,378,267 \nTOTAL FGN BUDGET (Including GOEs & Project-tied Loans) 54,990,165,355,398 54,458,969,035,507 59,617,902,561,004 57,969,710,013,409 \nSPECIAL INTERVENTIONS (Recurrent)\nFISCAL ITEMS\n \n \n6.2.4 Fiscal Deficit and Deficit Financing \nThe fiscal deficit for the 2026 financial year is projected at \u20a620.12 trillion, compared to \u20a614.10 \ntrillion in 2025. This represents approximately 59% of total projected FGN revenues and \n3.61% of the estimated Gross Domestic Product (GDP). \nThe widening deficit primarily reflects increased wage pressures, rising pension obligations, \nand higher debt service costs, following adjustments to the public sector remuneration \nframework and the impact of elevated domestic interest rates. These factors underscore the \nfiscal challenges of balancing expenditure priorities with the need for prudent debt \nmanagement in an evolving macroeconomic environment. \nThe 2026 fiscal deficit will be financed mainly through domestic borrowing, given the \nconstrained access to external financing amid global interest rate tightening, heightened \ngeopolitical risks, and cautious investor sentiment in emerging markets. Nonetheless, the \nfinancing mix will continue to emphasize debt sustainability, with a deliberate focus on \noptimizing the maturity profile of public debt, deepening the domestic debt market, and \nminimizing refinancing risks. \nOver the medium term, government strategy is geared towards a gradual fiscal consolidation \npath, anchored on enhancing non-oil revenue performance, rationalizing recurrent \nexpenditure, and improving capital project efficiency. Key measures include broadening the \ntax base through ongoing tax policy and administration reforms, strengthening the \nperformance and remittance compliance of Government-Owned Enterprises (GOEs), and \npromoting public-private partnerships (PPPs) to leverage private capital for infrastructure \ndelivery. \nCollectively, these reforms are expected to reduce the fiscal deficit progressively over the \nmedium term, create greater fiscal space for priority investments, and ensure long-term debt \nsustainability. \n\n34 \n \nTable 6.5: Deficit, Financing and Critical Ratios \n2025 NASS \nApproval \n2026 Budget \nProposal 2027 2028\n(14,100,426,044,419) (20,124,108,823,173) (22,550,173,026,418) (16,884,680,865,472)\n338,038,823,908,356 557,419,032,134,958 695,335,340,927,507 880,630,834,934,894\n(4.17%) (3.61%) (3.24%) (1.92%)\n54,990,165,355,398 54,458,969,035,507 59,617,902,561,004 57,969,710,013,409\nTotal Non-Debt Expenditure 40,673,022,665,850 38,549,607,403,851 39,823,517,081,446 38,175,324,533,851\n64% 58% 59% 59%\n48% 41% 39% 40%\n40% 32% 32% 34%\n52% 59% 61% 60%\n34% 45% 53% 47%\n34% 59% 61% 48%\nADDITIONAL FINANCING\na Privatization Proceeds 312,332,810,711 189,156,395,258 197,230,395,258 486,544,709,205 \nb Multi-lateral / Bi-lateral Project-tied Loans 3,364,281,435,450 2,048,915,491,962 1,174,964,091,755 556,655,155,419 \nc New Borrowings 10,423,811,798,257 17,886,036,935,953 21,177,978,539,404 15,841,481,000,848 \n8,580,142,011,270 14,308,829,548,762 16,942,382,831,524 12,673,184,800,678 \nForeign Borrowing 1,843,669,786,987 3,577,207,387,191 4,235,595,707,881 3,168,296,200,170 \nSub-Total 14,100,426,044,419 20,124,108,823,173 22,550,173,026,418 16,884,680,865,472 \n54,990,165,355,398 54,458,969,035,507 59,617,902,561,004 57,969,710,013,409\nDomestic Borrowing\nGrand -Total (Revenue + Financing)\nCapital Expenditure as % of Non-Debt Expenditure\nCapital Expenditure as % of total FGN Expenditure\nCapital Expenditure (Inclusive of Transfers, but exclusive of GOEs Capital & \nProject-tied loans) as % of FGN Expenditure\nRecurrent Expenditure as % of total FGN Exp (incl. GOEs + Project-tied \nDebt Service to Revenue Ratio (incl. GOEs + Project-tied Loans)\nDeficit as % of FGN Revenue (incl. GOEs + Project-tied Loans)\nTotal Fiscal Deficit (including Project-tied Loans)\nGDP\nDEFICIT/GDP (including Project-tied Loans)\nTOTAL FGN EXPENDITURE\nFISCAL ITEMS\n \n \n \n \n\n35 \n \n7.0 MEDIUM TERM OBJECTIVES, POLICIES AND STRATEGIES \nThis Fiscal Strategy Paper highlights the macroeconomic and fiscal policy objectives of the \ngovernment over the period 2026-2028 and the strategies to be implemented to achieve them. \nIt was prepared against the backdrop of low economic growth, declining inflation, rising \ninvestment, improving revenues performance, infrastructure gaps and weak social indicators \nin the country. \n7.1 Macroeconomic Policy Objectives \nThe outlook is very optimistic, supported by steady growth, easing inflation, political and fiscal \nstability, and a strong external position. However, the economy is vulnerable to oil price and \nclimate shocks. In the medium term, government aims at consolidating macroeconomic \nstability, improving the business and investment environment, and achieving high, sustained \nand inclusive economic growth. Other key macroeconomic objectives include accelerating job \ncreation and harnessing demographic dividends and improving human capital development \nand strengthening social protection. To achieve these objectives, fiscal, monetary and other \neconomic policies will be sequenced, aligned and implemented in a very coordinated manner. \n7.1.1 Consolidating Macroeconomic Stability \nA stable macroeconomic environment is important to catalyse private investment and \naccelerate economic growth. It enables businesses and households to effectively plan their \nproduction, investment and consumption activities. Over the last two years, bold and \ncomprehensive reforms implemented have removed distortions and laid a strong foundation \nfor macroeconomic stability, resilience and positive economic outcomes. The output \nexpansion, stabilized naira, falling inflation, rebuilt external buffers and rebalanced external \naccounts all signal a more stable macroeconomic environment and resilience to shocks. The \nauthorities remain committed over the medium term to prudent policymaking and the durability \nof the reforms, ensuring a lasting and positive impact on the economy and growing prosperity. \nGovernment will further strengthen macroeconomic management and implement policies \naimed at promoting higher investment and sustained inclusive growth. This includes strategies \nto moderate inflationary pressures, provide critical infrastructure to lower the cost of doing \nbusiness, lower market interest rates, and sustain market reflective exchange rate through \npragmatic foreign exchange management. Nigeria has transitioned to a transparent, market-\ndriven foreign exchange management system. It is projected that the Naira will stabilize and \nappreciate as macro-fiscal, monetary and trade policies become better aligned. The \nmacroeconomic environment will be further stabilized as Nigeria implements the fiscally \nsustainable path of accelerated domestic revenue mobilisation and better expenditure \nmanagement, moderate fiscal deficits, and a healthy debt sustainability framework. \n7.1.2 Improving Business and Investment Environment \nThere is considerable improvement in consumer and investor confidence in the Nigerian \neconomy in response to current efforts aimed at improving the business and investment \nenvironment for private investment to thrive and drive growth. Reduced distortions improve \nbusiness climate and growth prospects and greater policy transparency from fiscal and \nmonetary authorities raises confidence. In the medium term, Government is committed to \naddressing subsisting challenges impeding business growth. Additional business and \ninvestment-friendly reform measures will be introduced for businesses to thrive nationwide. \n\n36 \n \nTo foster healthy competition and investment, a level playing field will be provided among \nfirms. The goal is to attract private sector led investment by maintaining strong business \nclimate. \nGovernment will pursue necessary reforms in the regulatory environment to position Nigeria \nas an investment destination of choice. The leadership has demonstrated strong commitment \nand political will for the implementation of bold and progressive policies required to transform \nthe economy. Government is also committed to ensuring robust and wide consultation with \nstakeholders in the medium term, within the framework of predictable, coherent and consistent \npolicies. \nThe Investment and Securities Act 2025 was enacted to improve regulatory oversight, market \nintegrity and transparency to attract investment. The Act strengthens investor protection with \nstricter regulations and penalties for fraudulent activities, to bolster market confidence, and \nalign Nigeria\u2019s securities laws with global standards. In addition, the new tax Acts simplify the \ntax system, increasing compliance, and improving transparency to support investment. The \nfiscal incentives introduced in the oil and gas sector also aims to attract investments. \nPower infrastructure underpins economic growth, job creation, productivity, competitiveness, \nlogistics, manufacturing and trade. In the medium term, government will further promote the \nideals of a viable, competitive and inclusive electricity market by opening the sector to \ncompetition, provide affordable electricity to consumers and increase power \ngeneration/distribution capacity. Adjustments to cost reflective electricity tariffs will be phased, \nand targeted electricity subsidy will be implemented to protect vulnerable households. \nGovernment will also increase investment in capacity expansion of critical power grid \ninfrastructure under the Presidential Power Initiative, especially projects that can be delivered \nquickly to optimize grid reliability, interconnectedness and wheeling capacity. \nSupported by the World Bank and the AfDB, a total of $32 billion has been mobilized under \nthe Mission 300 initiative to ensure energy access and security and lower the cost of doing \nbusiness. The Federal Government is also mobilising about $2 billion through key facilities \nsuch as the World Bank DARES programme, the NSIA RIPLE platform and the JICA fund, to \ndrive renewable energy expansion and increase power access across rural and underserved \ncommunities. \nThe sum of $2 billion has also been secured for the implementation of the Building Resilient \nDigital Infrastructure for Growth initiative (Project BRIDGE), a major digital-infrastructure PPP \nproject. The main goal is to expand fibre optic coverage by 90,000 km, building on the existing \n35,000 km network with support from the World Bank and the AfDB. It is expected that these \nefforts will incentivize private investment thereby contributing towards realising the projected \ngrowth. Government will also enhance the access of small and medium enterprises in key \nsectors of the economy to sustainable finance to enhance their activities and improve their \noperational efficiency. \nThe security situation has improved nationwide with recent Government defence and security \ninvestments. Expenditures related to national defence, border security, intelligence \noperations, internal security, counter-terrorism measures, and other security initiatives will \ncontinue to be accorded top priority in the medium term. The internal security architecture will \nbe further overhauled, and new operational strategies will be introduced to enhance law \nenforcement capabilities and safeguard lives, properties and investments across the country. \n\n37 \n \nThe various services will work proactively and collaboratively to address subsisting security \nchallenges nationwide to achieve enduring peace, stability and public safety in order to provide \nthe secure environment required for economic activities to thrive in various communities. The \nimprovement in the security situation will further improve the business and investment \nenvironment. \n7.1.3 Achieving High, Sustained and Inclusive Economic Growth \nThe Nigerian economy is now more resilient to shocks and growth is expected to be broad-\nbased over the medium term, led by Agriculture, Industries, and Services sectors, all \nbenefiting from the improved economic landscape. To realize the projected real GDP growth \ntarget and the trillion-dollar GDP in the medium term, high-impact initiatives will be \nimplemented. The most productive and growth-enhancing sectors will continue to be \nprioritised in the allocation of resources, especially sectors that have job creation capacity and \nexports-orientation. The 2026 Budget will also integrate sustainability measures and green \ninitiatives into budget planning to address climate change concerns, promote renewable \nenergy sources, and reduce carbon emissions. \nAchieving high, sustained, and inclusive growth in the medium term requires a coordinated \nstrategy focused on increasing productivity and diversifying the economic base. This involves \ndeepening infrastructure investment to lower production costs and improve competitiveness, \nwhile promoting value-chain development in agriculture, manufacturing, and services to \nexpand employment and exports. Fostering innovation and digitalisation will further stimulate \nprivate-sector-led growth and job creation. \nAt the same time, growth must be inclusive and equitable, ensuring that its benefits are broadly \nshared across regions and social groups. Human-capital investments in education, \nhealthcare, and skills development will enable greater participation in the economy, while \nsocial-protection programmes should safeguard vulnerable groups from shocks. By combining \nproductivity-driven growth with inclusivity and institutional reform, a more resilient, broad-\nbased, and sustainable development trajectory will be achieved over the medium term. \nThe Federal Government regards business owners/investors, whether local and foreign, as \ncritical engines in the effort to achieve high, sustained and inclusive GDP growth. Government \nwill design and implement measures to formalize the informal sector businesses and enhance \ntheir capacity to produce. In addition to improving their operational efficiency, enhancing the \naccess of small and medium enterprises to sustainable financing would further accelerate \neconomic growth. \nThe establishment of the Renewed Hope Infrastructure Development Fund is a key driver for \nunlocking the country's economic potential, attracting investments, and positioning Nigeria as \na competitive player in the global economy. Significant resources have been allocated to the \nFund for critical infrastructure projects across the country, including roads, bridges, airports, \nrailways, and energy facilities. By investing in infrastructure, Nigeria seeks to enhance \nconnectivity, stimulate economic activities, create jobs, and improve its citizens' overall quality \nof life. The establishment of this fund underscores the government's commitment to \nmodernizing the country's infrastructure backbone and fostering long-term growth and \ndevelopment. \n\n38 \n \n7.1.4 Accelerating Job Creation and Harnessing Demographic Dividends \nEfforts aimed at harnessing the vast economic potentials of the very large Nigerian youthful \npopulation will be renewed in the medium term. To create adequate productive employment \nand prepare young people to fill them, government will further review the implementation \nframework of current interventions aimed at ensuring productive engagement of the youths to \nprevent them from engaging in criminal activities and other anti-social behaviour. Existing \ninitiatives will be improved for delivery effectiveness as well as improved outcomes and \nimpact. The goal of the government is to create the enabling environment for the growth of \nhigh-quality jobs and empower youths with entrepreneurship opportunities. \nIn the medium term, several skills development and work opportunity programmes will be \nimplemented to enhance the employability of our youths, tackle the high level of youth \nunemployment and further promote youth development. Government will open up more \nsectors of the economy to competition and investment and intensify efforts to enhance the \ncapacity of the workforce, through investment in education and training, and through \nmeasures to support people to set up businesses or move between jobs and sectors as the \neconomy grows. Government will ensure that economic growth is jobs-rich to further boost \nconsumer income. \nAbout three million Nigerian youths will be trained in digital/technical skills (AI, data science, \ncloud, cybersecurity, etc) the 3 Million Technical Talent (3MTT) Programme by 2027. Other \ninitiatives include the Labour Employment and Empowerment Programme (LEEP), and \nCreative Economy Development Fund (CEDF). The LEEP aims to create 2.5 million jobs over \ntwo years for youths and the unemployed, while the CEDF is designed to catalyse growth and \nsustainable financing for the creative and cultural sectors (film, music, fashion, gaming, \npublishing, cultural tourism, etc.) as engines of job creation, economic diversification and \nglobal cultural impact. \nTo promote and reap demographic dividend, the Government will facilitate new economic \nopportunities for more Nigerian youths to gain skills for employability and entrepreneurship. \nTo enhance education access and equity, the Federal Government has introduced free tuition \npolicy in federal technical colleges. The technical and vocational education curriculum has \nalso been significantly reviewed to equip youths with hands-on skills and better prepare them \nfor labour market realities. \nMore importantly, about one million youths will be trained under the federal Vocational Skills \nTraining Scheme across 2,600 training centres nationwide. The technical and vocational \ntraining initiative aims to create entrepreneurs rather than job seekers. The scheme provides \nopportunity for university, polytechnic and colleges of education graduates to acquire practical \nskills for self-reliance. In addition to grants and starter packs, graduates will receive affordable \nsingle-digit loans to help them establish small-scale businesses. \n7.1.5 Improving Human Capital Development and Strengthening Social Protection \nHuman capital remains the most important resource for national development in Nigeria. \nSocial investments also serve as a catalyst for economic growth through larger labour force, \nhigher labour productivity, innovations, income growth and improved wellbeing of the \npopulation. Hence, government will both prioritise and scale up human capital investment in \nthe medium term, focusing on health, nutrition, education, and social protection for the poor, \nvulnerable and lower income groups. Increasing public spending on human capital \n\n39 \n \ndevelopment is crucial, considering the declining development assistance in health and \neducation. Overall, focus will be on integrated investments in Early Childhood Development \n(ECD) as a foundation for long-term productivity and economic growth. \nHuman Capital Opportunities for Prosperity and Equity (HOPE) programme is being \nimplemented in partnership with the World Bank (and other partners) to strengthen human \ncapital through improvements in governance, basic education, primary health care and service \ndelivery. The components are: HOPE-Governance, focusing on cross-cutting governance \nissues in human capital sectors, HOPE-Primary Health Care, targeting primary health care \ndelivery improvements, and HOPE-Education, aimed at quality basic education for all. \nTo increase access to higher education, over 418,000 students have been supported under \nthe Nigerian Education Loan Fund in partnership with 229 tertiary institutions nationwide. \nEfforts will be geared towards scaling up and ensuring sustainability of the fund through strong \ngovernance, good recovery strategies, minimal leakages and careful cost management. The \nscheme will also be expanded to facilitate the participation of trainees in vocational and skills \nacquisition centres and students in state institutions. \nKey governance and financing reforms are being implemented under the Nigeria Health \nSector Renewal Investment Programme to revitalize the Nigeria health sector across all levels. \nand accelerate Nigeria\u2019s progress towards Universal Health Coverage (UHC). The initiative \nfocuses on primary care, system governance, financing, infrastructure and human resources \nunder a unified sector-wide approach (SWAp) framework. Efforts are geared towards \nunlocking healthcare value chain, boosting domestic production of essential medical supplies \nand drugs, strengthening primary healthcare, and improving access to affordable healthcare \nand improved service delivery. \nUnder the sector-wide approach, federal, state, local government, development partner and \nprivate-sector resources are aligned under one plan, one budget, and one report to avoid \nfragmentation and siloed interventions. Key targets include renovating or upgrading over \n17,000 primary healthcare centres, training 120,000 frontline health workers and doubling \nhealth-insurance coverage within three years. \nNigeria\u2019s health-information ecosystem has been characterised by fragmentation across \nfederal, state, and local levels: multiple electronic medical record systems, many stand-alone \nhealth ICT tools, weak interoperability and data silos. The lack of integrated, real-time data \nundermines efficient service delivery, disease surveillance, health-financing transparency, \nhuman-resource planning, and monitoring of UHC goals. The National Digital Health Initiative \nwill be effectively implemented to digitise and integrate the healthcare system through data \nand digital technologies. A key goal is to build a national digital health architecture that \nunderpins interoperable systems, electronic medical records, health information exchange, a \nhealth-claims exchange and data analytics, to reduce costs of duplicative data systems, \nenhance health system efficiency, support evidence-based policy making, and improve \nservice delivery and health outcomes. \nGovernment will ensure significant investment in social services, effective service delivery and \nequitable access to social services in Nigeria. As social spending had been characterized by \nlow efficiency and value for money, government will focus on ensuring value for money, \ngreater transparency and accountability and scaled up collaboration with the private sector to \nexpand access to services and improve outcomes. Government will also leverage private and \n\n40 \n \ndevelopment capital to address gaps in human capital investment. \nThe fuel subsidy and exchange rate reforms raised consumer prices and inflationary \npressures. To mitigate the living-cost shocks of policy reforms, about 15 million households \ncurrently receive direct cash transfers. Government plans to expand the programme using \ndomestic resources to reach additional ultra-poor and vulnerable beneficiaries to further \ncushion the negative effects of economic reforms. Interventions are administered through a \nrobust, transparent, accountable, auditable and verifiable system. Payments are made \ndigitally through bank account or mobile wallet. Updated National Social Registry will be \nused as the primary tool for targeting beneficiaries. A new ward-based development \ninitiative is being introduced to channel resources to Nigeria\u2019s 8,809 wards across the 774 \nlocal government areas. This will empower economically active small businesses and \ncottage industries with support and financing. \nTo revitalize the tertiary education sector, Government will address long standing welfare and \nfunding challenges in a transparent and sustainable manner. To ensure long-term financial \nstability, the Earned Academic Allowance will be mainstreamed into university staff salaries \nstarting in 2026 and the Needs Assessment of Nigerian Universities project will be effectively \nresourced and implemented to sustain the initiative. More importantly, a more sustainable, \ndiversified, and self-financing tertiary education funding model will be promoted. Globally, \npublic universities sustain themselves through diverse revenue streams, including tuition fees, \ngovernment support, patents, project-based research grants, endowments, partnership with \nprivate entities, alumni support. \n7.2 Fiscal Policy Objectives and Strategies \nFour inter-related laws were recently introduced to enhance Nigeria\u2019s fiscal policy environment \nwith effect from 1 January 2026. The Nigeria Tax Act 2025 consolidates and modernizes major \nexisting tax laws into a unified national tax code, replacing many legacy statutes. The Nigeria \nTax Administration Act 2025 establishes a single, coherent set of procedures to administer \nfederally collected taxes, ensuring consistency and improving compliance. \nThe Nigeria Revenue Service (Establishment) Act 2025 replaces the previous Service with a \nfederal tax authority responsible for tax and non-tax revenue collection and enforcement. The \nService was created to centralize and professionalize federal tax administration, improve \ncompliance, reduce leakages, and create uniform processes for taxpayers regarding \nregistrations, audits, and appeals. \nThe Joint Revenue Board (Establishment) Act 2025 creates a statutory mechanism for \nrevenue sharing and coordination between federal and sub-national revenue authorities. This \naims to eliminate multiple taxation, reduce disputes over tax jurisdiction and improve sub-\nnational revenue mobilization. These Acts are intended to simplify law, reduce overlapping \nstatutes, strengthen administration and compliance, and clarify federal-state revenue roles. \nIn the medium term, Government aims to promote a coherent, equitable and efficient fiscal \nsystem that supports productive activities, delivers sustainable fiscal outcomes, and inclusive \nand competitive to achieve shared prosperity for the citizenry. Fiscal policy will be designed \nto empower and provide social support for the poor and vulnerable people. Government is \ncommitted to implementing transformative fiscal policy reforms aimed at achieving the \n\n41 \n \nfollowing: \ni. Enhancing government revenue; \nii. Improving expenditure management \niii. Increasing fiscal space for human capital and infrastructural investments; \niv. Promoting fiscal prudence and transparency; and, \nv. Ensuring sustainable deficit and debt levels. \nCurrent reforms are designed to complement monetary policy measures with appropriate \nfiscal interventions to strengthen the naira and sustain exchange rate convergence. The \nmedium-term fiscal strategy takes account of current events with important implications for \nGovernment\u2019s ability to raise revenues, finance its expenditures and manage its debt. The \nstrategy recognizes the need to cushion the effects of economic reform measures on the poor \nand economically vulnerable in ways that create opportunities for job creation, increased \nproductivity and inclusiveness. \n7.2.1 Enhancing Government Revenue \nOil and non-oil revenue collections increased significantly recently driven by improved tax \nadministration, revenue gains from the discontinuation of fuel subsidy payments, and higher \noil production. Between October 2023 and September 2025, a total of N47.39 trillion was \nmobilized. This represents about 15 percent increase over the revenue target during the \nperiod, and a sharp jump from the N21.97 trillion recorded between October 2021 and \nSeptember 2023. The revenue improvement reflects stronger taxpayer compliance and \nstricter enforcement of tax remittances across sectors, the direct results of ongoing tax reforms \nand modernisation initiatives. Non-oil revenue collections accounting for 76 percent of total \ncollections signal diversification and reform success. \nTo safeguard national resources, close revenue leakages, strengthen accountability and boost \ntransparency, the government recently introduced the Federal Treasury Receipt, a single, \nstandardised and digitally verifiable proof of all payments into federal coffers. The system aims \nto ensure that all government-issued receipts correspond to funds received into government \naccounts, thus checking abuses and improving public trust in revenue collection and \nmanagement processes. \nThe landmark fiscal management innovation gives the government real-time visibility of all \nrevenues from MDAs through the treasury. It also automates the settlement and sharing of \nrevenues between the federal government and relevant agencies, ensuring that funds due to \nthe federation are captured, reconciled and accounted for. \nIn addition, the government has deployed the Central Billing System which standardises the \npricing and billing of government services. Together, the FTR and the CBS form integral \ncomponents of the Revenue Optimization and Assurance Platform (RevOp) which was \nlaunched in August 2025. These reforms further lay the foundation for the operational take-off \nof the National Revenue Service in January 2026. The institutional shift consolidates and \nprofessionalises revenue administration under one unified structure. \nIn the medium term, the Federal Government will implement more innovative measures to \nfurther expand fiscal space, diversify revenue sources and raise the revenues required to \nfinance its expenditure. It is expected that growth, in the medium term, will generate the \nrevenue necessary for more effective public service delivery, create fiscal space, and narrow \n\n42 \n \nnew borrowing requirement. Higher revenue collections would enable the Government to \ndeliver public services more effectively, enhance infrastructure investment, improve \ninvestment in human capital booster-establish fiscal buffers. \nOil Revenue: Government will implement new measures aimed at revamping oil revenue \nreceipts while promoting diversification from dependence on oil revenues to finance the \nbudget. In particular, government will stamp out oil theft and pipeline vandalism in the Niger \nDelta in order to substantially grow oil and gas production. Government will introduce \ntechnology to track crude oil production and trace crude taken illegally from Nigeria. The \nimplementation of the Host Community Trust Fund, in line with the Petroleum Industry Act, will \nsecure the cooperation of oil producing communities. \nTax Revenues: Non-oil revenues are more stable and sustainable sources than oil revenue. \nThe new tax laws were enacted to improve tax collection and grow non-oil revenue. The \nunification of the tax statute, the simplification of the tax system and the introduction of new \nprovisions such as the global minimum tax are expected to significantly improve non-oil \nrevenue collections in the medium term. \nSome tax administration reform measures have recently been introduced for seamless \npayment of taxes. Additional measures will be implemented in the medium term to further \nimprove tax administration and expand the non-oil revenue base. In addition, measures to \nsupport the growth of businesses in the informal sector are expected to bring more of them \ninto the tax net and increase tax revenue. \nStrategies to achieve the revenue projections in the medium term include: \na. Leveraging on relevant provisions in the new tax Acts to support businesses, expand \nthe tax net and optimise revenue collection. \nb. Further simplifying tax compliance procedures, including ease of registration, filing of \nreturns and payment, ensuring fairness and promoting formalisation. \nc. Protecting businesses from exploitative informal taxes by non-State actors. \nd. Adopting a risk-based approach to compliance monitoring and enforcement focusing \non areas of high risk and using data to target tax non-compliant payers. \ne. Reduction of revenues forgone through tax expenditures (corporate bonds interest \nincome exemptions, Capital Gains Tax exemptions, etc) and eliminating double \ndipping. \nf. Leveraging technology, such as the automated tax administration system (TaxPro Max \nand E-services) to further simplify tax processes, drive voluntary tax compliance, \nincrease revenue collection and create a tax environment that is conducive for \ntaxpayers to fulfil their tax obligations. \ng. Scaling up automation of VAT in major supermarkets, the hospitality sector and other \nkey retail outlets nationwide to block leakages and improve VAT collection. \nh. Effectively using technology and tax intelligence to further support compliance efforts \n\u2013 desk reviews, audits, and investigation. Create a Real Time Online Data Mining \nPortal to enhance the Strategic Intelligence and Data Mining function of the Nigeria \n\n43 \n \nRevenue Service and access data to validate information provided by taxpayers or \nreveal non-compliant taxpayers. \ni. Linking NRS database to those of relevant agencies such as NIBSS, NCS, NCC, CAC, \netc, which can be harnessed for third-party intelligence gathering to improve and \nenforce compliance. \nj. Enhancing stakeholder collaboration and engagement to check leakages, evasion as \nwell as enforce and improve compliance. \nk. Sustenance of taxpayer education, engagement/enlightenment through the print and \nelectronic media (TVs, Radios, Newspapers), workshops as well as other outreaches. \nl. Enhancing the capacity of tax revenue officials to effectively discharge their \nresponsibilities in handling of tax cases. \nm. Monitoring banks and other financial institutions to conduct reconciliation and to ensure \ndeduction and remittance of Stamp Duty. \nn. Intensifying engagements with the Federal and States MDAs as well as other \ntaxpayers for effective collaboration and enhanced taxpayer confidence and \ncompliance. \nCustoms Revenue \nIn the medium term, the Nigeria Customs Service will continue to prioritize its trade facilitation \nrole, driving efficiency in service delivery, enforcement and revenue generation. There will be \nrenewed emphasis on transparency, professionalism and compliance in customs operations. \nThrough technologically driven, collaborative and intelligence-led customs operations, the \nService will further enhance collaboration with international partners, law enforcement \nagencies, customs administration of other countries to combat illicit trade and secure national \ninterests. \nThe following strategies will be implemented to improve Customs revenue collections over the \nperiod 2026 - 2028: \ni. Leveraging on Technology: The commencement of the United Customs Information \nSystem (UCIS) is expected to enhance revenue collection efficiency and improve \nservice deliveries. Popularly known as B\u2019Odogwu, it is a robust automated platform \nthat harnesses all customs procedures. \nii. Revenue Recovery Interventions: Risk Management and Audit tools have been \nadopted by the Service to curb tax evasions in line with global best practices. The Post \nClearance Auditing mechanism of the Service has undergone notable reforms to \nstrengthen revenue recoveries. \niii. Real-Time Systems Audit: The Systems Audit Unit has been equipped with relevant \nprofessional techniques to validate all declarations made into the Customs Trade \nPortal, enforce compliance, and ensure proper declaration of goods for payment of \nappropriate Customs taxes. \niv. Trade Facilitation: The adoption of Authorised Economic Operator and Advanced \nRulings programme will boost trade facilitation drive, enhance release time of goods, \nimprove import and export turn over time, and improve revenue generation. \n\n44 \n \nv. Addressing all subsisting challenges to the transition and successful implementation \nof the e-Customs Project geared towards complete automation and simplification of \nCustoms processes, including payments, in every Customs formation. \nvi. Anti-smuggling: Pursuant to the Service\u2019s initiative on effective border management \nand prevention of revenue leakages across the national borders, geospatial \ntechnology has been adopted to provide effective border surveillance, check \nsmuggling and enhance revenue collection. This is in addition to the existing Joint \nBorder Patrol and acquisition of other border-manning machineries. \nThe robust and intensified anti-smuggling campaign and border management drive in \ncollaboration with other government law enforcement agencies, will reduce to the \nbarest minimum, illicit trade. and acts of economic sabotage that create sphere for \nrevenue leakages. \nStaff capacity development through training of the existing workforce and recruitment \nof new officers to promote efficiency, intelligence gathering, manpower re-enforcement \nand most importantly, improved revenue generation. \nvii. Effective implementation of the customs trade modernization project and ensuring that \nthe modernized and digitalized service do not lead to job losses. The Fast Track Trader \nProgramme has been introduced to enhance trade facilitation; \nviii. Assessment and monitoring of all revenues collected on behalf of the Service by the \nvarious designated commercial banks. This will create avenue for genuine \nreconciliation of all accrued revenues against claimed remittances to the various \ndesignated government accounts. This will also guide against diversion of any \ncollectible revenue; \nix. Automation of all transit procedures from mother Ports to Bonded terminals and/or \nfrom Command to Command to drastically reduce transit leakages occasioned by \ndiversion of cargo among others; \nx. Stakeholders Collaboration: The Service is strengthening its synergy with all other \nrelevant stakeholders to actualise its mandate, even beyond revenue generation. The \ncontinuous collaboration and engagement with relevant players in the medium term \nwill enhance compliance by bridging gaps and building confidence among players in \nthe trade chain. \nIndependent Revenues \nTo further enhance independent revenue generation and collection, Government will continue \nto optimize the operational and collection efficiency of the GOEs. The key elements of the \nmedium-term initiatives include: \n\u2022 Regular monitoring and monthly/quarterly reporting of revenue and expenditure \nperformance of GOEs by the Office of the Accountant General of the Federation and \nBudget Office of the Federation; Monthly remittance of interim operating surplus and \nreconciliation of cumulative remittances at year end after audit; \n\u2022 Submission of annual budgets by GOEs to the Budget Office of the Federation for \nreview and compilation for submission to the National Assembly along with the national \n\n45 \n \nbudget; Consideration and passage of budget of the GOEs alongside the main budget \nof the Federal Government; \n\u2022 Subjecting annual budget of GOEs to the same level of scrutiny, procurement and \nmonitoring processes as for MDAs; \n\u2022 Exclusion of agencies with capacity for self-funding from allocations in the Federal \nBudget; \n\u2022 Mandatory use of the Treasury Single Account for all financial transactions; and \n\u2022 Imposition of appropriate sanctions for unauthorized use of Internally Generated \nRevenues. \n7.2.2 Improving Expenditure Management \nRevenue growth is critical. Nonetheless, expenditure rationalization is equally vital. Authorities \nhave improved public financial management by reducing leakages, cutting waste, and \nprioritizing productive capital spending. Authorities are also working closely with the National \nAssembly to restore the January \u2013 December budget cycle and curtail budget extensions from \nthe 2026 fiscal year. In the medium term, government spending policy will reflect its priorities, \ngoals, values, fiscal capacity and constraints. Emphasis will be on effective and efficient \nallocation of public resources. The government will assess and manage fiscal and other risks \nassociated with spending and develop contingency plans to address unforeseen \ncircumstances. To curb waste, build credible personnel database and improve payroll \nintegrity, the Government is currently conducting a personnel audit. \nA data effective budget monitoring and evaluation framework will be implemented to achieve \nbudget objectives and ensure effective utilisation of allocated resources. MDAs, especially in \nthe Defence sector, must be more cooperative during budget monitoring exercises, for \neffective oversight of MDAs' spending. Furthermore, the outcomes of the budget monitoring \nexercise will be designed to influence subsequent allocations as an incentive for better \nperformance. \n7.2.3 Increasing Fiscal Space for Infrastructure and Human Capital Investments \nIn the medium term, Government will ensure strict compliance with tax laws in all sectors and \nthe remittance of all revenues due to government. The new fiscal reform measures being \nimplemented by the government are expected to create additional fiscal room for increased \ninvestment in priority sectors such as health, education and infrastructure in the medium term. \nThis will significantly contribute towards the effective implementation of the Renewed Hope \nInfrastructure Development Plan. More importantly, the investments will directly improve the \nlives of Nigerians and secure a prosperous future for the country. In the 2026 Budget, \ninfrastructure investment will be targeted and public-private partnerships, private-sector \ninfrastructure financing, and multilateral and bilateral project-tied loans will be optimized. \nGovernment will further strengthen the frameworks for concessions and public private \npartnerships (PPPs) to enhance private sector participation and value for money. Capital \nprojects that are good candidates for PPP by their nature will be developed for private sector \nparticipation. Under the Highway Development and Management Initiative (HDMI), \nGovernment will crowd-in private capital through robust PPP frameworks in roads and rail and \nfoster a network economy, reduce logistics costs, and boost productivity. Federal highways \n\n46 \n \nwill be concessioned for development and management by private sector firms thereby \ntapping into private sector technical expertise, managerial capacity and capital for road \ninfrastructure. In addition to sustainable private sector investment, government will maximize \nthe use of assets along the Right of Way (ROW) of federal highways. The initiative is expected \nto reduce travel time, lower vehicle operating cost, improve safety, create jobs, and unlock \neconomic activity along highway corridors. The goal is to raise the infrastructure stock from \nthe current level of less than 40 percent of output towards the global benchmark of 75 percent. \nGovernment will leverage donor capital to supplement human capital investment from the \nBudget. The Nigeria Health Sector Renewal Investment Programme is being delivered \nthrough a Sector Wide Approach that converges external actors around one plan, one budget \nand a common set of results. About US$3 billion external financing commitments has been \nmobilised for health-sector reform under the Initiative. The initiative emphasises Federal-\nState-Local Government alignment through National Council on Health and state health \nministries, and development partner alignment. The collaboration involves improving access, \nequity, quality, and efficiency of PHC services such as immunization, maternal and child \nhealth, disease control, and community health systems. \nThe implementation of the Presidential Initiative for Unlocking the Healthcare Value Chain \n(PVAC) will be accelerated to unlock new investments to quicken Nigeria\u2019s healthcare value-\nchain transformation by expanding private sector participation and fostering cross-institutional \ncoordination and collaboration. In addition, the strategic initiative aims to transform Nigeria \ninto one of the world\u2019s key emerging hubs of health products and technologies, manufacturing \nand service delivery. By 2030, Nigeria aims to be producing at least 70 percent of its essential \nhealthcare products locally. \n7.2.4 Promoting Fiscal Prudence and Transparency \nThe consolidated fiscal position is improving. Revenue has grown by over 70 percent in \nnominal terms over the past two years. Fiscal management will be further strengthened in the \nmedium term. Government will enhance budget formulation and implementation processes \nand improve the efficiency and quality of its spending. MDA budget proposals will be properly \nscrutinized to allow only priority spending and ensure value for money. Measures will be \nintroduced to significantly improve the implementation of MDA programmes and projects. \nGovernment intends to enhance fiscal prudence and transparency through the following \nmeasures: \na) Stronger oversight of MDAs to enhance fiscal discipline \nb) Deemphasis of non-critical administrative spending; \nc) Prioritization of critical high-impact projects; \nd) Implementation of civil service reforms to reduce the cost of governance including \npolicies that facilitate merger and streamlining of overlapping MDA functions and \nimposition of moratorium on the creation of new agencies. The need to create new \nagencies will be rigorously evaluated; only when no agency exists that performs the \nsame function that such need may be considered; \ne) Agency-by-agency review of functions, expenditure patterns and staffing levels, \noutputs and results, and identification of functions to privatize. \n\n47 \n \nf) Increased public-sector transparency, especially for oil and gas operations and \naccounting; \ng) Review of current suite of fiscal incentives to further improve fiscal transparency; and \nh) Further strengthening treasury controls to speedily detect, eliminate and sanction \ninstances of financial waste, misappropriation and corruption. \n7.2.5 Ensuring Sustainable Deficit and Debt Levels \nFiscal resilience, stronger growth, and exchange rate appreciation have helped to ease debt \npressures. The significant federal budget deficit overtime reflects the systemic revenue \nmobilization challenges, low oil and gas revenues, and sharp increase in fuel subsidy costs. \nThe discontinuation of fuel subsidy payments, streamlining of waivers and tax expenditures, \nhigher crude oil production, rising revenues from tax reforms and lower interest payments are \nexpected to reduce the growth of budget deficits and government fiscal pressures. \nEfforts will be geared towards promoting a debt restructuring strategy to free up resources for \nincreasing government spending on critical. Non-commercial long-term facilities with tenors \nranging between 10 and 50 years, with a significant moratorium of 5 to 7 years will be explored. \nEfforts are currently underway to use of cheaper options like sukuk/green or diaspora bonds \nto refinance expensive debts and thus reduce both the debt service cost and the cost of \nborrowing. In the medium-term, Government will further explore cheaper and more flexible \nborrowing options to finance fiscal deficits, while holding monetary financing to legal limit. \nFocus in the medium term will also be on asset optimization and accelerated implementation \nof revenue mobilization measures for enhanced revenue performance. Borrowed funds will be \nused for productive supply-side investments and capital projects that contribute to long-term \neconomic growth. \n7.3 Monetary Policy Objectives, Strategy and Forecasts \n7.3.1: Monetary Policy Objectives and Strategy \nThe Central Bank of Nigeria continues to implement a medium-term monetary policy \nframework (2025 \u2013 2027) to reduce the risk of time inconsistencies in policymaking and \nminimise overreaction due to temporary shocks. Consequently, benchmarks for monetary \naggregates are generated using a Monetary Programme adapted from the IMF Financial \nProgramming framework. The Programme takes into consideration the current \nmacroeconomic fundamentals to achieve the Bank\u2019s mandate of maintaining monetary, price, \nand financial system stability as a catalyst for inclusive growth. To better address persistent \ninflationary pressures and strengthen macroeconomic stability, the Bank is currently on the \npath of transition to the Inflation Targeting (IT) framework. To complement the new inflation-\ntargeting environment, the CBN will continue to monitor the growth in monetary aggregates to \nensure that it remains consistent with the Bank's inflation objective. \n \nThis report introduces annual inflation targets into the CBN benchmark in line with the inflation \ntargeting implementation timelines. Like the experiences of most IT central banks, transitional \ninflation targets are set to enhance commitment and accountability from both the government \nand the central bank towards the achievement of low and stable inflation. The set targets were \ngenerated from the monetary programming scenario analysis. \n\n48 \n \n \nIn terms of implementation of monetary policy, the major goal of the Programme remains the \nminimisation of the adverse impact of market liberalisation, to ensure that the resulting \ninflationary buildup, following the policy implementation, is temporary. This policy is therefore \nintended to steer the economy towards achieving a low and stable inflation towards providing \na conducive macroeconomic environment for sustainable growth. \n \nFurthermore, the conduct of monetary policy would align with the goals of the Medium-Term \nExpenditure Framework 2025-2030. In developing the Programme, the Bank carefully \nensured that the projected monetary aggregates would support the Federal Government\u2019s \nbroader macroeconomic objectives. Though the economic fundamentals are bound to change \nwhile implementing these policies, the Bank, in collaboration with the fiscal authority, would \nperiodically review the Programme and incorporate new developments in the macroeconomic \nlandscape to ensure that monetary and fiscal policies follow a set path to reduce fiscal \nsurprises that could adversely affect the actualisation of the programme outcomes. \n \n7.3.2 Monetary Policy Stance \nThe Bank\u2019s Monetary policy will primarily be aimed at ensuring macroeconomic and financial \nsystem stability, while the developmental roles would ensure that the Bank continues to \nsupport the overall macroeconomic objectives of the Federal Government, including the eight-\npoint agenda of the administration - food security, poverty eradication, economic growth and \njob creation, access to capital, inclusivity, improving security, rule of law and fighting \ncorruption. In addition, the monetary policy of the Bank will support the long-term plan of \nthe one trillion-dollar economy project of the current administration, which has already kick-\nstarted in 2024. \n \nSpecifically, the Programme aims at moderating the subsisting high inflation and creating an \nenabling environment for growth. Monetary policy will be flexible to rein in adverse trends in \ninflation in the programme period; hence, the Bank will continue to manage the growth in \nmoney supply within a non-inflationary path. Thus, the Monetary Policy Rate (MPR) and the \nCash Reserve Ratio (CRR) and other instruments would be wielded appropriately in the \nprogramme period. These measures will complement the unification of the exchange rate \nwindows and support broader policies aimed at achieving medium-term stability in the naira \nexchange rate. \n \nThe Monetary Programme projections consider the various items in the 2025 FGN \nAppropriation Act and are built on more realistic projections of key economic indicators like \nGross Domestic Product (GDP), Current and Capital Account Balances, Oil Production, Fiscal \nDeficit, etcetera. Specifically, the projections are predicated on the likely behaviour of key \nmacroeconomic indicators in the short to medium term, and inflationary pressures could \npersist due to structural rigidities in the economy. Furthermore, a tighter monetary policy \nstance and supportive fiscal-monetary policy coordination, resulting in lower inflation and \nslower economic growth, is envisaged. \n \nIn summary, the Bank will continue to focus on monetary, price, and financial system stability \nthat delivers inclusive and sustainable growth in the period 2025 - 2030. Therefore, the Bank \nwould maintain a contractionary monetary policy stance to subdue the current inflationary \npressures. This would be achieved by the continuous deployment of prudential and liquidity \n\n49 \n \nmanagement tools to deliver the medium-term growth plan. The Bank would maintain a \nproactive stance to ensure the delivery of a sound, stable, and efficient financial system in the \nconduct of monetary policy. Meanwhile, recent developments in the general price level and \nexchange rate management necessitated the Bank's transition to an IT framework. Currently, \nbroad money growth remains the intermediate target, while movements in the short-term \ninterest rate would be closely monitored to counteract volatility and appropriately anchor \nmarket expectations. \n \nThe current Programme projections are designed to accommodate the tightening stance of \nthe Bank in the short to medium term, with the view to providing appropriate growth in \nmonetary aggregates to achieve price stability in the economy. Additionally, the Programme \nis anchored on the assumptions of market liberalization and the private sector as a catalyst \nfor economic growth. \n \n7.3.3 Monetary Programme Assumptions and Projections for 2025 - 2030 \nThe following outlines some assumptions underlying the preparation of the monetary sector \nprojections. \n \na. Crude oil price \nThe programme projection assumes that the average price of crude oil will hover around \nUS$58.20 pb and US$66.20 pb across the forecast horizon. The rationale for these projections \nis premised on the rising demand and reduction in supply, following geopolitical tensions in \nthe Middle East. \n \nb. Crude oil production \nThe production volume is expected at 1.77 mbpd in the remainder of 2025. Thereafter, it is \nforecast to rise to 1.8 - 2.12 mbpd between 2026 and 2030. This is based on expectations of \nimproved security leading to lower oil theft due to the launch of production monitoring \ncommand centre (PMCC) by NNPC to enhance hydrocarbon operations, enhanced \nsurveillance of pipeline infrastructure in the Niger Delta region, increased number of active rig \ncount, and the full implementation of the Petroleum Industry Act (PIA) along with the Executive \nOrders related to the Oil and Gas sector. \n \nc. Government expenditure \nBased on the 2026 - 2030 MTEF and FSP projections, government spending is expected to \nmaintain a moderate and gradual upward trend to achieve the attainment of the US$1.0 trillion \neconomy by 2030. \n \nd. PMS price \nThe PMS pump price will average N950.00 per litre for the remainder of 2025. It is, however \nexpected to hover on average around N910.00 per litre and N970.00 per litre during the \nforecast period, 2026 - 2030 due to the anticipated fall in crude oil price, increase in domestic \nrefining capacity, as well as the enhanced operations at Dangote and Port-Harcourt refineries. \n \ne. Exchange Rate \nThe exchange rate is expected to vary within the band of N1514.38/US$ and N1350.63/US$ \nthrough the forecast horizon. Specifically, it will hover around N1500/US$ for the rest of 2025 \ndue to the tamed demand pressure in the FX market, supported by reduced demand for FX \n\n50 \n \non account of improved local oil refining, and coupled with increased inflow of foreign \nexchange. However, we expect slight depreciation in 2026 and 2030, attributable to increased \ndemand pressure occasioned by the pre-election era. \n \nGiven the above, the detailed projections for the monetary variables during the period 2025 \u2013 \n2030 are presented in Table 1.0. \n \nTable 7.1: Selected Monetary Sector Projections for the 2025 \u2013 2030 \nS/N Indicator 2024 2025 2026 2027 2028 2029 2030 \n1 Narrow Money (M1) \n(Billion Naira) 38,814.08 41,964.62 44,309.76 47,656.83 49,245.44 50,185.08 50,237.62 \n2 M1 Growth Rate(%) 28.86 8.12 5.59 7.55 3.33 1.91 0.10 \n3 Broad Money (M3) \n(Billion Naira) 113,355.07 122,554.67 133,882.11 145,961.40 151,853.00 155,806.68 156,181.68 \n4 M3 Growth Rate(%) 43.03 8.12 9.24 9.02 4.04 2.60 0.24 \n5 Net Foreign Assets \n(NFA) Billion Naira) 31,961.02 48,867.96 61,675.17 75,414.31 88,014.13 97,572.33 103,400.24 \n6 NFA Growth Rate(%) 242.25 52.90 26.21 22.28 16.71 10.86 5.97 \n7 Net Domestic Credit \n(NDC) (Billion Naira) 105,162.71 121,487.30 156,904.96 196,831.07 241,972.99 293,156.10 351,455.72 \n8 NDC Growth Rate(%) 9.45 15.52 29.15 25.45 22.93 21.15 19.89 \n9 \nNet Credit to \nGovernment (NCg) \n(Billion Naira) \n27,140.25 29,530.80 30,391.43 31,183.91 31,901.39 32,537.51 33,086.50 \n10 NCg Growth Rate(%) -19.10 8.81 2.91 2.61 2.30 1.99 1.69 \n11 Private Sector (DCp) \n(Billion Naira) 78,022.46 91,956.50 126,513.53 165,647.16 210,071.60 260,618.59 318,369.22 \n12 DCp Growth Rate(%) 24.76 17.86 37.58 30.93 26.82 24.06 22.16 \n13 External Reserves \n(US$ billion) 40,878.00 42,104.34 42,714.52 44,253.47 44,908.70 45,728.34 46,788.28 \n14 Average Exchange \nRate (N/$) 1,467.00 1,459.00 1,512.00 1,432.15 1,383.18 1,338.11 1,350.63 \n15 Inflation Targets (+/-\n2.00) (%) 34.80 18.55 16.50 13.00 9.00 8.00 7.00 \nSource: CBN \n \nThe above projections reflect the inflation and growth trajectory for the period and are based \non expected money growth, as inflation is considered, partly, a monetary phenomenon. In \naddition, an adjustment factor is included to capture the structural component of inflation. The \nprojections of the Net Foreign Assets are based on the expected moderation in foreign \nexchange market intervention and expectation of higher foreign investment, with an attendant \nincrease in claims on non-residents in the economy, compared to the liabilities to non-\nresidents. \n \nThe projection for the Net Domestic Credit is expected to hover around 19.89 percent, \nreflecting the expected credit dynamics in the economy. Credit to the Government is \nenvisaged to slow over the period due to the significant reduction in fiscal deficit financing and \nan increase in government revenue due to tax reforms. On the other hand, credit to the private \nsector is projected to increase owing to the government\u2019s plan to achieve a higher level of \ngrowth driven by the private sector, to support the One Trillion Dollar Economy Plan. \n \n\n51 \n \nThe projection for a moderate appreciation of the exchange Rate is built into the full \nimplementation of foreign exchange policies by the Bank. Meanwhile, External Reserves is \nexpected to increase moderately in the medium-term period, reflecting the anticipated \naccretion to reserves attributable to both Portfolio and Foreign Direct Investment. These \nprojections could further be revised if the outcomes of the various reforms vary considerably \nfrom what is anticipated. \n \n7.3.4 Inflation Target and Forecasts \nThe analysis incorporates a baseline inflation forecast, with transitional inflation targets \ndeclining from 18.5 percent in 2025 to 7.0 percent \u00b12.0 by 2030. \nTable 7.2: Inflation Forecasts and Targets \nYear \nBaseline \nInflation \nForecast \nInflation \nTarget \nTarget \nRange1 \nMoney \nGrowth \nExpected \nExch. Rate \n2025 21.84% 18.50% \u00b1 2.00 8.12 1,459.00 \n2026 18.37% 16.50% \u00b1 2.00 9.24 1,512.00 \n2027 16.82% 13.00% \u00b1 2.00 9.02 1,394.00 \n2028 13.85% 9.00% \u00b1 2.00 4.04 1,429.00 \n2029 9.96% 8.00% \u00b1 2.00 2.60 1,367.00 \n2030 6.63% 7.00% \u00b1 2.00 0.24 1,399.00 \nSource: CBN Staff Estimates \n \n \nFig. 7.1: Inflation Scenario Analysis for Nigeria (2023 \u2013 2030) \n \n \n \n1 1 Inflation Target range was empirically determined from the optimal inflation threshold study produced in \npreparation for the transition to IT \n\n\n52 \n \n7.3.4.1 Assumptions and Method for Setting Inflation Targets \nSetting the inflation targets under the monetary programming framework, we considered a mix \nof theoretical and empirical issues. The following were the considerations: \n\u25aa Quantity Theory of Money (QTM) was adopted \u2013 where inflation is expected to be equal \nto the difference between growth in money and real output, under the assumption of \nconstant velocity and stable money multiplier. \n\u25aa The historical pattern or behaviour of inflation since the year 2003: \n\u25aa The pre- and post-election dynamics were analysed in adjusting the inflation \noutcomes \n\u25aa The averages of selected years reflecting the change in actual inflation were used \nto determine an adjustment factor \u2013 0.87 per cent; \n\u25aa This factor was applied in adjusting the baseline inflation forecast for the period 2025 \u2013 \n2030. \n\u25aa The baseline forecast for 2028 was further discounted (0.70 per cent) to arrive at a target \nof 9.70 as a deliberate decision to achieve a single-digit inflation in the period. \n\u25aa The trend in global inflation was also considered in the determination of the final inflation \ntargets. \nFig 7.2 presents inflation dynamics between 2003 and 2025. \n \n \nFigure 7.2: Inflation and Change in Inflation, 2003 - 2025 \n \n \n7.3.4.2 Key Risks to the Forecast and Mitigants \na. Election-Related Spending (2026) \nThe upcoming 2027 general elections are expected to trigger significant fiscal outlays in 2026, \npotentially fuelling demand-side inflation. This risk is embedded in the pessimistic scenario, \nwhere inflation remains elevated due to expansionary fiscal pressures. \nb. Exchange Rate Volatility \nAlthough, the implementation of a market-based exchange rate regime is expected to stabilize \nthe naira, external shocks (e.g., oil price fluctuations or global monetary tightening) could still \nlead to FX volatility. A \u00b15.0 per cent exchange rate band is assumed across scenarios. \n-20.00\n-10.00\n0.00\n10.00\n20.00\n30.00\n40.00\nInflation Change on Actual Inflation\n\n53 \n \nc. Oil Price Shocks \nGiven Nigeria\u2019s dependence on oil exports, a \u00b120% swing in oil prices could significantly \nimpact fiscal revenues, FX reserves, and inflation. The optimistic scenario assumes a \nfavourable oil price environment, while the pessimistic scenario reflects a downturn. \nd. Food Import Waiver and Agricultural Competitiveness \nThe Federal Government introduced a 150-day duty-free import window for essential food \nitems (maize, rice, wheat, beans) to curb food inflation. While this measure temporarily eased \nprice pressures, it also exposed local farmers to competition from cheaper foreign products, \nundermining domestic agricultural viability. \nIf the waiver is discontinued without complementary support for local production, food prices \nmay spike again, especially in urban centres. This could reverse recent gains in inflation \ncontrol and exacerbate rural poverty due to reduced farm incomes. \n7.3.5 Challenges to Monetary Policy and Domestic Economic Outlook \nTo achieve the set goals, monetary policy would require to sustain its tight policy stance until \nrisks to inflation recedes significantly. Pressure at the foreign exchange market, extra-\nbudgetary allocations, and expanding budget deficit constitute challenges for the projections \nduring the period 2025 - 2030. Furthermore, domestic challenges like insecurity, flooding, and \nother unanticipated global shocks could further induce inflationary pressures with negative \nconsequences for the general price level. \n \nDespite the above challenges, the Bank would continue to fine-tune its foreign exchange \nmanagement policy to achieve stability. Generally, monetary policy would remain proactive \nand data driven in achieving a safe, stable, and sound financial system in line with its mandate. \nMore importantly, the Bank would continue to monitor the monetary policy environment and \ncomplement the efforts of the fiscal authority by strengthening fiscal-monetary policy \ncoordination to ensure the attainment of stable prices conducive to sustainable growth and \ndevelopment. \nThe inflation target range is achievable with the adoption of realistic macroeconomic policy \nactions, including improved fiscal discipline and strong policy coordination. Effectively \naddressing the identified fiscal vulnerabilities will be essential to fostering medium to long-\nterm macroeconomic stability. The Bank must remain vigilant and proactive, using its suites \nof policy toolkit to navigate the complex macroeconomic landscape ahead. \n \n \n \n\n54 \n \n8.0 ANALYSIS & STATEMENT ON CONSOLIDATED DEBT & CONTINGENT \nLIABILITIES \n8.1 Nigeria\u2019s Current Debt Profile \n8.1.1 Debt Stock \nNigeria\u2019s Total Public Debt Stock (comprising the External and Domestic Debts of the Federal \nGovernment, thirty six (36) State Governments and the Federal Capital Territory) was N149.39 \nTrillion (US$ 97.23 Billion) as at March 31, 2025 compared to N144.67 Trillion (US$ 94.23 \nBillion) as at December 31, 2024, representing an increase of N4.72 Trillion (US$ 3.01 Billion) \nor 3.26 percent in Naira and 3.19 percent in US Dollar terms. The increase in the Total Public \nDebt Stock was mainly in the Domestic Debt Stock. The External Debt stock stood at N70.63 \ntrillion (US$45.98 Billion) or 47.28 percent of the Total Public Debt Stock, while the Domestic \nDebt Stock was N78.76 trillion (US$51.3 Billion) or 52.72 percent of the Total Public Debt \nstock as at March 31, 2025. \nThe External Debt Stock of N70.29 trillion (US$ 45,780.45 Million) as at December 2024 \nincreased by N0.34 trillion (US$ 194.57 Million) to N70.63 trillion (US$ 45,975.02 Million) as \nof March 2025. The increase which was marginal at 0.43% in US$ terms was due to net \ndisbursements from existing Multilateral and Bilateral creditors. \nTotal Domestic Debt Stock grew by N4.38 trillion (US$2.82 Billion) from N74.38 trillion (US$ \n48.44 Billion) in December 2024 to N78.76 trillion (US$51.26 Billion). The FGN Domestic Debt \nStock which grew from N70.41 trillion (US$45.86 Billion) or 6.36 percent was the source of the \nincrease, as the Domestic Debt Stock of the thirty-six (36) State Governments and the FCT \ndeclined by 2.49 percent to N3.97 trillion. The increase in the FGN Domestic Debt Stock was \ndue to new borrowings to part-finance the 2025 Appropriation Act. \nNigeria\u2019s Total Public Debt as a percentage of GDP was 55.48 percent as of March 31, 2025 \ncompared to 53.72 percent as of December 31, 2024. This ratio is below the World Bank and \nInternational Monetary Fund Market Access Countries Debt Sustainability Framework (MAC-\nDCF) Threshold and the Economic Community of West African States' Convergence \nThreshold of 70 percent. The Figure below shows the trend in total public debt outstanding for \nthe period, 2020 - Q1, 2025. \nFigure 8.1: Trend in Nigeria\u2019s Total Public Debt Outstanding, 2020 \u2013 Q1,2025. \n \n Source: DMO \n0.00\n20.00\n40.00\n60.00\n0.00\n20,000.00\n40,000.00\n60,000.00\n80,000.00\n2020\n 2021\n 2022\n 2023\n 2024\n Q1, 2025\nPercentage\nUS$'Million\nAxis Title\nFGN External Debt Stock\n States & FCT's External Debt Stock\nFGN Domestic Debt Stock\n States & FCT's Domestic Debt Stock\nDebt as % of GDP\n\n55 \n \nThe Total Public Debt Service for 2024 stood at N12.62 trillion. This comprises total external \ndebt service (N6.75 trillion) and the FGN Domestic Debt Service (N5.87 trillion). Compared to \nN6.78 trillion for 2023, it was higher by N5.84 trillion or 86.14% mainly due to Debt Service \npayments of N2.23 trillion and N0.46 trillion from International Monetary Fund and a \nSyndicated Loan respectively, Naira depreciation and high cost of Domestic borrowing \nresulting from the tightening stance of Monetary Policy and higher Debt Stock. In Q1 2025, \nTotal Public Debt service was N4.73 trillion (N2.12 trillion for External Debt and N2.61 trillion \nfor Domestic Debt), exceeding both N2.27 trillion in Q1 2024 and N3.33 trillion in Q4 2024, \nlargely for the same reasons. \nFigure 8.2: Debt Service between 2018 and June 2023 \n \n Source: DMO \n \n \n8.1.2 Debt Management Strategy \nThe Medium-Term Debt Management Strategy (MTDS) serves as a guide to the borrowing \nactivities of the Government in the medium-term of four (4) years. The MTDS focuses on the \ndevelopment of an optimal borrowing structure to meet the Government\u2019s financing needs, \ntaking into consideration cost of borrowing and the associated risks. \nNigeria\u2019s Total Public Debt as percentage of GDP was 42.35% as at December 2023, which \nexceeded the Nigeria\u2019s self-imposed limit of 40% set in MTDS 2020-2023, but still remains \nwithin the 55% and 70% thresholds recommended by the World Bank and International \nMonetary Fund (WB/IMF) and the Economic Community of West African States respectively. \nA proposal to increase this limit is being prepared. \n \nThe exposure of the Total Public Debt portfolio to exchange rate risk is decreased marginally, \nas the share of Domestic Debt in the Total Public Debt has increased to 61% in 2023 from \n60% in 2022. The Target Ratio of 70:30 for Domestic to External Debt under the MTDS 2020-\n2023 which, the DMO is expected to achieve by end of the year 2023 was not attained. \nHowever, the exposure to refinancing risk remained stable as a result of the strategy of \nissuance of long-term securities in the domestic and international markets in addition to \naccessing long term funds from multilateral and bilateral lenders. Moreover, the share of short-\nterm debt in the Total Domestic Debt was only 12%, well within the 25% target. Sovereign \nGuarantees as percentage of GDP stood at 2.14% as of December 31, 2023, which was well \nwithin the 5% self-imposed threshold. \n. \n\n\n56 \n \nTable 8.1: Medium Term Debt Strategy [MTDS] 2020 \u2013 2023 Key Targets \nS/N Indicator Targets Actual Remarks \n2020-2023 2020 2021 2022 2023 \n1 Fiscal Sustainability \ni. Total Public Debt/GDP \nii. Sovereign \nGuarantees/GDP \n \nMax. 40% \n \nMax. 5% \n \n21.61% \n \n2.75% \n \n22.80% \n \n2.64% \n \n23.20% \n \n1.88% \n \n42.35% \n \n \n2.14% \nWithin the \nWB/IMF \nthreshold \nOn Target \n2 Portfolio Composition \ni. Domestic: External \nDebt mix. \nii. Domestic Debt. Long \nTerm: Short Term \n \nMax 70: Min \n30 \nMin 75: Max \n25 \n \n61:39 \n \n73:17 \n \n60:40 \n \n80:20 \n \n60:40 \n \n80:20 \n \n61:39 \n \n \n88:12 \n \nNot \nAchieved \nOn Target \n3 Refinancing Risk \ni. Average Time to \nMaturity (Years) \nii. Variable Rate Debt as \n% of Total Debt \niii. Debt maturing in 1 \nyear as % of total debt \n \nMin. 10 Years \n \nLess than 5% \n \nMax. 20% \n \n10.74 \nYears \n2.75% \n \n14.50% \n \n10.28 \nYears \n \n2.90% \n \n14.60% \n \n9.70 \nYears \n \n3.16% \n \n14.37% \n \n12.8 \nYears \n \n2.8% \n \n \n10.4 \n \nOn Target \n \nOn Target \n \nOn Target \n Source: DMO \n \n8.1.3 Nature and Fiscal Implications of Contingent Liabilities \nThe Government will continue to encourage private sector operators to participate in financing \ninfrastructure development using Public-Private Partnership (PPP) arrangements to address \nthe issue of infrastructure deficit in Nigeria in the face of fiscal constraints. PPP arrangements \nmay require the issuance of Sovereign Guarantees to private sector entities to execute \nprojects, which are explicit Contingent Liabilities. \n \nThe FGN has improved its ability to monitor and manage the risks associated with Contingent \nLiabilities that could arise from these projects and mitigate against the crystallization of the \nGuarantees. As of December 31, 2023, the Contingent Liabilities of Government amounted to \nN4.93trillion, representing 2.14% of GDP, compared to N3.74 trillion or 1.88% of GDP in 2022. \nThe increase was due to exchange rate movement. \n \nAs part of efforts to manage and mitigate the risk associated with Contingent Liabilities, a \nFramework on Sovereign Guarantees and Contingent Liabilities is being developed. \n \n \n\n57 \n \nTable 8.2 FGN Contingent Liabilities, Actual (2023), Projections 2024-2027 (N\u2019 Billion) \nS/N Liability Type 2023 2024 2025 2026 2027 \n1 \nFederal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria \n(FMBN) \n \n5.24 \n \n5.24 \n \n- \n \n- \n \n- \n2 FCDA - Katampe Infrastructure Project \n \n7.44 - - - - \n3 \nNigeria Ports Authority - Lekki Deep \nSeaport \n \n445.20 \n \n2,161.97 \n \n2,161.97 \n \n2,161.97 \n \n2,161.97 \n4 \nNigeria Mortgage Refinance Company \n(NMRC) Plc \n \n20.70 \n \n19.49 \n \n16.40 \n \n13.31 \n \n10.23 \n5 \nPower Sector Contingent Liabilities (Put-\nCall Option Agreement) \n \n1,226.57 \n \n8,883.44 \n \n8,883.44 \n \n8,412.01 \n \n7,913.10 \n6 \nPower Sector Contingent Liabilities \n(Partial Risk Guarantee) \n \n215.57 \n \n1,262.80 \n \n1,262.80 \n \n1,262.80 \n \n1,262.80 \n7 Bank of Industry \n \n- \n \n1,324.12 \n \n1,324.12 \n \n1,324.12 \n \n1,324.12 \n8 \nLegacy FGN Exposure from PHCN \nSuccessor Companies \n \n2,283.05 - - - - \n9 Pension Arrears for MDAs \n \n726.43 \n \n726.43 \n \n690.11 \n \n655.60 \n \n622.82 \n10 Others \n \n- \n \n6,000.00 \n \n6,000.00 \n \n6,000.00 \n \n6,000.00 \n Total \n \n4,930.20 \n \n20,383.48 \n \n20,338.84 \n \n19,829.81 \n \n19,295.03 \nSource: DMO \n \n* CBN Official Exchange rate of US$1/N899.393 as of December 31 for 2023 Actual and US$1/N1,596.14 as at August 30, \n2024 for 2024-2027 Projections \n Notes: \n1. The FGN provided a Guarantee for the Bond issued by FMBN to refinance mortgages provided for purchases of FGN \nnon-essential residential houses. Out of the N32 billion Bonds issued N5.238 billion is outstanding. \n \n2. The Guarantee was issued, on the behalf of the Federal Capital Development Authority (FCDA), in favour of FBN Capital \nLimited and FBN Trustees Limited, in respect of a bank facility granted to Deanshanger Projects Limited for the provision \nof integrated civil infrastructure to Katampe District, Abuja. The facility has been fully repaid and closed out. \n3. NPA LEKKI PORT-Amount guaranteed is US$1,354,500,000.00 billion. However, only US$555 million has been drawn \nout of the Guarantee amount as at August 30, 2024. \n4. The Guarantee enables NMRC to raise long-term funds from the Capital Market by issuing Bonds to refinance mortgages \ncreated by Eligible Mortgage Lenders. N29 billion (N8 billion Series 1 Bond; N11 billion Series 2 Bond; N10 billion Series \n3) has been utilised out of the total Guarantee available in the sum of N440 billion. However, NMRC is servicing the bond \nand thus has reduced the outstanding bond to N19.4 billion as of August 30, 2024. \n5. Figures are based on NBET's submissions on Put-Call option Agreements (PCOA) with Gencos. \n6. Figures are based on NBET's submissions on Partial Risk Guarantees (PRG) issued to Gencos. \n7. FGN Guarantee in respect of the 750 million Euro Bank of Industry (BOI) Eurobonds \n8. Figures provided by BPE on FGN's exposure in respect of the debt component of the purchase price of the PHCN \nsuccessor companies. US$2,538,430,000.00. The facility has been closed out. \n9. Pension arrears for MDAs Data provided by PENCOM to DMO. Outstanding Retirement Benefits Liability of the FGN for \ncertain categories of its employees. Amount has been projected to reduce by 5% annually. \n10. Others: Based on pipeline requests including the expected PCOA arising from the FGN's Highway Development \nManagement Initiative (HDMI). It should be noted that as part of the FGN's efforts to further develop infrastructure, there \nwill be a growing use of off-balance sheet products such as Guarantees, Letters of Comfort and similar instruments, by \nthe Government. Thus, it is expected that Contingent Liabilities will increase, as more off-balance sheet products are \nused to support infrastructural development. \n \n \n\n58 \n \n9.0 RISKS TO THE MEDIUM-TERM OUTLOOK \nNavigating the medium-term global economic landscape requires careful risk assessment to \nensure fiscal stability. For the Federal Government of Nigeria, a Medium - Term Expenditure \nFramework and Fiscal Strategy Paper (MTEF/FSP) demands a comprehensive analysis of \nhow global, regional, and domestic risks are interconnected. \n9.1 Global Economic Trends & Geo-Political Tensions \nThe global economy is navigating a period of sustained transformation, defined by persistent \ninflation, aggressive monetary responses, and shifting supply chains against a backdrop of \nheightened geopolitical risk. This challenging environment has moderated global growth, \nwhich slowed to an average of 3.1% over 2023 - 2024 as rising interest rates and volatile \nenergy prices dampened worldwide demand. Looking ahead to 2026 - 2028, growth is \nexpected to remain modest, guided by a gradual easing of monetary policy in the US and EU, \nthe strategic rebalancing of China's economy, and a powerful shift of capital toward green and \ndigital investments. \nHowever, the path forward is lined with significant uncertainty. Lingering inflationary pressures \nin major economies like the United States and the European Union, combined with the ongoing \neffects of tighter monetary policy, threaten to suppress global demand and slow growth among \nkey trade partners. Further supply chain disruptions whether from geopolitical strife, energy \ncrises, or climate events could trigger renewed volatility in essential commodity markets, \nparticularly for oil, which remains a cornerstone of Nigeria's revenue and foreign exchange \nearnings. These interconnected risks are amplified by the potential for sharp fluctuations in \nexchange and interest rates, which may in turn increase the cost of debt servicing and disrupt \nthe flow of international trade. \n \nSub-Saharan Africa \nThe Sub-Saharan Africa (SSA) region is projected to sustain moderate growth over the \nmedium-term period, but the trajectory will remain fragile and uneven across countries. Debt \nvulnerabilities are expected to persist, with several economies approaching or already in debt \ndistress, leaving limited fiscal space to respond to shocks. Inflation is forecast to moderate but \nremain elevated relative to historical averages, partly due to climate induced disruptions to \nfood production and volatility in global energy and fertiliser prices. \nInvestment recovery may be constrained by tighter global financial conditions, subdued \ninvestor confidence, and structural bottlenecks in infrastructure and human capital \ndevelopment. In addition, heightened climate related risks, including recurrent flooding and \ndroughts, pose a growing threat to food security, rural livelihoods, and fiscal stability, while \ngovernance and security challenges in parts of the region could delay much needed economic \nreforms and weaken capital inflows. \n \nWest Africa \nWest Africa faces a delicate balance between growth opportunities from natural resource \nexploitation and risks stemming from political fragility and security tensions. Over the medium \nterm, ongoing instability in the Sahel, including military takeovers and insurgency activities, \nwill likely disrupt regional trade flows, increase security related public spending, and strain \n\n59 \n \nfiscal balances. The region\u2019s commodity dependence will remain a key vulnerability, with \nglobal price volatility influencing export earnings and fiscal revenues. \nRising global interest rates could further elevate debt-servicing costs for highly leveraged \ncountries, limiting fiscal space for infrastructure and social spending. Persistently high \ninflation, compounded by exchange rate volatility and imported food price shocks, will continue \nto erode household purchasing power and may intensify poverty rates. The risk of social unrest \nremains significant, particularly if economic reforms are perceived as inequitable or if external \nshocks deepen hardship. Political instability in the Sahel, particularly in Niger, Burkina Faso, \nand Mali, continues to disrupt vital regional trade corridors and is compelling neighbouring \nstates to divert significant resources toward security spending. \nFurthermore, the planned currency reforms within the WAEMU bloc, involving the replacement \nof the CFA franc with the ECO, could fundamentally alter long-standing monetary coordination \nand introduce new uncertainties. This combination of fragile security conditions and potential \nmonetary policy shifts continues to pose substantial risks to the region\u2019s economic stability. \n \nNigeria \nNigeria\u2019s medium-term outlook remains highly sensitive to both domestic reforms and external \nconditions. Exchange rate convergence and ongoing subsidy reforms present opportunities to \nrestore macroeconomic balance but also carry short-term social costs that will require careful \nmanagement. Fiscal risks will be dominated by elevated debt service obligations, with debt \nservice to revenue ratios projected to remain above sustainable thresholds unless revenue \nmobilisation accelerates meaningfully. \n \nOil sector underperformance remains a critical vulnerability, as production constraints, \ninfrastructure decay, and crude theft could limit gains from higher global oil prices. Inflationary \npressures, driven by supply chain bottlenecks, currency depreciation, and energy costs, may \ncontinue to challenge monetary and fiscal coordination. Structural weaknesses, including \ninfrastructure deficits, a large informal sector, and persistent insecurity in food producing and \noil-bearing regions threaten to dampen growth potential and investor confidence. Externally, \nNigeria remains exposed to a stronger U.S. dollar, tightening global liquidity, and geopolitical \ndisruptions affecting shipping lanes and energy markets, all of which could heighten exchange \nrate volatility and pressure foreign reserves. \nAgainst this backdrop, Nigeria\u2019s medium-term fiscal strategy must focus on building resilience \nthrough conservative macroeconomic assumptions, diversifying revenue sources, and \nreprioritising expenditures toward growth enhancing sectors such as power, transport, and \nhuman capital. Strengthening public financial management, accelerating the clearance of \narrears, and developing risk mitigation tools such as FX hedging for debt service and \ncontingency buffers for climate and security shocks will be essential to safeguard fiscal \nsustainability and support inclusive growth through 2028. \n\n60 \n \n9.2 International Oil Market Developments \n9.2.1 Oil Prices \n \nOver the medium \nterm, the crude oil \nprice outlook is \ndefined by a tension \nbetween a baseline \nforecast of moderate \nstability and \nsignificant underlying \nvolatility risks. \n \nThe current \nprojection, which \nanticipates prices \nmoderating around \n$76-$78 per barrel, is \npremised on a balanced view of market fundamentals. \n \nHowever, this stability remains highly susceptible to familiar drivers, primarily geopolitical \ntensions and strategic production decisions by OPEC+. Historical precedents, such as the \nprice surge following the Russia-Ukraine conflict, and instability in the Middle East \ndemonstrate how swiftly geopolitical events can override market fundamentals, implying that \nsimilar unforeseen disruptions could occur within the medium-term horizon. \n \nConsequently, while forecasts show promise and may trend above these conservative \nestimates, the Federal Government recognizes that the period will be characterized by \npersistent volatility risk. This volatility will stem not only from the potential for supply shocks \nbut also from shifts in global economic growth and associated demand fluctuations. Therefore, \na core policy priority remains building robust fiscal buffers and maintaining flexible economic \nstrategies to mitigate the national economy's exposure to these inevitable price swings in the \ninternational oil market. \n \n9.2.2 Oil Demand & Supply Risks \nOver the medium-term horizon, the balance of risks surrounding the oil price forecast is \nshaped by potent forces on both the demand and supply sides. On the supply front, significant \nvulnerability stems from geopolitical volatility in key producing regions and the continued \ncapacity of the OPEC+ alliance to enforce production discipline, where any unilateral action \nor discord could trigger price instability. Conversely, the demand outlook is clouded by \nuncertainty regarding the pace of global economic growth, particularly in major economies, \nand the structural, long-term pressure from the accelerated global energy transition. This \ninterplay between constrained supply and potentially plateauing demand creates a high-risk \nenvironment for sustained price volatility, as the market remains acutely sensitive to shocks \nfrom either dimension. \n \n \n0\n20\n40\n60\n80\n100\n120\n140\nJan-17\nJun-17\nNov-17\nApr-18\nSep-18\nFeb-19\nJul-19\nDec-19\nMay-20\nOct-20\nMar-21\nAug-21\nJan-22\nJun-22\nNov-22\nApr-23\nSep-23\nFeb-24\nJul-24\nDec-24\nMay-25\nEIA Brent Spot Price FOB (Dollars per Barrel)\n\n61 \n \n9.2.3 Energy Transition and Decarbonization Efforts \nThe global imperative for environmental sustainability is fundamentally reshaping the oil \nmarket, introducing significant long-term transition risks. The concerted worldwide push to \ndecarbonize economies through stricter emissions regulations, carbon pricing, and ambitious \nrenewable energy targets is systematically depressing long-term oil demand forecasts. This \nstructural shift is already forcing a strategic re-evaluation within the oil and gas industry, as \ncompanies and investor nations like Nigeria adapt their investment portfolios and corporate \nstrategies to a future increasingly defined by cleaner energy sources. Therefore, the very \nlandscape of the oil market is being reconfigured, making the energy transition a dominant \nand enduring risk to hydrocarbon-dependent economies. \n9.3 Exchange Rate Risks \nThe significant exchange rate reforms undertaken over the past year, particularly the \nunification of windows and the ensuing convergence between the official and parallel market \nrates, have markedly improved foreign exchange market transparency and laid a crucial \nfoundation for stability. This reform has been instrumental in rebuilding foreign exchange \nreserves and bolstering investor confidence. Notwithstanding these improvements, exchange \nrate risk remains a critical factor for Nigeria's economic stability and fiscal planning over the \nmedium term (2026-2028). \n \nLooking ahead, the primary risk is the sustainability of this stability. The ability of the Central \nBank of Nigeria (CBN) to maintain adequate reserve buffers will be tested by substantial \nexternal debt servicing obligations, ongoing import financing needs, and potential capital flow \nvolatility. A depletion of reserves could renew depreciation pressures on the Naira. Such a \nscenario would have immediate fiscal consequences, significantly escalate the domestic cost \nof external debt servicing and crowding out essential capital expenditure. Furthermore, any \nresurgence of volatility would directly fuel imported inflation, erode the real value of \ngovernment revenues, and undermine the investment climate. \n \nTherefore, consolidating the gains of recent reforms and mitigating these risks will require \nsustained policy focus. The priority over the medium term must be to transition from \nstabilization to building structural resilience. This entails aggressively implementing policies \nthat boost non-oil exports, attract stable foreign direct investment, and deepen the domestic \nforex market. By reducing the economy's dependence on oil imports and speculative capital \nflows, these measures are essential to shoring up the external sector, managing volatility, and \nenhancing Nigeria's long-term resilience to external shocks. \n9.4 Risks to FGN Revenue Outlook \nThe medium-term revenue outlook for the Federal Government of Nigeria (FGN) is subject to \nseveral risks that could impact fiscal sustainability. Key concerns include the volatility in global \noil prices, which exposes government revenues to significant fluctuations, alongside persistent \ndomestic production challenges that constrain output. These factors could lead to substantial \nrevenue shortfalls against projections. Furthermore, the non-oil revenue base continues to \nface structural constraints, including a narrow tax base and compliance gaps, which limit its \npotential contribution. External risks, such as a global economic downturn, could further \nsuppress demand and complicate the achievement of revenue targets. \n\n62 \n \nIn direct response to these vulnerabilities, the government is proactively advancing a suite of \nstrategic reforms to bolster revenue resilience. A central pillar of this strategy is the planned \namendment to the Petroleum Industry Act (PIA), designed to enhance the fiscal terms and \nregulatory framework of the hydrocarbon sector. This initiative aims to attract critical \ninvestment, reverse production declines, moderate management fees and ultimately boost oil \nand gas revenue. Concurrently, the full implementation of new tax laws effective from January \n2026 is critical to strengthening domestic revenue mobilization. These laws are designed to \nwiden the tax base, improve compliance, and create a more efficient and predictable tax \nsystem. \nThese targeted interventions in the hydrocarbon and fiscal regimes are complemented by \nongoing efforts to diversify the economy and rationalize public expenditure. The combined \nfocus on optimizing oil sector earnings through the PIA amendment and fortifying non-oil \ncollections via the new tax framework provides a balanced and robust approach to revenue \nmobilization. This comprehensive strategy is designed to mitigate exposure to external \nshocks, reduce fiscal deficits, and foster greater fiscal stability over the medium term. \n \n9.5 Sensitivity of Budget aggregates to Macro-economic \nconditions \nNigeria's fiscal aggregates over the medium term will be highly contingent on the performance \nof key macroeconomic variables. The budget faces significant exposure to oil price volatility, \nexchange rate fluctuations, and domestic inflationary pressures. A decline in oil prices would \ndirectly impair revenue collection. Inflation, meanwhile, risks eroding the real value of revenue \nand increasing program implementation costs. \nFurthermore, the fiscal outlook is sensitive to debt servicing requirements and economic \ngrowth rates. Rising debt service costs, susceptible to interest rate changes, may crowd out \nvital capital expenditure. Additionally, revenue forecasts are predicated on growth \nassumptions, with any shortfall likely to result in budgetary underperformance. \nIn response, the Government's medium-term policy framework will focus on fostering \neconomic diversification, strengthening non-oil revenue streams, and the new tax law \nexpected to kick in from 2026 will serve as implementing shock absorbing policies to safeguard \nfiscal sustainability. \nThe analysis of the above risks, as well as others being monitored, is summarized in Table \n9.1 \n \n \n \n\n63 \n \nTable 9.1: Risks Likelihood, Impact and Mitigation Strategies \nRisk Likelihood \n(H/M/L) \nImpact \n(H/M/L) \nMitigation \nSubnational Fiscal \nStress & Bailout Risk \n \nM M Encourage state-level fiscal rules, subnational \nwide domestication of Fiscal Responsibilty Act, \nsupport state bond issuance, incentivise fiscal \ndiscipline. \nDebt service overruns & \nrefinancing risks \nM H Concessional borrowing, debt reprofiling, \nextend maturities, implement MTDS strictly. \nThe DMO shall closely monitor market \ndevelopments. \nHigh Inflation M H Enhance monetary policy coordination and \ncontrol inflation drivers. \nManage money supply, further stabilize the \nexchange rate, and implement structural \nreforms to boost domestic production and \nreduce import dependence. \nStrengthening agricultural policies to enhance \nfood supply and expanding social protection \nprogrammes will also help manage inflationary \npressures and support vulnerable groups. \nSocial and Security \nChallenges post PMS \nSubsidy removal \nM H A robust social protection strategy that \nincludes targeted cash transfers, subsidized \npublic transportation, and investment in critical \nsocial services to cushion the impact on \nvulnerable groups. \nA clear communication strategy about the \nbenefits of subsidy removal and transparent \nreinvestment of savings into infrastructure and \nsocial programs will be crucial to garner public \nsupport and reduce resistance via develop \ntargeted support programs. \nDomestic oil production \nshocks \nL H Strengthen security, realistic production \nbenchmarks, incentivise upstream investment, \nexpand refining capacity. \n \nImprove surveillance capabilities, enhance law \nenforcement efforts, collaborate with local \n\n64 \n \ncommunities and international partners, \nengage host communities through benefit-\nsharing programs, and accelerate the \namendment of the Petroleum Industry Act \n(PIA) to improve regulatory certainty and \nattract new investments. \nVolatility in Oil Prices M H To mitigate this risk, the Government will \nprioritize revenue diversification, maintain \nrobust fiscal buffers, and employ hedging \nmechanisms to manage oil price volatility. \nForeign Exchange \nSupply / Rate Risk \nM H \nImprove FX market transparency, build \nreserves buffer, adopt conservative FX \nassumptions. \nFailure to meet revenue \nprojection \nM M Improve tax administration, enhance tax \ncompliance, and diversify the economy to \nreduce dependence on oil revenue. \n \nEnsure strict monitoring and reconciliation of \nprojected revenues. \nEconomic Growth and \nBusiness Environment \nM M Implement growth-enhancing policies, support \nprivate sector development, and attract \ninvestment to stimulate economic growth. \nInsecurity in parts of the \ncountry \nM H The mitigation action largely remains the \nsame from previous year; a twin track \napproach of dialogue and military force shall \nbe deployed in the event of an upsurge in \nmajor security breaches \nThis is affecting economic activities in virtually \nall states. Governments ongoing efforts in \nrestoring order, enforcement and socio-\neconomic interventions shall continue and are \nexpected to mitigate this risk \nSlow implementation of \nthe Energy Transition \nPlan \nM L The FGN approved Energy transition plan is \nambitious but implementable. Funding \nNigeria\u2019s energy mix for a sustainable \neconomic growth will be critical to mitigating \nthis risk \n\n65 \n \nClimate & Disaster \nrelated shocks \nL H This risk remains unchanged from the previous \nyear. No indication or warning issued from any \nof the responsible FGN agencies monitoring. \nFlash floods however remain a recurring \nmanageable incidence. \nEnhance disaster preparedness and response \nmechanisms. Develop fiscal risk assessment, \nclimate-resilient infrastructure planning, \ncatastrophe risk financing. \nFailure of Power Sector \nRecovery Operations \nM H The Power Sector Recovery Operations has \nbeen largely successful, and an additional \nfinancing approved, now requiring \nrestructuring. \nFull implementation of cost reflective tariff \nreforms expected over the medium term. \nFederation to jointly fund shortfall moving \nforward during transition to cost reflective \ntariffs. \nRisk to Nigeria\u2019s $1 \ntrillion target by 2030 \nM M The National Economic Council (NEC) has \nendorsed a strategic framework aimed at \nachieving a $1 trillion economy by 2030, \nanchored on macroeconomic stabilization, \ninfrastructure expansion, and sectoral reforms. \nThis includes measures already in motion such \nas subsidy removal, foreign exchange reforms, \nand higher oil output. The plan also prioritizes \nunlocking growth in key sectors like agriculture, \ngas, manufacturing, broadband, housing, and \npower, with the Renewed Hope \nInfrastructure Fund (RHIF) as a critical \nfinancing vehicle. \n \nFurthermore, NEC approved several \nrecommendations to strengthen national \ncoordination, including regular economic \nbriefings, retaining RHIF, expanding the scope \nof crude oil theft prevention to include solid \nminerals, enhancing security collaboration \nthrough a dedicated resource mobilization \ncommittee, and sustaining the Renewed Hope \nWard Development Initiative. \nIt is important to note that the likelihood, impact, and potential mitigations provided in the table above \nare largely subjective assessments and may vary based on specific circumstances and future \ndevelopments. Regular monitoring, risk assessment, and proactive policy measures are necessary to \neffectively address these risks and mitigate their potential impacts. \n \n ", "content_hash": "0c3670e64f9aa0613543e60491161c6c7fa43f542188d8bf70fda90be883a471", "domain": "budgetoffice.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:27:01.805410+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1924", "title": "SON DG Participates in American Association for Laboratory Accreditation Annual Conference in Dallas", "url": "https://son.gov.ng/2025/04/08/son-dg-participates-in-american-association-for-laboratory-accreditation-annual-conference-in-dallas/", "final_url": "https://son.gov.ng/2025/04/08/son-dg-participates-in-american-association-for-laboratory-accreditation-annual-conference-in-dallas/", "text": "SON DG PARTICIPATES IN AMERICAN ASSOCIATION FOR LABORATORY ACCREDITATION ANNUAL CONFERENCE IN DALLAS The Director General/Chief Executive of the Standards Organisation of Nigeria (SON) Dr. Ifeanyi Chukwunonso Okeke is currently participating in AnnCon25: American Association for Laboratory Accreditation\u2019s (A2LA) Annual Conference, taking place from the 6th \u2013 9th April,2025, at the Hilton Anatole in Dallas, Texas. This flagship annual event, organized by the American Association for Laboratory Accreditation (A2LA), brings together accreditation professionals and stakeholders from around the globe. With over 45 expert-led sessions, AnnCon25 covers a wide range of thematic tracks, including quality, operational excellence, standards, calibration, leadership, and emerging sectors like cannabis testing. The participation of the SON Director General underscores Nigeria\u2019s commitment to advancing international best practices in standardization, quality assurance, and laboratory accreditation. The presence of SON at the conference also fosters collaboration with global counterparts and provides insights into evolving trends and regulatory frameworks. AnnCon25 has attracted representatives from a broad spectrum of industries, including automotive, environmental science, electronics, bio banking, information technology, and forensics. The conference features an extensive program of technical meetings, networking opportunities, and an exhibition with over 30 companies showcasing the latest innovations and tools in the accreditation space. Highlights of the event include sessions such as \u201cBuilding a Successful Quality Management System Using the AOAC Laboratory Accreditation Guidelines\u201d by Brad Stawick of the AOAC Research Institute, and presentations by the ASQ Measurement Quality Division, including \u201cValidating Excel Spreadsheet Templates\u201d by Dilip Shah and \u201cThe Future of Work: A Deep Dive into Artificial Intelligence & ChatGPT\u201d by Zac Jarrard. The Director General\u2019s engagement in AnnCon25 reflects SON\u2019s ongoing efforts to enhance its operational capacity, align with global standards, and strengthen Nigeria\u2019s competitiveness in international markets through robust accreditation systems. AnnCon25 continues through April 9, serving as a vital hub for learning, networking, and driving excellence in the global accreditation community. Below are pictures from the events:", "content_hash": "ff43a67399843a3025ecef86b29933a02d34ecb735002f731b68807ab2680995", "domain": "son.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:27:01.933135+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1925", "title": "SON, ECOWAS-GIZ Strengthen Regional Food Safety Systems to Combat Aflatoxin Contamination", "url": "https://son.gov.ng/2025/04/17/son-ecowas-giz-strengthen-regional-food-safety-systems-to-combat-aflatoxin-contamination/", "final_url": "https://son.gov.ng/2025/04/17/son-ecowas-giz-strengthen-regional-food-safety-systems-to-combat-aflatoxin-contamination/", "text": "SON, ECOWAS-GIZ STRENGTHEN REGIONAL FOOD SAFETY SYSTEMS TO COMBAT AFLATOXIN CONTAMINATION The Standards Organisation of Nigeria (SON) has reaffirmed its commitment to strengthening Nigeria\u2019s food safety framework through the second phase of a capacity-building workshop held at its Laboratory Complex in Ogba, Lagos. The training, organized in collaboration with the ECOWAS Agricultural Trade-GIZ Programme and hosted by SON, aims to build the capacity of food safety and quality inspectors in detecting and managing aflatoxins in agricultural products. Director General/Chief Executive of SON, Dr. Ifeanyi Chukwunonso Okeke, welcomed participants to the workshop, emphasizing its importance in enhancing Nigeria\u2019s food safety management systems. Represented by the Head of Codex, Nutrition, and Tobacco Division, Mr. Y.B. Mohammed, Dr. Okeke highlighted the success of the program\u2019s first phase, which provided comprehensive insights into Nigeria\u2019s regulatory framework, HACCP principles, food safety risk analysis, and the Sanitary and Phytosanitary (SPS) Agreement in West Africa. The initial phase concluded with field visits to supermarkets and abattoirs. Building on this foundation, the second phase is focused on fostering regional collaboration to harmonize sanitary inspection practices and improve aflatoxin testing capabilities. Dr. Okeke further cautioned that aflatoxin contamination continues to be a major impediment to agricultural exports, posing significant risks to farmers, exporters, and the national economy. He advocated for a holistic approach that includes rigorous supply chain testing, farmer education, improved storage infrastructure, and robust public-private partnerships. In her remarks, the representative of the Comptroller General of the National Agricultural Quarantine Service (NAQS), Dr. Joy Ichue, expressed deep appreciation to GIZ and ECOWAS for their consistent support. She stressed the growing complexity of global agricultural trade and the urgent need for harmonized SPS procedures to reduce export rejections and facilitate regional trade integration. \u201cThe future of ECOWAS as a cohesive trading bloc hinges on our ability to trust one another, share data, and align with international standards,\u201d Dr. Ichue stated. Participants, particularly from the Nigeria Agricultural Quarantine Service and relevant farmers\u2019 associations, were encouraged to actively engage, share their experiences, and contribute to building a more resilient food safety system across the region. A key highlight of the event was the formal presentation of test kits to the National Agricultural Quarantine Service.", "content_hash": "4bd9ff7ae2bedb0a7332188bb0ba32525b55d206b7ec972e0ce67bddf8cc87ed", "domain": "son.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:27:10.522862+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1926", "title": "SON Holds QMS Management Review Meeting, Reaffirms Commitment to Quality", "url": "https://son.gov.ng/2025/04/17/son-holds-qms-management-review-meeting-reaffirms-commitment-to-quality/", "final_url": "https://son.gov.ng/2025/04/17/son-holds-qms-management-review-meeting-reaffirms-commitment-to-quality/", "text": "SON HOLDS QMS MANAGEMENT REVIEW MEETING, REAFFIRMS COMMITMENT TO QUALITY The Standards Organisation of Nigeria (SON) successfully held its Quality Management System (QMS) Management Review Meeting on Wednesday, April 9, 2025. The meeting was conducted in strict adherence to the requirements of the ISO 9001:2015 standard, underscoring SON\u2019s unwavering commitment to maintaining a robust and effective quality management system. While the Director General/Chief Executive, Dr. Ifeanyi Chukwunonso Okeke, was unavoidably absent due to other pressing official engagements, his dedication to quality was strongly conveyed through his Chief of Staff, Mr. Uchenna Okafor, who represented him at the crucial session. In his address delivered by Mr. Okafor, Dr. Okeke emphasized the paramount importance of a strong QMS in achieving SON\u2019s mandate of ensuring the quality of products and services in Nigeria. He stated: A well-functioning Quality Management System is not merely a certification; it is the bedrock upon which we build trust and confidence in the standards we uphold. This Management Review Meeting serves as a vital platform to critically assess our processes, identify areas for enhancement, and ultimately strengthen our ability to serve the Nigerian people with excellence. While I am unable to be with you in person today, my commitment to continuous improvement and the principles of ISO 9001:2015 remains absolute. The insights and outcomes of this meeting are crucial in guiding our strategic direction and ensuring that SON remains a leading force in quality assurance.\u201d The QMS Management Review Meeting provided a comprehensive platform for key stakeholders within SON to evaluate the effectiveness of the organization\u2019s quality management system. Discussions centered around several critical areas, including: Audit Results: A thorough analysis of internal audit findings was conducted to identify areas of strength and potential non-conformities requiring corrective action. Customer Feedback: The meeting addressed valuable feedback received from stakeholders and customers, recognizing its significance in driving improvements and ensuring customer satisfaction. Process Performance: Detailed reviews of key organizational processes were undertaken to assess their efficiency, effectiveness, and alignment with established quality objectives. Improvement Opportunities: Participants actively engaged in brainstorming and identifying potential areas for innovation and enhancement within the QMS, fostering a culture of continuous improvement. The successful conduct of this QMS Management Review Meeting underscores SON\u2019s proactive approach to maintaining a responsive and adaptable quality management system. The outcomes of the discussions will be instrumental in shaping future strategies and initiatives aimed at further strengthening SON\u2019s capacity to deliver on its mandate and contribute to the economic growth and well-being of Nigeria through standardization and quality assurance.", "content_hash": "5c30a949aaec702d2e2454ee9228260b909f8b32e82f78315d6b4cc38084462a", "domain": "son.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:27:18.728423+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1927", "title": "SON University of Ilorin Collaborate to Promote Standards and Innovation", "url": "https://son.gov.ng/2025/04/17/son-university-of-ilorin-collaborate-to-promote-standards-and-innovation/", "final_url": "https://son.gov.ng/2025/04/17/son-university-of-ilorin-collaborate-to-promote-standards-and-innovation/", "text": "SON, UNIVERSITY OF ILORIN COLLABORATE TO PROMOTE STANDARDS AND INNOVATION The Standards Organisation of Nigeria (SON) has reaffirmed its commitment to institutional collaboration as it welcomed the Vice Chancellor of the University of Ilorin, Professor Wahab Olasupo Egbewole, SAN, on a strategic engagement visit its laboratory complex in Ogba ,on the 9th of April. Receiving the Vice Chancellor and officials of the University of Ilorin, the Director General/Chief Executive of SON, Dr. Ifeanyi Chukwunonso Okeke, who was represented by the Head, Mechanical/Quality Control, SON Laboratory Services, Engr. Abayomi Danmola, emphasized the importance of partnerships between regulatory agencies and academic institutions. He noted that such collaborations are essential to fostering innovation, promoting quality education, and supporting the development of national standards. In his remarks, Professor Wahab Egbewole expressed gratitude to SON for receiving the university\u2019s delegation on short notice. He stated that the university is focused on enhancing learning experiences and is confident that this objective will be better achieved through collaboration with SON. The Vice Chancellor further emphasized that meaningful knowledge is gained when all processes and procedures are well structured. He expressed confidence that with access to SON\u2019s fully equipped laboratory facilities, students\u2019 knowledge will be significantly enhanced, and the standard of learning outcomes will be greatly improved. The visit marks a significant step toward building long-term relationships that will drive sustainable national development through shared expertise and institutional synergy.", "content_hash": "c93e8b608280a459af500bcd205788da2d3b8095b546c3aad098d6f1397222d6", "domain": "son.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:27:37.788898+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1928", "title": "SON Retirees Commend DG SON for Payment of Benefits", "url": "https://son.gov.ng/2025/04/21/son-retirees-commend-dg-son-for-payment-of-benefits/", "final_url": "https://son.gov.ng/2025/04/21/son-retirees-commend-dg-son-for-payment-of-benefits/", "text": "DG SON and SON Retirees in a Group PhotopreghCross Section of the Retirees at the EventSON Retirees at the MeetingOther Retirees Present SON RETIREES COMMEND DG SON FOR PAYMENT OF BENEFITS Retirees of the Standards Organisation of Nigeria (SON) recently visited the Director General and Chief Executive, Dr. Ifeanyi C. Okeke, at the Operational Headquarters in Lagos to express their appreciation for his intervention in facilitating the payment of their retirement benefits. Speaking on behalf of the group, Dr. (Mrs.) Margaret Eshiet, former Director and Codex Contact Point, commended the DG for his leadership and described the gesture as timely and impactful. She noted that the disbursement of benefits had restored dignity to the retirees and demonstrated a high level of empathy and administrative responsibility. In his remarks, Dr. Okeke thanked the retirees for their service and reiterated his commitment to fairness and equity in the treatment of all staff\u2014past and present. He emphasized that the payment of entitlements is not merely a statutory obligation, but a moral duty to honour the sacrifices made by those who contributed to the organisation\u2019s growth. He urged current staff to draw inspiration from the retirees\u2019 dedication and integrity, reminding them that the legacy they leave behind will define how they are remembered. Also speaking at the event, Engr. Richard Adewumi, former Director of Laboratory Services, presented a token of appreciation to the Director General and led a prayer on behalf of the group.", "content_hash": "86bc7ce98b6f22c732fe2de3b20550d548c029504662294e274dec53c2e2f8e3", "domain": "son.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:28:19.117574+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1929", "title": "download", "url": "https://budgetoffice.gov.ng/index.php/2026-appropriation-bill-details/2026-appropriation-bill-details/download", "final_url": "https://budgetoffice.gov.ng/index.php/2026-appropriation-bill-details/2026-appropriation-bill-details/download", "text": "APPROPRIATION BILL (DETAILS)Federal Government of Nigeria\nCODE MDA TOTAL ALLOCATIONCAPITALOVERHEADPERSONNELNO\nRETAINED\nINDEPENDENT\nREVENUE\nAI