COST2202 556,912,285\nTRAVEL& TRANSPORT - GENERAL220201 75,560,000\n22020102 LOCAL TRAVEL & TRANSPORT: OTHERS 75,560,000\nMATERIALS & SUPPLIES - GENERAL220203 142,659,425\n22020301 OFFICE STATIONERIES / COMPUTER CONSUMABLES 142,659,425\nMAINTENANCE SERVICES - GENERAL220204 153,773,699\n22020406 OTHER MAINTENANCE SERVICES 153,773,699\nTRAINING - GENERAL220205 62,826,387\n22020501 LOCAL TRAINING 62,826,387\nCONSULTING & PROFESSIONAL SERVICES - GENERAL220207 122,092,774\n22020701 FINANCIAL CONSULTING 122,092,774\nCAPITAL EXPENDITURE23 838,426,276\nFIXED ASSETS PURCHASED2301 249,900,000\nPURCHASE OF FIXED ASSETS - GENERAL230101 249,900,000\n23010105 PURCHASE OF MOTOR VEHICLES 162,400,000\n23010128 PURCHASE OF SECURITY EQUIPMENT 87,500,000\nCONSTRUCTION / PROVISION2302 342,022,801\nCONSTRUCTION / PROVISION OF FIXED ASSETS - GENERAL230201 342,022,801\n23020103 CONSTRUCTION / PROVISION OF ELECTRICITY 162,323,293\n23020114 CONSTRUCTION / PROVISION OF ROADS 179,699,508\nREHABILITATION / REPAIRS2303 109,278,446\nREHABILITATION / REPAIRS OF FIXED ASSETS - GENERAL230301 109,278,446\n23030121 REHABILITATION / REPAIRS OF OFFICE BUILDINGS 109,278,446\nOTHER CAPITAL PROJECTS2305 137,225,029\nACQUISITION OF NON TANGIBLE ASSETS230501 137,225,029\n23050101 RESEARCH AND DEVELOPMENT 137,225,029\n0TOTAL PERSONNEL\n556,912,285TOTAL OVERHEAD\n556,912,285TOTAL RECURRENT\n838,426,276TOTAL CAPITAL\n0TOTAL RETAINED INDEPENDENT REVENUE\n-1,395,338,561\nTOTAL AID AND GRANTS 0\nTOTAL ALLOCATION 1,395,338,561\nSURPLUS (DEFICIT) REVENUE OVER EXPENDITURE\nCODE PROJECT NAME AMOUNTTYPE\nMILITARY PENSION BOARD0116021001\nERGP12228160 CONSTRUCTION OF 450M MPB ACCESS ROAD 179,699,508ONGOING\nERGP14204171 PURCHASE OF TOYOTA CAMRY VEHICLE 162,400,000ONGOING\nPage 120 / 2790 2026 FGN BUDGETBudget Office of the Federation", "content_hash": "06601c5861e6cbf48afe625f72d01f19633061d603c2e671a38d3b547d53a3f6", "domain": "budgetoffice.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:29:50.207172+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1930", "title": "SON Engages Stakeholders in Asaba to Champion Quality Standards", "url": "https://son.gov.ng/2025/05/14/son-engages-stakeholders-in-asaba-to-champion-quality-standards/", "final_url": "https://son.gov.ng/2025/05/14/son-engages-stakeholders-in-asaba-to-champion-quality-standards/", "text": "SON ENGAGES STAKEHOLDERS IN ASABA TO CHAMPION QUALITY STANDARDS In a determined move to combat the influx of substandard products nationwide, the Standards Organisation of Nigeria (SON) convened a crucial sensitization workshop in Asaba on Friday, May 9th, 2025. The forum aimed to galvanize stakeholders from diverse sectors on the critical importance of upholding quality standards through self-regulation and ethical practices. Mrs. Talatu Ethan Director, Corporate Affairs The Director General/Chief Executive, Standards Organisation of Nigeria, Dr. Ifeanyi Chukwunonso Okeke, emphasized that standardization and quality assurance are collective responsibilities demanding strong collaboration between public and private sector players. Represented by the Director, SON Corporate Affairs Directorate, Mrs. Talatu Ethan, the DG implored participants to: Actively embrace self-regulatory conduct within their operations. Support market associations as effective platforms for peer enforcement of standards. Champion ethical behavior across all industry sectors. Ensure that only certified, high-quality products are traded and utilized. Mrs. Talatu reiterated the DG\u2019s powerful call to action, urging everyone \u2013 manufacturers, traders, importers, exporters, and all stakeholders \u2013 to \u201cdo the right thing\u201d in their respective roles to safeguard product quality and consumer safety. Mrs. P.O Kussy-Wanza, Head South-South region The Head, SON South-South Region, Mrs. P. O. Kussy-Wanza, in her remarks warmly welcomed participants and clearly articulated the workshop\u2019s objective, which is to significantly reduce the prevalence of substandard goods in the Nigerian market. The Group Head, Mechanical Engineering at SON, Engr. I. P. Ega enlightened attendees on the profound impact of SON\u2019s activities on the nation\u2019s economic advancement. Dr. T. Oni, the Head of the Science Laboratory Technology (SLT) Department at Delta State Polytechnic, delivered a compelling presentation on fostering a culture of quality and standards through proactive self-regulatory measures and robust ethical codes. Group Photograph of SON and the Stakeholders The interactive session saw participants raise pertinent questions, which were thoroughly addressed by SON representatives. A recurring sentiment among stakeholders was a strong demand for increased sensitization and awareness campaigns at the grassroots level to effectively tackle the issue of substandard products. This workshop underscores SON\u2019s proactive approach to partnering with stakeholders in building robust, quality infrastructure that protects consumers and fosters sustainable economic growth in Nigeria.", "content_hash": "b4818c966fb4f7faaf45b2ae70327141073f212dff232b01cf844f7ec9677ca4", "domain": "son.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:29:56.477728+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1931", "title": "Rules and Regulations Published: August 14, 2023", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/regulation/rules-and-regulations/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/regulation/rules-and-regulations/", "text": "Rules and Regulations Published: August 14, 2023 In order to effectively and efficiently carry out the objectives of securities regulation as embedded in the Investments and Securities Act, SEC has prescribed these Consolidated Rules and Regulations Rules for public offering of securities by a free trade zone entity (FTZE) Applicability(1) These Rules shall apply to the offering and issuance of securities by a free trade zone entity (FTZE).(2) These rules shall only be applicable to companies or businesses licensed by a Free Trade Zone Authority, and operating within the free zones. (3) Entities licensed by a free trade zone but operating outside the free zone shall not issue securities to the public under these Rules.(4) Securities of an FTZE shall not be issued or offered to the public, without the approval of the Commission in accordance with the provisions of this Rule. [Download Full Document pdf \u2022 208.2 KB](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1514/Rules_for_Public_Offering_of_Securities_by_A_Free_Trade_Zone_Entity_FTZE.pdf) Rules On Shariah Advisory Services For Non-Interest Capital Market Products And Services Definition of TermsShariah Adviser means an independent professional (individual or corporate), who is a trained Shariah scholar, or in the case of a corporation has a minimum of two (2) sponsored individuals trained as Shariah scholars, registered by the SEC to advise and/or provide certification to a financial institution or entity on Shariah compliance of its products or services provided that the Commission may allow/recognize foreign Shariah Advisors to participate in any kind of transaction.Shariah Scholar means an individual who possess relevant qualifications and experience in Islamic Jurisprudence as set out in these Rules.Scope of Products and ServicesThe products and services which require the services of a Shariah Adviser include all Islamic/Non-Interest Capital Market Products and Services as may be approved by the Commission.Appointment of Shariah Adviser(1) An issuer or fund manager with the consent of the trustee (where applicable) shall appoint a Shariah Adviser to provide Shariah Advisory services for Shariah products, issuances and schemes.(2) A capital market operator seeking to provide Shariah-compliant products and services shall appoint a registered Shariah Adviser for the firm and notify the Commission of such appointment within five (5) business days of the appointment.(3) In the event of replacement or change of existing Shariah adviser, the consent of the trustee shall be obtained prior to filing with the Commission.Downloading full document--> [Rules On Shariah Advisory Services For Non-Interest Capital Market Products And Services pdf \u2022 241.6 KB](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1506/Rules_on_Sharia_Advisory_Services.pdf) Amendment to Rules on Fund Portfolio Management Operations A. Existing Rules on Fund/Portfolio Management OperationsDefinitions:Within the context of this part of the Rules the following terms shall have the following meaning:a) Discretionary Fund/Portfolio Management means the exercise of any degree of discretion and independence in investment and management of securities, portfolio of securities, pooled funds on behalf of a client, or a group of clients by a Fund/Portfolio Manager in accordance with investment objectives expressed in an agreement.b) Non-Discretionary Fund/Portfolio Management means the investment in and the management of securities, portfolio of securities on behalf of a client or a group of clients in accordance with the direction of the client or group of clients without any exercise of discretion by the Fund/Portfolio Manager under an expressed agreement.c) Retail Investor means an individual not otherwise classified as \u2018High Net worth Investor (HNI) or as \u2018Qualified Institutional Investor (QII) who invests with a registered Capital Market Operator and:More-----> [Download full document pdf \u2022 253.8 KB](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1505/Amendment_to_Rules_on_Fund_Portfolio_Management_Operations.pdf) Rules On Credit Enhancement Facility Providers & Sundry Amendment To Existing Rules Of The Commission SummaryA. New RulesRules on Credit Enhancement Facility Providers.B. Sundry AmendmentAmendment to Rule 45 on Registrable Functions.Amendment to Parts A and B of Schedule 1 (Registration Fees, Minimum Capital Requirements, Securities and others).[Download Full Document](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1385/APPROVED_RULES_ON_CREDIT_ENHANCEMENT_FACILITY_PROVIDERS2_g9yubf1.pdf) New Rules And Sundry Amendments To The Rules And Regulations Of The Commission (April 2025) New RuleNew Rules on Issuance and Allotment of Private Companies\u2019 SecuritiesSundry AmendmentsAmendments to Rules on Corporate BondsAmendments to Rule 39- Annual ReportAmendments to Rule 41- Quarterly ReportsAmendments to Part N- Miscellaneous RulesAmendments to Schedule 1- Part D- SEC Fees on Market DealsAmendments to the Rules on Private Equity FundsSundry Amendments to CIS RulesAmendments to Rule 450- New Sub-rule (9) General Rules for CIS[Download Full Document](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1323/New-Rules-and-sundry-amendments-April-2025.pdf) New Rules And Sundry Amendments To The Rules And Regulations Of The Commission (23 December 2024) New RulesNew Rule on Issuance of Commercial PapersSundry AmendmentAmendment to Rule 8 (Exemptions)Amendment to the Rule on Annual Supervision Fees for Collective Investment Schemes[Download Full Document](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1322/Executed-Rules-Dec-2024.pdf) Securities And Exchange Commission (Capital Market Operators Anti-Money Laundering And Combating The Financing Of Terrorism) Regulations \u2013 12 May 2022 These regulations, dated May 12, 2022, represent a comprehensive framework aimed at combating money laundering, financing of terrorism and proliferation financing within Nigeria's capital market.The document outlines specific objectives, the scope of application, and the core focus on Anti-Money Laundering, Countering the Financing of Terrorism and Countering Proliferation Financing (AML/CFT/CPF) institutional policies.[Download Full Document](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/7/SEC-AMLCFTCPF-REGULATIONS-12-MAY-2022.pdf) Rules on Issuance, Offering Platforms and Custody of Digital Assets \u2013 11 May 2022 Dated May 11, 2022, these rules introduce a comprehensive framework for the issuance and registration of digital assets within the Nigerian capital market. The document is divided into five parts, each addressing a specific aspect of the digital asset landscape.Part A outlines rules governing the issuance of digital assets as securities, while Part B focuses on the registration requirements for Digital Assets Offering Platforms (DAOPs). Part C elaborates on the registration requirements for Digital Asset Custodians (DACs), and Part D covers rules pertaining to Virtual Asset Service Providers (VASPs). Lastly, Part E provides guidelines for Digital Assets Exchanges (DAX).These rules set the stage for a regulated digital asset ecosystem, fostering transparency and investor protection within Nigeria's capital market.[Download Full Document](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/8/Rules-on-Issuance-Offering-and-Custody-of-Digital-Assets.pdf) Amendments as at December 20, 2021 Dated December 20, 2021, this document comprises major amendments to existing regulations governing Nigeria's capital market. It primarily focuses on adjustments related to registration fees, minimum capital requirements, securities, and other essential facets of the market.These amendments reflect the evolving nature of the market and are critical for market participants and investors to stay in compliance with the latest regulatory requirements.[Download Full Document](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/9/SEC-NG-Approved-Major-Ammendments-to-Rules-Dec-20-2021_V2.pdf) New Rules as at October 29, 2021 (Social Bonds Rule New \u2013 October 29, 2021) Issued on October 29, 2021, these rules introduce guidelines for social bonds, a significant development in the Nigerian capital market.The document outlines the specific requirements and procedures for the issuance and management of social bonds. It addresses important aspects such as the criteria for social bonds, disclosure requirements, and the roles and responsibilities of various market participants in the issuance process.These rules contribute to the growth of sustainable and socially responsible investments within Nigeria's capital market, aligning it with international best practices.[Download Full Document](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/10/Social-Bond-Rule-New-October-29-2021.pdf) New Rules and Amendments as at August 30, 2021 Published on August 30, 2021, this document signifies a significant regulatory update in Nigeria's capital market. It introduces rules and incorporates amendments across various segments of market operations. Among the notable additions are rules on robo-advisory services and trade repositories, which aim to enhance market efficiency and investor protection. Additionally, the document features a substantial amendment related to the fairness review of mergers, takeovers, and acquisitions, emphasizing transparency and fairness in these transactions.The document also addresses several other aspects, making it essential reading for market participants keen on staying updated with the evolving regulatory landscape.[Download Full Document](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/11/New-Rules-and-Amendments-_Executed-30-August-2021.pdf) Amendments as at May 24, 2021 Dated May 24, 2021, this document primarily focuses on amendments to Rule 45, specifically regarding registerable functions.These amendments reflect changes in the regulatory framework aimed at refining and strengthening the registration process for market operators. Market participants need to be aware of these adjustments to ensure compliance with the updated regulations.[Download Full Document](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/12/Executed-Rules-May-24-2021.pdf) Major Amendments as at April 22, 2021 Released on April 22, 2021, this document brings substantial changes to the existing regulatory landscape of Nigeria's capital market. These major amendments are significant for market participants, as they impact various aspects of market operations. Notably, the document includes amendments to Rule 67 concerning sub-brokers and Rule 70 relating to records of transactions with clients. Moreover, a new rule, Rule 76, is introduced, creating additional regulatory provisions.These changes demonstrate the commitment to maintaining the market's integrity and efficiency while keeping pace with evolving market dynamics.[Download Full Document](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/13/Major-Amendments-to-the-SEC-NIGERIA-rules-26-April-2021.pdf) New Rules and Sundry Amendments as at March 22, 2021 Published on March 22, 2021, this document introduces new rules and sundry amendments, covering a broad spectrum of capital market operations. The new rules include provisions for warehousing and collateral management, reflecting evolving market practices. Additionally, there are sundry amendments, particularly related to Rule 19 (reinstating Individual Sub-broker registration), Rule 27 (Fidelity Bond), and Rule 42 (minimum disclosure requirements by public companies on their websites), among others.These changes underscore the continuous effort to adapt the regulatory framework to emerging trends and ensure market transparency and investor protection.[Download Full Document](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/14/Executed-Rules-and-Amendments_22-March-2021.pdf) New Rules and Sundry Amendments as at January 21, 2021 Issued on January 21, 2021, this document presents a significant update to Nigeria's capital market regulations. It introduces new rules and encompasses sundry amendments that address various facets of market operations.The newly introduced rules cover areas such as fund management products, nominee companies, and general rules for collective investment schemes. These additions reflect the evolving nature of the market and enhance the regulatory framework to better serve market participants. Additionally, there are sundry amendments that touch upon several regulations, including those related to trust deeds, collective investment schemes, and disclosure requirements.These changes collectively contribute to ensuring transparency and investor protection within the capital market.[Download Full Document](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/15/Jan-2021-Executed-Rules.pdf) Sundry Amendments as at September 10, 2020 Dated September 10, 2020, this document comprises sundry amendments that impact various aspects of Nigeria's capital market regulations. The amendments cover a range of rules, including those concerning legal practitioners, removal from listing, additional requirements for sukuk programs, and registration requirements for incidental functions, to name a few.These amendments reflect the authorities' ongoing efforts to refine and strengthen the regulatory framework, aligning it with market dynamics and international best practices. Market participants should closely review these changes to ensure compliance with the updated regulations.[Download Full Document](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/16/New-Rules-and-Sundry-Amendments-as-at-10-September-2020.pdf) New Rules and Sundry Amendments as at December 23, 2019 Released on December 23, 2019, this document represents a significant update to Nigeria's capital market regulations. It introduces new rules and incorporates sundry amendments across various aspects of market operations.The newly introduced rules cover areas such as the regulation of derivatives trading, central counter parties (CCP), and the registration of fixed income existing securities. These additions aim to enhance market efficiency, risk management, and transparency. Additionally, major amendments are made to rules concerning collective investment schemes. Sundry amendments further refine the regulatory framework, touching upon aspects like fidelity bonds, disclosure requirements for public companies, and shelf registration.These changes collectively contribute to ensuring a robust and adaptable regulatory environment for the capital market.[Download Full Document](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/17/SEC-New-Rules-and-Ammendments-23-December-2019.pdf) New Rules and Sundry Amendments as at August 2019 Dated 21st of August 2019This document brings forward rules on specialised funds/schemes and a host of other amendments.[Download full document](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1352/New_Rules_and_Sundry_Amendments_August_21_2019.pdf) New Rules and Sundry Amendments as at October 14, 2019 Published on October 14, 2019, this document introduces new rules and incorporates sundry amendments, reflecting the commitment to enhancing Nigeria's capital market regulations.The new rules encompass specialized funds/schemes, registration requirements for commodity brokers, and the regulation of capital market holding companies (CMHCs). These additions cater to evolving market dynamics and emerging market players. Additionally, sundry amendments touch on various aspects of capital market operations, further refining the regulatory framework.These amendments include changes to rules on sponsored individuals, minimum paid-up capital, and reporting requirements for different market participants. Market stakeholders should stay informed about these changes to ensure compliance with the updated regulations.[Download Full Document](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/18/Rules-For-Upload-October-2019_final.pdf) New rules and amendments as at 12 October 2018 Dated October 12, 2018, this document brings forward new rules and amendments that have a significant impact on Nigeria's capital market regulations.The introduction of rules on green bonds stands out as a notable addition, aligning the market with sustainable finance initiatives. Additionally, sundry amendments include the inclusion of the Bank Verification Number (BVN) as a valid means of individual client identification in the capital market, a measure to enhance security and traceability. Furthermore, there's a rule on investment advisory services, demonstrating the market's commitment to investor protection and quality advisory practices.These changes reflect a dynamic and evolving capital market landscape, ensuring alignment with international standards and best practices.[Download Full Document](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/19/12-October-2018-SEC-NG-Approved-Rules.pdf) New rules and amendments as at 22 November 2017 \u2013 BATCH B Released on November 22, 2017, this document presents a set of new rules and amendments in two distinct batches. In this BATCH B release, a new rule focusing on the regulation of revenue bonds is introduced, indicating a diversification of investment options within the capital market. Additionally, sundry amendments cover various areas, including dividend/return money warrants, the code of conduct for capital market operators and their employees, and adjustments to rules concerning inter-dealer brokers and mergers and takeovers.These changes collectively serve to refine and strengthen the regulatory framework, fostering transparency and investor confidence.[Download Full Document](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/20/SEC-APPROVED-RULES-NOV-2017_BATCH-B.pdf) New rules and amendments as at 22 November 2017 \u2013 BATCH A Published on November 22, 2017, in BATCH A of this document, significant updates to Nigeria's capital market regulations are introduced.This includes the introduction of the Asset Manager Code of Professional Conduct, emphasizing ethical practices in the market. Furthermore, changes in primary issuance fees for both equity and fixed income instruments aim to enhance market efficiency. Notably, the waiver of the naira value cap on transaction fees payable to various professionals' underscores efforts to streamline market operations. Sundry amendments cover a range of topics, including registration requirements for capital market operators, minimum paid-up capital definitions, and reporting obligations.These changes reflect the commitment to improving market transparency, efficiency, and investor protection.[Download Full Document](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/21/RULES-JULY-2017-final-for-execution-11-10-2017.pdf) New Rules and Amendments as at June 2017 Dated June 2017, this document introduces new rules and amendments that are pivotal in shaping Nigeria's capital market regulations.The rules on the return of unclaimed dividends to paying companies represent a crucial step in investor protection and asset retrieval. Additionally, the inclusion of the Independent National Electoral Commission (INEC) voter's registration card as a valid means of identification for individual clients is a significant step towards ensuring efficient and secure market operations. The adoption of a three-tiered Know-Your-Customer (KYC) framework further enhances market integrity and transparency. Furthermore, rules governing fund and portfolio management operations contribute to the efficient operation of collective investment schemes. Major amendments include changes to the National Investor Protection Fund (NIPF) rules and rules on real estate investment schemes, fostering investor confidence.[Download Full Document](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/22/June-2017-SEC-Executed-Rules-Regulations.pdf) Consolidated Rules 2013 Dated June 2013[Download full document](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1347/SEC_Consolidated_JUNE2013_SIGNEDWEBSITE_1.pdf) Additional Rules and Regulation Documents in the Repository Below is a comprehensive collection of early documents that form the backbone of regulations and rules governing Nigeria's capital market.These documents serve as the guiding principles for market participants, ensuring transparency, fairness, and investor protection. While some documents introduce new rules and amendments to keep the market aligned with global standards, others focus on specific financial instruments and market practices:[Sundry Amendments to SEC Rules and Regulations - April 13, 2015](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/23/AMENDMENTS_17415.pdf): Explore amendments that refine and enhance existing market regulations.[Rules for Demutualization of Securities Exchanges in Nigeria - April 27, 2015](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/24/Rules-on-Demutualization-of-Securities-Exchanges-in-Nigeria-April-27-2015.pdf): Learn about the rules governing the transformation of securities exchanges in Nigeria.[Rules on Securitization - April 13, 2015](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/25/RULES_ON_SECURITIZATION_17415.pdf)[:](https://chat.openai.com/documents) Dive into the regulations surrounding securitization, a critical financial practice in the capital market.[Rules on Trading in Unlisted Securities - April 13, 2015](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/26/RULES_ON_TRADING_IN_UNLISTED_SECURITIES_17415.pdf): Discover how unlisted securities are traded within the Nigerian capital market.[Rules Relating to the Complaints Management Framework of the Nigerian Capital Market - February 12, 2015](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/27/Rules_on_Complaints_Mgt_Framework_of_NCM_February_12_2015.pdf)[:](https://chat.openai.com/documents) Gain insights into the framework for addressing complaints in the capital market.[Rules on Infrastructure Funds, Foreign Collective Investment Schemes and Sundry Amendments - May 12, 2014](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/28/RULES_ON_INFRASTRUCTURE_FUNDS_and_OTHERS__2014.pdf)[:](https://chat.openai.com/documents) Explore rules related to infrastructure funds, foreign collective investment schemes, and various amendments that shape the market's landscape.These documents collectively define the regulatory landscape that governs Nigeria's capital market, ensuring its integrity, efficiency, and investor confidence. Feel free to access any of these documents to stay informed about the latest market regulations and practices. [SEC Rules For FinTechs Navigate regulatory standards for fintech firms operating in Nigeria\u2019s capital market; uncover li](https://www.sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/regulation/rules-and-regulations/sec-rules-for-fintechs/) [SEC Rules Related to Bonds Delve into guidelines governing bond issuance and trading; learn about compliance steps, disc](https://www.sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/regulation/rules-and-regulations/sec-rules-related-to-bonds/)", "content_hash": "0c32996f404068a4f9811b07567b1047a42824507807c8f3e1b30b6d1601c279", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1932", "title": "Investor Education", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/development/investor-education/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/development/investor-education/", "text": "Investor Education Elevate your financial literacy and confidently navigate the capital market with the Securities and Exchange Commission's comprehensive Investor Education program. Gain essential investment insights, delve into market risks and regulations, and make informed decisions that align with your financial aspirations. blocks section [Frequently Asked Questions](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/frequently-asked-questions/) [Glossary of Capital Market Terms](https://www.sec.gov.ng/glossary-of-capital-market-terms/) [Webinars](https://www.sec.gov.ng/events/webinars/) [Investment Basics](https://www.sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/development/investor-education/investment-basics/) [Investment Basics](https://www.sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/development/investor-education/investment-basics/) [Investment Strategies](https://www.sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/development/investor-education/investment-strategies/) [Investment Strategies](https://www.sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/development/investor-education/investment-strategies/) [Investor Protection](https://www.sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/development/investor-education/investor-protection/) [Investor Protection](https://www.sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/development/investor-education/investor-protection/)", "content_hash": "4e538e648e9c6273bbf9894a384eb66e83a35e3399022475a74036505d4d52d9", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1933", "title": "Enforcements", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/", "text": "Enforcements Access comprehensive information on enforcement actions taken by the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) to maintain market integrity and protect investors. blocks section [Cos. Facing Enforcement Action](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/companies-facing-enforcement-action/) [Cos. Facing Enforcement Action](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/companies-facing-enforcement-action/) [Referred Cases](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/referred-cases/) [Referred Cases](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/referred-cases/) [A.P.C. Matters](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/apc-matters/) [A.P.C. Matters](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/apc-matters/) [Litigation](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/litigation/) [Litigation](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/litigation/) [Keep Track of Enforcement Updates](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/) [Keep Track of Enforcement Updates](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/) [Information for Harmed Investors](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/information-for-harmed-investors/) [Information for Harmed Investors](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/information-for-harmed-investors/)", "content_hash": "040cadc85b4ce54ddea1068cc8649f2c41d2f3e4e0bac071992a8b56311004f7", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1934", "title": "Keep Track of Enforcement Updates Published: November 16, 2023", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/", "text": "Keep Track of Enforcement Updates Published: November 16, 2023 Receive timely updates on the latest enforcement actions taken by the SEC to ensure market fairness and investor protection. [Subscribe to RSS Feed](https://www.sec.gov.ng/feeds/enforcement-updates.rss) [Illegal Operator Alert - Shalom Coin (SHLM) The attention of the Securities and Exchange Commission, Nigeria (\u201cthe Commi](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/illegal-operator-alert-shalom-coin-shlm/) [Illegal Operator Alert \u2013 GVEST GLOBAL /GVEST (ALAUSA) COOPERATIVE MULTIPURPOSE LIMITED/GVEST INVESTMENT LIMITED/GTEXT HO](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/illegal-operator-alert-gvest-global-gvest-alausa-cooperative-multipurpose-limited-gvest-investment-limited-gtext-holdings/) [Illegal Operator Alert \u2013 Pocket Option July 21, 2025](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/illegal-operator-pocket-option/) [Illegal Operator \u2013 Forsman & Bodenfors LTD (F&B) July 14, 2025](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/illegal-operator-forsman-bodenfors/) [Illegal Operator Alert \u2013 Value Growth Platform June 30, 2025](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/illegal-operator-value-growth-platform/) [Illegal Operator \u2013 CMTrading June 20, 2025](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/illegal-operator-cmtrading/) [Illegal Operator Alert \u2013 Sapphire Scents Limited June 19, 2025](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/illegal-operator-alert-sapphire-scents-limited/) [Illegal Operator Alert \u2013 ZugaCoin and Samzuga GPT June 17, 2025](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/illegal-operator-alert-zugacoin-and-samzuga-gpt/) [Illegal Operator Alert -CBEX (Crypto Bridge Exchange)-Super Technology-ST Technologies International Ltd June 11, 2025](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/illegal-operator-alert-cbex-crypto-bridge-exchange-super-technology-st-technologies-international-ltd/) [Illegal Operator Alert \u2013 Punisher Coin June 5, 2025](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/illegal-operator-alert-punisher-coin/) [Illegal Operator Alert \u2013 Silverkuun Investment Cooperative Society/Silverkuun Limited May 28, 2025](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/illegal-operator-alert-silverkuun-investment-cooperative-societysilverkuun-limited/) [Public Notice \u2013 Property World Africa Network (PWAN)/PWANMax May 6, 2025](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/public-notice-property-world-africa-network-pwanpwanmax/) [Public Notice \u2013 Tofro.com April 30, 2025](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/public-notice-tofrocom/) [CBEX(Crypto Bridge Exchange) / Super Technology/ ST Technologies International Ltd April 17, 2025](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/cbexcrypto-bridge-exchange-super-technology-st-technologies-international-ltd/) [Notice Of Cancellation Of Registration Of Mainland Trust Limited March 14, 2025](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/notice-of-cancellation-of-registration-of-mainland-trust-limited/) [Illegal Operator Alert \u2013 Provest March 6, 2025](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/illegal-operator-alert-provest/) [Illegal Operator Alert \u2013 My Shares And UYJ Multitrade Limited March 6, 2025](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/illegal-operator-alert-my-shares-and-uyj-multitrade-limited/) [Illegal Operator Alert \u2013 Marino FX Ltd Nov. 25, 2024](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/illegal-operator-alert-marino-fx-ltd/) [Scammer Alert July 5, 2024](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/scammer-alert/) [Disclaimer On Activity Of Alpha Trust Investment Club (ATIC) June 26, 2024](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/disclaimer-on-activity-of-alpha-trust-investment-club-atic/) [Ponzi: Famzhi Boss Jailed Five Years for Investment Scam June 19, 2024](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/ponzi-famzhi-boss-jailed-five-years-for-investment-scam/) [Blacklisting Of Six (6) Unregulated Online Trading Platforms April 27, 2023](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/blacklisting-of-six-6-unregulated-online-trading-platforms/) [Investors Alert \u2013 Blacklisting Of Five (5) Italian E-Commerce Websites March 30, 2023](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/investors-alert-blacklisting-of-five-5-italian-e-commerce-websites/) [Ponzi: Court Sets March 16 for Trial of Vektr Capital & 2 Ot Feb. 13, 2023](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/ponzi-court-sets-march-16-for-trial-of-vektr-capital-2-ot/) [Blacklisting Of Four (4) Italian E-Commerce Companies Dec. 22, 2022](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/blacklisting-of-four-4-italian-e-commerce-companies/) [Activities Of Some Unregistered Investments Schemes March 17, 2022](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/activities-of-some-unregistered-investments-schemes/) [Activities Of FinAfrica Investment Limited (Chinmark Group) Dec. 25, 2021](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/activities-of-finafrica-investment-limited-chinmark-group/) [Activities Of Poyoyo Investment (Pilvest) Nigeria Limited Dec. 24, 2021](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/activities-of-poyoyo-investment-pilvest-nigeria-limited/)", "content_hash": "2462eed9b881436d3f5a786b0a639a4b524beb7e87cb8b9121e7e48abf6a6cac", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1935", "title": "Information for Harmed Investors Published: November 27, 2023", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/information-for-harmed-investors/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/information-for-harmed-investors/", "text": "Information for Harmed Investors Published: November 27, 2023 Have you been harmed by the actions of a company in the Nigerian capital markets? This guide provides essential steps to protect your interests and seek redress. Empower yourself as an investor. Understand your rights and options when dealing with potential investment malpractice. The Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) Nigeria is committed to safeguarding the integrity of the capital markets and protecting the rights of investors. If you have been harmed by the actions of a company in the Nigerian capital markets, whether through fraudulent activities, misrepresentation, or other malpractices, you have the right to seek redress. This guide provides essential steps to protect your interests and empower you as an investor.Understanding Your RightsAs an investor, you have certain fundamental rights, including the right to:Receive accurate and complete information about investments: Companies must provide investors with truthful and transparent information about their financial standing, operations, and risks associated with their investments.Fair treatment and protection from manipulation: Investors are entitled to fair treatment and protection from market manipulations that could disadvantage them.Access to remedies: If you have been harmed by the actions of a company, you have the right to seek compensation and hold the responsible parties accountable.Identifying Potential Investment MalpracticeSeveral red flags may indicate potential investment malpractice, including:Unrealistic or guaranteed returns: Promises of high or guaranteed returns without corresponding risks should raise concerns.Unsolicited or high-pressure sales tactics: Be wary of aggressive sales tactics or unsolicited investment offers that pressure you to make quick decisions.Complex or unclear investment products: If you don't understand the investment products or their associated risks, seek clarification before investing.Unlicensed or unregistered investment firms: Ensure that the company you are investing with is licensed and registered with the SEC.Steps to Take if You Have Been HarmedIf you suspect that you have been harmed by the actions of a company in the Nigerian capital markets, take the following steps:Gather Evidence: Collect any documentation, correspondence, or communication related to your investment. This may include account statements, investment contracts, and any records of interactions with the company.Report the Malpractice: Notify the SEC by filing a complaint through their online portal or by contacting their Enforcement Division. Provide detailed information about the alleged malpractice, including the company involved, the nature of the misconduct, and the financial impact on you.Seek Legal Counsel: Consider consulting with a qualified securities attorney to discuss your legal options and representation in pursuing a claim against the company.Stay Informed: Keep yourself updated on the SEC's investigations and enforcement actions related to the company. You can follow the SEC's website and subscribe to their news feeds to receive updates.Additional Resources and SupportSecurities and Exchange Commission (SEC) Nigeria: Visit the SEC's website for investor education resources, complaint filing procedures, and information on enforcement actions. [Click to Access the Investor Education Page](https://www.sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/development/investor-education/) [Click to Keep Up-to-date with Capital Market Enforcement News and Circulars](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/) 2. Investor Protection Fund (IPF): The IPF is a government-funded program that provides compensation to investors who have suffered losses due to fraudulent activities in the capital markets. [Learn more about the fund here.](https://ngxgroup.com/exchange/trade/investor-protection-education/investors-protection-fund/)3. Legal Aid Council of Nigeria (LACON): LACON provides legal aid and representation to individuals who cannot afford legal services. [Contact LACON](https://legalaidcouncil.gov.ng) for assistance in pursuing claims against companies involved in investment malpractice.Remember, you are not alone. The SEC is committed to protecting investors and ensuring a fair and transparent capital market. By taking action and seeking redress, you can contribute to safeguarding the integrity of the Nigerian capital markets and protecting other investors from harm.", "content_hash": "a31461ae4ca4ccae532e094329d2978f182937319fc889daf4192a62f14aa10a", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1936", "title": "About", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/", "text": "About The Securities and Exchange Commission, Nigeria, is the primary regulatory institution of the Nigerian Capital Market. blocks section [Enforcements](https://www.sec.gov.ng/enforcements/) [Corporate Social Responsibility](https://www.sec.gov.ng/corporate-social-responsibility/) [Events](https://www.sec.gov.ng/events/) [Our Role](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/our-role/) [Our Role](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/our-role/) [Organizational Chart](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/organizational-chart/) [Organizational Chart](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/organizational-chart/) [Corporate Governance](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/corporate-governance/) [Corporate Governance](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/corporate-governance/) [Resources](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/) [Resources](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/) [International Relations](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/) [International Relations](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/) [Media Gallery](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/media-gallery/) [Media Gallery](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/media-gallery/) [News](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/news/) [News](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/news/) [Careers At SEC](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/careers/) [Careers At SEC](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/careers/) [Contact Us](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/contact-us/) [Contact Us](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/contact-us/)", "content_hash": "c05ad3e66f77fe580138d9fd85a97b3f055cac4bafcfbcd91ddd7ab14bd6eae1", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1937", "title": "Who We Are Published: December 22, 2025", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/our-role/who-we-are/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/our-role/who-we-are/", "text": "Who We Are Published: December 22, 2025 Our Corporate Identity, Rich History, and Guiding Ethos at the Securities and Exchange Commission, Nigeria The Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) is the apex regulatory body for the Nigerian capital market. It was established in 1979 and operates under the purview of the Investments and Securities Act (ISA) of 2007.The SEC's primary mission is to protect investors, maintain fair and efficient markets, and ensure the integrity of the Nigerian capital market. It achieves this by regulating various market participants, including securities exchanges, investment firms, and public companies issuing securities. The SEC also sets rules and standards for the market and monitors compliance.Our MissionTo Develop and Regulate a Capital Market that is Dynamic, Fair, Transparent and Efficient, to Contribute to the Nation\u2019s Economic Development.Our VisionTo be Africa\u2019s Leading Capital Market Regulator.Our Core ValuesOur values form the bedrock of our organization, guiding every decision and action we take. We hold ourselves to the highest standards of:Governance: Upholding the principles of good governance.Fairness: Ensuring equitable opportunities and outcomes for all market participants.Integrity: Operating with the utmost honesty and transparency.Excellence: Striving for continuous improvement and excellence in all we do.Loyalty: Demonstrating unwavering dedication to our stakeholders and mission.Discipline: Enforcing rigorous standards and adherence to regulations.Our HistoryThe origins of the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) trace back to 1962, when an ad hoc advisory Capital Issues Committee was set up under the Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) to review and time capital\u2010raising applications\u2014avoiding market oversaturation. Operating informally within the CBN, this body provided consultative oversight without formal regulatory authority.1973: Creation of the Capital Issues CommissionIn response to expanding economic activity and under the Nigerian Enterprises Promotion Decree (1972), the Capital Issues Commission was legally constituted by decree in March 1973, inheriting the Committee\u2019s mandate. Its nine\u2010member board\u2014including a CBN\u2010appointed Chairman and representatives from federal ministries, industry and finance\u2014gained formal power to regulate the timing, pricing and allocation of new securities.1979\u20131980: Emergence of the SECA Federal Financial System Review Committee (1976) recommended further strengthening Nigeria\u2019s capital\u2010market oversight. Consequently, the Securities and Exchange Commission Decree No. 71 of 1979 transformed the Capital Issues Commission into today\u2019s SEC. Key changes effective 1 January 1980 included:Independent status: Excised from the CBN, though initially still funded by itExpanded board: Twelve members, with a CBN representative as Chairman and others drawn from the Ministries of Finance, Trade & Industry, the Nigerian Stock Exchange and the Nigerian Enterprises Promotion Board, plus individuals nominated on meritBroader powers: Authority to regulate, develop the market, set issue prices and allotment criteriaStaffing: Launched with 51 staff (including seven seconded from the CBN for three years)Legal Framework Enhancements1988: The original SEC Decree was re-enacted as Decree No. 29 of 1988, plugging gaps and bolstering enforcement.1996\u20131999: A high-level review headed by Chief Dennis Odife led to the Investments and Securities Act No. 45 of 1999, repealing the 1988 law and aiming to foster a more efficient, vibrant market.2007: The ISA underwent comprehensive amendments (ISA 29 of 2007), further strengthening the SEC\u2019s regulatory authority. Today, the SEC derives its powers from the ISA 29 of 2007.International CollaborationJune 1985: Nigeria\u2019s SEC became a member of the International Organization of Securities Commissions (IOSCO), committing to globally recognized regulatory standards.2006: Attained Appendix \u2018A\u2019 Signatory status under the IOSCO Multilateral Memorandum of Understanding (MMOU), enabling mutual cooperation, information sharing and benchmarking of market rules against international best practices.Our Corporate PhilosophyBuilt upon a foundation of mission, vision and values, the SEC remains dedicated to:Investor Protection: Safeguarding market participants through transparent regulationMarket Development: Fostering growth, innovation and efficiency in Nigeria\u2019s capital marketsIntegrity and Accountability: Upholding the highest ethical standards in all operationsGlobal Engagement: Collaborating internationally to align with best practices and support cross-border investmentAt the SEC, we are not just shaping markets; we are shaping the future. Join us on this journey towards a more vibrant and prosperous financial landscape for Nigeria and beyond.", "content_hash": "1415fe1b9249c885fe4a97df2923e91acbbfd42753a5a75bda08f4a74b50bf92", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1938", "title": "How We Function Published: August 15, 2024", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/our-role/how-we-function/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/our-role/how-we-function/", "text": "How We Function Published: August 15, 2024 Promoting investor protection, ensuring market integrity, and fostering capital market development for a thriving Nigerian economy. Discover how we function and work diligently to make the financial landscape a safer and more accessible place for all. Market Regulation: Protecting Investors and Market IntegrityRegistrationAt SEC Nigeria, we meticulously assess individuals and institutions seeking entry into the market. Whether it's securities, exchanges, futures, options, or more, we set the bar for market participation, ensuring only fit and proper entities join the fray.InspectionTransparency and accountability are at the heart of our mission. To maintain market integrity, we conduct regular information calls, inquiries, and comprehensive audits of market participants. Our rigorous inspections serve as a cornerstone of trust in the financial ecosystem.SurveillanceTo maintain a level playing field, we maintain vigilant surveillance over exchanges and trading systems. By doing so, we thwart potential breaches of market rules and detect manipulative practices. Our watchful eyes ensure a fair and orderly marketplace.InvestigationWhen the integrity of the market is called into question, our dedicated investigative arm springs into action. We dive deep into alleged breaches of market laws and regulations, and when required, we enforce sanctions to uphold the rule of law.Enforcement ActionsAt SEC Nigeria, we leave no room for compromise when it comes to market integrity. We swiftly take actions against market operators found in breach. These actions may include fines, bans, suspensions, or legal proceedings. Our robust enforcement measures are the bedrock of trust and confidence in the market.Rule MakingIn an ever-evolving financial landscape, we remain adaptable. We continually align our regulations with international best practices, ensuring our market remains competitive and attuned to global standards. Our commitment to excellence drives our rule-making process.Market Development: Fostering Investor ParticipationIn the realm of market development, we collaborate with stakeholders to encourage investor participation through various activities:Workshops, Seminars, and Town Hall Meetings: We facilitate knowledge-sharing and engagement through events that empower investors with valuable insights.Introduction of e-processes like e-dividend: Embracing technology to simplify processes and enhance the investor experience.Educational Initiatives: We promote financial literacy through quizzes, essays, and the introduction of capital market studies in tertiary institutions.Publications and Media Programs: Our informative publications and media programs disseminate market-related information to a wider audience.How We WorkGovernance: We operate under the Investments and Securities Act 29 of 2007, which provides the legal framework for our activities.Leadership: Our nine-member Board, supported by a four-member Management Executive Committee, steers our strategic direction.Dedicated Team: With over 500 dedicated employees across various 4 Directorates, 3 Zonal Offices, and 15 Departments, and 42 Divisions, we form a robust force in the financial services sector.Adaptability: We continuously adapt to the dynamic financial landscape by conducting research and holding enlightenment programs to educate the public about the capital market.", "content_hash": "1dcbb35ffaa77de76f076347780dd23b0fcf2a3111f7832ef99e50d44232a382", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1939", "title": "Who We Regulate Published: August 14, 2023", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/our-role/who-we-regulate/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/our-role/who-we-regulate/", "text": "Who We Regulate Published: August 14, 2023 Understanding Our Regulatory Reach Explore the Diverse Entities Under SEC Nigeria's Oversight and Learn About Our Regulatory Measures At the Nigerian Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC), our mission is to foster a vibrant and secure capital market by regulating a diverse range of entities. We work tirelessly to ensure the integrity, transparency, and fairness of the market for the benefit of investors and the Nigerian economy.Entities We Regulate1. Securities/Commodity Exchanges/Capital Trade PointsWe oversee the operations of securities and commodity exchanges, as well as capital trade points, ensuring they adhere to best practices and maintain market integrity.2. Futures, Options, and Derivatives ExchangesOur regulatory oversight extends to futures, options, and derivatives exchanges, where we enforce rules and regulations to safeguard market stability.3. Depository, Clearing, and Settlement AgenciesWe closely monitor depository, clearing, and settlement agencies, ensuring the efficient and secure transfer of securities and funds.4. Capital Market OperatorsOur oversight encompasses a wide spectrum of capital market operators, including Auditor, Broker, Broker/Dealer, Central System Depository, Corporate Investment Advisers, Corporate Sub-Broker, Credit Rating Agency, Custodian, Dealer, Estate Surveyors/Valuers, Floor Broker, Floor Trader, FMDQ OTC Dealer, Fund Manager, Fund/Portfolio Manager, Individual Sub-Broker, Inter-Dealer Broker, Investment Advisers, Issuing House, Market Maker, Over-the-Counter Exchange, Portfolio Manager, Property Manager, Private Equity Fund Manager, Rating Agency, Receiving Banker, Registrars, Reporting Accountants, Securities Lending Agent, Self-Regulatory Organizations, Solicitors, Sub-Broker, Trustees, Underwriters, Venture Capital Manager.5. SecuritiesWe regulate various types of securities, including Equities, Debentures, Debt Instruments, and Collective Investment Schemes, ensuring transparency and investor protection.Our Regulatory Activities1. Registration of Securities and Market IntermediariesSEC Nigeria registers securities and market intermediaries to guarantee their fitness and suitability to operate in the capital market.2. InspectionThrough both on-site and off-site inspections, we maintain market integrity and scrutinize market participants to ensure compliance with regulations.3. Vigilant SurveillanceOur vigilant surveillance prevents market rule violations and manipulations, safeguarding the interests of investors.4. Investigation and EnforcementIn cases of alleged market law breaches, we conduct thorough investigations and enforce sanctions to maintain market discipline.5. Rule-makingWe continuously develop and implement rules to align with international best practices, fostering a competitive and secure capital market environment.Our Varied Oversight1. Securities IssuersWe oversee securities offered to the public, ensuring full disclosure and transparency in offerings, including public offers, rights issues, private placements, bonus issues, and existing securities.2. Market IntermediariesSEC monitors various market participants, including securities exchanges, clearing agencies, and capital market operators, ensuring compliance with regulations.3. Business CombinationsWe oversee transactions involving private companies with foreign interest, ensuring they adhere to regulatory requirements.4. Mergers & AcquisitionsSEC reviews and processes applications for acquisitions, takeovers, and management buyouts, guaranteeing compliance with regulations.5. Investment SchemesWe review and authorize various investment schemes, including unit trust schemes, real estate investment schemes, private equity, venture capital funds, and specialized schemes offered to the public.6. Market OperatorsOur Registration and Exchanges Department registers market operators and regulates stock, commodity, futures, and options exchanges.7. Financial InstitutionsOur Investment Management Department safeguards investor interests in various financial products.8. InvestorsWe do our best to foster financial inclusion and investor education, ensuring all segments of the population are well-informed about the capital market.9. New ProductsThe commission identifies and promotes the development of innovative financial products and processes in the securities market.Our Primary Regulatory Departments and Their Regulatory Duties1. Monitoring DepartmentResponsible for identifying, assessing, and monitoring the operations and risks of registered operators.Ensures market operators maintain healthy financial states.Ensures compliance with laws, rules, and regulations.Ensures compliance with the Anti-money laundering prohibition Act (2004).2. Enforcement DepartmentEnsures compliance with regulations and imposes sanctions for infractions.Investigates unresolved complaints and protects investors.Conducts on-site inspections and resolves disputes involving Capital Market Operators (CMOs) and investors.At SEC Nigeria, our commitment to regulatory excellence is unwavering. We continue to evolve and adapt to the dynamic landscape of the capital market, working diligently to maintain trust, transparency, and investor confidence. Together, we build a brighter financial future for Nigeria.", "content_hash": "c0b8815866e3779f30f0a3180e51c4df12b5d61fe1ec976cd5c6329c324c9783", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1940", "title": "Divisional Structure Published: August 14, 2023", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/organizational-chart/divisional-structure/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/organizational-chart/divisional-structure/", "text": "The Office of the Director General serves as the apex of the Nigerian Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) hierarchy. It plays a pivotal role in overseeing the overall functions of the Commission. This Directorate, led by the Director General, sets the strategic direction for SEC, ensuring that its objectives align with the broader goals of the Nigerian capital market.Organizational Structure:Under the Office of the Director General, there are four key Departments and three Offices. These components work collaboratively to execute the Commission's mandate effectively.1. Internal Control DepartmentFunctions:The Internal Control Department serves as a crucial management tool, providing independent appraisal of SEC's activities. Its primary functions include ensuring compliance with relevant policies and regulations, enhancing efficiency and effectiveness, and safeguarding the Commission's assets.Objectives:Ensure proper adherence to internal control procedures and established policies.Enhance efficiency and effectiveness of the Commission.Safeguard the assets of the Commission.Divisions and Activities:Budget Control Division: Monitors and controls expenditures, ensuring they align with the Commission's budget.Financial Audit Division: Reviews accounting records and financial transactions to ensure accuracy and compliance.Systems Audit and Organization and Methods Division: Assesses information technology systems and conducts special investigations as needed to enhance processes.2. External Relations DepartmentFunctions:The External Relations Department manages all market-related internal and external information for the Commission. It generates publicity, maintains media relationships, and coordinates training programs for media practitioners.Objective:Enhance the Commission's image and facilitate communication with stakeholders.Divisions and Activities:Public Relations: Develops and implements public relations strategies and activities.Corporate Communications: Manages internal and external communication channels.Stakeholder Complaint Management Division: Facilitates the efficient and fair resolution of complaints raised by stakeholders of the Commission.3. Strategy DepartmentFunctions:The Strategy Department conducts in-depth analyses, formulate long-term vision and goals, and develop strategic initiatives to achieve them. This includes identifying emerging trends, assessing risks and opportunities, and translating the Commission's objectives into actionable plans.Objectives:Develop and implement strategic plans that align with the Commission's mission and vision.Conduct research and analysis to inform strategic decision-making.Identify and prioritize key strategic initiatives for the Commission.Monitor and evaluate the effectiveness of strategic initiatives.Foster a culture of strategic thinking and planning within the SEC.Stand-Alone Offices in the DirectorateWithin the Office of the Director General, there are three offices:1. Office of the Director General (DGO)Function:The Office of the Director General (DGO) provides essential support and strategic direction for the Securities and Exchange Commission of Nigeria (SEC Nigeria). It serves as the central point for leadership, policy development, and coordination across all Commission departments.Objectives:Ensure the efficient and effective operation of the Commission in line with its mandate.Provide strategic guidance and direction to achieve the Commission's vision and mission.Foster a culture of collaboration and transparency within the Commission.Represent the Commission in official engagements and liaise with key stakeholders.2. Office of the Commission's SecretaryResponsible for preparing Board meetings, acting as a bridge between the Board and Management, providing secretarial services, and reviewing and sealing all contracts and agreements approved by the Commission.3. Office of the Chief EconomistFocuses on economic analysis and research to support SEC's decision-making processes.The Office of the Director General sets the tone for the entire Commission, ensuring that SEC operates efficiently, adheres to regulations, and maintains its reputation as a reliable and effective regulator of the Nigerian capital market. It provides strategic leadership and direction for the various functions and activities carried out by SEC's Departments and Offices.", "content_hash": "9edb0b31846c9c52112c05efc7f30ddef899dbd13a39ed8f21c12973428081bf", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1941", "title": "Our Principles Published: August 14, 2023", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/corporate-governance/our-principles/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/corporate-governance/our-principles/", "text": "Our Principles Published: August 14, 2023 Discover SEC's guiding principles Explore the core operational values shaping Nigeria's Securities and Exchange Commission's actions and commitments. The Nigerian Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) is the apex regulatory body for the Nigerian capital market. We are committed to protecting investors, maintaining market integrity, and promoting market development.Our principles guide us in everything we do. We believe in transparency, accountability, innovation, excellence, and integrity.We are committed to upholding our principles and conducting ourselves in a manner that is worthy of the public's trust. We understand that our actions have a direct impact on the lives of investors and the Nigerian economy.We are working tirelessly to protect investors, maintain market integrity, and promote market development. We believe that by upholding our principles, we can help to create a fair, orderly, and efficient capital market that contributes to the economic growth and development of Nigeria.TransparencyAt the Nigerian Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC), we believe that transparency is essential for building trust and confidence in the capital market. That's why we are committed to being transparent in all of our operations.We publish a variety of reports and documents on our website, including our annual report, budget, and rules and regulations. We also hold regular meetings with our stakeholders to provide updates on our activities.We believe that transparency is essential for protecting investors and ensuring fair and orderly markets.AccountabilityWe are accountable to our stakeholders for our performance. That's why we have a number of oversight mechanisms in place, including a board of directors, audit committee, and risk management committee.We also subject ourselves to regular external audits. We believe that accountability is essential for maintaining the public's trust in our organization.InnovationWe encourage innovation in all that we do. We believe that innovation is essential for the capital market to remain competitive and efficient.We adopt new technologies and experiment with new approaches to our work. We also support the development of innovative new financial products and services.We believe that innovation is essential for promoting economic growth and development.ExcellenceWe strive for excellence in all that we do. We believe that excellence is essential for fulfilling our mission and achieving our goals.We have high standards of performance and we are committed to continuous improvement. We believe that excellence is essential for maintaining the public's trust in our organization.IntegrityWe are committed to integrity in all of our dealings. We believe that integrity is essential for building trust and confidence in the capital market.We have a code of conduct that requires all employees to act ethically and professionally. We also have a zero-tolerance policy for corruption and fraud.We believe that integrity is essential for protecting investors and ensuring fair and orderly markets.AssuranceWe are committed to upholding our principles and conducting ourselves in a manner that is worthy of the public's trust. We understand that our actions have a direct impact on the lives of investors and the Nigerian economy.We are committed to working tirelessly to protect investors, maintain market integrity, and promote market development. We are also committed to being transparent, accountable, innovative, and excellent in all that we do.We believe that by upholding our principles, we can help to create a fair, orderly, and efficient capital market that contributes to the economic growth and development of Nigeria.", "content_hash": "135594b0211a16eac9189a75df7df932bf0f195d4b4529413aae722ecf64a16b", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1942", "title": "Performance Pledges Published: August 14, 2023", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/corporate-governance/performance-pledges/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/corporate-governance/performance-pledges/", "text": "Performance Pledges Published: August 14, 2023 We\u2019re here to serve. We commit to transparency and efficiency through Performance Pledges, outlining our service standards and dedication to enhancing Nigeria's securities and exchange ecosystem. The Securities and Exchange Commission of Nigeria (SEC) is the apex regulatory body for the Nigerian capital market. Because of this, we are committed to fostering a vibrant and secure capital market by regulating a diverse range of entities and promoting investor confidence.Our performance pledges reflect our commitment to protecting investors, maintaining market integrity, and promoting market development. We are committed to working with all stakeholders to create a capital market that is fair, transparent, and efficient.Protecting InvestorsAt the Securities and Exchange Commission of Nigeria (SEC), our top priority is to protect investors. We do this by:Registering only fit and proper securities and market intermediaries. This means that we carefully assess all individuals and institutions seeking to operate in the Nigerian capital market to ensure that they meet our high standards of integrity and competency.Ensuring full disclosure and transparency in securities offerings. We require all public offerings to include comprehensive information about the issuer and the securities being offered. This information helps investors make informed investment decisions.Monitoring market participants to ensure compliance with regulations. We regularly inspect market participants to ensure that they are adhering to our rules and regulations. This helps to protect investors from fraud and other abuses.Investigating alleged breaches of market laws and enforcing sanctions. If we receive a complaint about a market participant, we will thoroughly investigate the matter and take appropriate action, which may include fines, suspensions, or even criminal charges.Maintaining Market IntegrityWe are committed to maintaining a fair and orderly capital market. We do this by:Conducting vigilant surveillance of exchanges and trading systems. We use state-of-the-art technology to monitor trading activity and identify any potential irregularities. This helps to prevent market manipulation and insider trading.Developing and implementing rules in line with international best practices. We regularly review our rules and regulations to ensure that they are aligned with the latest international standards. This helps to promote confidence in the Nigerian capital market among domestic and foreign investors.Promoting Market DevelopmentWe believe that a vibrant and efficient capital market is essential for economic growth. We support market development by:Overseeing the development of innovative financial products and processes. We encourage market participants to develop new products and services that meet the needs of investors and issuers. This helps to make the Nigerian capital market more attractive to both domestic and foreign investors.Fostering financial inclusion and investor education. We are committed to making the capital market more accessible to all Nigerians. We do this by providing educational resources and programs to help investors understand the market and make informed investment decisions.We Give You Our AssuranceSEC Nigeria is committed to fulfilling its performance pledges. We are working tirelessly to create a capital market that is fair, transparent, and efficient. We are confident that our efforts will help to promote economic growth and prosperity for all Nigerians.Invest with ConfidenceWhen you invest in the Nigerian capital market, you can do so with confidence knowing that the SEC is working to protect your interests. We are committed to creating a fair and transparent market where all investors have an equal opportunity to succeed.", "content_hash": "808695d614ae99b38fa3b8144b89c6509b2ec4a7c8e17c41ba477cd1ad16ff3a", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1943", "title": "Independent Panels and Tribunals Published: August 14, 2023", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/corporate-governance/independent-panels-and-tribunals/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/corporate-governance/independent-panels-and-tribunals/", "text": "Independent Panels and Tribunals Published: August 14, 2023 Understand the independent panels and tribunals that protect investors and enforce the rules and regulations of the Securities and Exchange Commission. The Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) of Nigeria has two established panels and tribunals to adjudicate disputes and enforce its rules and regulations:The Administrative Proceedings Committee (APC); andThe Investment and Securities Tribunal (IST).The APC is a quasi-judicial body that hears and determines cases of alleged violations of the laws and regulations governing the capital market. It can impose a variety of sanctions on violators, including fines, bans, and suspensions.The IST is a specialized tribunal that hears and determines appeals against the decisions of the APC. It can also hear and determine cases of alleged violations of the laws and regulations governing the capital market that are not brought before the APC.How the APC and IST Protect InvestorsThe APC and IST play an important role in protecting investors in the Nigerian capital market. They provide a forum for investors to resolve disputes with market operators and to seek redress for alleged violations of the laws and regulations governing the capital market. The APC and IST also play a deterrent role by imposing sanctions on violators.Here are some specific examples of how the APC and IST have protected investors in the Nigerian capital market:The APC has imposed fines on market operators for insider trading, market manipulation, and other violations of the laws and regulations governing the capital market.The IST has overturned the decisions of the APC in some cases, where it has found that the APC's decisions were not supported by the evidence or the law.The IST has also issued orders against market operators for insider trading, market manipulation, and other violations of the laws and regulations governing the capital market.How to File a Complaint with the APC or ISTIf you are an investor and you believe that your rights have been violated by a market operator, you can file a complaint with the APC or IST.To file a complaint with the APC, you must submit a written complaint to the Secretary to the APC. The complaint must include the following information:Your name and contact informationThe name of the market operator against whom you are complainingA detailed description of the alleged violationAny supporting documentationTo file a complaint with the IST, you must submit a written petition to the Registrar of the IST. The petition must include the following information:Your name and contact informationThe name of the market operator against whom you are complainingA detailed description of the alleged violationAny supporting documentationThe APC and IST are important institutions in the Nigerian capital market. They help to protect investors and maintain the integrity of the market. If you are an investor and you believe that your rights have been violated by a market operator, you should contact the APC or IST to file a complaint.", "content_hash": "f3d38e2548211cdb32b3325f48fcda2a4b227ba9132e033917419706ec09cf3f", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1944", "title": "International Relations", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/", "text": "International Relations As a key player in the global regulatory arena, the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) actively collaborates with multinational and international organizations to promote cross-border cooperation, facilitate information exchange, and bolster investor protection. Explore SEC's membership and engagement with various organizations, highlighting its commitment to fostering a harmonized and efficient capital market ecosystem. blocks section [MOU with Other Countries](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/mou-with-other-countries/) [MOU with Other Countries](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/mou-with-other-countries/) [SEC and FATF](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-fatf/) [SEC and FATF](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-fatf/) [SEC and GIABA](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-giaba/) [SEC and GIABA](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-giaba/) [SEC and IFSB](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-ifsb/) [SEC and IFSB](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-ifsb/) [SEC and IOSCO](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-iosco/) [SEC and IOSCO](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-iosco/) [SEC and NIGSAC](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-nigsac/) [SEC and NIGSAC](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-nigsac/) [SEC and WASRA](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-wasra/) [SEC and WASRA](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-wasra/)", "content_hash": "895aae5087262dd7ac897f2dfae7bbb7b85267779c423a1d41c608c7bf02b5f7", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1945", "title": "MOU with Other Countries Published: November 15, 2023", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/mou-with-other-countries/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/mou-with-other-countries/", "text": "MOU with Other Countries Published: November 15, 2023 The SEC has established Memoranda of Understanding (MOUs) with securities regulators in numerous countries, fostering cooperation and facilitating information exchange on securities regulation, enforcement, and investor protection. This page provides an overview of the SEC's bilateral MOUs and highlights the benefits of these agreements in promoting a stable and interconnected global capital market. The Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) has established Memoranda of Understanding (MOUs) with securities regulators in ten countries, demonstrating its commitment to fostering international cooperation and promoting high standards of capital market regulation. These MOUs provide a framework for information sharing, consultation, and cooperation on a range of regulatory matters, including:Investor protection and market integrityCross-border enforcementRegulatory harmonizationCapacity building The SEC's MOU partners are: Country Regulator MOU Signing Date Oman Capital Market Authority (CMA) February 20, 2012 Mauritius Financial Services Commission (FSC) May 15, 2012 Kenya Capital Markets Authority (CMA) February 23, 2012 Malaysia Securities Commission Malaysia (SC) November 15, 2007 India Securities and Exchange Board of India (SEBI) April 10, 2007 Tanzania Capital Markets and Securities Authority (CMSA) December 7, 2005 Uganda Capital Markets Authority (CMA) July 25, 2005 China China Securities Regulatory Commission (CSRC) June 14, 2005 Ghana Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) August 27, 2003 South Africa Financial Services Board (FSB) August 27, 2002 These MOUs have played a significant role in facilitating cross-border cooperation and enhancing investor protection.SEC's commitment to international cooperation is further reflected in its active participation in multilateral organizations such as the International Organization of Securities Commissions (IOSCO) and the West African Capital Markets Integration Council (WACMIC). Through these organizations, the SEC collaborates with other regulators to develop global regulatory standards and promote cross-border enforcement activities.", "content_hash": "114817dcbf28a8b59d468f57a567c37a89f60579a564a900bb59d2d1adb1c00e", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1946", "title": "SEC and IOSCO Published: November 15, 2023", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-iosco/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-iosco/", "text": "SEC and IOSCO Published: November 15, 2023 The SEC is a member of the International Organization of Securities Commissions (IOSCO), a global body of securities regulators committed to promoting high standards of regulation and investor protection. The Nigerian Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) has maintained a long-standing and active partnership with the International Organization of Securities Commissions (IOSCO), a global body of securities regulators dedicated to promoting high standards of regulation and investor protection. This partnership has been instrumental in fostering cross-border cooperation, enhancing market integrity, and promoting the development of a sound and vibrant capital market in Nigeria.IOSCO: A Global Champion of Securities RegulationIOSCO, established in 1983, is a recognized standard setter for the securities sector, bringing together securities regulators from over 130 jurisdictions. The organization's objectives include:Developing and implementing internationally recognized and consistent standards of securities regulationProtecting investorsEnhancing market integrity and efficiencyPromoting cross-border cooperationNigeria's Enduring Commitment to IOSCOThe SEC joined IOSCO in June 1985, demonstrating Nigeria's early recognition of the importance of international cooperation in securities regulation. Over the years, the SEC has actively participated in IOSCO initiatives, contributing to the development of global regulatory standards and collaborating on cross-border enforcement activities.Key Milestones in the SEC-IOSCO Partnership1985: Nigeria joins IOSCO, marking the beginning of a strong and enduring partnership.2006: The SEC qualifies as an Appendix 'A' Signatory to the IOSCO MMOU, further strengthening its commitment to international regulatory standards.2013: IOSCO conducts a Detailed Assessment of Implementation of IOSCO Objectives and Principles of Securities Regulation in Nigeria, recognizing the SEC's progress in upholding global regulatory standards.The SEC-IOSCO Partnership in ActionThe SEC's collaboration with IOSCO has yielded tangible benefits for the Nigerian capital market, including:Enhanced investor protection: IOSCO's Principles of Securities Regulation have informed the SEC's regulatory framework, ensuring that investors in Nigeria enjoy high levels of protection.Improved market transparency: IOSCO's initiatives on market transparency, such as the Disclosure of Cross-Border Transactions, have enhanced market transparency in Nigeria.Strengthened cross-border cooperation: The SEC's participation in IOSCO's Multilateral Memoranda of Understanding (MMOUs) has facilitated cross-border cooperation and information sharing, enabling the SEC to effectively address cross-border regulatory issues.Explore IOSCO's ResourcesTo learn more about IOSCO's work and its impact on the global securities landscape, we encourage you to visit the IOSCO website: [www.iosco.org](https://www.iosco.org/) Through its active engagement with IOSCO, the SEC remains committed to upholding high standards of securities regulation, fostering a vibrant capital market, and protecting the interests of investors in Nigeria. [Explore Publications from IOSCO](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/papers-and-publications/publications-from-international-partners/iosco-publications/)", "content_hash": "278102248ba0cdf3916bce9987b32632e5f907ad64eafe9aad9d7d11ea0ef2e4", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1947", "title": "SEC and the Nigeria Sanctions Committee Published: November 15, 2023", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-nigsac/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-nigsac/", "text": "SEC and the Nigeria Sanctions Committee Published: November 15, 2023 The SEC is a member of the Nigeria Sanctions Committee, a body responsible for implementing targeted financial sanctions in support of national and international security objectives. The Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) plays a crucial role in the implementation of targeted financial sanctions, collaborating closely with the Nigeria Sanctions Committee (NigSac) to safeguard the integrity of the Nigerian capital market and combat the financing of terrorism and other illicit activities. This partnership ensures that the Nigerian capital market adheres to international standards and contributes to global efforts to counter financial crime.The Nigeria Sanctions Committee: Enforcing Targeted Financial SanctionsEstablished in 2022, NigSac is the national body responsible for implementing targeted financial sanctions under the United Nations Security Council Resolutions (UNSCRs). NigSac's mandate includes:Identifying and designating individuals and entities subject to targeted financial sanctionsFreezing the assets of designated persons and entitiesProhibiting transactions with designated persons and entitiesImplementing travel bans on designated personsThe SEC's Role in Sanctions EnforcementThe SEC plays a critical role in NigSac's work by:Identifying and verifying the identities of designated persons and entitiesFreezing the assets of designated persons and entities held within the capital marketDisseminating sanctions information to regulated entitiesInvestigating potential violations of sanctions regulationsStrengthening Cooperation for Effective Sanctions ImplementationThe SEC and NigSac have established a strong working relationship, ensuring seamless coordination and information sharing. This collaboration has been instrumental in:Timely identification and designation of individuals and entities subject to sanctionsEffective freezing of assets and prevention of prohibited transactionsEnhanced compliance with sanctions regulations by market participantsLearn More About Sanctions ComplianceTo learn more about NigSac's activities and the SEC's role in sanctions enforcement, we encourage you to visit the NigSac website [www.nigsac.gov.ng](https://nigsac.gov.ng/) Through its active participation in the NigSac's work, the SEC remains committed to safeguarding the integrity of the Nigerian capital market, protecting investors, and upholding the nation's international obligations in combating financial crime.", "content_hash": "c35d9611d2ee9fc916db32e73c8b5201993cc53925cf0dd94e431ce30f4270de", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1948", "title": "SEC and the West African Securities Regulators Association (WASRA) Published: November 15, 2023", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-wasra/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-wasra/", "text": "SEC and the West African Securities Regulators Association (WASRA) Published: November 15, 2023 The SEC collaborates with the West African Securities Regulators Association (WASRA), a regional body that facilitates cooperation and information exchange among West African securities regulators. The Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) is a founding member and active participant in the West African Securities Regulators Association (WASRA), a regional body dedicated to promoting the harmonization of securities regulation and enhancing investor protection across West Africa. This partnership has been instrumental in fostering a more integrated and efficient capital market ecosystem within the region.WASRA: Advancing Regulatory Harmonization in West AfricaFounded in 2015, WASRA brings together securities regulators from 15 West African countries, with the mission to:Harmonize securities regulation and promote a level playing field in the regionStrengthen investor protection and market integrityEnhance market transparency and efficiencyFacilitate cross-border capital flows and investmentsFoster capacity building and knowledge sharing among regulatorsThe SEC's Pioneering Role in WASRAThe SEC has been a driving force behind WASRA's establishment and success, playing a key role in shaping the organization's vision and strategic direction. The SEC's leadership contributions include:Hosting the inaugural meeting of WASRA in 2015Providing technical expertise and guidance in developing WASRA's regulatory frameworkChampioning initiatives to promote cross-border listings and tradingAdvocating for enhanced investor education and protectionKey Achievements of the SEC-WASRA PartnershipThe SEC-WASRA partnership has yielded significant milestones in promoting regional capital market integration, including:Adoption of the WASRA Common Core Principles for Securities RegulationEstablishment of a regional cross-border surveillance systemDevelopment of harmonized regulatory requirements for collective investment schemesImplementation of investor education initiatives across the regionExplore WASRA's InitiativesTo learn more about WASRA's initiatives and its impact on the development of West African capital markets, we encourage you to visit the WASRA website [www.wasra-amfao.org](https://www.wasra-amfao.org/) Through its active engagement with WASRA, the SEC remains committed to promoting a harmonized and well-regulated capital market in West Africa, fostering regional economic growth and prosperity.", "content_hash": "1773e1888f67c41d035de3772cefadd29d428d7e1463c879b05f9f9e38304c8e", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1949", "title": "SEC and the Financial Action Task Force Published: May 22, 2025", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-fatf/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-fatf/", "text": "SEC and the Financial Action Task Force Published: May 22, 2025 The SEC actively collaborates with the Financial Action Task Force (FATF), an international organization setting standards to combat money laundering, terrorist financing, and proliferation financing. The Nigerian Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) shares a strategic alliance with the Financial Action Task Force (FATF), an intergovernmental body leading the global fight against money laundering, terrorist financing, and the financing of proliferation of weapons of mass destruction (WMD). This partnership forms a cornerstone of the SEC's efforts to uphold financial integrity and safeguard the Nigerian capital market.FATF: Setting the Global Standard on Combating Financial CrimeEstablished in 1989, FATF boasts 39 member jurisdictions and a vast network of Associate and Regional Partner Countries. Its 40 Recommendations act as the international benchmark for combating financial crime, promoting consistent regulatory measures and effective cross-border cooperation.Nigeria's Steadfast Commitment to FATFJoining FATF in 2003, Nigeria acknowledged the vital role of international collaboration in curbing financial crime. The SEC, as the primary regulator of the Nigerian securities market, has been a fervent advocate for implementing FATF Recommendations within its regulatory framework.Milestones in the SEC-FATF Partnership:2003: Nigeria's official entry into FATF marks a significant commitment to combatting financial crime.2007: The SEC establishes a dedicated Anti-Money Laundering/Combating the Financing of Terrorism (AML/CFT) Unit, demonstrating its focus on addressing financial crime within the securities sector.2016: Nigeria undergoes its Mutual Evaluation Assessment (MEA) by FATF, acknowledging progress and recommending areas for further improvement.2023: The SEC actively participates in the FATF-Regional Centre for North Africa and West Africa (NARWAC) initiatives, fostering regional cooperation on AML/CFT within the West African sub-region.The SEC-FATF Partnership in ActionThis collaboration has borne significant fruits for the Nigerian financial system:Enhanced Market Integrity: Implementing FATF Recommendations strengthens corporate governance and market practices, deterring criminal activity and protecting investors.Financial System Resiliency: By implementing robust AML/CFT controls, the SEC helps safeguard the Nigerian financial system from infiltration by illicit funds.Cross-border Cooperation: Participation in FATF's global network facilitates information exchange and coordinated action against cross-border financial crime.Looking Ahead: Continued Commitment to International CooperationThe SEC remains steadfast in its commitment to upholding FATF standards and collaborating with international partners. This ongoing partnership paves the way for a more secure and transparent financial system in Nigeria, fostering investor confidence and promoting sustainable economic growth.To delve deeper into FATF's work and its global impact, please visit the official FATF website: [www.fatf-gafi.org](https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/home.html) Through its unwavering commitment to FATF, the SEC strives to create a financial landscape free from the taint of financial crime, safeguarding the prosperity of the Nigerian capital market and ensuring a brighter future for investors.", "content_hash": "83ac7195687ea6903113f2dd54146ad104aa2483984886d76bb5c96a6154a5e2", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1950", "title": "SEC and the Inter-Governmental Action Group against Money Laundering in West Africa (GIABA) Published: May 22, 2025", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-giaba/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-giaba/", "text": "SEC and the Inter-Governmental Action Group against Money Laundering in West Africa (GIABA) Published: May 22, 2025 SEC, focused on protecting investors, collaborates with GIABA, strengthening West African AML/CFT frameworks. Sharing expertise and training regional counterparts, they combat financial crime and boost investor confidence across the region. The Nigerian Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) and the Inter-Governmental Action Group against Money Laundering in West Africa (GIABA) stand shoulder-to-shoulder in the fight against financial crime within the West African sub-region. This strategic partnership leverages expertise and fosters regional cooperation to safeguard the integrity of the Nigerian capital market and build a robust financial system across West Africa.GIABA: Championing Anti-Money Laundering and Counter-Terrorist Financing in West AfricaEstablished in 2000, GIABA boasts 15 member states and serves as a leading advocate for implementing international Anti-Money Laundering (AML) and Counter-Terrorist Financing (CFT) standards within the West African region. By providing technical assistance, promoting regional cooperation, and conducting mutual evaluations, GIABA empowers member states to effectively combat financial crime and terrorist financing.Nigeria's Pioneering Role in GIABARecognizing the critical role of regional collaboration, Nigeria was among the founding members of GIABA. The SEC has consistently championed GIABA's initiatives, actively participating in its programs and contributing to the development of regional AML/CFT frameworks.Milestones in the SEC-GIABA Partnership2000: Nigeria becomes a founding member of GIABA, demonstrating its commitment to regional cooperation in combating financial crime.2008: The SEC hosts the GIABA Technical Assistance Mission, focusing on strengthening Nigeria's AML/CFT framework within the securities sector.2014: Nigeria actively participates in the GIABA Mutual Evaluation Assessment process, receiving valuable recommendations for further strengthening its AML/CFT compliance.2022: The SEC collaborates with GIABA on developing and implementing training programs for securities market participants on AML/CFT best practices.The SEC-GIABA Partnership in ActionThis collaboration has yielded tangible benefits for both Nigeria and the broader West African region:Enhanced Regional Cooperation: Shared expertise and resources within GIABA facilitate information exchange and joint operations against cross-border financial crime.Strengthened Regulatory Frameworks: Through collaboration, both the SEC and other West African financial regulators refine their AML/CFT frameworks, leading to a more robust regional financial system.Increased Investor Confidence: Effective measures against financial crime enhance investor confidence, attracting investments and fostering economic growth within the region.Looking Ahead: A Shared Vision for a Secure Financial FutureThe SEC remains committed to working closely with GIABA to achieve a shared vision of a West African financial system free from the scourge of financial crime. By leveraging collective expertise and resources, this partnership will continue to build a safer and more prosperous future for the region.To learn more about GIABA's work and its impact on West Africa, please visit the official GIABA website: [www.giaba.org/](https://www.giaba.org/) Together, the SEC and GIABA stand as a resolute force against financial crime, safeguarding the Nigerian capital market and contributing to a stronger, more secure financial future for all of West Africa.", "content_hash": "8ff049d38c90c9f4cc62fa6ed8177af2474f46943fca8889d74a0f87893e7829", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1951", "title": "SEC and the Islamic Financial Services Board (IFSB) Published: May 22, 2025", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-ifsb/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/international-relations/sec-and-ifsb/", "text": "SEC and the Islamic Financial Services Board (IFSB) Published: May 22, 2025 SEC maintains a cooperative relationship with the Islamic Financial Services Board (IFSB), engaging in information sharing and dialogue on issues like regulatory developments, Shariah compliance, and mitigating financial risks in the Islamic finance sector. This collaboration aims to foster a sound and stable Islamic financial environment, ultimately benefiting investors and the broader financial landscape. The Nigerian Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) fosters a strong and collaborative relationship with the Islamic Financial Services Board (IFSB), a leading international standard-setting body dedicated to the development and harmonization of regulations for the Islamic finance industry. This partnership plays a crucial role in strengthening the Nigerian Islamic capital market, promoting investor protection, and ensuring its alignment with global best practices.IFSB: Championing Sound and Stable Islamic FinanceEstablished in 2002, the IFSB brings together regulatory authorities, financial institutions, and industry experts from over 50 jurisdictions. Its core objectives include:Issuing globally recognized Sharia-compliant regulatory standardsFostering the stability and integrity of the Islamic financial systemProtecting the rights and interests of investorsFacilitating cross-border cooperation and information sharingNigeria's Commitment to Islamic Finance DevelopmentRecognizing the growing significance of Islamic finance, the SEC joined the IFSB in 2010. Since then, the SEC has actively participated in the IFSB's initiatives, contributing to the development of international standards and best practices for the Islamic capital market.Key Milestones in the SEC-IFSB Partnership2010: The SEC joins the IFSB, signaling its commitment to the growth and development of the Islamic finance sector in Nigeria.2013: The SEC publishes its Guidelines on Non-Interest Capital Market Operations, outlining the regulatory framework for Sharia-compliant financial products and services.2016: The SEC hosts the IFSB's 27th Council Meeting, further solidifying its position as a leader in Islamic finance regulation.2023: The SEC and IFSB jointly organize the 1st International Forum on Non-Interest Capital Markets, addressing key challenges and opportunities for the Nigerian NICM.The SEC-IFSB Partnership in ActionThe SEC's collaboration with the IFSB has yielded significant benefits for the Nigerian Islamic capital market, including:Enhanced Regulatory Framework: The IFSB's standards have informed the development of the SEC's regulatory framework for Islamic finance, ensuring investor protection and market integrity.Product Innovation: The SEC's collaboration with the IFSB has facilitated the development and introduction of new Sharia-compliant financial products and services, catering to the diverse needs of investors.Capacity Building: The SEC and IFSB have partnered on capacity-building initiatives, equipping stakeholders with the necessary knowledge and skills to navigate the Islamic finance landscape.Cross-Border Cooperation: The SEC's participation in the IFSB's Multilateral Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) enables efficient information sharing and regulatory cooperation with other IFSB member jurisdictions.To learn more about the IFSB's work and its impact on the global Islamic finance industry, we encourage you to visit the IFSB website: [www.ifsb.org](https://www.ifsb.org) Through its active engagement with the IFSB, the SEC remains committed to fostering a robust and vibrant Islamic capital market in Nigeria, aligned with international best practices and dedicated to serving the needs of all stakeholders.", "content_hash": "e8ffb724ca843df09c355c5c814cd0970850ea3821b5afd4c6e2202a6718a2e2", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1952", "title": "Resources", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/", "text": "Resources Explore essential resources for informed investing and regulatory compliance. Access downloadable guides, reports, and tools for a deeper understanding of Nigeria's financial markets. blocks section [Checklists](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/checklists/) [Checklists](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/checklists/) [Forms](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/forms/) [Forms](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/forms/) [Templates](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/templates/) [Templates](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/templates/) [Frequently Asked Questions](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/frequently-asked-questions/) [Frequently Asked Questions](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/frequently-asked-questions/) [Papers and Publications](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/papers-and-publications/) [Papers and Publications](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/papers-and-publications/) [Annual Reports](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/annual-reports/) [Annual Reports](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/annual-reports/)", "content_hash": "4fbfd87cdbc255ba04d9747b59b5504ba8ce4f6ee8d83abeb7e27ec30eaa068b", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1953", "title": "Checklists Published: September 15, 2023", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/checklists/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/checklists/", "text": "Checklists Published: September 15, 2023 Ensure you're on the right track with our helpful checklists. These tools assist you in staying organized and prepared, whether you're an investor planning your portfolio, a capital market operator managing submissions, or a business contemplating market listing. [Ease of Doing Business - 2025 SLA](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/checklists/ease-of-doing-business-2025-sla/) [Individual Registration Requirements for Each CMO A comprehensive checklist to guide each capital market operator throug](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/checklists/individual-registration-requirements-for-each-cmo/) [Accelerated Regulatory Incubation Program (ARIP) Checklist for VASP Onboarding A comprehensive checklist to guide Virtua](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/checklists/accelerated-regulatory-incubation-program-arip-checklist-for-vasp-onboarding/) [Important Notes for Regulatory Incubation Applications (Checklist) A comprehensive checklist to guide fintech innovators](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/checklists/important-notes-for-regulatory-incubation-applications-checklist/) [Corporate Updates Checklist for CMOs A comprehensive checklist to guide CMOs through essential corporate updates\u2014address](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/checklists/corporate-updates-checklist-for-cmos/) [Withdrawal of CMO and Function Checklist A comprehensive checklist to guide CMOs through the withdrawal of their registr](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/checklists/withdrawal-of-cmo-and-function-checklist/) [Checklist for Sponsored Individuals A comprehensive checklist to navigate the sponsored individuals lifecycle\u2014registrati](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/checklists/checklist-for-sponsored-individuals/) [CMOs Application Requirements, Fees and Implementation Time-Frame Navigating the Path to Becoming a Capital Market Opera](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/checklists/cmos-application-requirements-fees-and-implementation-time-frame/) [Registration of Securities Checklist Navigate the Securities Registration Process with Clarity This comprehensive checkl](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/checklists/registration-of-securities-checklist/) [Registration of IPOs (CIS, REIT, ETF), PE/VC, Infrastructure and Specialized Funds Empowering Capital Formation: A Guide](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/checklists/registration-of-ipos-cis-reit-etf-pevc-infrastructure-and-specialized-funds/) [Requirements for Filing of Returns by Capital Market Operators Ensuring Market Transparency and Compliance: A Guide to R](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/checklists/requirements-for-filing-of-returns-by-capital-market-operators/) [Regulatory Returns Filing Calendar For Public Companies Ensuring Timely Compliance: A Comprehensive Guide to Regulatory ](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/checklists/regulatory-returns-filing-calendar-for-public-companies/) [Minimum Capital Requirement For CMOs This page serves as an amendment to Schedule I of the Rules and Regulations, dated ](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/checklists/minimum-capital-requirement-for-cmos/) [Mergers and Acquisitions Checklist A comprehensive checklist to guide businesses and advisers through the mergers and ac](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/checklists/mergers-and-acquisitions-checklist/) [Cross-Reference Checklist Review of Bond/Fixed Income Applications This page offers comprehensive guides for issuers see](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/checklists/cross-reference-checklist-review-of-bondfixed-income-applications/)", "content_hash": "17f1bda67ee40ec140f8e4d1b4808f9ca7a3298a876ec5af5ddc851dab14bc42", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1954", "title": "Forms Published: September 15, 2023", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/forms/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/forms/", "text": "Forms Published: September 15, 2023 Gain access to an extensive array of meticulously crafted forms, thoughtfully designed to cater to the unique requirements of investors, capital market operators, and businesses engaged with us. [Application Forms for CMOs This page provides a centralized resource for all the necessary application forms for a caree](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/forms/application-forms-for-cmos/) [Capital Market Operators Data Update Form](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/forms/capital-market-operators-data-update-form/) [Download e-Dividend Forms In the dynamic world of capital markets, dividend payments represent a significant source of i](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/forms/download-e-dividend-forms/) [Interpretive Guidance Notes (IGN)](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/forms/interpretive-guidance-notes-ignn/) [National Balance of Payment Forms Access and complete quarterly questionnaires reporting Nigeria\u2019s foreign and domestic ](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/forms/national-balance-of-payment-forms/) [Returns Forms and Templates Timely and accurate reporting is essential for maintaining transparency, ensuring compliance](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/forms/returns-forms-and-templates/)", "content_hash": "b524f96a85249a77abae4887ca101469f83e98c63d1dab9e51894d7fefc7f3e5", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1955", "title": "Templates Published: September 15, 2023", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/templates/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/templates/", "text": "Templates Published: September 15, 2023 Streamline your processes with our Templates. Find practical tools to aid your financial and operational needs, catering to investors seeking efficient reporting, capital market operators looking for standardized forms, and businesses not yet listed, preparing for potential market entry. [AML/CFT Returns Template Advancing Anti-Money Laundering and Combating the Financing of Terrorism (AML/CFT) Efforts: A C](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/templates/amlcft-returns-template/) [Reporting Template on Sustainable Financial Principles for The Nigerian Capital Market Advancing Sustainable Financial P](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/templates/reporting-template-on-sustainable-financial-principles-for-the-nigerian-capital-market/) [Returns Forms and Templates Timely and accurate reporting is essential for maintaining transparency, ensuring compliance](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/templates/returns-forms-and-templates/)", "content_hash": "b12d35f0151b863ed9b5ef0a5151f3f98b32c62646e2d64ebe77dc309e6555a2", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1956", "title": "Frequently Asked Questions Published: September 15, 2023", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/frequently-asked-questions/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/frequently-asked-questions/", "text": "Frequently Asked Questions Published: September 15, 2023 Delve into our comprehensive FAQs section and uncover answers to your burning questions. We simplify complex topics, catering to investors seeking clarity, capital market operators seeking guidance, and businesses exploring market entry. [FAQs for Bonds Bonds are an important asset class for many investors. This FAQ section provides insights into bond basic](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/frequently-asked-questions/faqs-for-bonds/) [FAQs for CMOs Capital markets officers (CMOs) face a unique set of challenges and opportunities. This page addresses key](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/frequently-asked-questions/faqs-for-cmos/) [FAQs for Investors As an investor, it's important to stay informed about your rights and responsibilities. This page pro](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/frequently-asked-questions/faqs-for-investors/) [FAQs for Mergers and Acquisitions (M&A) Mergers and Acquisitions transactions can be complex and have significant implic](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/frequently-asked-questions/faqs-for-mergers-and-acquisitions-ma/) [FAQs on \"Net Asset Value\" and \"Unit Price\" Understand how Net Asset Value and Unit Price are determined, why they matter](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/frequently-asked-questions/faqs-on-net-asset-value-and-unit-price/) [FAQs on Collective Investment Schemes Get clarity on Collective Investment Schemes\u2014explore scheme types, registration re](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/frequently-asked-questions/faqs-on-collective-investment-schemes/) [FAQs on Differentiating Ponzi/Pyramid Schemes from Registered Fund/Portfolio Managers Protect yourself from fraudulent i](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/frequently-asked-questions/faqs-on-differentiating-ponzipyramid-schemes-from-registered-fundportfolio-managers/) [FAQs on FinTechs Fintech innovations are transforming Nigeria\u2019s financial landscape, but navigating the regulatory frame](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/frequently-asked-questions/faqs-on-fintechs/) [FAQs on Procurement Process and Award of Contracts Learn about the SEC\u2019s procurement and contract award framework, inclu](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/frequently-asked-questions/faqs-on-procurement-process-and-award-of-contracts/) [FAQs on Sponsored Individuals Find answers on Sponsored Individuals\u2014discover eligibility conditions, sponsorship respons](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/frequently-asked-questions/faqs-on-sponsored-individuals/) [FAQs on Unclaimed Dividends Retrieval Process A procedural guide to reclaiming unclaimed dividends, detailing how to sea](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/frequently-asked-questions/faqs-on-unclaimed-dividends-retrieval-process/) [FAQs on the New Merger Regime Dive into the new merger regime\u2014clarify filing requirements, approval processes, stakehold](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/frequently-asked-questions/faqs-on-the-new-merger-regime/) [FAQs on the Regularization of Multiple Subscriptions Address multiple subscription irregularities\u2014understand the steps t](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/frequently-asked-questions/faqs-on-the-regularization-of-multiple-subscriptions/) [FAQs on the SEC Regulatory Incubation (RI) Program Regulatory Incubation (RI) is opening new pathways for fintech firms ](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/frequently-asked-questions/faqs/)", "content_hash": "adc13765a677ddf8d67d1a885be1ee22a237fb5ea09afc82734d3aff48786ee5", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1957", "title": "Papers and Publications", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/papers-and-publications/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/papers-and-publications/", "text": "Papers and Publications Discover a wealth of knowledge and insights through the Securities and Exchange Commission's (SEC) diverse collection of research papers, publications, and periodicals. Explore a range of topics, from securities market analysis to economic trends, and gain valuable perspectives on the Nigerian capital market and beyond. blocks section [Nig. Commodities Trading Bulletin](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/papers-and-publications/nigerian-commodities-trading-ecosystem-bulletin/) [Nig. Commodities Trading Bulletin](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/papers-and-publications/nigerian-commodities-trading-ecosystem-bulletin/) [Nig. Journal of Securities Market](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/papers-and-publications/nigerian-journal-of-securities-market/) [Nig. Journal of Securities Market](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/papers-and-publications/nigerian-journal-of-securities-market/) [Policy Briefs](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/papers-and-publications/policy-briefs/) [Policy Briefs](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/papers-and-publications/policy-briefs/) [Publications From International Partners](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/papers-and-publications/publications-from-international-partners/) [Publications From International Partners](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/papers-and-publications/publications-from-international-partners/) [Publications on Bonds](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/papers-and-publications/publications-on-bonds/) [Publications on Bonds](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/papers-and-publications/publications-on-bonds/) [Speeches and Presentations](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/papers-and-publications/repository-of-speeches-and-presentations/) [Speeches and Presentations](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/papers-and-publications/repository-of-speeches-and-presentations/) [Weekly Economic Update](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/papers-and-publications/weekly-economic-update/) [Weekly Economic Update](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/papers-and-publications/weekly-economic-update/)", "content_hash": "40de877211a639aa4c55af3b0fe583e2e8c96515604795c8edc23b20a1b56933", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1958", "title": "Annual Reports", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/annual-reports/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/annual-reports/", "text": "Annual Reports Explore comprehensive insights into the Nigerian capital market through the Securities and Exchange Commission\u2019s (SEC) official collection of annual reports. Delve into detailed reviews of market performance, regulatory milestones, and strategic initiatives across each fiscal year, and gain a clear understanding of the trends, achievements, and outlook that shape Nigeria\u2019s securities landscape. [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2022 pdf \u2022 35.8 MB December 23, 2022](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1165/SEC_NG_ANNUAL_REPORT_AND_ACCOUNTS_2022_UWGMODH.pdf) [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2021 pdf \u2022 1.5 MB December 24, 2021](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1164/SEC_NG_ANNUAL_REPORT_AND_ACCOUNTS_2021_GdH9ojl.pdf) [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2020 pdf \u2022 1.4 MB December 25, 2020](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1163/SEC_NG_ANNUAL_REPORT_AND_ACCOUNTS_2020_rXp8PRv.pdf) [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2019 pdf \u2022 27.8 MB December 20, 2019](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1161/SEC_NG_ANNUAL_REPORT_AND_ACCOUNTS_2019_kmgl0K0.pdf) [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2018 pdf \u2022 27.3 MB December 21, 2018](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1162/SEC_NG_ANNUAL_REPORT_AND_ACCOUNTS_2018_tF7Rr6G.pdf) [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2017 pdf \u2022 25.3 MB December 22, 2017](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1159/SEC_NG_ANNUAL_REPORT_AND_ACCOUNTS_2017_WpCckQ6.pdf) [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2016 pdf \u2022 23.7 MB December 23, 2016](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1158/SEC_NG_ANNUAL_REPORT_AND_ACCOUNTS_2016_cpoh8oG.pdf) [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2015 pdf \u2022 20.5 MB December 18, 2015](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1160/SEC_NG_ANNUAL_REPORT_AND_ACCOUNTS_2015_o7vZnz0.pdf) [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2014 pdf \u2022 12.3 MB December 19, 2014](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1157/SEC-Nigeria-annual-report-and-accounts-2014_BpLtLil.pdf) [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2013 pdf \u2022 1.3 MB December 20, 2013](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1156/2013_Annual_Report_of_SEC_cXv23Mn.pdf) [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2012 pdf \u2022 1.1 MB December 21, 2012](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1155/2012_Annual_Report_of_SEC_5tS0FQW.pdf) [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2011 pdf \u2022 3.6 MB December 23, 2011](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1167/SEC_ANNUAL_REPORTS__ACCOUNTS_2011_X8fXkJ3.pdf) [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2010 pdf \u2022 1.4 MB December 24, 2010](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1154/SEC_ANNUAL_REPORTS__ACCOUNTS_2010_lite_wQPHl2o.pdf) [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2009 pdf \u2022 993.1 KB December 25, 2009](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1153/SEC_ANNUAL_REPORTS__ACCOUNTS_2009_lite_doK6lBb.pdf) [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2008 pdf \u2022 1.4 MB December 26, 2008](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1152/SEC_ANNUAL_REPORTS_AND_ACCOUNTS_2008_lite_8rwyOVS.pdf) [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2007 pdf \u2022 12.7 MB December 21, 2007](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1151/2007_ANNUAL_REPORT_optimized_owzsNuP.pdf) [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2006 pdf \u2022 12.8 MB December 22, 2006](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1150/Annual_report_2006_optimized_yn7E2js.pdf) [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2005 pdf \u2022 5.6 MB December 23, 2005](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1149/Annual_report_2005_optimized_RouIArZ.pdf) [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2004 pdf \u2022 18.0 MB December 24, 2004](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1148/2004_annual_report_Optimized2_CtS6KIr.pdf) [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2003 pdf \u2022 12.8 MB December 26, 2003](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1147/SEC-ANNUAL-REPORT-AND-ACCOUNTS-2003_lA4A55Q.pdf) [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2002 pdf \u2022 9.4 MB December 27, 2002](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1146/Annual_report_2002_optimized_p0pt94B.pdf) [SEC ANNUAL REPORTS AND ACCOUNTS 2000 pdf \u2022 4.1 MB December 29, 2000](https://www.sec.gov.ng/documents/1166/SEC_NG_Annual_report_2000_optimized_Lbg5PRO.pdf)", "content_hash": "8fb5dc2cbc7f5c84783b558825f48227d69f614b8cf83b14fee19fb7af316456", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1959", "title": "News Published: September 24, 2023", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/news/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/news/", "text": "News Published: September 24, 2023 Stay informed with the latest SEC updates and official announcements [Subscribe to RSS Feed](https://www.sec.gov.ng/feeds/news.rss) [SEC NIGERIA 2026 Service Level Agreement (SEC NG SLA) The primary purpose of this SLA is to create a transparent framewo](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/news/sec-nigeria-2026-service-level-agreement-sec-ng-sla/) [IST/OA/04/2024 SEC vs Crypto Bridge Exchange (CBEX) and 22 Ors Aug. 8, 2025](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/news/ist-oa-04-2024-sec-vs-crypto-bridge-exchange-cbex-and-22-ors/) [New SEC Nigeria Website May 22, 2025](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/news/new-sec-nigeria-website/) [WACMaC Conference 2023 Sept. 20, 2023](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/news/wacmac-conference-2023/) [Notification for 2022 second CMC Webinar Meeting July 18, 2022](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/news/notification-for-2022-second-cmc-webinar-meeting/) [Activities Of FinAfrica Investment Limited (Chinmark Group) Dec. 25, 2021](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/news/activities-of-finafrica-investment-limited-chinmark-group/)", "content_hash": "07b2196125ca064e0b8ee39f07d6d7c52595fff21a4e5cea591a02464795d39d", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1960", "title": "Media Gallery", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/media-gallery/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/media-gallery/", "text": "Media Gallery Welcome to our Media Gallery, your source for official photos capturing engaging moments from SEC events and activities. Explore our visual journey in the world of finance. [Subscribe to RSS Feed](https://www.sec.gov.ng/feeds/media-gallery.rss) [Meeting between Senate Committee on Capital market and SEC in Abuja Weekend May 23, 2025](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/media-gallery/meeting-between-senate-committee-on-capital-market-and-sec-in-abuja-weekend/) [Press Briefing on the ongoing Capital Market initiatives in Abuja May 23, 2025](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/media-gallery/press-briefing-on-the-ongoing-capital-market-initiatives-in-abuja/) [IST VISIT May 23, 2025](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/media-gallery/ist-visit/) [Meeting between SEC and FICAN in Abuja Nov. 22, 2023](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/media-gallery/meeting-between-sec-and-fican-in-abuja/) [SECOND (2ND) CAPITAL MARKET COMMITTEE (CMC) MEETING HELD ON AUGUST 15, 2017 Aug. 17, 2017](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/media-gallery/second-2nd-capital-market-committee-cmc-meeting-held-on-august-15-2017/) [Third Capital Market Committee (CMC), 2016 Dec. 15, 2016](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/media-gallery/third-capital-market-committee-cmc-2016/) [Advocacy Visit to Ogun State Governor, Ibukunle Amosun and Former President Olusegun Obasanjo, by the DG SEC and Members](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/media-gallery/advocacy-visit-to-ogun-state-governor-ibukunle-amosun-and-former-president-olusegun-obasanjo-by-the-dg-sec-and-members-of-the-technical-committee-on-financial-literacy-november-2016/) [Visit of the AIH to the SEC, October, 2016 Oct. 12, 2016](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/media-gallery/visit-of-the-aih-to-the-sec-october-2016/) [Visit of ACA to the SEC, October, 2016 Oct. 6, 2016](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/media-gallery/visit-of-aca-to-the-sec-october-2016/) [Visit of NBA to the SEC, October, 2016 Oct. 4, 2016](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/media-gallery/visit-of-nba-to-the-sec-october-2016/) [Lagos Public Enlightenment Campaign on E-Dividend Registration Aug. 24, 2016](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/media-gallery/lagos-public-enlightenment-campaign-on-e-dividend-registration/) [Visit by Foreign Investors to the Securities and Exchange Commission, Monday, February 1, 2016 Feb. 1, 2016](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/media-gallery/visit-by-foreign-investors-to-the-securities-and-exchange-commission-monday-february-1-2016/) [Visit by BusinessDay Jan. 29, 2016](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/media-gallery/visit-by-businessday/) [Abuja Public Enlightenment Campaign on E-Dividend Registration Jan. 6, 2016](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/media-gallery/abuja-public-enlightenment-campaign-on-e-dividend-registration/)", "content_hash": "2579c346ca29bccbb8a7b0c93620f1ef6014624215c075014e367d26c0ca0998", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1961", "title": "Working With Us Published: August 14, 2023", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/careers/working-with-us/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/careers/working-with-us/", "text": "Working With Us Published: August 14, 2023 Thinking of working with us? Always committed to employee fulfillment and community development, the Securities and Exchange Commission is one of the best places to grow your career at any level. The Nigerian Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) is the apex regulator of the Nigerian capital market. Our vision is \u201cto be the leading capital market regulator in Africa,\u201d and our mission is \u201cto develop and regulate a capital market that is dynamic, fair, transparent and efficient to contribute to the nation\u2019s economic development.\u201d We are committed to protecting investors, maintaining market integrity, and promoting sustainable market growth.What It\u2019s Like to Work at SECThe SEC is a vibrant, fast\u2010paced, and challenging environment\u2014but also a rewarding one. Whether you are a fresh graduate, young professional or experienced hire, you\u2019ll work alongside some of the brightest minds in the industry to make a real difference in the development of Nigeria\u2019s economy and the futures of its investors. We value diversity and inclusion and strive to maintain a healthy work\u2013life balance through competitive compensation, training programs, and supportive policies.Career OpportunitiesWe welcome applications from top talent across a wide range of disciplines. Our core recruiting focus includes:AccountancyLawEconomicsWe also periodically seek professionals in:Information TechnologyBusiness AdministrationThe HumanitiesWithin SEC you can specialize in areas such as compliance, corporate finance, enforcement, examinations, investor education, market regulation, policy and research, public relations, technology\u2014and more.Our Recruitment ProcessTo ensure we only bring on board individuals who meet our high standards, all candidates undergo a structured Competency Assessment Centre. This rigorous process evaluates both the technical skills and behavioural competencies most relevant to the role. Only those who demonstrate the right blend of expertise, judgment, and commitment receive offers.Why Work at SEC?Make a difference: Contribute directly to a fair, transparent, and efficient capital market.Work with the best: Collaborate with experienced professionals and thought leaders.Competitive rewards: Enjoy a comprehensive salary and benefits package\u2014including health insurance, retirement savings, and paid time off.Professional growth: Access ongoing training, mentorship, and clear pathways for advancement.Inclusive culture: Thrive in a workplace that values every voice and fosters mutual respect.How to ApplyVisit our [Openings Page](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/careers/openings/) to see current vacancies and sign up for job alerts.We look forward to welcoming you to the SEC family!", "content_hash": "f2a9d1a0a8b9c0e41812923dec85d498d20e25469a285004b5c8d3531b222a3f", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1962", "title": "Talk to Us", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/careers/talk-to-us/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/careers/talk-to-us/", "text": "Talk to Us This page is for career-seekers to make inquiries about SEC jobs, ask questions about specific advertised positions, and learn more about what it is like to work at the SEC.We understand that you may have many questions about working at the SEC. What types of jobs do we offer? What are the qualifications? What is the application process like? What is it like to work in a fast-paced, dynamic environment that is at the forefront of regulating the Nigerian capital market?We are here to answer all of your questions. Please fill out the form below and we will get back to you as soon as possible.We are committed to responding to all inquiries promptly and professionally. We will protect your privacy and will not share your information with any third parties without your consent. Full Name:* Surname first Email Address:* Format: example@email.com Phone Number:* Format: 08001112222 Area of Interest Indicate the type of job or department you are interested in working for Accounting and finance Compliance Corporate finance Enforcement Examinations Economics Investor education Legal Market regulation Policy and research Public relations Technology Other (Please specify in next field) Area of Interest (Other) Please fill the area(s) of interest you may have in working with SEC Nigeria, if not listed in the field above Specific Job Title (If Available) If you have a specific job title you want to inquire about instead, please indicate here Highest Level of Education:* Please select which one appropriately applies to you Primary education Junior secondary education Senior secondary education National Diploma (ND) Higher National Diploma (HND) Bachelor's degree Master's degree Doctorate degree Professional qualification (e.g., Chartered Accountant, Chartered Insurance Practitioner, etc.) Inquiry Subject* Please provide a subject/title for your inquiry Your Questions* Please be as detailed as possible, and ask as many questions as you may have Why Are You Interested in Working At SEC?* Submit Need some more clarity? [Visit our FAQ page](https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/resources/frequently-asked-questions/) for answers to common questions about working at SEC.", "content_hash": "be9226a241b516536ef8d8b0d112a3d46895c2be417e8a78176a8f6a9ce6d8ca", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1963", "title": "Contact Us", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/contact-us/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/about/contact-us/", "text": "Contact Us Our Locations Connect with us for inquiries, assistance, and information. Our Office Addresses Headquarters Kano SEC Towers,Plot 272, Samuel Adesujo Ademulegun Street,Central Business District,Abuja, F.C.T. African Alliance House (4th Floor),F1, Sani Abacha Way/Airport Road (Opposite KLM Airlines),Kano,Kano State. Lagos Port Harcourt 3, Idejo Street (Opposite ICON House),Off Adeola Odeku Street,Victoria Island,P.M.B:12638 Marina,Lagos State. 5b Isaiah Odolu Street,GRA Phase 2Port Harcourt,Rivers State. Contact Center:+234 (0) 2094621168 (8.30am \u2013 4pm)+234 (0) 9167723240 (8.30am \u2013 4pm) - WhatsAppEmail: sec@sec.gov.ng Contact Form If you have a question or comment, please fill out the contact form below and we will get back to you as soon as possible. Name:* Surname First Email:* Format: example@email.com Phone:* Format: 08001112222 Subject* Give a topic for what you want to say here Message* Say what you want to say here Submit", "content_hash": "3e3f02f396b5825163c01851026e24c8200c0152ceb4bab683c19cb10010bbd7", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1964", "title": "About FinPort Published: May 23, 2025", "url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/fintech-and-innovation-hub-finport/about-finport/", "final_url": "https://www.sec.gov.ng/fintech-and-innovation-hub-finport/about-finport/", "text": "About FinPort Published: May 23, 2025 Discover how SEC Nigeria empowers and guides FinTech innovators through its regulatory mandate, objectives, and dedicated portal. Welcome to FinPortWelcome to FinPort, the SEC\u2019s hub/portal where innovations and Financial Technology (FinTech) are encouraged and facilitated. The SEC believes that the driver to transforming Nigeria into a smart financial centre is the provision of a regulatory environment that is conducive for innovative use of technology. FinPort has been established to assist all new and existing FinTech businesses to understand the regulatory demands and requirements relevant to the Nigerian capital market.The SEC\u2019s Mandate in InnovationAs the apex regulator of the Nigerian Capital Market, the Securities and Exchange Commission is empowered by the [Investment and Securities Act 2007](https://www.sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/regulation/acts/) to regulate and develop the market in a manner that ensures fairness, integrity, and ease of participation (both entry and exit). In this capacity, the SEC remains fully alert to new developments in the financial markets\u2014especially those driven by technology\u2014and adapts its processes and tools to fulfil its mandate without stifling innovation.An Evolving Financial Innovation LandscapeFinancial innovation is by nature dynamic and evolving. Like leading regulators worldwide, the SEC is committed to evolving alongside these changes. We have implemented mechanisms to:Monitor emerging FinTech solutionsBuild internal capacity to assess novel business modelsDeploy adaptive strategies and regulatory responsesThis ongoing commitment ensures that the Nigerian Capital Market remains both progressive and secure.Our Three-Pronged Innovation ObjectiveTo balance innovation with investor protection and market stability, the SEC has articulated a three-pronged objective to guide all FinTech engagement:Safety: Ensure that new products and services do not expose investors to undue risk or systemic vulnerabilities.Market Deepening: Foster tools and platforms that broaden participation, increase liquidity, and deepen the capital market\u2019s reach.Solution-Driven Regulation: Develop and apply regulatory frameworks that solve existing market challenges\u2014rather than simply retrofitting old rules to new technologies.These pillars drive our strategy, shape our regulations, and define how we interact with innovators seeking legitimacy and relevance in Nigeria\u2019s capital market.International CollaborationsFinPort doesn\u2019t just foster home-grown innovation\u2014it connects you to the global FinTech community through strategic partnerships with leading regulatory and research networks:Global Financial Innovation Network (GFIN): A collaboration of 60+ financial regulators and related organizations that share insights, run cross-border trials, and harmonize FinTech rules.[Visit the GFIN Website](https://www.thegfin.com)Cambridge Centre for Alternative Finance (CCAF): A world-leading research hub on emerging finance models, providing data-driven studies and benchmarking on FinTech trends across over 100 jurisdictions.[Visit the CCAF Website](https://jbs.cam.ac.uk/faculty-research/centres/alternative-finance)IOSCO FinTech Network: The International Organization of Securities Commissions\u2019 dedicated forum for regulators to collaborate on digital asset policies, sandbox frameworks, and supervisory approaches.[Visit the IOSCO Fintech Network Website](https://iosco.org/about/?subsection=fintech_network)By tapping into these networks, SEC Nigeria brings global best practices, cutting-edge research, and coordinated regulatory approaches directly into your sandbox experience.Your Next StepsReady to engage with the SEC and find clarity on your FinTech model?Explore our Programs (RI and ARIP) to find the right path for your innovation.Read up on the Rules and Guidelines guiding SEC's activities as it pertains FinTech and InnovationGo through the checklists of what you need to get ready to begin your FinTech journeyComplete an Initial Assessment to determine your eligibility for the Regulatory Incubation programs.Got questions? Go through the FAQs on FinTech and InnovationWant to know who to do business with? Go through our list of registered Fintech OperatorsWant to know how we collaborate with international bodies on FinTech regulation? Click here. Contact Our Innovation Office SEC Nigeria Innovation Office Contacts Email(s) Phone(s) Address Work Hours innovation@sec.gov.ng;fintech@sec.gov.ng +234 (209) 46211168 (Ext. 1277, 1405);+234 (209) 46211277 2nd Floor, SEC Towers, CBD, Abuja Tuesdays \u2013 10.00a.m \u2013 2.30p.m", "content_hash": "db29346b0fce16b5359ece14785856a2df6a970535fa43850dc969b5d5610058", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1965", "title": "INEC and neutrality test", "url": "https://dailytrust.com/topics/news", "final_url": "https://dailytrust.com/topics/news", "text": "LOGO script async src=\"https://static.addtoany.com/menu/page.js\"></script NAVIGATION dt_mini_MENU USING CHECKBOX HACK \u2630 NAVIGATION dt_mini_MENUS [Change Of Name](https://ad-booking.dailytrust.com/change-of-name/) [Trust TV](https://trusttv.com) [Teen Trust](https://teentrust.dailytrust.com/) [Trust Radio](https://trustradio.com.ng) [Aminiya](https://aminiya.ng) [Nigeria Daily](https://dailytrust.com/topics/podcast/) [Najeriya a Yau](https://aminiya.ng/category/saurari-shirye-shiryenmu/) /113471580/dailytrust.com_masthead_468_60 <div class=\"header__advert\" style=\"display:block;\"> <div id=\"masthead_ad\" class=\"ad_container\" style=\"max-height:90px !important;\"> </div> </div> [Search](https://dailytrust.com/search) [E-Paper](https://epaper.dailytrust.com/) [Home](https://dailytrust.com/) [News](https://dailytrust.com/topics/news)[Business](https://dailytrust.com/topics/business)[Politics](https://dailytrust.com/topics/politics)[Podcast](https://dailytrust.com/topics/podcast)[Videos](https://dailytrust.com/topics/videos)[Agriculture](https://dailytrust.com/topics/agriculture)[Sports](https://dailytrust.com/topics/sports)[Opinion](https://dailytrust.com/topics/opinion)[Education](https://dailytrust.com/topics/education)[Entertainment](https://dailytrust.com/topics/entertainment)[International](https://dailytrust.com/topics/international) \u2630 Click Here To Listen To Trust Radio Live script src=\"https://cdn.plyr.io/3.7.8/plyr.polyfilled.js\"></scrip