04a070b09) [Follow us on](https://news.google.com/publications/CAAqKQgKIiNDQklTRkFnTWFoQUtEbVJoYVd4NWRISjFjM1F1WTI5dEtBQVAB?ceid=NG:en&oc=3) li style=\"background-color:#AAAAAA55;\"> <a href=\"https://truck-accident.usattorneys.com/\" target=\"truck-accident\"> Truck Accident Attorneys </a> <br/> <small>Partner Site</small> </li 1998 - 2026 Media Trust Limited. All rights reserved. #page Facebook Pixel Code End Facebook Pixel Code Event snippet for Website traffic conversion page <script> gtag('event', 'conversion', {'send_to': 'AW-11061949242/XkLgCMrVzYcYELrm35op'}); </script> <script> fbq('track', 'InitiateCheckout', { value: 1200, }); </script> <script> fbq('track', 'ViewContent'); </script> Google tag (gtag.js) Add To ANy Script ========== Moved Scripts =========== ============ OCM =================== ========== Plyr.IO =========", "content_hash": "d98695fbc07febcb7e1e2151328b9562652a35c6e59b561bb3e0bdfc2207c6d5", "domain": "dailytrust.com", "fetched_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1969", "title": "About Us", "url": "https://dailytrust.com/about-us", "final_url": "https://dailytrust.com/about-us", "text": "About Us .entry-header Company Background MEDIA TRUST LIMITED is a media company engaged in Consultancy, Printing and Publishing. The company believes in efficient, honest and qualitative service to the customer. Using the latest technology and employing self-motivated workers, the company\u2019s objective is to grow steadily into a world class, integrated communication company. The company believes that its workers are the most valuable and dependable asset, to meet the challenges of its objectives. While the company will strive to create a congenial and harmonious working environment, offer equal opportunity for advancement and reward workers as appropriate, it will also demand dedication to service and loyalty from employees. Our Offices Corporate Office: <script async src=\"https://pagead2.googlesyndication.com/pagead/js/adsbygoogle.js?client=ca-pub-7943548946436796\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\"></script> career_inarticle-2 <style> /* Fixed Heigh for CLS - Cumulative Layout Shift */ @media only screen and (min-width: 992px) { #div-vi-1716478739 { width: 728px !important; height: 90px !important; } } </style> <div id=\"div-vi-1716478739\" style=\"width: 300px;height: 250px;border: 1px solid #EEEEEE;margin: 0 auto;display: flex;justify-content: center;align-items: center;font-weight: bold;color: #EEEEEE;\"><span>SPONSOR AD</span></div> <script type=\"text/javascript\"> (vitag.Init = window.vitag.Init || []).push(function () { if (viAPItag.mobileDetect()) { document.getElementById(\"div-vi-1716478739\").innerHTML = '<div class=\"adsbyvli\" style=\"width:300px;height:250px;display:inline-block;\" data-ad-slot=\"pw_31467\"></div>'; viAPItag.display(\"pw_31467\"); } else { document.getElementById(\"div-vi-1716478739\").innerHTML = '<div class=\"adsbyvli\" style=\"width:728px;height:90px;display:inline-block;\" data-ad-slot=\"pw_31466\"></div>'; viAPItag.display(\"pw_31466\"); } }) </script> 20 P.O.W Mafemi Crescent, Off Solomon Lar Way, Utako District, Abuja /113471580/dailytrust.com_inarticle-1 <div id='div-gpt-ad-1669272607411-0' style='min-width: 120px; min-height: 20px;'> <script> googletag.cmd.push(function() { googletag.display('div-gpt-ad-1669272607411-0'); }); </script> </div> Tel: 07001777577 Lagos office: 10 Acme Road, (Textile Labour House), Agidingbi \u2013 Ikeja, Tel: 07002777577 Kaduna office: Abdussalam Ziza House, A9 Mogadishu City Center, Kaduna Tel: 07003777577 Kano Regional Office: Plot 56, Behind Kano Internationa Trade Fair complex, off Zoo Road. P.O. Box 2277, Kano Tel: 07004777577 All correspondence to P.O.Box 6873, Wuse, Abuja E-mail: [[email protected]](https://dailytrust.com/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection) A member of the Newspaper Proprietors\u2019 Association of Nigeria. Corporate Social Responsibility ENTREPRENEURSHIP AWARD: This scheme is part of the Company\u2019s area of Corporate Social Responsibility intervention, the aim is to empower emerging entrepreneurs with business ideas but having financial constrained. The Company nominates a team to visit and inspect whether there is judicious utilization of the first payments of the award for the awardees to be entitled to the second installment. OPHANAGES: As part of the Company\u2019s Corporate Social Responsibility Scheme area of intervention, funds are approved to buy and distribute welfare items to orphanages, ranging from food items, toiletries, laundries etc. Items are bought and distributed to the following orphanages: 1. Abuja Children Home, Karu, Abuja 2. Kuje Prison, Kuje, Abuja 3. Jamaiyar Matan Arewa, Kaduna SCHOLARSHIPS: Under this scheme, three categories of people are beneficiaries to wit: 1. Female Medical Students from three Universities of Usman Danfodio University, Sokoto, University of Ilorin, Kwara and University of Maiduguri, Borno. -Old Beneficiaries: The old beneficiaries\u2019 academic records are verified in the three universities. -New Applicants: New scholarship applications are received from the three Universities and ten applicants are shortlisted from each university and ten placed on a reserved list. 2. Shareholders nominees. 3. Children of deceased staff. BOREHOLES: The project for this scheme is made in partnership with Water Support Charity (an NGO), the projects are funded on 50/50 basis. Water Support Charity usually submit proposal with its cost implication, Daily Trust considers ones that fall within Northern States and approve partnership. DONATIONS: The company makes donations to associations and bodies on discretionary basis. The most recent beneficiaries are \u2013 Barumark Estate Resident Muslims Association, Wuye, Abuja \u2013 Nigerian Turkish International Colleges Foundation DAILY TRUST IDP RELIEF FUND: The following areas of intervention are currently under consideration: 1. Women and youth empowerment through engagement of experts on vocational training and liaison with vocational schools and institutions and other relevant stakeholders to reduce menace of unemployment in the North. 2. Identify a particular disease and provide assistance to its patients and championing enlightenment campaign to its causes and cure. 3. Emergency relief intervention. Management & Editorial Team <div> <h4>Group Chief Executive Officer</h4> <b>Mounir Haliru Gwarzo</b> <img fetchpriority=\"high\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-775241 alignleft\" src=\"https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/Former-Director-General-of-the-Securities-and-Exchange-Commission-SEC-Mounir-Gwarzo.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\" height=\"300\" /> </div> <hr style=\"margin-bottom: 20px;\"> h4>Group Executive Director, Business Development</h4 Group Chief Executive Officer Shekarau, Ahmed Ibrahim, anipr. Ahmed I. Shekarau obtained a Bachelor\u2019s Degree in Mass Communication from the University of Maiduguri, Nigeria. He has attended numerous professional courses in Journalism and Corporate Communications, and is a member of various professional bodies, including the Nigerian Guild of Editors (NGE), where he was Vice President (North) from 2012 to 2014; the Advertising Practitioners\u2019 Council of Nigeria (APCON); and the Nigerian Institute of Public Relations (NIPR). He joined Weekly Trust, the flagship publication of Media Trust Limited, as a Freelance Reporter in 1999. He was at different times Group Business Editor, Daily Trust and Weekly Trust; Group Political Editor, Daily Trust and Weekly Trust. He reached the zenith of his career in journalism in 2005 when he was appointed pioneer Editor, Sunday Trust (now Daily Trust on Sunday). Shekarau had stints in the corporate communications departments of Unity Bank Plc and defunct Oceanic Bank Plc and also Abuja Electricity Distribution Company (AEDC). He re-joined the services of Media Trust Limited as the General Manager, Business and Strategy, where he oversees the Business Department, which is responsible for sourcing advertisements, promoting sales, commercial printing, events & conferences, brand marketing, as well as research. He also provides support to the CEO/Editor-in-Chief in developing and launching initiatives geared towards opening new marketing opportunities the company. <hr style=\"margin-bottom: 20px;\" /> <div> <h4>Group Executive Director, Finance, Investment, and Infrastructure</h4> <b>Mamman, Nura Daura</b> <img decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-775241 alignleft\" src=\"https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/nura-245x367.jpeg\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\" height=\"300\" /><span lang=\"en-GB\">A seasoned accountant, Nura from Ahmadu Bello University, Zaria, Nigeria with a B.Sc. Accounting in 1996. He obtained Master\u2019s Degree in Business Administration from the same institution in 2007. He started his professional career in 1997 at the New Nigeria Development Company Limited (NNDC) as Officer, Treasury Services. He moved up the ladder at NNDC to the position of a Senior Manager, Group Internal Auditor, before leaving in April, 2010.</span> <p class=\"western\" lang=\"en-US\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><span lang=\"en-GB\">He joined the services of Media Trust Limited in May, 2010 as Asst. General Manager/Internal Audit Department and in October 2015 he was promoted to Head the Finance Department of the Company. He is saddled with formulating and ensuring adherence to company\u2019s financial policies, as well as overseeing company\u2019s budget, credit control, costing and profitability analysis. In January, 2019, he was appointed as the company\u2019s Ag. Executive Director (Finance, Accounts and Investment) </span>and the acting appointment was confirmed on 1st January 2020. He has attended several International and Local courses over the years. </span>He was appointed <span style=\"font-size: large;\"> <i>Acting Chief Executive Officer in June, 2020, following the retirement of Malam Mannir Dan-Ali.</i></span></p> <span style=\"font-size: large;\">He is a member of the Institute of Chartered Accountants of Nigeria (ICAN) and the Nigeria Institute of Management (NIM).</span> </div> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><b>Mamman, Nura Daura</b></span></p> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><i>Group Executive Director Finance </i></span></p> <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-775241 alignleft\" src=\"https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/nura-245x367.jpeg\" alt=\"\" width=\"186\" height=\"279\" srcset=\"https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/nura-245x367.jpeg 245w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/nura-768x1152.jpeg 768w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/nura-267x400.jpeg 267w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/nura.jpeg 1160w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 186px) 100vw, 186px\" /> <p class=\"western\" lang=\"en-US\" align=\"justify\" data-wp-editing=\"1\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><span lang=\"en-GB\">A seasoned accountant, Nura </span></span><span style=\"font-size: large;\">graduated </span><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><span lang=\"en-GB\">from Ahmadu Bello University, Zaria, Nigeria with a B.Sc. Accounting in 1996. </span></span><span style=\"font-size: large;\">He obtained </span><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><span lang=\"en-GB\">Master\u2019s Degree in Business Administration from the same institution in 2007. He started his professional career in 1997 at the New Nigeria Development Company Limited (NNDC) as Officer, Treasury Services. He moved up the ladder at NNDC to the position of a Senior Manager, Group Internal Auditor, before leaving in April, 2010.</span></span></p> <p class=\"western\" lang=\"en-US\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><span lang=\"en-GB\">He joined the services of Media Trust Limited in May, 2010 as Asst. General Manager/Internal Audit Department and in October 2015 he was promoted to Head the Finance Department of the Company. He is saddled with formulating and ensuring adherence to company\u2019s financial policies, as well as overseeing company\u2019s budget, credit control, costing and profitability analysis. In January, 2019, he was appointed as the company\u2019s Ag. Executive Director (Finance, Accounts and Investment)</span></span><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><span lang=\"en-US\"> and the acting appointment was confirmed on 1</span></span><sup><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><span lang=\"en-US\">st</span></span></sup><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><span lang=\"en-US\"> January 2020. </span></span><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><span lang=\"en-GB\">He has attended several International and Local courses over the years.</span></span></p> <p lang=\"en-US\" align=\"justify\">He was appointed <span style=\"font-size: large;\"><i>Acting Chief Executive Officer in June, 2020, following the retirement of Malam Mannir Dan-Ali.</i></span></p> <p class=\"western\" lang=\"en-US\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><span lang=\"en-GB\">He is a member of </span></span><span style=\"font-size: large;\">the Institute of Chartered Accountants of Nigeria (ICAN)</span><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><span lang=\"en-GB\"> and the Nigeria Institute of Management (NIM).</span></span></p> <div> <h4>Group Executive Director, Digital and Editorial</h4> <b>Naziru Mikailu</b> <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-775241 alignleft\" src=\"https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/12/Naziru-Mikailu-Abubakar.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\" height=\"300\" /> Before joining Daily Trust, Naziru spent over a decade at the BBC World Service in senior management roles. He started his journalism career as an intern with the Triumph newspaper in his Kano hometown, northern Nigeria before moving to the country\u2019s capital, Abuja, to work with the defunct Standard Voice newspaper. Naziru joined the BBC World Service in 2009 as a multimedia broadcast journalist. Stationed in Nigeria and the UK, he covered global and regional news before becoming the BBC\u2019s Nigeria Bureau Editor in 2016, leading the organisation\u2019s teams in Nigeria, Niger, Ghana and Cameroon. The Kano-born journalist was a recipient of the 2018 World Journalist Fellowship at New York University where he earned an MA in digital media and innovation. While studying in the US, he developed a working document on Augmented Reality news app for Voice of America Digital team. He also worked with American news site, Axios, to help segment their audience and develop some new products. Before joining NYU, he had obtained an MA in New Media at the University of Leicester and a BA in Mass Communication at Bayero University, Kano. He has attended several editorial, technical, senior management and leadership courses at the BBC\u2019s Academy in the UK. He is a member of the Online News Association, the National Association of Black Journalists in the United States and the National Institute of Public Relations in Nigeria. Twitter: <a href=\"https://twitter.com/nazirumikailu\" target=\"_twitter\" rel=\"noopener\">@nazirumikailu</a> </div> <hr style=\"margin-bottom: 20px;\" /> <p lang=\"en-US\" align=\"justify\"><b>Naziru Mikailu </b> <i>Executive Director/Editor-in-Chief </i> <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-780230 alignleft\" src=\"https://cdn.dailytrust.com.ng/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/Naziru-Mikailu-317x367.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"263\" height=\"304\" srcset=\"https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/Naziru-Mikailu-317x367.jpg 317w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/Naziru-Mikailu-346x400.jpg 346w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/Naziru-Mikailu.jpg 617w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 263px) 100vw, 263px\" />Before joining Daily Trust, Naziru spent over a decade at the BBC World Service in senior management roles. He started his journalism career as an intern with the Triumph newspaper in his Kano hometown, northern Nigeria before moving to the country\u2019s capital, Abuja, to work with the defunct Standard Voice newspaper. Naziru joined the BBC World Service in 2009 as a multimedia broadcast journalist. Stationed in Nigeria and the UK, he covered global and regional news before becoming the BBC\u2019s Nigeria Bureau Editor in 2016, leading the organisation\u2019s teams in Nigeria, Niger, Ghana and Cameroon. The Kano-born journalist was a recipient of the 2018 World Journalist Fellowship at New York University where he earned an MA in digital media and innovation. While studying in the US, he developed a working document on Augmented Reality news app for Voice of America Digital team. He also worked with American news site, Axios, to help segment their audience and develop some new products. Before joining NYU, he had obtained an MA in New Media at the University of Leicester and a BA in Mass Communication at Bayero University, Kano. He has attended several editorial, technical, senior management and leadership courses at the BBC\u2019s Academy in the UK. He is a member of the Online News Association, the National Association of Black Journalists in the United States and the National Institute of Public Relations in Nigeria. Twitter: <a href=\"https://twitter.com/nazirumikailu\" target=\"_twitter\" rel=\"noopener\">@nazirumikailu</a> &nbsp; Group Executive Director Corporate Services Dr (h.c) Bala, Hadiza I. Acipm. CTC, mitd Hadiza I. Bala is a human resources specialist with about 28 years cognate experience. She graduated with a B.Sc. in Political Science from Bayero University, Kano, Nigeria in 1989. She obtained a Post Graduate Diploma in Management in 1993 and a Master in Business Administration (MBA) in 2006 both from the same institution. Her experience in Human Resource Management began in 1991 as a Personnel Officer in the Establishment, Pension & Training Directorate, Audu Bako Secretariat, Kano State, Nigeria. In 1993, she joined the defunct Bank of the North as a personnel officer and was also an Industrial Relations Officer in the bank. She was part of several successful negotiations with unions and management of the defunct bank. She served in Unity Bank as the Employee Relations Officer, Kano, Zonal Head, Human Capital Management Department (North West), Unit Head, Employee and Industrial Relations in the Head Office Abuja, a position she held until her resignation from the bank in October 2012. She joined Jaiz Bank Plc in November, 2012 as Deputy Head, Human Capital Management Department and was promoted to Group Head, Human Capital Management Department in June, 2016. She was responsible for implementing the Human Resource Transformation in the Bank. One of her greatest achievements was setting up the Jaiz Bank Training Academy in Zaria. She resigned from Jaiz Bank as Group Head, Human Capital in February, 2019. She joined the services of Media Trust Limited on March 25, 2019 as the General Manager, Human Resources and Management Services. She is a member of several professional bodies including the Chartered Institute of Personnel Management where she holds a Human Resource Practitioner\u2019s License (HRPL). She is a certified trainer and consultant (Centre for Management Development) and a member of the Nigerian Institute of Training and Development. She has attended many courses on labour relations and Executive Management and Leadership programmes. Editorial Director/Editor in Chief Ibrahim Shehu Adamu Ibrahim Shehu Adamu, Editorial Director/Editor in Chief Ibrahim Shehu Adamu is a senior media executive and journalist with over two decades of experience spanning national and international broadcast media. He is currently the Editorial Director at Media Trust Group, publishers of Daily Trust, Aminiya, and operators of Trust TV and Trust Radio. Adamu joined Media Trust Group in 2021 as General Manager and Chief Executive Officer of Trust TV, where he played a pivotal role in building the station from the ground up. Working with other members of the management team, he oversaw the development of world-class studios and broadcast facilities and helped position Trust TV as one of Nigeria\u2019s fastest-growing television networks. He later assumed broader editorial and operational leadership responsibilities across the Group. Before returning to Nigeria, Adamu worked internationally with TRT World in Istanbul, Turkey, from 2017 to 2021. As Programme Editor he led editorial teams across multiple locations, including Istanbul, London, and Washington, contributing to the channel\u2019s global news output and original programming. Between 2009 and 2015, Adamu served with the British Broadcasting Corporation (BBC). He began with the digital team of the BBC Hausa Service, later becoming a producer. In 2012, he moved to London to join the BBC World Service radio programme Newsday as a producer. He subsequently worked on Focus on Africa as a multimedia producer before joining the Focus on Africa television team in 2015, where he rose to the position of Senior Output Producer. Adamu began his journalism career in 2002 at the Nigerian Television Authority (NTA) as an intern and later as a reporter, covering sensitive beats including the Kaduna State Government House. In 2005, he joined Nagarta Radio in Kaduna, where he worked as a reporter, news editor, and presenter, further honing his editorial and broadcast skills. He holds a Master\u2019s degree in Development Communication from Kaduna State University and an MSc in Peace and Conflict Studies from the National Open University of Nigeria. He has also completed the Senior Management Programme of Lagos Business School, Pan-Atlantic University, further strengthening his expertise in leadership, strategy, and corporate governance. He is a graduate of Kaduna Polytechnic, where he studied Mass Communication with a specialisation in broadcast journalism. In addition to his formal education, Adamu has attended numerous local and international journalism and leadership training programmes that have shaped and strengthened his career in editorial leadership, media management, and broadcast journalism. <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><b>Bala, Hadiza I. Acipm. CTC, mitd</b></span></p> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><i>General Manager, Human Resources and Management Services</i></span></p> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"size-medium wp-image-780223 alignleft\" src=\"https://cdn.dailytrust.com.ng/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/Hadiza-I-Bala-280x367.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"280\" height=\"367\" srcset=\"https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/Hadiza-I-Bala-280x367.jpg 280w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/Hadiza-I-Bala-305x400.jpg 305w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/Hadiza-I-Bala.jpg 587w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 280px) 100vw, 280px\" />Hadiza I. Bala is a human resources specialist with about 28 years cognate experience. She graduated with a B.Sc. in Political Science from Bayero University, Kano, Nigeria in 1989. She obtained a Post Graduate Diploma in Management in 1993 and a Master in Business Administration (MBA) in 2006 both from the same institution.</span></p> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\">Her experience in Human Resource Management began in 1991 as a Personnel Officer in the Establishment, Pension &amp; Training Directorate, Audu Bako Secretariat, Kano State, Nigeria. </span></p> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\">In 1993, she joined the defunct Bank of the North as a personnel officer and was also an Industrial Relations Officer in the bank. She was part of several successful negotiations with unions and management of the defunct bank.</span></p> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\">She served in Unity Bank as the Employee Relations Officer, Kano, Zonal Head, Human Capital Management Department (North West), Unit Head, Employee and Industrial Relations in the Head Office Abuja, a position she held until her resignation from the bank in October 2012. </span></p> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\">She joined Jaiz Bank Plc in November, 2012 as Deputy Head, Human Capital Management Department and was promoted to Group Head, Human Capital Management Department in June, 2016. She was responsible for implementing the Human Resource Transformation in the Bank. One of her greatest achievements was setting up the Jaiz Bank Training Academy in Zaria. She resigned from Jaiz Bank as Group Head, Human Capital in February, 2019.</span></p> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\">She joined the services of Media Trust Limited on March 25, 2019 as the General Manager, Human Resources and Management Services.</span></p> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\">She is a member of several professional bodies including the Chartered Institute of Personnel Management where she holds a Human Resource Practitioner\u2019s License (HRPL). She is a certified trainer and consultant (Centre for Management Development) and a member of the Nigerian Institute of Training and Development. She has attended many courses on labour relations and Executive Management and Leadership programmes.</span></p> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><b>Shekarau, Ahmed I., anipr </b></span></p> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><i>General Manager, Business and Strategy</i></span></p> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"size-medium wp-image-775240 alignleft\" src=\"https://cdn.dailytrust.com.ng/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/shekarau_ahmed-245x367.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"245\" height=\"367\" srcset=\"https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/shekarau_ahmed-245x367.jpg 245w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/shekarau_ahmed-768x1152.jpg 768w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/shekarau_ahmed-267x400.jpg 267w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/shekarau_ahmed.jpg 1160w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 245px) 100vw, 245px\" />Ahmed I. Shekarau obtained a Bachelor\u2019s Degree in Mass Communication from the University of Maiduguri, Nigeria. He has attended numerous professional courses in Journalism and Corporate Communications, and is a member of various professional bodies, including the Nigerian Guild of Editors (NGE), where he was Vice President (North) from 2012 to 2014; the Advertising Practitioners\u2019 Council of Nigeria (APCON); and the Nigerian Institute of Public Relations (NIPR).</span></p> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\">He joined Weekly Trust, the flagship publication of Media Trust Limited, as a Freelance Reporter in 1999. He was at different times Group Business Editor, Daily Trust and Weekly Trust; Group Political Editor, Daily Trust and Weekly Trust. He reached the zenith of his career in journalism in 2005 when he was appointed pioneer Editor, Sunday Trust (now Daily Trust on Sunday).</span></p> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\">Shekarau had stints in the corporate communications departments of Unity Bank Plc and defunct Oceanic Bank Plc and also Abuja Electricity Distribution Company (AEDC).</span></p> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\">He re-joined the services of Media Trust Limited as the General Manager, Business and Strategy, where he oversees the Business Department, which is responsible for sourcing advertisements, promoting sales, commercial printing, events &amp; conferences, brand marketing, as well as research. He also provides support to the CEO/Editor-in-Chief in developing and launching initiatives geared towards opening new marketing opportunities the company.</span></p> <div> <h4>Company Secretary/Legal Adviser</h4> <b>Muhammad, Kabiru Bala</b> <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-775241 alignleft\" src=\"https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/kabiru_bala-245x367.jpeg\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\" height=\"300\" /> A legal practitioner with over 30 years post-bar experience, Kabiru Bala Muhammad, is a graduate of the University of Jos, Nigeria where he obtained LLB (Hons) in 1988 before proceeding to the Nigeria Law School in the same year and was called to the bar of the Supreme Court of Nigeria in 1989. He started his career as a Legal Officer at the Nigeria Railway Corporation (NRC) in 1989 before joining the services of Baba Adi/D.D. Rimdan &amp; Co. in Jos as an Associate Legal Practitioner in 1990. He joined the Independent National Electoral Commission (INEC) in 1993 as a Principal Legal Officer where he rose to become an Assistant Director in 2006.</span> <span style=\"font-size: large;\">He joined the services of Media Trust Limited in 2009 as the Company Secretary/Legal Adviser. Among other things, he provides legal advice, vets documents, monitor court cases, prepares minutes of meetings and performs other administrative duties for the company. He is the Secretary to the Board of Directors of the company.</span> <span style=\"font-size: large;\">He has attended several courses including Defamation and Libel in the Commonwealth and an Executive Management Training.</span> </div> <hr style=\"margin-bottom: 20px;\"> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><b>Muhammad, Kabiru Bala</b></span></p> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><i>Company Secretary/Legal Adviser</i></span></p> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"> <span style=\"font-size: large;\"> <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"size-medium wp-image-775243 alignleft\" src=\"https://cdn.dailytrust.com.ng/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/kabiru_bala-245x367.jpeg\" alt=\"\" width=\"245\" height=\"367\" srcset=\"https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/kabiru_bala-245x367.jpeg 245w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/kabiru_bala-768x1152.jpeg 768w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/kabiru_bala-267x400.jpeg 267w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/kabiru_bala.jpeg 1160w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 245px) 100vw, 245px\" /> A legal practitioner with over 30 years post-bar experience, Kabiru Bala Muhammad, is a graduate of the University of Jos, Nigeria where he obtained LLB (Hons) in 1988 before proceeding to the Nigeria Law School in the same year and was called to the bar of the Supreme Court of Nigeria in 1989. He started his career as a Legal Officer at the Nigeria Railway Corporation (NRC) in 1989 before joining the services of Baba Adi/D.D. Rimdan &amp; Co. in Jos as an Associate Legal Practitioner in 1990. He joined the Independent National Electoral Commission (INEC) in 1993 as a Principal Legal Officer where he rose to become an Assistant Director in 2006.</span> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"> <span style=\"font-size: large;\">He joined the services of Media Trust Limited in 2009 as the Company Secretary/Legal Adviser. Among other things, he provides legal advice, vets documents, monitor court cases, prepares minutes of meetings and performs other administrative duties for the company. He is the Secretary to the Board of Directors of the company.</span> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"> <span style=\"font-size: large;\">He has attended several courses including Defamation and Libel in the Commonwealth and an Executive Management Training.</span> <div> <h4>Assistant General Manager Special Projects</h4> <b>Mainasara, Abdullahi</b> <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-775241 alignleft\" src=\"https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/Mainasara-use-330x367.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\" height=\"300\" /> Abdullahi Mainasara graduated with an Upper Credit in Accounting in 2003 from Kaduna Polytechnic, Kaduna, Nigeria. He obtained a Post Graduate Diploma in Management in 2007 from Ahmadu Bello University, Zaria, Nigeria. Mr. Mainasara also obtained a Masters of Treasury Management with Distinction from Bayero University, Kano, Nigeria in 2016. <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\">He started his career in 2005 as the Cash officer of the New Nigeria Development Company Limited (NNDC). As a Deputy Manager from 2011 on posting to NNDC subsidiaries, he served as Head, Finance &amp; Accounts at Aso Motel, Kaduna and Principal Accountant, Hamdala Hotels, Kaduna up till 2013.</span> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\">He joined the services of Media Trust Limited in April, 2013 as Manager, Audit at the company\u2019s Kano Regional Office. Between October, 2016 and February, 2018, he served as the company\u2019s Copy Sales Manager, where he was responsible for the supervision of efficient distribution and sales of newspaper produced nationwide by the company. He became a Senior Manager, Audit, in February, 2018.</span> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\">He is a member of the Association of National Accountants of Nigeria (ANAN) and also a member of Chartered Institute of Loan &amp; Risk Management. He has attended several courses within and outside Nigeria. Most recently, he attended an Executive Development Programme organized by TEXEM UK in October, 2019.</span> </div> <hr style=\"margin-bottom: 20px;\" /> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"> <span style=\"font-size: large;\"> <b>Mainasara, Abdullahi</b> </span> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"> <span style=\"font-size: large;\"> <i>Senior Manager, Audit</i> </span> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"> <span style=\"font-size: large;\"> <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-781585 alignleft\" src=\"https://cdn.dailytrust.com.ng/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/Mainasara-use-330x367.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"227\" height=\"252\" srcset=\"https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/Mainasara-use-330x367.jpg 330w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/Mainasara-use-768x855.jpg 768w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/Mainasara-use-359x400.jpg 359w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/Mainasara-use.jpg 865w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 227px) 100vw, 227px\" /> Abdullahi Mainasara graduated with an Upper Credit in Accounting in 2003 from Kaduna Polytechnic, Kaduna, Nigeria. He obtained a Post Graduate Diploma in Management in 2007 from Ahmadu Bello University, Zaria, Nigeria. Mr. Mainasara also obtained a Masters of Treasury Management with Distinction from Bayero University, Kano, Nigeria in 2016. </span> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"> <span style=\"font-size: large;\">He started his career in 2005 as the Cash officer of the New Nigeria Development Company Limited (NNDC). As a Deputy Manager from 2011 on posting to NNDC subsidiaries, he served as Head, Finance &amp; Accounts at Aso Motel, Kaduna and Principal Accountant, Hamdala Hotels, Kaduna up till 2013.</span> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"> <span style=\"font-size: large;\">He joined the services of Media Trust Limited in April, 2013 as Manager, Audit at the company\u2019s Kano Regional Office. Between October, 2016 and February, 2018, he served as the company\u2019s Copy Sales Manager, where he was responsible for the supervision of efficient distribution and sales of newspaper produced nationwide by the company. He became a Senior Manager, Audit, in February, 2018.</span> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"> <span style=\"font-size: large;\">He is a member of the Association of National Accountants of Nigeria (ANAN) and also a member of Chartered Institute of Loan &amp; Risk Management. He has attended several courses within and outside Nigeria. Most recently, he attended an Executive Development Programme organized by TEXEM UK in October, 2019.</span> General Editor Iyaji, Stella O Miss Iyaji Stella Ojochide holds a Bachelor\u2019s Degree in English Language from the University of Maiduguri and Master\u2019s Degree also in English Language from the University of Abuja. She started her journalism career at the Imo Broadcasting Corporation in Owerri as a youth corps member. She later moved to DBN Television, where she worked as a reporter and presenter. While there, she covered several beats including the police, judiciary, Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Senate. Iyaji joined the Daily Trust newspaper in 2009 as an Assistant News Editor and in January 2016, she was elevated to the position of Deputy Editor, Daily Trust on Sunday. In September 2019, she was made the Deputy Editor of Daily Trust, a position she held until September 2020, when she was appointed the Managing Editor and Secretary of the Editorial Board. She was appointed as the Group Planning and Commissioning Editor on August 14, 2023; and became the General Editor in charge of the Daily Trust and The Weekend on September 26, 2024. Miss Iyaji is a member of various professional bodies and has attended several trainings within and outside Nigeria as had served as Secretary of the Editorial Board. <h3 class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"> <strong>Iyaji, Stella O</strong></h3> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"> <span style=\"font-size: large;\"> <i>Managing Editor</i> </span> <span style=\"font-size: large;\"> <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"wp-image-897405 alignleft\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\" src=\"https://cms.dailytrust.com.ng/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/stella-275x367.jpeg\" alt=\"\" width=\"151\" height=\"201\" srcset=\"https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/stella-275x367.jpeg 275w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/stella-300x400.jpeg 300w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/stella-768x1024.jpeg 768w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/stella.jpeg 810w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 151px) 100vw, 151px\" /> <span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Miss Iyaji Stella Ojochide holds a bachelor's degree in English language from the University of Maiduguri and master's degree also in English language from the University of Abuja.</span> </span> She started her journalism career at the Imo Broadcasting Corporation in Owerri as a youth corps member. She later moved to DBN Television, where she worked as a reporter and presenter. While there, she covered several beats including the police, judiciary, Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Senate. Iyaji joined the Daily Trust newspaper in 2009 as an Assistant News Editor and in January 2016, she was elevated to the position of Deputy Editor, Daily Trust on Sunday. In September 2019, she was made the Deputy Editor of Daily Trust, a position she held till September 2020, when she was appointed the Managing Editor. She is also the secretary of the Daily Trust Editorial Board. Miss Iyaji is a member of various professional bodies and has attended several trainings within and outside Nigeria. &nbsp; Deputy Editorial Director (Print) Idris, Hamza Hamza Idris holds a Bachelor of Arts in Mass Communication from the University of Maiduguri, Nigeria and aMaster of Science in the same field from the National Open University of Nigeria (NOUN). He joined the services of Media Trust Limited in September 2005 as a Reporter/Researcher, and served as a correspondent of the newspaper in Adamawa, Borno and Yobe states. In 2010, he became the North East Bureau Chief of the newspaper and operated from Daily Trust\u2019s regional office in Maiduguri. In 2015, he was transferred to the headquarters in Abuja as Group Politics Editor, a positionhe held until his appointment as the Editor of the Daily Trust on September 2, 2019. He was appointed General Editor for all three titles: Daily Trust, Daily Trust Saturday and Daily Trust Sunday on November 9, 2020 until September 26, 2024 when he became the Deputy Editor-in-Chief/Chairman Editorial Board; and three months after, he assumed his current role of Acting Editor-in-Chief on December 23, 2024. Among others, Idris is a Fellow of the International Centre for Journalists (ICFJ, 2013), Alumnus of Professional Fellows Programme (Washington D.C., 2013), Member, Swiss Peace (Switzerland, 2015) and member, Nigerian Guild of Editors (NGE). <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"> <span style=\"font-size: large;\"> <b>Idris, Hamza</b> </span> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"> <span style=\"font-size: large;\"> <i>Editor, Daily Trust</i> </span> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"> <span style=\"font-size: large;\"> <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"size-medium wp-image-775244 alignleft\" src=\"https://cdn.dailytrust.com.ng/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/idris_hamza-245x367.jpeg\" alt=\"\" width=\"245\" height=\"367\" srcset=\"https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/idris_hamza-245x367.jpeg 245w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/idris_hamza-768x1152.jpeg 768w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/idris_hamza-267x400.jpeg 267w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/idris_hamza.jpeg 1160w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 245px) 100vw, 245px\" /> Hamza Idris holds a Bachelor of Arts in Mass Communication from the University of Maiduguri, Nigeria and a Master of Science in the same field from the National Open University of Nigeria (NOUN). </span> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"> <span style=\"font-size: large;\">He joined the services of Media Trust Limited in September 2005 as a Reporter/Researcher, and served as a correspondent of the newspaper in Adamawa, Borno and Yobe states. In 2010, he became the North East Bureau Chief of the newspaper and operated from Daily Trust\u2019s regional office in Maiduguri. In 2015, he was transferred to the headquarters in Abuja as Group Politics Editor, a position he held until his appointment as the Editor of the Daily Trust in September, 2019. He is also a member of the Daily Trust Editorial Board.</span> <p class=\"western\" align=\"justify\"> <span style=\"font-size: large;\">Among others, Idris is a Fellow of the International Centre for Journalists (ICFJ, 2013), Alumnus of Professional Fellows Programme (Washington D.C., 2013), and Member, Swiss Peace (Switzerland, 2015).</span> <div> <h4>Acting Editor Aminiya</h4> <b>Makera, Salihu</b> <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-775241 alignleft\" src=\"https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/Salihu-Ishaq-Makera-313x367.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\" height=\"300\" /> Salihu Ishaq Ibrahim, also known as Salihu Makera, was a teacher before he became a journalist. Born August 20, 1968 in Makera village, Zaranda Ward, Toro LGA, Bauchi State, he started his education at a Qur'anic School in Bauchi town from 1975 to 78, before proceeding to the then Tudun Yaya Primary School (now Baba Sidi Pri. Sch.), Bauchi, from 1978 to 83. He also attended College of Islamic Studies Jos, Bauchi Campus, from 1989 -1991. For his tertiary education, Makera was at the Abubakar Tatari Ali Polytechnic, Bauchi, where he obtained a Diploma in Hausa-Education between 1993 and 1996. He was also at the International Institute of Journalism, Kaduna Campus, from 2004 to 2005. From 1993 to 1997, he was Class Teacher with Bauchi Local Government Education Authority. He was promoted to Headmaster II from 1997 to 1998, before he resigned voluntarily in November 1998. He joined the Gaskiya Ta Fi Kwabo as Freelancer Writer 1989 and became a Stringer Correspondent in Bauchi for the same paper between 1997 and 2000. He worked with the New Nigerian Newspaper Ltd, Kaduna, as a translator, reporter and special correspondent, from February 2000 to June 2006. He was the founding Editor of Nasara Newspaper (Hausa Newspaper published by Nasarawa State Government) from June 2006 to July 2007. He joined Media Trust Ltd, as reporter with Aminiya on August 7, 2007, and was promoted to Senior Reporter with Daily Trust in December 2012. In March 2016, he became Assistant Editor, Aminiya Newspaper before he was promoted to Deputy Editor of the same paper in June, 2016. On July 2, 2018, he was appointed Acting Editor Aminiya to date. With over 20 years of experience in journalism, Makera has translated hundreds of articles from English and Arabic Languages to Hausa. </div> <hr style=\"margin-bottom: 20px;\" /> <p align=\"justify\"> <strong>Makera, Salihu</strong> <p align=\"justify\"> <em>Acting Editor Aminiya</em> <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-787327 alignleft\" src=\"https://cdn.dailytrust.com.ng/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/Salihu-Ishaq-Makera-313x367.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"245\" height=\"288\" srcset=\"https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/Salihu-Ishaq-Makera-313x367.jpg 313w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/Salihu-Ishaq-Makera-341x400.jpg 341w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/Salihu-Ishaq-Makera.jpg 720w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 245px) 100vw, 245px\" /> Salihu Ishaq Ibrahim, also known as Salihu Makera, was a teacher before he became a journalist. Born August 20, 1968 in Makera village, Zaranda Ward, Toro LGA, Bauchi State, he started his education at a Qur'anic School in Bauchi town from 1975 to 78, before proceeding to the then Tudun Yaya Primary School (now Baba Sidi Pri. Sch.), Bauchi, from 1978 to 83. He also attended College of Islamic Studies Jos, Bauchi Campus, from 1989 -1991. For his tertiary education, Makera was at the Abubakar Tatari Ali Polytechnic, Bauchi, where he obtained a Diploma in Hausa-Education between 1993 and 1996. He was also at the International Institute of Journalism, Kaduna Campus, from 2004 to 2005. From 1993 to 1997, he was Class Teacher with Bauchi Local Government Education Authority. He was promoted to Headmaster II from 1997 to 1998, before he resigned voluntarily in November 1998. He joined the Gaskiya Ta Fi Kwabo as Freelancer Writer 1989 and became a Stringer Correspondent in Bauchi for the same paper between 1997 and 2000. He worked with the New Nigerian Newspaper Ltd, Kaduna, as a translator, reporter and special correspondent, from February 2000 to June 2006. He was the founding Editor of Nasara Newspaper (Hausa Newspaper published by Nasarawa State Government) from June 2006 to July 2007. He joined Media Trust Ltd, as reporter with Aminiya on August 7, 2007, and was promoted to Senior Reporter with Daily Trust in December 2012. In March 2016, he became Assistant Editor, Aminiya Newspaper before he was promoted to Deputy Editor of the same paper in June, 2016. On July 2, 2018, he was appointed Acting Editor Aminiya to date. With over 20 years of experience in journalism, Makera has translated hundreds of articles from English and Arabic Languages to Hausa. &nbsp; <!-- <em>General Manager/Head of Southern Nigeria Operations</em> <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-820592 alignleft\" src=\"https://cdn.dailytrust.com.ng/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/Kamaldeen-Ademola-rotated-e1592388937904-266x367.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"241\" height=\"333\" srcset=\"https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/Kamaldeen-Ademola-rotated-e1592388937904-266x367.jpg 266w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/Kamaldeen-Ademola-rotated-e1592388937904-290x400.jpg 290w, https://dailytrust.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/Kamaldeen-Ademola-rotated-e1592388937904.jpg 468w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 241px) 100vw, 241px\" /> A consummate Advertising and Marketing Communications practitioner, Kamaldeen Ademola Oladosu, began his foray into the Media with Turning Point Newspapers Limited, publishers of the defunct The Comet titles at its inception in 1999 where he rose to become Group Head [Financial Institutions] of the Advert Dept. He led the team to win many businesses for the relatively new publication. He had earlier worked with Olusola Adekanola &amp; Co, a firm of Chattered and Tax Consultants as Audit personnel from 1997 to 1999. In August 2003, he joined TELL Communications Limited, publishers of TELL magazine, as Assistant Manager, Advert &amp; Promotions and Deputy Head of Advert Dept. At TELL, he was responsible for revenue generation mainly from the Business Sector including the Financial and Non-Financial institutions. He also deputized for the Head of Dept in policy formulation as well as in the general administration of both human and material resources. He rose to become Deputy Manager and Manager in the organization. In 2009, he was appointed Manager, Advertisement and Promotions of BroadStreet Journal, a TELL Communication\u2019s business news magazine and thus, became the chief revenue officer of the publication. A good team player, Ademola left TELL in 2012 and joined Newswatch Communications Limited as its Head, Strategic and Business Development where he was in charge of Newspapers and Advert Sales as well as Special Project. In May 2013, Ademola moved to LEADERSHIP Newspapers to become its Controller, Lagos Operations. He was in the driver seat of the organization in Lagos and Southwest Nigeria, managing human and material resources and coordinating the activities of various departments including Sales &amp; Marketing, Press, Administration and Editorial. As the chief revenue officer, he did creditably well and was promoted Director, Lagos Operations in less than a year of joining the organization. Ademola joined the publishers of DAILY TRUST, AMINIYA and TEEN TRUST, among other publications, on Monday October 20, 2014. He oversees the operations of the organization in the Region with special focus on Business Development in the areas of Advertisements, Newspapers Sales, Commercial Printing as well as Press, Editorial and General Administration. Ademola holds M.Sc [Economics] from the University of Lagos. He has attended many training programmes in Nigeria and abroad. He is an associate registered practitioner in Advertising, [arpa of APCON] and a Full Member of the Nigeria Economics Society [NES]. He is married with four children. Mission/Vision The Vision: A world class media company that earns public trust. Mission Statement: To disseminate credible information for the good of all stakeholders. Core Values Editorial integrity Ethical conduct Professionalism Constant Innovation Production Excellence Market Driven National Unity & African Brotherhood Wold Peace & Sustainable Development <span style=\"position: absolute; top: -10px; left: 15px; background-color: #ffcc00; padding: 5px; font-weight: bold; font-size: 12px; border-radius: 3px;\">ADVERT</span> UPDATE NEWS: Nigerians can now invest \u20a62.5 million on premium domains and profit about \u20a617-\u20a625 million. All earnings paid in US Dollars. Rather than wonder, [click here to find out how it works](https://softbrite.com/l/dttxtdec2025). [Join Daily Trust WhatsApp Community For Quick Access To News and Happenings Around You.](https://bit.ly/3ULAYGr) /113471580/dailytrust_article_bottom div id='div-gpt-ad-1670511129540-0' style='min-width: 300px; min-height: 60px;'> <script> googletag.cmd.push(function() { googletag.display('div-gpt-ad-1670511129540-0'); }); </script> </div /113471580/dailytrust_article_bottom script defer> window.googletag = window.googletag || {cmd: []}; googletag.cmd.push(function() { googletag.defineSlot('/113471580/dailytrust.com_300_600_article_right', ['fluid', [300, 600]], 'div-gpt-ad-1669708905704-0').addService(googletag.pubads()); googletag.pubads().enableSingleRequest(); googletag.enableServices(); }); </script script defer> window.googletag = window.googletag || {cmd: []}; googletag.cmd.push(function() { googletag.defineSlot('/113471580/dailytrust_article_bottom', [[640, 360], [468, 60], [400, 300], [728, 90], [300, 250]], 'div-gpt-ad-1670932839695-0').addService(googletag.pubads()); googletag.pubads().enableSingleRequest(); googletag.enableServices(); }); </script .entry-content", "content_hash": "2beff132e4695a0bc1b6e3aa4ee5c134e1b8bf47a71292a4c19a7502e9d7287c", "domain": "dailytrust.com", "fetched_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1970", "title": "Makinde: Tinubu govt under scrutiny over alleged withheld N250bn NLNG dividends to states amid hardship", "url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/20/assigemt-for-tuesday", "final_url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/20/assigemt-for-tuesday", "text": "[News](https://dailypost.ng/hot-news/)Makinde: Tinubu govt under scrutiny over alleged withheld N250bn NLNG dividends to states amid hardshipPublished on October 20, 2026By [Ogaga Ariemu](https://dailypost.ng/author/ogaga-ariemu/)[Comment](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/20/assigemt-for-tuesday/comments/)Transparency International Nigeria and the Executive Director of the Centre for Social Justice, CSJ, Eze Onyekpere, have criticised President Bola Ahmed Tinubu\u2019s administration over the alleged withholding of N250 billion in dividends from Nigeria LNG Limited, NLNG, meant for states.The head of TI Nigeria, Auwal Rafsanjani and Onyekpere criticised Tinubu\u2019s administration in separate interviews with DAILY POST on Monday.DAILY POST reports that Oyo State Governor and presidential candidate of the Allied Peoples Movement, APM, Seyi Makinde, had accused the Tinubu administration of withholding N250 billion in NLNG dividends due to states.According to Makinde, the Tinubu administration had planned to delay the disbursement until after the 2027 general election.Recommended[Taraba council workers sue chairman over alleged political coercion](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/20/employees-sue-taraba-lga-chairman-over-alleged-political-intimidation-3/)[Supplementary Budget: Edo Assembly cuts capital expenditure by N49bn, increases recurrent spending](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/20/supplementary-budget-edo-assembly-cuts-capital-expenditure-by-n49bn-increases-recurrent-spending-2/)[SSANU, NASU threaten strike over unpaid salary adjustments at Yobe varsity](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/20/non-teaching-staff-unions-threaten-strike-over-unpaid-salary-agreement-at-yobe-varsity/)However, the Presidency, in a statement by Bayo Onanuga, dismissed Makinde\u2019s claim as false.In response to the Presidency\u2019s position, Makinde, one of the 16 presidential candidates seeking to replace Tinubu in the January 16, 2027, election, said he stood by his claim.The controversy comes amid a report released by the United Nations Development Programme, UNDP, last Monday, which indicated that Nigeria\u2019s multidimensional poverty rate rose to a higher level.\u2018It\u2019s undemocratic\u2019 \u2014 Rafsanjani blasts TinubuAccording to Rafsanjani, it is undemocratic and anti-development for the Federal Government under Tinubu to withhold funds that could help alleviate the economic hardship Nigerians face.Rafsanjani described the alleged withholding of funds due to states by the Federal Government as undemocratic and detrimental to development.He said withholding funds meant for developmental projects would hinder progress in states and deprive residents of the benefits of public resources.\u201cWell, first and foremost, it\u2019s a very sad situation that the government, you know, is having this kind of challenge of its money not being released by the Federal Government.\u201cThere\u2019s no way development can happen when resources that are earmarked to carry out certain developmental projects in the states are not, you know, released simply because of the policy of withholding allocations.\u201cThese allocations are not personal allocations. They are meant for the states to govern effectively and efficiently.\u201cAnd if the Federal Government is, because of political differences, depriving states of their entitlements, whatever the reason behind this, it is anti-development and undemocratic.\u201cTherefore, we urge the Federal Government to stop, you know, withholding state and local government funds meant for these states. This is simply because you are depriving the people of development in the states.\u201cIf you have any issue, because we are operating a federal system, it is not within your powers to use party differences to deprive states of their own resources,\u201d he told DAILY POST.Rafsanjani, however, called on state and the local governments to ensure transparency and accountability in the management of their allocations, arguing that proper utilisation of the funds would enable citizens to demand their timely release.He said state governments must demonstrate that public resources were being spent to improve residents\u2019 lives through development projects and poverty reduction.\u201cAnd if spending is done transparently for the benefit of the people in the states, and the people see development, they will even be the ones to agitate for the Federal Government to release this money.\u201cBut because this money apparently has not been utilised in a manner that will bring development and lift people out of poverty, unemployment and poor infrastructure at the state level, people don\u2019t even know that there is a link between these resources meant for the states and what the Federal Government is doing.\u201cSo this is why we are encouraging states to be as transparent and responsive as possible, and to spend this money judiciously and transparently for the benefit of the people in their states.\u201cEnsure, you know, effective utilisation.\u201cIf that is done, I am telling you, people will be the ones agitating and demanding that money meant for their states must be released,\u201d he added.The CISLAC director further argued that the dispute over the funds had largely become a contest between government officials rather than a struggle involving the citizens who should benefit from the allocations.He said the lack of transparency in the management of public funds had weakened citizens\u2019 interest in demanding accountability from both tiers of government.Rafsanjani added: \u201cSo now this fight has been reduced to a contest between the beneficiaries, the state government and the Federal Government.\"\u201cAnd that is why the Federal Government has the courage to do what it is doing, which is also not transparent,\u201d he told DAILY POST.\u2018Tinubu wants Nigerians to suffer\u2019 \u2014 OnyekpereOn his part, Onyekpere backed Makinde\u2019s claim that the Federal Government had yet to release N250 billion in NLNG dividends to states, accusing the Tinubu administration of lacking transparency and credibility.Onyekpere said he was inclined to believe Makinde because the Federal Government had failed to publish key fiscal documents, including budget implementation reports and actual revenue reports.He made the remarks while reacting to Makinde\u2019s claim that the Federal Government was withholding N250 billion in NLNG dividends meant for states.\u201cWho do you want to believe? Because, of course, you know we have a Presidency that is very opaque. They don\u2019t publish any reports.\u201cWhen last did you see any budget implementation report? They don\u2019t even publish anything except to give us a general picture of how much we owe.\u201cSo, I am inclined to believe Makinde because he has more credibility than the Federal Government, which simply makes promises, removes the oil subsidy, gets the benefits and then shares them among its cronies. Nothing for the people of Nigeria,\u201d he said.Onyekpere faulted the Federal Government for failing to make its revenue and expenditure records publicly available, arguing that the lack of transparency had undermined public confidence in its claims.\u201cBudget implementation reports are not published. Actual revenue reports are not published. Can\u2019t you see what they are quarrelling with the World Bank and the IMF about?\u201cWhen the IMF and World Bank say that tax revenue grew by 126 per cent in the first quarter of 2026, they are now saying that there are some funds and revenue that the World Bank did not take into account in its October update.\u201cBut why would they have to hide some funds for the World Bank not to know? Why don\u2019t they publish everything as it comes? The Federal Government lacks credibility. I believe Makinde,\u201d he told DAILY POST.The analyst further argued that the government\u2019s record on revenue disclosure and budget implementation gave Nigerians reasons to question its position on the alleged unpaid dividends.\u201cBecause of their antecedents, they will not tell us how much has accrued as revenue. They will not implement the budget.\u201cThey don\u2019t publish any budget implementation reports. Nothing at all. So, I still believe they lack credibility.\u201cMakinde is likely telling the truth,\u201d Onyekpere added in an interview with DAILY POST.Asked how the alleged withholding of the funds related to the government\u2019s achievements, Onyekpere said the money could have been used to ease the economic hardship facing Nigerians.\u201cOf course, it shows that there are resources that should be used to ameliorate the suffering, but they are not using them.\u201cSo, that\u2019s the way I would look at it. It just shows that the government is insensitive and does not care about the plight of the people,\u201d he told DAILY POST.NLNG keeps mumWhen DAILY POST contacted NLNG spokesperson, Anne Palmer for a reaction, she did not respond to the inquiry sent via WhatsApp.OAGF promises responseThe spokesperson for the Office of the Accountant-General of the Federation, OAGF, Bawa Mokwa, promised to respond to the controversy after consultations on Tuesday.Share this story[WhatsApp](https://wa.me/?text=Makinde%3A%20Tinubu%20govt%20under%20scrutiny%20over%20alleged%20withheld%20N250bn%20NLNG%20dividends%20to%20states%20amid%20hardship%20https%3A%2F%2Fdailypost.ng%2F2026%2F10%2F20%2Fassigemt-for-tuesday%2F) FacebookYou may like[Nigerian Newspapers: 10 things you need to know Tuesday morning](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/20/nigerian-newspapers-10-things-you-need-to-know-tuesday-morning-1792470586066/)[Physicians\u2019 Week: NMA FCT highlights challenges facing doctors in Nigeria](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/physicians-week-fct-nma-highlights-challenges-facing-doctors-in-nigeria/)[BREAKING: Tinubu extends tenures of Immigration, Correctional Service heads](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/breaking-tinubu-extends-tenure-of-immigration-correctional-service-heads-2/)[Join the conversation](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/20/assigemt-for-tuesday/comments/)", "content_hash": "8039d4772909f492937d7e6d9a87dd219fe6da1b891896436e29f6e586556366", "domain": "dailypost.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1971", "title": "BREAKING: Tinubu extends tenures of Immigration, Correctional Service heads", "url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/breaking-tinubu-extends-tenure-of-immigration-correctional-service-heads-2", "final_url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/breaking-tinubu-extends-tenure-of-immigration-correctional-service-heads-2", "text": "[Top Headlines](https://dailypost.ng/headlines/)BREAKING: Tinubu extends tenures of Immigration, Correctional Service headsPublished on October 19, 2026By [Matthew Atungwu](https://dailypost.ng/author/matthew-atungwu/)[Comment](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/breaking-tinubu-extends-tenure-of-immigration-correctional-service-heads-2/comments/)President Bola Tinubu has extended the tenure of two service chiefs by 18 months.Those affected are Sylvester Ndidi Nwakuche, Controller-General of the Nigerian Correctional Service and Kemi Nanna Nandap, Comptroller-General of the Nigeria Immigration Service.The approval was contained in a statement issued on Monday by the President's Special Adviser on Information and Strategy, Bayo Onanuga.According to the statement, Nwakuche will now stay in office till May 26, 2028, while Nandap's tenure will end on June 30, 2028.Recommended[NNRA begins nuclear security training for airport, border officials](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/nnra-trains-airport-officials-to-detect-illicit-nuclear-materials/)[Akwa Ibom Govt denies alleged airline monopoly](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/akwa-ibom-govt-denies-alleged-airline-monopoly/)[We hand over Yakowa Memorial Hospital to FG for better healthcare - Gov Uba Sani](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/we-handover-sir-patrick-ibrahim-yakowa-memorial-hospital-to-fg-for-quality-affordable-health-delivery-services-gov-uba-sani/)Onanuga said the extension followed a recommendation by the Minister of Interior, Olubunmi Tunji-Ojo.The President commended both officers for the reforms recorded under their leadership and asked them to build on the gains.DAILY POST recalls that Tinubu appointed Nwakuche as CG of the Correctional Service in December 2024 and the Senate confirmed him in March 2025.Similarly, Kemi Nanna Nandap was nominated as Comptroller-General of Immigration on February 21, 2024, and her appointment was confirmed by the Senate on March 1, 2024.Share this story[WhatsApp](https://wa.me/?text=BREAKING%3A%20Tinubu%20extends%20tenures%20of%20Immigration%2C%20Correctional%20Service%20heads%20https%3A%2F%2Fdailypost.ng%2F2026%2F10%2F19%2Fbreaking-tinubu-extends-tenure-of-immigration-correctional-service-heads-2%2F) FacebookYou may like[Sanwo-Olu swears in six permanent secretaries, inaugurates two Lagos commissions](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/sanwo-olu-swears-in-six-permanent-secretaries-inaugurates-two-lagos-commissions/)[NAF neutralises terrorist figures in precision air strike](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/naf-neutralizes-terrorist-figures-in-precision-air-strike/)[Cristiano Ronaldo suspended by Portugal for 30 days, fined \u20ac255](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/breaking-cristiano-ronaldo-suspended-by-portugal-for-30-days-fined-255/)[Join the conversation](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/breaking-tinubu-extends-tenure-of-immigration-correctional-service-heads-2/comments/)", "content_hash": "fa8c6782433ca426b0730d823bb55ac472ab3761d153e7cbc2cd663fcfd9381a", "domain": "dailypost.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1972", "title": "Nigerian Newspapers: 10 things you need to know Tuesday morning", "url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/20/nigerian-newspapers-10-things-you-need-to-know-tuesday-morning-1792470586066", "final_url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/20/nigerian-newspapers-10-things-you-need-to-know-tuesday-morning-1792470586066", "text": "[Nigerian Newspapers](https://dailypost.ng/nigerian-newspapers-2/)Nigerian Newspapers: 10 things you need to know Tuesday morning Published on October 20, 2026By [Ochogwu Sunday](https://dailypost.ng/author/ochogwu-sunday/)[Comment](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/20/nigerian-newspapers-10-things-you-need-to-know-tuesday-morning-1792470586066/comments/)Good morning! Here is today\u2019s summary from [Nigerian newspapers:](https://dailypost.ng)1. Again, President Bola Tinubu has ruled out a return to fuel subsidy, saying his administration\u2019s decision to remove it is irreversible. Tinubu, who drew a comparison between the policy and the biblical account of Moses leading the Israelites out of Egypt, said those advocating a return to the subsidy were asking the country to reverse a decision it had already taken.2. Vice-President Kashim Shettima says Nigeria\u2019s economy would have collapsed without the intervention of President Bola Ahmed Tinubu. Shettima also noted that some of those criticising Tinubu would have been writing about the country\u2019s economic collapse from Dubai and New York. He spoke at the Banquet Hall of the Presidential Villa during an event organised by the City Boy Movement, which is working for Tinubu\u2019s re-election.3. The Director-General of the All Progressives Congress Presidential Campaign Council, PCC, Senator Abdulaziz Yari, says President Bola Tinubu cannot be blamed solely for the widespread insecurity in the country. Speaking on Monday during a visit to Kebbi State, Yari said that if former President Muhammadu Buhari, a retired general, could not completely tackle insecurity, Tinubu should not be blamed for the situation.Recommended[BREAKING: Tinubu extends tenures of Immigration, Correctional Service heads](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/breaking-tinubu-extends-tenure-of-immigration-correctional-service-heads-2/)[Cristiano Ronaldo suspended by Portugal for 30 days, fined \u20ac255](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/breaking-cristiano-ronaldo-suspended-by-portugal-for-30-days-fined-255/)[Bauchi NDC guber candidate resigns from party](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/breaking-ndc-guber-candidate-dumps-party/)4. Two people have lost their lives, while 18 communities have been submerged by massive flooding that ravaged parts of Nasarawa State. Some of the affected villages are Kwatan Bala, Olegokeme, Obendo, Achepe, Olegokpa, Ijiwo, Olegabgulu, Otiya-Gekye, Otiya-Giso, Odagede, Anagoga and Otariga, all located in Rukubi and Akpanaja wards of Doma Local Government Area of the state.5. Former Delta State Governor, Emmanuel Uduaghan has died just three days before his 72nd birthday, according to his family. A statement signed by his son, Toju Anire Uduaghan, on behalf of the family, said the former governor died on Monday.6. The Catholic Bishops Conference of Nigeria, CBCN, has declared a nationwide prayer and fasting programme to promote free, fair, credible and peaceful elections in 2027. According to the bishops, the programme, themed \u2018Let Justice and Peace Flow\u2019, is drawn from Amos 5:24. They announced the initiative in a statement signed by their president, Archbishop Matthew Ndagoso, and secretary, Bishop Peter Odetoyinbo, in Abuja on Monday.7. The Nigeria Democratic Congress, NDC, has faulted the Federal Government\u2019s warning that a return to petrol subsidy could push the naira to between N3,000 and N4,000 per dollar, demanding explanations for the failure of the country\u2019s refineries to operate effectively. The party also questioned why Nigeria had mortgaged a sizeable portion of its crude oil to secure foreign loans.8. The Nigeria Labour Congress, NLC, has warned that Nigerian workers remain under severe economic pressure despite the increase in the national minimum wage to N70,000. It insisted that its two-week ultimatum to the Federal Government over rising petrol prices and fresh wage negotiations remains in force. NLC President Joe Ajaero made the position clear on Monday in Abuja, where he criticised the implementation of the 2024 minimum wage agreement and called for urgent measures to address the rising cost of living.9. The Benue State Police Command has rescued eight kidnap victims, including a Catholic priest, Rev. Fr. John Otache of the Otukpo Diocese, and arrested two suspects in connection with the abduction. The command\u2019s spokesperson, DSP Peter Aondongu, disclosed this in a statement issued on Monday and made available to journalists in Makurdi.10. The Rivers State House of Assembly has recalled three lawmakers loyal to Governor Siminalayi Fubara who were suspended during the political crisis that rocked the state. The Assembly rescinded its decision to declare the seats of the three lawmakers vacant during plenary on Monday.Share this story[WhatsApp](https://wa.me/?text=Nigerian%20Newspapers%3A%2010%20things%20you%20need%20to%20know%20Tuesday%20morning%20%20https%3A%2F%2Fdailypost.ng%2F2026%2F10%2F20%2Fnigerian-newspapers-10-things-you-need-to-know-tuesday-morning-1792470586066%2F) FacebookYou may like[Ex-Delta Governor, Uduaghan is dead](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/news-former-delta-governor-uduaghan-is-dead/)[Court dismisses El-Rufai's N120m rights enforcement suit against DSS, EFCC, ICPC, AGF](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/court-dismisses-el-rufais-n120m-rights-enforcement-suit-against-dss-efcc-icpc-agf/)[2027: Edo APC chair, Tenebe\u2019s AK-47 remarks worsen tension ahead general elections](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/2027-edo-apc-chair-tenebes-ak-47-remarks-worsen-tension-ahead-general-elections/)[Join the conversation](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/20/nigerian-newspapers-10-things-you-need-to-know-tuesday-morning-1792470586066/comments/)", "content_hash": "82e4dadc14a08c5b1682eb219ec8a931c8a7fcd6b6e31abb4ac8d3f661a89afb", "domain": "dailypost.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1973", "title": "2027: Zamfara ADC candidate accuses party members of plotting to replace him", "url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/20/2027-zamfara-adc-candidate-accuses-party-members-of-plotting-to-replace-him", "final_url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/20/2027-zamfara-adc-candidate-accuses-party-members-of-plotting-to-replace-him", "text": "[Politics](https://dailypost.ng/politics/)2027: Zamfara ADC candidate accuses party members of plotting to replace himPublished on October 20, 2026By [Mikal Attah](https://dailypost.ng/author/mikal-attah/)[Comment](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/20/2027-zamfara-adc-candidate-accuses-party-members-of-plotting-to-replace-him/comments/)The African Democratic Congress, ADC, governorship candidate in Zamfara State, Mahdi Aliyu Mohammed has accused some party members of allegedly doctoring documents to replace his name with that of another aspirant ahead of the 2027 general elections.Mohammed alleged in a statement on Monday that the party submitted the name of Bilyamu Shinkafi to the Independent National Electoral Commission, INEC, instead of his, despite his claim that he won the party\u2019s governorship primary held on May 22, 2026.He said he participated in the primary, won the election and completed INEC Form EC9, which he returned to the party for submission to the commission.According to him, the submission of Shinkafi\u2019s name prompted him to approach the court for redress.Recommended[Ndume reveals why he does not wear Tinubu's crested cap](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/20/ndume-reveals-why-he-does-not-wear-tinubus-crested-cap/)[2027: Tambuwal breaks silence on alleged defection to APC](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/2027-tambuwal-breaks-silence-on-alleged-defection-to-apc/)[2027: Our people should defend their votes - Ohanaeze warns election riggers](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/2027-our-people-should-defend-their-votes-ohanaeze-warns-election-riggers/)Mohammed further alleged that some individuals within the party were preparing backdated documents to support Shinkafi\u2019s candidacy, including letters claiming that he participated in the primary.He said the primary election report obtained from INEC and filed in court contradicted the alleged documents.Mohammed warned that anyone found to have forged or submitted false documents would be reported to the relevant security agencies, INEC and the Legal Practitioners Disciplinary Committee for investigation and possible disciplinary action.Share this story[WhatsApp](https://wa.me/?text=2027%3A%20Zamfara%20ADC%20candidate%20accuses%20party%20members%20of%20plotting%20to%20replace%20him%20https%3A%2F%2Fdailypost.ng%2F2026%2F10%2F20%2F2027-zamfara-adc-candidate-accuses-party-members-of-plotting-to-replace-him%2F) FacebookYou may like[Matawalle takes eight suspended Zamfara lawmakers to Tinubu](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/matawalle-takes-eight-suspended-zamfara-lawmakers-to-tinubu/)[2027: 'Tinubu doing politics, wickedness since becoming president' - Atiku's aide replies Alake](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/2027-tinubu-doing-politics-wickedness-since-becoming-president-atikus-aide-replies-alake/)[2027: Only POS business this govt has given you - ADC urges Nigerians to reject APC](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/2027-only-pos-business-this-govt-has-given-you-adc-urges-nigerians-to-reject-apc/)[Join the conversation](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/20/2027-zamfara-adc-candidate-accuses-party-members-of-plotting-to-replace-him/comments/)", "content_hash": "32c737d8ddfe9b74cec475b26f51e2af01843c25c05dc3bece334a85ccb13615", "domain": "dailypost.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1974", "title": "Police arrest six over alleged armed robbery in Jigawa \u200e \u200e", "url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/20/police-arrest-six-suspects-over-armed-robbery-in-jigawa", "final_url": "https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/20/police-arrest-six-suspects-over-armed-robbery-in-jigawa", "text": "[Metro](https://dailypost.ng/metro/)Police arrest six over alleged armed robbery in Jigawa \u200e \u200ePublished on October 20, 2026By [Khaleel Muhammad](https://dailypost.ng/author/ibrahim-ibrahim/)[Comment](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/20/police-arrest-six-suspects-over-armed-robbery-in-jigawa/comments/)The Jigawa State Police Command has arrested six suspects in connection with armed robbery incidents in Jahun Local Government Area of the state, during which cash and valuables worth millions of naira were stolen.The Police Public Relations Officer, SP Lawan Shiisu Adam, disclosed this in a statement issued to journalists on Monday.He said the arrests followed investigations into two armed robbery incidents reported in Jahun on September 24, 2026.According to the statement, armed men invaded the residence of a 35-year-old man in Kofar Daru Quarters, Jahun, at about 2 a.m. and stole N20.18 million in cash, iTel mobile phones valued at N13.13 million, recharge cards worth N1 million, Android phones and other valuables.Recommended[FRSC blames speeding for Otedola Bridge tanker fire](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/frsc-blames-speeding-for-otedola-bridge-tanker-fire-2/)[Police arrest robbery suspects, probe four deaths in Osun](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/police-arrest-robbery-suspects-probe-four-deaths-in-osun/)[Troops arrest three suspected terrorists in Kogi hills](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/troops-arrest-three-suspected-terrorists-in-kogi-hills/)He explained that the same gang also attacked the residence of a 37-year-old man in Shagari Quarters, where they stole N45,000, two blankets, rice and a woman\u2019s wrapper.Shiisu said detectives attached to the Jahun Police Division launched an investigation following the incidents, leading to the arrest of five suspects.The suspects were identified as Sale Ibrahim, 25; Abubakar Abdullahi, 28; Babangida Ya\u2019u, 35; Babannan Hassan, 27; and Isah Isibu, 35.The police spokesperson further disclosed that on October 15, 2026, operatives of the Maigatari Police Division, working with the Border Patrol Team, arrested another suspect, Isa Yusuf, 35, from Hantsu village in Miga Local Government Area.He said the suspect was arrested during a routine patrol at Maigatari Market, where officers recovered 13 new iTel mobile phones, 13 chargers, 13 batteries and N102,500 in cash.According to Shiisu, the suspect confessed during interrogation at the State Criminal Investigation Department (SCID) in Dutse to being a member of the gang responsible for the Jahun robbery incidents.He added that investigations confirmed that the recovered items were part of the property stolen during the attacks.Shiisu said the six suspects had been charged to court, while efforts were ongoing to arrest other fleeing members of the gang and recover the remaining stolen property.Share this story[WhatsApp](https://wa.me/?text=Police%20arrest%20six%20over%20alleged%20armed%20robbery%20in%20Jigawa%20%E2%80%8E%20%E2%80%8E%20https%3A%2F%2Fdailypost.ng%2F2026%2F10%2F20%2Fpolice-arrest-six-suspects-over-armed-robbery-in-jigawa%2F) FacebookYou may like[Lagos shuts Ejigbo Fire Station over repeated attacks on firefighters](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/lagos-shuts-ejigbo-fire-station-over-repeated-attacks-on-firefighters-2/)[Two severely burnt as diesel tanker plunges from Otedola bridge in Lagos](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/two-severely-burnt-as-diesel-tanker-plunges-from-otedola-bridge-in-lagos/)[Tanker catches fire under Otedola Bridge, Lagos](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/19/tanker-catches-fire-under-otedola-bridge-lagos/)[Join the conversation](https://dailypost.ng/2026/10/20/police-arrest-six-suspects-over-armed-robbery-in-jigawa/comments/)", "content_hash": "6744d32d856a1543a9f26d34058f6c689398d53336de30e290f4522066667e33", "domain": "dailypost.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1975", "title": "Toxic Food Series: Inside food markets where cancer-linked chemicals kill consumers", "url": "https://businessday.ng/news/article/toxic-food-series-inside-food-markets-where-cancer-linked-chemicals-kill-consumers", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/news/article/toxic-food-series-inside-food-markets-where-cancer-linked-chemicals-kill-consumers", "text": "Share In the first of a five-part BusinessDay series on hazardous chemicals and fake products in Nigeria\u2019s food chain, Josephine Okojie-Okeiyi and Cynthia Egboboh reports on how banned pesticides and harmful substances enter everyday food, killing and exposing consumers to health risks. At the entrance of Ile-Epo Market, large metal bowls filled with beans sit in front of a trader who calls out to attract customers moving in and out of the market. To shoppers, the beans are an affordable staple used to prepare everyday meals in Nigerian households. BusinessDay found that farmers are using banned and harmful pesticides linked to cancer to protect the beans, casting doubts about the safety of food sold in local markets. Findings by BusinessDay showed that excessive amounts of the pesticide dichlorvos are used in bean production to safeguard the harvest and prevent loss. Research also found that excessive amounts and prolonged exposure to the dichlorvos pesticide through food consumption could cause death, genotoxic, neurological, reproductive, carcinogenic, immunological, hepatic, renal, respiratory, metabolic, dermal, and other systemic effects. This goes beyond beans, further findings by BusinessDay across major Lagos markets found a wide range of staples laced with harmful chemicals to make produce look reddish and boost profits. Palm oil tinted with dyes, and plantain ripened with industrial calcium carbide are sold openly across Nigerian markets. The practice is exposing millions of consumers to chemicals linked to kidney failure and organ damage in Africa\u2019s most populous nation, where food safety oversight remains fragmented. According to the Alliance for Action on Pesticide in Nigeria (AAPN), over 65 percent of active ingredients across various pesticides and brands in Nigerian homes, stores, farms and markets are classified as Highly Hazardous Pesticides (HHPs). The group added that Nigeria records about 200,000 deaths annually due to food poisoning, while the cost of illnesses associated with foodborne diseases is estimated at $3.6 billion per year. Read also: [Food inflation diverges across states as Lagos hits 34.4%, -7.5% in Taraba, Borno records 2.2%](https://businessday.ng/news/article/food-inflation-diverges-across-states-as-lagos-hits-34-4-7-5-in-taraba-borno-records-2-2/) A deadly trend across markets BusinessDay observed that food items contaminated with hazardous pesticides are being sold across Nigerian markets. At Mile 12 and Ile-Epo markets, traders said calcium carbide is essential for daily supply. Traders who asked not to be identified said that without forced ripening, they would have no plantain to sell the next day, as consumers prefer ripe produce. Another trader who sells palm oil said buyers equate deep red palm oil with quality. \u201cThe redder it is, the faster it sells,\u201d he said, noting that this is why some traders add colour ingredients to their produce to sell faster. Consuming these chemicals over time carries severe health risks, according to doctors. \u201cThe health effects range from kidney failure to cancer,\u201d said Patrick Ijewere, medical director at The Nutrition Hospital in Lagos. \u201cThese diseases were not prevalent before but are now rising at an alarming rate because of unwholesome foods,\u201d he said. NAFDAC has banned Sudan dyes, calcium carbide for ripening and dichlorvos above 0.01mg/kg, but enforcement at open markets is weak. Qrisstuberg Amua, a professor and executive director of the Centre for Food Safety and Agricultural Research (CEFSAR), said processing is another area where harmful chemicals are used aside from markets and farms. Cassava, for instance, is highly carcinogenic if not properly processed, Amua noted. It used to take four days to process garri, but these days, it takes food processors a day, he added. \u201cThey add hypo and detergents to quicken fermentation and whitening. This is wrong and must stop to save lives,\u201d Amua noted. On bean preservation, he said analysis of sniper, officially dichlorvos, shows adulteration. \u201cWhen we subject it to analysis, we found it contains more hazardous chemicals like chlorpyrifos and paraquat,\u201d he said. \u201cThese are on NAFDAC\u2019s banned list, yet they pour into the country at an alarming rate,\u201d he added. Read also: [Food inflation edges to 19.59% as Nigeria\u2019s headline rate drops to 14.63%](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/food-inflation-edges-to-19-59-as-nigerias-headline-rate-drops-to-14-63/) Fuelling export rejection The use of chemicals by traders and farmers has continued to drive rejection of Nigerian food exported to the European Union (EU), the U.S. and others. The European Union (EU) had in 2016 rejected 24 food products from Nigeria. Groundnuts were rejected because they contained aflatoxin, while palm oil had a colouring agent that was carcinogenic. The European Food Safety Authority had likewise rejected beans from Nigeria in 2015 because they contained between 0.03 mg/kg and 4.6mg/kg of dichlorvos pesticide, when the acceptable maximum residue limit was 0.01mg/kg. The ban is still in place, indicating that Nigerian farmers, traders and exporters are yet to change from such practices. \u201cIn the long run, produce from this country doesn\u2019t meet requirements for international export because of these unwholesome practices,\u201d Amua said. \u201cOver there, foods are tested before they are allowed for human consumption.\u201d Low awareness fuelling food poisoning Despite the danger that these chemicals in food portend, most farmers and traders in Africa\u2019s most populous nation are not aware. This widespread dearth of knowledge by players across the food value chain has continued to fuel their use and is responsible for the loss of billions of naira Nigeria would have generated from non-oil exports if agro commodities were not rejected. \u201cI never knew that adding red dye to ground dried pepper to sell is dangerous to human health,\u201d a trader who gave her name as Bose told BusinessDay. \u201cI have stopped using it since someone told me because I also consume what I sell,\u201d she noted. Experts say traders\u2019 and farmers\u2019 education on the dangers of these harmful chemicals, strong regulation, and appropriate infrastructure will help reduce their use in food. \u201cPeople adding those materials don\u2019t even know the chemical composition or the toxicological implications,\u201d Amua said. Related News [Airlines pivot to new funding models as bank loan rates surge](https://businessday.ng/aviation/article/airlines-pivot-to-new-funding-models-as-bank-loan-rates-surge/) [2027 Election: N3.8trn campaign spending cap faces enforcement test](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/2027-election-n3-8trn-campaign-spending-cap-faces-enforcement-test/) [Rising oil prices could push petrol discount bill to N114bn](https://businessday.ng/energy/article/rising-oil-prices-could-push-petrol-discount-bill-to-n114bn/) Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Tinubu extends tenures of heads of Correctional and Immigration Services](https://businessday.ng/news/article/tinubu-extends-tenures-of-heads-of-correctional-and-immigration-services/) [Tinubu rules out petrol subsidy return, pledges reform continuity](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/tinubu-rules-out-petrol-subsidy-return-pledges-reform-continuity/) [INEC shifts 2027 candidate eligibility battle to the courts](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/inec-shifts-2027-candidate-eligibility-battle-to-the-courts/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "8b3f1a88b8d49458bfbc468badda937a0bc75cfd3adfffc2d79f42da8a7d771c", "domain": "businessday.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1976", "title": "Airlines pivot to new funding models as bank loan rates surge", "url": "https://businessday.ng/aviation/article/airlines-pivot-to-new-funding-models-as-bank-loan-rates-surge", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/aviation/article/airlines-pivot-to-new-funding-models-as-bank-loan-rates-surge", "text": "Share \u2026United Nigeria Airlines launches first commercial paper in industry\u2019s history Nigerian airlines are turning to capital markets and state government partnerships to ease a working capital squeeze as high borrowing costs and rising operating expenses put pressure on their finances. United Nigeria Airlines Company Limited has become the first domestic carrier to issue commercial paper, launching a N20 billion first tranche under a N100 billion programme, according to its September 2026 investor presentation. The move offers an alternative to bank loans that analysts say now attract interest rates of between 29 and 35 percent, while commercial paper typically costs 22 to 26 percent. Domestic carriers are accumulating substantial short-term liabilities, including more than N60 billion tied up in aviation fuel (Jet A1) financing. Their reliance on high-cost bank loans has added to the pressure on working capital. According to its September 2026 investor presentation, which outlined its financial performance and five-year expansion strategy, the commercial paper will be issued in two series, with tenors of 270 days and 364 days. The proposed issuance is the first under the airline\u2019s N100 billion commercial paper programme. The transaction has Meristem Capital Limited as lead arranger, while Cordros Advisory Services Limited, Coronation Merchant Bank Limited, FCMB Capital Markets Limited, FSL Advisory Limited and Quantum Zenith Capital & Investments Limited are dealers. The airline is seeking to strengthen its financial position and expand its domestic, regional and international operations. United Nigeria Airlines has also secured ratings from two rating agencies. Agusto & Co. rated it A3 for the short term and BBB- for the long term, with a stable outlook, while DataPro assigned the airline short-term and long-term ratings of A1 and BBB, respectively. BusinessDay\u2019s findings show that airline operators are also partnering with state governments to gain access to financing and guarantees. Financial institutions may be more willing to lend to state governments because of the legal and contractual obligations associated with government borrowing. Airlines are using these partnerships to address challenges including inadequate maintenance facilities, a global shortage of aircraft and insurance constraints, which affect their ability to secure dry-leased aircraft from lessors. Read also: [Domestic airlines risk N6.6bn on 25-day Owerri Airport closure](https://businessday.ng/aviation/article/domestic-airlines-risk-n6-6bn-on-25-day-owerri-airport-closure/) Some state governments have launched airlines that operate through existing carriers while completing their own certification processes. Finding alternative models BusinessDay\u2019s findings show that Aero Contractors is working with Cross River State to operate Cally Air, the state\u2019s airline. The state has two Boeing 737 aircraft operated by Aero Contractors, and the airline is in talks with the Cross River State government to acquire additional aircraft. The latest example is the partnership between Enugu Air and XEJET. Enugu Air has designated XEJET as its operational partner while it completes its Air Operator Certificate (AOC) process. XEJET has completed a variation of its operational approvals to include the EMB 170 aircraft in its Operations Specifications (OpsSpecs), in line with the Nigeria Civil Aviation Authority\u2019s (NCAA) five-phase certification procedure. Ibom Air, the Akwa Ibom State-owned airline, has partnered with Airbus and ordered 10 Airbus A220-300 aircraft. It has already received two aircraft. Ibom Air is also establishing a maintenance, repair and overhaul (MRO) facility intended to serve the African market, following the examples of EgyptAir, Ethiopian Airlines and Kenya Airways. Ibom Air is also working with Airbus Consulting on a maintenance strategy covering the next six to eight years, which includes plans for full-scope A220 maintenance. For Enugu Air, the airline said it is prioritising operational stability and service delivery before considering commercial paper as a source of funding. Okey Nnake, chief financial officer of Enugu Air, said the airline is currently funded by strategic long-term partners whose financing arrangements match the long-term nature of aviation assets. \u201cCommercial paper is an excellent financing option that we will keep in view for the future. However, it also requires a mature foundation, including multi-year audited financial statements and established credit ratings,\u201d Nnake said. He said the airline would consider the instrument after establishing a consistent operational track record. Its immediate priorities, he added, were premium service delivery, operational excellence, route stabilisation and safety. While commercial paper marks a milestone for domestic aviation financing, analysts emphasise that market debt is a funding tool rather than a complete solution to the industry\u2019s long-term fleet financing needs. Samuel Oyekanmi, head of research at Norrenberger, said commercial paper offers a cheaper alternative to bank lending rates of 29 to 35 percent, with commercial paper typically priced between 22 and 26 percent. However, he cautioned against using short-term market instruments to finance long-term assets. \u201cCommercial paper is cheaper. I\u2019d call it a smarter use of the market than a fundamental fix. CP suits working capital and bridging, not aircraft. If CP proceeds fund fleet expansion, the airline will have to keep refinancing, and a bad market or a weak quarter could close that door,\u201d Oyekanmi said. Alex Nwuba, president of the Aircraft Owners and Pilots Association of Nigeria, commended the move but urged carriers to engage professional advisers to access international structured finance and leasing arrangements. \u201cCommercial paper can provide short-term working capital, but it does not resolve the underlying pressures of fuel prices, foreign exchange, and maintenance. Financial engineering and professional funding advisory should be as important to an airline as fleet planning and route development. The objective should be to secure capital at the right cost, for the right duration, and against a credible repayment plan,\u201d Nwuba said. Samuel Caulcrick, former rector of the Nigerian College of Aviation Technology (NCAT), highlighted the risk of currency mismatches, noting that airlines earn revenue in naira while heavy maintenance and aircraft leases require US dollars. \u201cGoing to the market gives an airline room to breathe compared to rigid, high-interest local bank loans, but it is not a cure-all. Because Nigeria lacks major local MRO facilities, an unprogrammed engine swap requires massive USD liquidity. If an airline spends market capital on expansion without keeping a disciplined cash reserve, a single grounded engine can trigger a revenue death spiral,\u201d Caulcrick said. Olumide Ohunayo, director of research at Zenith Travels, praised United Nigeria Airlines for taking the lead in capital market financing, saying the model could attract other carriers if supported by strong revenue. \u201cWith revenue growing toward N90 billion, this move shows United Nigeria Airlines is ready to invest and grow. Every option carries risk, but taking this path brings down funding costs. Other airlines should examine this model, especially when single-digit bank loans remain out of reach, while aiming for longer tenors to allow sufficient operational breathing room,\u201d Ohunayo said. Related News [Toxic Food Series: Inside food markets where cancer-linked chemicals kill consumers](https://businessday.ng/news/article/toxic-food-series-inside-food-markets-where-cancer-linked-chemicals-kill-consumers/) [2027 Election: N3.8trn campaign spending cap faces enforcement test](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/2027-election-n3-8trn-campaign-spending-cap-faces-enforcement-test/) [Rising oil prices could push petrol discount bill to N114bn](https://businessday.ng/energy/article/rising-oil-prices-could-push-petrol-discount-bill-to-n114bn/) [Ifeoma Okeke-Korieocha](https://businessday.ng/author/ifeoma-okeke/) Ifeoma Okeke-Korieocha is the Aviation Correspondent at BusinessDay Media Limited, publishers of BusinessDay Newspapers. She is also the Deputy Editor, BusinessDay Weekender Magazine, the Saturday Weekend edition of BusinessDay. She holds a BSC in Mass Communication from the prestigious University of Nigeria, Nsukka and a Masters degree in Marketing at the University of Lagos. As the lead writer on the aviation desk, Ifeoma is responsible and in charge of the three weekly aviation and travel pages in BusinessDay and BDSunday. She also overseas and edits all pages of BusinessDay Saturday Weekender. She has written various investigative, features and news stories in aviation and business related issues and has been severally nominated for award in the category of Aviation Writer of the Year by the Nigeria Media Nite-Out awards; one of the Nigeria\u2019s most prestigious media awards ceremonies. Ifeoma is a one-time winner of the prestigious Nigeria Media Merit Award under the 'Aviation Writer of the Year' Category. She is the 2025 Eloy Award winner under the Print Media Journalist category. She has undergone several journalism trainings by various prestigious organisations. Ifeoma is also a fellow of the Female Reporters Leadership Fellowship of the Wole Soyinka Centre for Investigative Journalism. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [FG releases additional N4bn for 600 ex-Nigeria Airways workers](https://businessday.ng/aviation/article/fg-releases-additional-n4bn-for-600-ex-nigeria-airways-workers/) [20yrs of aviation fuelling: CITA Energies eyes expansion, diversification amid supply chain volatility](https://businessday.ng/aviation/article/20yrs-of-aviation-fuelling-cita-energies-eyes-expansion-diversification-amid-supply-chain-volatility/) [Talks on financing for Lagos airport rail project at advanced stage, says MD FAAN](https://businessday.ng/aviation/article/talks-on-financing-for-lagos-airport-rail-project-at-advanced-stage-says-md-faan/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "a98d3631b63a3bcff6b770c8e18887c60dc32061807a5fb0f6125f7e5c5e7851", "domain": "businessday.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1977", "title": "2027 Election: N3.8trn campaign spending cap faces enforcement test", "url": "https://businessday.ng/politics/article/2027-election-n3-8trn-campaign-spending-cap-faces-enforcement-test", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/politics/article/2027-election-n3-8trn-campaign-spending-cap-faces-enforcement-test", "text": "Share Nigeria has raised the legal limits on campaign spending ahead of the 2027 general elections, but concerns are mounting about whether the Independent National Electoral Commission (INEC) can effectively track what candidates and their political networks actually spend. The Electoral Act 2026 doubles the maximum spending for presidential candidates to N10 billion from N5 billion under the repealed 2022 Act, while the limit for governorship candidates has tripled to N3 billion from N1 billion. For senatorial candidates, the spending ceiling rose from N100 million to N500 million, while for House of Representatives candidates, it increased from N70 million to N250 million. State House of Assembly candidates can now spend up to N100 million, up from the previously approved N30 million. The limit for Area Council chairmanship candidates has risen from N30 million to N100 million, while the councillorship ceiling doubled to N10 million from N5 million. The maximum donation from an individual or entity has also increased from N50 million to N500 million. While political observers argue that the revisions reflect inflation and the rising cost of campaigns, the increases have renewed questions about transparency, disclosure and enforcement, particularly in a political system where campaign finance records are rarely made readily available to the public. Political analysts argue that the challenge is not simply setting spending limits but establishing whether candidates comply with them. N3.88trn spending ceiling Candidates contesting Nigeria\u2019s 2027 elections face a combined statutory campaign spending ceiling of N3.88 trillion, a BusinessDay analysis of official candidate lists and spending limits under the Electoral Act 2026 has shown. The estimate covers presidential candidates, Senate candidates, House of Representatives candidates, State Houses of Assembly candidates and governorship candidates across 28 states. The 18 presidential candidates on the INEC-approved list have a combined spending ceiling of N180 billion. The 1,303 Senate candidates account for N651.5 billion, while the 3,394 House of Representatives candidates have a combined ceiling of N848.5 billion. The 9,402 State Assembly candidates account for another N940.2 billion, while 421 governorship candidates across 28 states have a combined ceiling of N1.263 trillion. Together, the five categories represent a maximum permitted expenditure of N3.8832 trillion. The figure represents the maximum amount candidates could legally spend if they all exhausted their individual limits. The calculation excludes separately assessed political-party expenditure. It provides an indication of the scale of candidate spending permitted under the law ahead of the 2027 elections. Campaign spending beyond the party structure Presidential candidates travel across the 36 states and the Federal Capital Territory, maintain campaign offices, deploy vehicles, organise rallies and pay for advertising, publicity and logistics over several months. Governorship candidates undertake similar activities across local government areas in their states. Billboard permits alone can cost hundreds of millions of naira in some jurisdictions, while rallies featuring prominent entertainers, branded materials, consultants and social media influencers add to campaign costs. Such expenditure can be difficult to capture when political support groups operate alongside formal party structures. Groups including the City Boy Movement and Relax Tinubu is Fixing Nigeria have mobilised support for President Bola Tinubu, the APC candidate. The Obidient Movement and OK Movement have also been associated with support for Peter Obi, the NDC candidate. The extent to which expenditure by such groups is captured in candidates\u2019 official campaign returns is a critical enforcement question. Without comprehensive monitoring, candidates could benefit from spending undertaken by supporters without that expenditure being fully reflected in their declared accounts. Festus Adedayo, a prominent political columnist, had earlier raised concerns about campaign finance transparency. \u201cThe kind of massive corruption that goes into campaign funding should be an issue of interest to Nigerians,\u201d he wrote, questioning where leading presidential candidates and governors would obtain the multibillion-naira funds required for their campaigns. Read also: [Tinubu campaign aims to buy northern votes, claims PRP chair](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/tinubu-campaign-aims-to-buy-northern-votes-claims-prp-chair/) He argued that campaign accounts should be managed by certified accountants, with donations and expenditure subjected to periodic audits. Legal requirements, limited public disclosure Nigeria\u2019s electoral framework provides for financial oversight. Section 90 of the Electoral Act 2026 requires political parties to submit annual statements of assets and liabilities, sources of funds and other assets, and expenditure to the Independent National Electoral Commission (INEC). The commission\u2019s 2026 regulations also require audited returns covering election expenditure, including amounts spent, expenditure items and donor details. However, the existence of these requirements does not automatically guarantee compliance or effective enforcement. Public access to comprehensive campaign finance information remains a major concern. Paul James, an electoral and political process specialist, described the monitoring of campaign finance in Nigeria as a \u201cwild goose chase\u201d, citing weak transparency and the tendency for scrutiny to intensify only after elections. \u201cTo be honest, for me, anything related to campaign finance, it\u2019s like a mirage. I always see it as a wild goose chase, especially in relation to the opacity of the whole conversations around tracking campaign finance and also the timing of it,\u201d he said. James argued that waiting until campaigns have ended to examine financial returns limits regulators\u2019 ability to identify violations while they are occurring. \u201cIt will ultimately have no value, talking about the campaign spending limit, if regulators cannot establish what candidates actually spend, including the spending on their behalf by other actors,\u201d he said. He urged INEC to deploy campaign finance monitoring teams nationwide, supported by digital reporting systems and collaboration with civil society organisations, the media, financial regulators and anti-corruption agencies. Monitoring, he said, should compare candidates\u2019 declarations with independently collected evidence of campaign activities and prevailing market prices for advertising, rallies, transportation, branded materials and other services. James warned that campaign funding could also provide cover for questionable sources of wealth if regulators fail to establish who ultimately finances political activities. \u201cMost times I see campaign spending, to be honest, my personal assessment is a money laundering venture, where people just try to clean up their money using campaign as excuses,\u201d he said. He urged authorities to identify the sources of donations, scrutinise suspicious transactions and ensure that third-party arrangements do not conceal the actual donors or circumvent spending limits. Lessons from other democracies Other countries offer examples of how campaign finance rules can be supported by disclosure and public scrutiny. In the United Kingdom, the Electoral Commission oversees political finance, requiring parties and campaigners to report relevant donations and expenditure. Canada requires political participants to appoint financial agents and submit financial returns that can be accessed through public registries. Germany requires political parties to submit accounting reports, with significant donations subject to disclosure requirements. South Africa also publishes political funding disclosures through the Independent Electoral Commission, including information on qualifying private donations. Read also: [Political calculations behind Tinubu\u2019s 12-state campaign rally strategy](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/political-calculations-behind-tinubus-12-state-campaign-rally-strategy/) These systems differ in their thresholds and reporting requirements, but they demonstrate the importance of making political finance information accessible and subjecting declarations to independent scrutiny. For Nigeria, the challenge is to translate statutory provisions into a monitoring system capable of tracking spending across formal party structures and associated campaign networks. Consequences beyond electoral integrity The Central Bank of Nigeria had earlier warned that rising election-related spending could fuel demand-side inflation. \u201cAs political activities intensify ahead of the 2027 elections, increased fiscal injections and consumption spending could elevate demand-side inflation,\u201d Muhammad Abdullahi, CBN deputy governor for Economic Policy, said earlier in the year. Abdullahi added that \u201cthe fiscal deficit has already increased significantly, and election-related spending is likely to exacerbate this trend in 2026 and early 2027.\u201d The Nigerian Economic Summit Group also cautioned that pre-election expenditure could weaken fiscal discipline, undermine investor confidence and slow structural reforms. The higher spending ceilings may reflect the realities of modern political campaigns, but their effectiveness depends on whether regulators can verify what candidates actually spend and enforce the rules when violations occur. Without timely disclosures, independent monitoring and credible sanctions, Nigeria risks maintaining campaign spending limits on paper while allowing actual expenditure to escape meaningful scrutiny. Policy and Legal Advocacy Centre (PLAC), a non-governmental organisation focused on democratic governance, had said the 100 percent \u2013 400 percent and the donation cap that rose by 900 percent go well beyond inflation adjustment. The organisation warned that the tenfold donation cap increase risks enabling elite capture of electoral financing where a single entity can now legally provide a candidate with N500 million. \u201cPenalties are stronger on paper, but enforcement credibility, not statutory language, is the real determinant of whether these provisions have any effect. Section 93\u2019s disclosure and publication requirements for party expenditure are a genuine transparency opportunity, contingent on consistent INEC and judicial enforcement. The Act strengthens the penalty framework on paper. However, Nigeria\u2019s electoral history reveals a consistent gap between statutory penalties and actual enforcement. INEC has historically lacked the investigative capacity and prosecutorial leverage to pursue campaign finance violations at scale. The 2022 Act contained similar provisions that were rarely enforced,\u201d it said. Related News [Toxic Food Series: Inside food markets where cancer-linked chemicals kill consumers](https://businessday.ng/news/article/toxic-food-series-inside-food-markets-where-cancer-linked-chemicals-kill-consumers/) [Airlines pivot to new funding models as bank loan rates surge](https://businessday.ng/aviation/article/airlines-pivot-to-new-funding-models-as-bank-loan-rates-surge/) [Rising oil prices could push petrol discount bill to N114bn](https://businessday.ng/energy/article/rising-oil-prices-could-push-petrol-discount-bill-to-n114bn/) [Taofeek Oyedokun](https://businessday.ng/author/oyedokunoyedele1gmail-com/) Taofeek Oyedokun is a correspondent at BusinessDay with years of experience reporting on political economy, public policy, migration, environment/climate change, and social justice. A graduate of Political Science from the University of Lagos, he has also earned multiple professional certificates in journalism and media-related training. Known for his clear, data-driven reporting, Oyedokun covers a wide range of national and international socioeconomic issues, bringing depth, balance, and public-interest focus to his work. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Tinubu rules out petrol subsidy return, pledges reform continuity](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/tinubu-rules-out-petrol-subsidy-return-pledges-reform-continuity/) [INEC shifts 2027 candidate eligibility battle to the courts](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/inec-shifts-2027-candidate-eligibility-battle-to-the-courts/) [INEC under fire over governorship candidates\u2019 exclusion, alleged double standards on Rivers](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/inec-under-fire-over-governorship-candidates-exclusion-alleged-double-standards-on-rivers/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "26d81673cc428a4276301cface86a3350f09ab18bdef5e9f08819757e292afcf", "domain": "businessday.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1978", "title": "Rising oil prices could push petrol discount bill to N114bn", "url": "https://businessday.ng/energy/article/rising-oil-prices-could-push-petrol-discount-bill-to-n114bn", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/energy/article/rising-oil-prices-could-push-petrol-discount-bill-to-n114bn", "text": "Share Nigeria\u2019s month-old programme to sell petrol at a discount through filling stations operated by state-owned Nigerian National Petroleum Company Limited (NNPC) faces mounting fiscal pressure as crude oil prices climb amid escalating conflict in the Middle East, BusinessDay analysis has revealed. Brent, the global benchmark for Nigeria\u2019s crude oil, approached $110 a barrel earlier on Monday, while Brent and US benchmark West Texas Intermediate (WTI) have both traded above $100 as fighting around the Strait of Hormuz and the Red Sea disrupts shipping. Goldman Sachs has warned that prices could reach $120 a barrel if attacks on vessels intensify. Each increase in crude prices widens the gap the government has to fill, experts said, and the scheme has no clear end date. How the scheme works Taiwo Oyedele, minister of finance, announced the 30-day intervention on October 8, which requires NNPC retail outlets to sell petrol at cost, without a profit margin. The government also proposed a wholesale price benchmark of N1,350 per litre while refiners and importers would absorb costs above that level and recover the losses when crude prices fall. Other measures include forward crude sales to domestic refineries, a national strategic fuel reserve, wider cash transfers and extra support for vulnerable households. The government has yet to publish what the package will cost. Read also: [Nigeria\u2019s $100 oil strengthens the case for freeing Gazelle crude](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/nigerias-100-oil-strengthens-the-case-for-freeing-gazelle-crude/) Rising bills Using a discount of \u20a666 per litre at NNPC stations and estimated national demand of about 50 million litres a day, the programme would cost approximately \u20a63.3 billion daily, or \u20a699 billion over 30 days. BusinessDay\u2019s scenario calculation shows that the estimated bill could rise to about \u20a6114 billion if Brent crude reaches $120 per barrel and the cost of the intervention increases in direct proportion to crude prices. This would represent an additional \u20a614.9 billion, or 15.1 percent, above the estimated 30-day cost of \u20a699 billion, based on Brent\u2019s closing price of $104.28 per barrel on October 8, when the government announced the intervention. The calculation illustrates the potential fiscal exposure as conflict in the Middle East threatens oil supplies and disrupts shipping routes. However, the estimate assumes that the cost of the scheme rises in line with Brent prices, a relationship the government has not publicly established. If the discount remains fixed at \u20a666 per litre and daily demand stays at 50 million litres, the estimated 30-day cost would remain \u20a699 billion under those assumptions. Extension would cost more BMI, a unit of Fitch Solutions, put the cost at 0.2 percent to 0.4 percent of gross domestic product in a report titled \u201cFuel Discount Mechanism Raises Macro Risks in Nigeria\u201d, assuming the measures end in early the second quarter of 2027. The research firm applied to the National Bureau of Statistics\u2019 2025 nominal GDP of about N441.5 trillion, that range equals about N883 billion to N1.77 trillion. Read also: [Nigeria exceeds OPEC quota for 5th month as oil production hits 1.71m barrels in September](https://businessday.ng/energy/article/nigeria-exceeds-opec-quota-for-5th-month-as-oil-production-hits-1-71m-barrels-in-september/) BMI said it expects the discount to stay in place for about six months rather than 30 days, and to be phased out in late first-quarter or early second-quarter 2027. It cited an expected U.S.-Iran agreement that could bring crude prices down. The research firm warned that a longer-running scheme could hurt government revenue, worsen the fiscal position and put fresh pressure on the naira. It kept its forecast for the budget deficit to widen to 3.3 percent of GDP in 2026 from 1.7 percent in 2025. It acknowledged that the scheme differs significantly from Nigeria\u2019s earlier petrol subsidy regime, which the government ended in 2023. Election year Nigeria holds general elections in January 2027, and BMI said the package fits with pre-election spending increases already built into its forecasts. Jide Pratts, country manager at digital trading platform TradeGrid, said the government risked sliding back into subsidies without a clear price window capturing the full cost of crude from refining to market. \u201cThe old subsidy was an aberration because we couldn\u2019t monitor it,\u201d he said, adding that without digital tools to trace spending, \u201cwe might be going back to the same thing.\u201d Olatide Jeremiah, chief executive of energy analysis firm Petroleumprice.ng, told BusinessDay that a more efficient scheme would be for the federal government to restructure subsidy in a way that\u2019s targeted to gantry and pump price reduction simultaneously. \u201cSubsidy should be for a specific set of targeted audience, not for everyone, just like it\u2019s done everywhere in the world,\u201d Jeremiah said. Global backdrop The outlook for crude offers little relief. Helima Croft of RBC Capital Markets said Brent could top $120 by year-end if fighting continues, according to Reuters. She said Yemen\u2019s Houthis had taken the Red Sea port of Mokha, which would allow more direct attacks on Saudi energy infrastructure and tankers, and that maritime traffic through the Bab el-Mandeb strait was \u201cgravely imperilled\u201d. The rally has pushed up bond yields and revived inflation fears. JP Morgan forecasts that eight to nine developed economies, including the United States, Japan, Australia, New Zealand and several European central banks, could raise interest rates by the end of the year. Asian governments are expected to extend financial help to businesses and households as diesel prices surge, Bloomberg reported. Donald Trump, United States president, said earlier this week the war would end after the November elections. Many analysts doubt that, with fighting continuing and neither side raising peace talks. Related News [Toxic Food Series: Inside food markets where cancer-linked chemicals kill consumers](https://businessday.ng/news/article/toxic-food-series-inside-food-markets-where-cancer-linked-chemicals-kill-consumers/) [Airlines pivot to new funding models as bank loan rates surge](https://businessday.ng/aviation/article/airlines-pivot-to-new-funding-models-as-bank-loan-rates-surge/) [2027 Election: N3.8trn campaign spending cap faces enforcement test](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/2027-election-n3-8trn-campaign-spending-cap-faces-enforcement-test/) [Feyishola Jaiyesimi](https://businessday.ng/author/feyishola-jaiyesimi/) Feyishola Jaiyesimi is a writer and storyteller. She currently writes for the energy desk at BusinessDay with a focus on renewable energy and environmental issues. She began her journalism career as an agricultural reporter and now covers the energy sector, including oil, gas, electricity, environment, and renewables. She has been selected for professional training by the US Consulate, Lagos. She is a 2025 Dataphyte Biodiversity Reporting Fellow. Feyishola holds a bachelor\u2019s degree in Zoology and Environmental Biology from Ekiti State University. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Power outage rises as generation plunges from 3,840mw to less than 500mw](https://businessday.ng/energy/power/article/power-outage-rises-as-generation-plunges-from-3840mw-to-less-than-500mw/) [Nigeria\u2019s gas boom faces a bankability test beyond LNG exports](https://businessday.ng/energy/oilandgas/article/nigerias-gas-boom-faces-a-bankability-test-beyond-lng-exports/) [TotalEnergies deepens Nigeria bet with Ubeta, Ima gas investments](https://businessday.ng/energy/article/totalenergies-deepens-nigeria-bet-with-ubeta-ima-gas-investments/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "854db3263d1e43ce35f8bc689fdce58aae0fab4e0815c911bdaffccacf6734bc", "domain": "businessday.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1979", "title": "Deutsche Bank bets on Naira, adds Nigeria to frontier FX monitor", "url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/deutsche-bank-bets-on-naira-adds-nigeria-to-frontier-fx-monitor", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/deutsche-bank-bets-on-naira-adds-nigeria-to-frontier-fx-monitor", "text": "Share Deutsche Bank has turned bullish on the naira, adding Nigeria to its frontier foreign exchange monitor as the lender sees room for further gains in a currency that has strengthened against the dollar this year. In a client note published on Monday, the German bank said the naira appears undervalued, with attractive interest rates offering investors an opportunity to earn returns from Nigerian assets while benefiting from a stronger currency. However, the bank warned that rising government borrowing could undermine the outlook, particularly as Nigeria approaches an election period. The naira has appreciated by nearly 8 percent against the dollar in 2026, placing it among the top 10 performers out of 144 currencies tracked by Bloomberg, according to the report. The recovery has improved returns for investors holding local currency assets, including government bonds and equities. Why Deutsche bank is backing the Naira The bank\u2019s positive outlook rests largely on Nigeria\u2019s interest-rate advantage and the potential for further currency appreciation. High yields on Nigerian government securities have made the market attractive to foreign portfolio investors seeking returns above those available in many other markets. When these yields exceed inflation, investors can also earn positive real returns, strengthening the appeal of naira-denominated assets. This strategy, known as a carry trade, involves borrowing or investing in currencies with lower interest rates to take advantage of higher returns elsewhere. For investors holding naira assets, however, currency depreciation can wipe out some or all of those gains. Deutsche Bank also pointed to foreign-exchange reserves, investor inflows and improved external balances as factors supporting African frontier currencies. For Nigeria, oil receipts remain important because stronger dollar inflows can improve foreign exchange supply and reduce pressure on the naira. Remittances provide another source of foreign currency. The bank\u2019s inclusion of Nigeria in its frontier FX monitor puts the naira alongside currencies such as Angola\u2019s kwanza and Ghana\u2019s cedi, reflecting renewed attention to investment opportunities in the region. Government borrowing poses a risk Despite its optimism, Deutsche Bank identified Nigeria\u2019s fiscal position as the biggest threat to its currency outlook. The lender pointed to the Debt Management Office\u2019s bond-issuance calendar for the current quarter as a sign of elevated government borrowing. Heavy issuance could push up borrowing costs and absorb liquidity that might otherwise flow into other investments. It could also complicate monetary policy if the Central Bank of Nigeria has to maintain high interest rates to support the naira and contain inflation. The bank also expects government spending to increase ahead of the January elections, raising concerns about the impact of fiscal expansion on the currency. For investors, the concern is whether the government\u2019s financing needs will remain manageable without creating additional pressure on domestic markets or weakening confidence in the naira. Deutsche Bank\u2019s position offers a positive signal for Nigeria\u2019s foreign-exchange market, but it does not guarantee further appreciation. Foreign investors considering Nigerian bonds must weigh the attraction of high yields against the possibility of currency losses. A stronger naira would improve dollar-denominated returns, while a reversal in the exchange rate could quickly reduce them. The outlook will depend on several factors, including oil revenue, foreign exchange liquidity, inflation, monetary policy and the scale of government borrowing. For now, Deutsche Bank sees value in the naira and attractive returns in Nigerian assets. The challenge for the market will be sustaining those gains without allowing government borrowing to erode investor confidence. Related News [Toxic Food Series: Inside food markets where cancer-linked chemicals kill consumers](https://businessday.ng/news/article/toxic-food-series-inside-food-markets-where-cancer-linked-chemicals-kill-consumers/) [Airlines pivot to new funding models as bank loan rates surge](https://businessday.ng/aviation/article/airlines-pivot-to-new-funding-models-as-bank-loan-rates-surge/) [2027 Election: N3.8trn campaign spending cap faces enforcement test](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/2027-election-n3-8trn-campaign-spending-cap-faces-enforcement-test/) Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [FAAC: Lagos, Delta lead as five states corner 33% of N10.96trn H1 allocations](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/faac-lagos-delta-lead-as-five-states-corner-33-of-n10-96trn-h1-allocations/) [Gold rally doubles African reserves as central banks seek dollar alternatives](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/gold-rally-doubles-african-reserves-as-central-banks-seek-dollar-alternatives/) [Naira risks weakening to N4,000/$ if fuel subsidy returns \u2014 Oyedele](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/naira-risks-weakening-to-n4000-if-fuel-subsidy-returns-oyedele/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "65a0e4b82580279f9913c6d44b3e21e386e2a519fc18b8f28939f5bc86f27b6f", "domain": "businessday.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1980", "title": "Gold rally doubles African reserves as central banks seek dollar alternatives", "url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/gold-rally-doubles-african-reserves-as-central-banks-seek-dollar-alternatives", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/gold-rally-doubles-african-reserves-as-central-banks-seek-dollar-alternatives", "text": "Share \u2026.gold reserves rise by 114% in three years African countries are seeing the value of their gold reserves surge as international bullion prices climb, strengthening central banks\u2019 balance sheets and renewing interest in gold as an alternative to dollar-denominated assets amid currency volatility and global economic uncertainty. The combined dollar value of gold reserves, based on available data for eight African countries, increased by 113.5 percent to $66.6 billion in the second quarter of 2026 from $31.2 billion in the same period of 2023, according to a BusinessDay analysis of World Gold Council data. The eight countries tracked are South Africa, Nigeria, Ghana, Mauritius, Algeria, Egypt, Morocco and Kenya. However, the increase in value has not been matched by a comparable rise in the amount of gold held. Physical gold holdings across the eight countries edged up to 509.2 tonnes in Q2 from 505 tonnes three years earlier, suggesting that higher international gold prices accounted for most of the increase in their reserves\u2019 dollar value. Gold averaged about $1,955 per troy ounce in 2023, compared with around $4,166 recently, more than doubling in value. The rally has significantly increased the dollar value of African central banks\u2019 gold reserves, even as the physical quantity held across the eight countries tracked remained broadly stable. Read also: [Ghana\u2019s reserves hit 12-month low despite strong gold exports](https://businessday.ng/africa/article/ghanas-reserves-hit-12-month-low-despite-strong-gold-exports/) The figures show that the gold rally is increasing the value of African central banks\u2019 existing holdings as governments look to diversify reserve assets, strengthen external buffers and reduce exposure to currency and geopolitical risks and improve the value of their currencies. Algeria recorded the largest gold reserves by value at $22.5 billion, followed by Egypt at $16.8 billion and South Africa at $16.3 billion. The combined gold reserves accounted for 20.6 percent of the countries\u2019 reported gross reserves of $322.6 billion in Q2. This compares with the 10 percent in the second quarter of 2023. The rising share underscores gold\u2019s growing financial significance, although the data alone does not show that all eight countries deliberately increased gold purchases or reduced their dollar holdings. Gold has gained prominence among central banks because it is not a liability of another government or financial institution. It can provide diversification within reserve portfolios and serve as a store of value during periods of geopolitical uncertainty. Unlike foreign-currency securities, however, gold does not generate interest income, and its market price can fluctuate substantially. \u201cOver the past several years, central banks have steadily increased their gold holdings, continuing to buy even as bullion reached historic highs,\u201d Alan Goldberg, lead data analyst at BestBrokers, a financial research and comparison platform, said in an email to BusinessDay. \u201cThis suggests that governments are not simply chasing returns, but reassessing the role of gold in a world shaped by geopolitical uncertainty, shifting currency dynamics and concerns over financial resilience,\u201d he said. Gold prices lift reserve values The rally in international gold prices has created a substantial valuation gain for countries holding the metal. Gold prices rose sharply during 2025 and climbed above $5,400 an ounce in January 2026 before retreating to around $4,100 in early October, according to figures from World Gold Council. Although the correction has reduced prices from their peak, gold remains an important focus for central banks and investors amid changing interest-rate expectations, geopolitical tensions and uncertainty in global markets. Read also: [Ethiopia joins African gold-refining push with $50m refinery](https://businessday.ng/africa/article/ethiopia-joins-african-gold-refining-push-with-50m-refinery/) The price increase is particularly significant for countries whose central banks already hold substantial quantities of gold. When bullion prices rise, the dollar value of those holdings increases even if the quantity stored in central-bank vaults remains unchanged. That distinction is important for African economies. A higher valuation can strengthen the reported value of reserve assets and improve the proportion represented by gold, but it does not necessarily mean a central bank has acquired more metal or has additional liquid dollars available to finance imports and service external debt. The World Gold Council has reported sustained demand from central banks globally. Its annual survey found that 89 percent of respondents expected global official gold reserves to increase over the following 12 months, while 45 percent expected their own institutions\u2019 holdings to rise. Meanwhile, 74 percent of respondents expected the US dollar to account for a moderately or significantly smaller share of global reserves over the next five years. Ghana, Tanzania build domestic gold programmes While higher international prices explain much of the increase in the combined value of gold reserves, some African countries have also adopted programmes designed to channel domestic production into reserve accumulation or foreign-exchange earnings. Ghana recorded the fastest increase in the dollar value of gold reserves among the countries tracked, with holdings rising from $634 million in Q2 2023 to $3.65 billion in Q2 this year, an increase of 476 percent. The West African country has increasingly used its gold resources to strengthen external buffers and generate foreign exchange following a period of economic and currency pressures. Thanks to the surge in gold prices, it became Africa\u2019s best performing currency against the US dollar last year. Ghana\u2019s gold-purchasing framework distinguishes between metal acquired from large-scale mines and gold sourced from artisanal production. Under the Ghana Gold Reserve Act Programme, adopted in February, gold acquired from large mines is intended to be added to physical reserves, with sales subject to government and parliamentary approval. Artisanal gold is collected through GoldBod and can be marketed to generate foreign currency for transfer to the Bank of Ghana. This gives gold two distinct functions: strengthening long-term reserve holdings and providing a means of obtaining foreign exchange. Read also: [How Niger State\u2019s gold rush became a death trap for miners](https://businessday.ng/news/article/how-niger-states-gold-rush-became-a-death-trap-for-miners/) Tanzania has also expanded its holdings through a domestic gold-purchasing programme launched in September 2023. The Bank of Tanzania had accumulated 27.5 tonnes by June 2026, according to the supplied data, after a long period in which it did not add to its gold reserves. Uganda launched a pilot programme in April 2026 under which gold is stored at the central bank before being incorporated into official reserves. Zimbabwe has taken a different approach by linking gold to its currency framework. The Zimbabwe Gold currency, introduced in April 2024, is backed by a combination of gold and foreign-exchange reserves. The central bank\u2019s gold holdings increased from 1.5 tonnes when the currency was launched to 4.03 tonnes at the end of 2025. These initiatives show that gold accumulation across Africa is not driven by a single objective. Some countries seek to diversify reserves, others aim to generate foreign exchange from domestic production, while some use gold as part of a broader monetary framework. Nigeria and South Africa among major holders Nigeria\u2019s gold reserves increased in dollar value from $1.31 billion to $2.79 billion, while South Africa\u2019s holdings rose from $7.71 billion to $16.3 billion. Egypt\u2019s gold reserves increased by 117 percent while Algeria\u2019s rose 110 percent. Mauritius\u2019 holdings grew by 111 percent while Morocco\u2019s rose by 110 percent. For Nigeria, gold offers a potential source of diversification in an economy where foreign-exchange earnings have historically depended heavily on crude oil exports. But gold cannot replace the liquid foreign-currency reserves needed to pay for imports, meet external obligations and support foreign-exchange market operations. Kenya\u2019s reported gold reserves fell from $1.8 billion in the second quarter of 2025 to zero in the second quarter of 2026. Gold alone cannot stabilise currencies For African economies facing currency depreciation and imported inflation, a stronger reserve position can help support confidence in their ability to meet external payment obligations. But a rise in gold prices does not automatically strengthen a currency or reduce domestic inflation. Exchange rates also depend on export earnings, capital flows, monetary and fiscal policy, external debt obligations and investor confidence. Gold can provide diversification and a store of value, but it does not generate the same immediate foreign-exchange liquidity as cash and readily marketable foreign-currency securities. Joseph Nnanna, chief economist at the Development Bank of Nigeria, told BusinessDay recently that African countries must build stronger value chains around gold rather than exporting raw resources. \u201cGold, like any other natural resource, can only deliver sustainable value when it is processed along a full value chain,\u201d he said. \u201cBuilding strong value chains is essential for achieving sustainable economic growth.\u201d For African governments, the challenge is therefore twofold: managing gold as a reserve asset while ensuring that domestic mining generates broader economic value through processing, refining, and stronger links with local industry. Related News [Toxic Food Series: Inside food markets where cancer-linked chemicals kill consumers](https://businessday.ng/news/article/toxic-food-series-inside-food-markets-where-cancer-linked-chemicals-kill-consumers/) [Airlines pivot to new funding models as bank loan rates surge](https://businessday.ng/aviation/article/airlines-pivot-to-new-funding-models-as-bank-loan-rates-surge/) [2027 Election: N3.8trn campaign spending cap faces enforcement test](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/2027-election-n3-8trn-campaign-spending-cap-faces-enforcement-test/) [Bunmi Bailey](https://businessday.ng/author/bunmi-bailey/) Bunmi holds a degree in Economics from the University of Lagos and has over eight years of experience in content writing and journalism. Her career spans roles as a financial and business journalist at BusinessDay Media and TechCabal, and as Head of Research at SBM Intelligence, an Africa-focused market intelligence and strategic consulting firm. She also served as Editor at Finance in Africa, a subsidiary of Businessfront and is currently Assistant Editor, Finance (Africa), at BusinessDay. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [South Africa has 50% chance of regaining investment grade by 2028, Investec says](https://businessday.ng/africa/article/south-africa-has-50-chance-of-regaining-investment-grade-by-2028-investec-says/) [Zimbabwean investors have until Wednesday to buy into Dangote refinery IPO](https://businessday.ng/africa/article/zimbabwean-investors-have-until-october-21-to-buy-into-dangote-refinery-ipo/) [Egypt clears $454m Banque du Caire share sale as IPO process advances](https://businessday.ng/africa/article/egypt-clears-454m-banque-du-caire-share-sale-as-ipo-process-advances/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "b599a4043409d4be6a27a8a3ebe1a6e05daa87a0fa7fe8f98eab1fd92c1c900c", "domain": "businessday.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1981", "title": "Rules and Regulations Published: August 14, 2023", "url": "https://sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/regulation/rules-and-regulations", "final_url": "https://sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/regulation/rules-and-regulations", "text": "Rules and Regulations Published: August 14, 2023 In order to effectively and efficiently carry out the objectives of securities regulation as embedded in the Investments and Securities Act, SEC has prescribed these Consolidated Rules and Regulations Rules for public offering of securities by a free trade zone entity (FTZE) Applicability(1) These Rules shall apply to the offering and issuance of securities by a free trade zone entity (FTZE).(2) These rules shall only be applicable to companies or businesses licensed by a Free Trade Zone Authority, and operating within the free zones. (3) Entities licensed by a free trade zone but operating outside the free zone shall not issue securities to the public under these Rules.(4) Securities of an FTZE shall not be issued or offered to the public, without the approval of the Commission in accordance with the provisions of this Rule. [Download Full Document pdf \u2022 208.2 KB](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/1514/Rules_for_Public_Offering_of_Securities_by_A_Free_Trade_Zone_Entity_FTZE.pdf) Rules On Shariah Advisory Services For Non-Interest Capital Market Products And Services Definition of TermsShariah Adviser means an independent professional (individual or corporate), who is a trained Shariah scholar, or in the case of a corporation has a minimum of two (2) sponsored individuals trained as Shariah scholars, registered by the SEC to advise and/or provide certification to a financial institution or entity on Shariah compliance of its products or services provided that the Commission may allow/recognize foreign Shariah Advisors to participate in any kind of transaction.Shariah Scholar means an individual who possess relevant qualifications and experience in Islamic Jurisprudence as set out in these Rules.Scope of Products and ServicesThe products and services which require the services of a Shariah Adviser include all Islamic/Non-Interest Capital Market Products and Services as may be approved by the Commission.Appointment of Shariah Adviser(1) An issuer or fund manager with the consent of the trustee (where applicable) shall appoint a Shariah Adviser to provide Shariah Advisory services for Shariah products, issuances and schemes.(2) A capital market operator seeking to provide Shariah-compliant products and services shall appoint a registered Shariah Adviser for the firm and notify the Commission of such appointment within five (5) business days of the appointment.(3) In the event of replacement or change of existing Shariah adviser, the consent of the trustee shall be obtained prior to filing with the Commission.Downloading full document--> [Rules On Shariah Advisory Services For Non-Interest Capital Market Products And Services pdf \u2022 241.6 KB](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/1506/Rules_on_Sharia_Advisory_Services.pdf) Amendment to Rules on Fund Portfolio Management Operations A. Existing Rules on Fund/Portfolio Management OperationsDefinitions:Within the context of this part of the Rules the following terms shall have the following meaning:a) Discretionary Fund/Portfolio Management means the exercise of any degree of discretion and independence in investment and management of securities, portfolio of securities, pooled funds on behalf of a client, or a group of clients by a Fund/Portfolio Manager in accordance with investment objectives expressed in an agreement.b) Non-Discretionary Fund/Portfolio Management means the investment in and the management of securities, portfolio of securities on behalf of a client or a group of clients in accordance with the direction of the client or group of clients without any exercise of discretion by the Fund/Portfolio Manager under an expressed agreement.c) Retail Investor means an individual not otherwise classified as \u2018High Net worth Investor (HNI) or as \u2018Qualified Institutional Investor (QII) who invests with a registered Capital Market Operator and:More-----> [Download full document pdf \u2022 253.8 KB](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/1505/Amendment_to_Rules_on_Fund_Portfolio_Management_Operations.pdf) Rules On Credit Enhancement Facility Providers & Sundry Amendment To Existing Rules Of The Commission SummaryA. New RulesRules on Credit Enhancement Facility Providers.B. Sundry AmendmentAmendment to Rule 45 on Registrable Functions.Amendment to Parts A and B of Schedule 1 (Registration Fees, Minimum Capital Requirements, Securities and others).[Download Full Document](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/1385/APPROVED_RULES_ON_CREDIT_ENHANCEMENT_FACILITY_PROVIDERS2_g9yubf1.pdf) New Rules And Sundry Amendments To The Rules And Regulations Of The Commission (April 2025) New RuleNew Rules on Issuance and Allotment of Private Companies\u2019 SecuritiesSundry AmendmentsAmendments to Rules on Corporate BondsAmendments to Rule 39- Annual ReportAmendments to Rule 41- Quarterly ReportsAmendments to Part N- Miscellaneous RulesAmendments to Schedule 1- Part D- SEC Fees on Market DealsAmendments to the Rules on Private Equity FundsSundry Amendments to CIS RulesAmendments to Rule 450- New Sub-rule (9) General Rules for CIS[Download Full Document](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/1323/New-Rules-and-sundry-amendments-April-2025.pdf) New Rules And Sundry Amendments To The Rules And Regulations Of The Commission (23 December 2024) New RulesNew Rule on Issuance of Commercial PapersSundry AmendmentAmendment to Rule 8 (Exemptions)Amendment to the Rule on Annual Supervision Fees for Collective Investment Schemes[Download Full Document](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/1322/Executed-Rules-Dec-2024.pdf) Securities And Exchange Commission (Capital Market Operators Anti-Money Laundering And Combating The Financing Of Terrorism) Regulations \u2013 12 May 2022 These regulations, dated May 12, 2022, represent a comprehensive framework aimed at combating money laundering, financing of terrorism and proliferation financing within Nigeria's capital market.The document outlines specific objectives, the scope of application, and the core focus on Anti-Money Laundering, Countering the Financing of Terrorism and Countering Proliferation Financing (AML/CFT/CPF) institutional policies.[Download Full Document](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/7/SEC-AMLCFTCPF-REGULATIONS-12-MAY-2022.pdf) Rules on Issuance, Offering Platforms and Custody of Digital Assets \u2013 11 May 2022 Dated May 11, 2022, these rules introduce a comprehensive framework for the issuance and registration of digital assets within the Nigerian capital market. The document is divided into five parts, each addressing a specific aspect of the digital asset landscape.Part A outlines rules governing the issuance of digital assets as securities, while Part B focuses on the registration requirements for Digital Assets Offering Platforms (DAOPs). Part C elaborates on the registration requirements for Digital Asset Custodians (DACs), and Part D covers rules pertaining to Virtual Asset Service Providers (VASPs). Lastly, Part E provides guidelines for Digital Assets Exchanges (DAX).These rules set the stage for a regulated digital asset ecosystem, fostering transparency and investor protection within Nigeria's capital market.[Download Full Document](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/8/Rules-on-Issuance-Offering-and-Custody-of-Digital-Assets.pdf) Amendments as at December 20, 2021 Dated December 20, 2021, this document comprises major amendments to existing regulations governing Nigeria's capital market. It primarily focuses on adjustments related to registration fees, minimum capital requirements, securities, and other essential facets of the market.These amendments reflect the evolving nature of the market and are critical for market participants and investors to stay in compliance with the latest regulatory requirements.[Download Full Document](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/9/SEC-NG-Approved-Major-Ammendments-to-Rules-Dec-20-2021_V2.pdf) New Rules as at October 29, 2021 (Social Bonds Rule New \u2013 October 29, 2021) Issued on October 29, 2021, these rules introduce guidelines for social bonds, a significant development in the Nigerian capital market.The document outlines the specific requirements and procedures for the issuance and management of social bonds. It addresses important aspects such as the criteria for social bonds, disclosure requirements, and the roles and responsibilities of various market participants in the issuance process.These rules contribute to the growth of sustainable and socially responsible investments within Nigeria's capital market, aligning it with international best practices.[Download Full Document](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/10/Social-Bond-Rule-New-October-29-2021.pdf) New Rules and Amendments as at August 30, 2021 Published on August 30, 2021, this document signifies a significant regulatory update in Nigeria's capital market. It introduces rules and incorporates amendments across various segments of market operations. Among the notable additions are rules on robo-advisory services and trade repositories, which aim to enhance market efficiency and investor protection. Additionally, the document features a substantial amendment related to the fairness review of mergers, takeovers, and acquisitions, emphasizing transparency and fairness in these transactions.The document also addresses several other aspects, making it essential reading for market participants keen on staying updated with the evolving regulatory landscape.[Download Full Document](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/11/New-Rules-and-Amendments-_Executed-30-August-2021.pdf) Amendments as at May 24, 2021 Dated May 24, 2021, this document primarily focuses on amendments to Rule 45, specifically regarding registerable functions.These amendments reflect changes in the regulatory framework aimed at refining and strengthening the registration process for market operators. Market participants need to be aware of these adjustments to ensure compliance with the updated regulations.[Download Full Document](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/12/Executed-Rules-May-24-2021.pdf) Major Amendments as at April 22, 2021 Released on April 22, 2021, this document brings substantial changes to the existing regulatory landscape of Nigeria's capital market. These major amendments are significant for market participants, as they impact various aspects of market operations. Notably, the document includes amendments to Rule 67 concerning sub-brokers and Rule 70 relating to records of transactions with clients. Moreover, a new rule, Rule 76, is introduced, creating additional regulatory provisions.These changes demonstrate the commitment to maintaining the market's integrity and efficiency while keeping pace with evolving market dynamics.[Download Full Document](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/13/Major-Amendments-to-the-SEC-NIGERIA-rules-26-April-2021.pdf) New Rules and Sundry Amendments as at March 22, 2021 Published on March 22, 2021, this document introduces new rules and sundry amendments, covering a broad spectrum of capital market operations. The new rules include provisions for warehousing and collateral management, reflecting evolving market practices. Additionally, there are sundry amendments, particularly related to Rule 19 (reinstating Individual Sub-broker registration), Rule 27 (Fidelity Bond), and Rule 42 (minimum disclosure requirements by public companies on their websites), among others.These changes underscore the continuous effort to adapt the regulatory framework to emerging trends and ensure market transparency and investor protection.[Download Full Document](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/14/Executed-Rules-and-Amendments_22-March-2021.pdf) New Rules and Sundry Amendments as at January 21, 2021 Issued on January 21, 2021, this document presents a significant update to Nigeria's capital market regulations. It introduces new rules and encompasses sundry amendments that address various facets of market operations.The newly introduced rules cover areas such as fund management products, nominee companies, and general rules for collective investment schemes. These additions reflect the evolving nature of the market and enhance the regulatory framework to better serve market participants. Additionally, there are sundry amendments that touch upon several regulations, including those related to trust deeds, collective investment schemes, and disclosure requirements.These changes collectively contribute to ensuring transparency and investor protection within the capital market.[Download Full Document](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/15/Jan-2021-Executed-Rules.pdf) Sundry Amendments as at September 10, 2020 Dated September 10, 2020, this document comprises sundry amendments that impact various aspects of Nigeria's capital market regulations. The amendments cover a range of rules, including those concerning legal practitioners, removal from listing, additional requirements for sukuk programs, and registration requirements for incidental functions, to name a few.These amendments reflect the authorities' ongoing efforts to refine and strengthen the regulatory framework, aligning it with market dynamics and international best practices. Market participants should closely review these changes to ensure compliance with the updated regulations.[Download Full Document](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/16/New-Rules-and-Sundry-Amendments-as-at-10-September-2020.pdf) New Rules and Sundry Amendments as at December 23, 2019 Released on December 23, 2019, this document represents a significant update to Nigeria's capital market regulations. It introduces new rules and incorporates sundry amendments across various aspects of market operations.The newly introduced rules cover areas such as the regulation of derivatives trading, central counter parties (CCP), and the registration of fixed income existing securities. These additions aim to enhance market efficiency, risk management, and transparency. Additionally, major amendments are made to rules concerning collective investment schemes. Sundry amendments further refine the regulatory framework, touching upon aspects like fidelity bonds, disclosure requirements for public companies, and shelf registration.These changes collectively contribute to ensuring a robust and adaptable regulatory environment for the capital market.[Download Full Document](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/17/SEC-New-Rules-and-Ammendments-23-December-2019.pdf) New Rules and Sundry Amendments as at August 2019 Dated 21st of August 2019This document brings forward rules on specialised funds/schemes and a host of other amendments.[Download full document](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/1352/New_Rules_and_Sundry_Amendments_August_21_2019.pdf) New Rules and Sundry Amendments as at October 14, 2019 Published on October 14, 2019, this document introduces new rules and incorporates sundry amendments, reflecting the commitment to enhancing Nigeria's capital market regulations.The new rules encompass specialized funds/schemes, registration requirements for commodity brokers, and the regulation of capital market holding companies (CMHCs). These additions cater to evolving market dynamics and emerging market players. Additionally, sundry amendments touch on various aspects of capital market operations, further refining the regulatory framework.These amendments include changes to rules on sponsored individuals, minimum paid-up capital, and reporting requirements for different market participants. Market stakeholders should stay informed about these changes to ensure compliance with the updated regulations.[Download Full Document](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/18/Rules-For-Upload-October-2019_final.pdf) New rules and amendments as at 12 October 2018 Dated October 12, 2018, this document brings forward new rules and amendments that have a significant impact on Nigeria's capital market regulations.The introduction of rules on green bonds stands out as a notable addition, aligning the market with sustainable finance initiatives. Additionally, sundry amendments include the inclusion of the Bank Verification Number (BVN) as a valid means of individual client identification in the capital market, a measure to enhance security and traceability. Furthermore, there's a rule on investment advisory services, demonstrating the market's commitment to investor protection and quality advisory practices.These changes reflect a dynamic and evolving capital market landscape, ensuring alignment with international standards and best practices.[Download Full Document](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/19/12-October-2018-SEC-NG-Approved-Rules.pdf) New rules and amendments as at 22 November 2017 \u2013 BATCH B Released on November 22, 2017, this document presents a set of new rules and amendments in two distinct batches. In this BATCH B release, a new rule focusing on the regulation of revenue bonds is introduced, indicating a diversification of investment options within the capital market. Additionally, sundry amendments cover various areas, including dividend/return money warrants, the code of conduct for capital market operators and their employees, and adjustments to rules concerning inter-dealer brokers and mergers and takeovers.These changes collectively serve to refine and strengthen the regulatory framework, fostering transparency and investor confidence.[Download Full Document](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/20/SEC-APPROVED-RULES-NOV-2017_BATCH-B.pdf) New rules and amendments as at 22 November 2017 \u2013 BATCH A Published on November 22, 2017, in BATCH A of this document, significant updates to Nigeria's capital market regulations are introduced.This includes the introduction of the Asset Manager Code of Professional Conduct, emphasizing ethical practices in the market. Furthermore, changes in primary issuance fees for both equity and fixed income instruments aim to enhance market efficiency. Notably, the waiver of the naira value cap on transaction fees payable to various professionals' underscores efforts to streamline market operations. Sundry amendments cover a range of topics, including registration requirements for capital market operators, minimum paid-up capital definitions, and reporting obligations.These changes reflect the commitment to improving market transparency, efficiency, and investor protection.[Download Full Document](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/21/RULES-JULY-2017-final-for-execution-11-10-2017.pdf) New Rules and Amendments as at June 2017 Dated June 2017, this document introduces new rules and amendments that are pivotal in shaping Nigeria's capital market regulations.The rules on the return of unclaimed dividends to paying companies represent a crucial step in investor protection and asset retrieval. Additionally, the inclusion of the Independent National Electoral Commission (INEC) voter's registration card as a valid means of identification for individual clients is a significant step towards ensuring efficient and secure market operations. The adoption of a three-tiered Know-Your-Customer (KYC) framework further enhances market integrity and transparency. Furthermore, rules governing fund and portfolio management operations contribute to the efficient operation of collective investment schemes. Major amendments include changes to the National Investor Protection Fund (NIPF) rules and rules on real estate investment schemes, fostering investor confidence.[Download Full Document](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/22/June-2017-SEC-Executed-Rules-Regulations.pdf) Consolidated Rules 2013 Dated June 2013[Download full document](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/1347/SEC_Consolidated_JUNE2013_SIGNEDWEBSITE_1.pdf) Additional Rules and Regulation Documents in the Repository Below is a comprehensive collection of early documents that form the backbone of regulations and rules governing Nigeria's capital market.These documents serve as the guiding principles for market participants, ensuring transparency, fairness, and investor protection. While some documents introduce new rules and amendments to keep the market aligned with global standards, others focus on specific financial instruments and market practices:[Sundry Amendments to SEC Rules and Regulations - April 13, 2015](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/23/AMENDMENTS_17415.pdf): Explore amendments that refine and enhance existing market regulations.[Rules for Demutualization of Securities Exchanges in Nigeria - April 27, 2015](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/24/Rules-on-Demutualization-of-Securities-Exchanges-in-Nigeria-April-27-2015.pdf): Learn about the rules governing the transformation of securities exchanges in Nigeria.[Rules on Securitization - April 13, 2015](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/25/RULES_ON_SECURITIZATION_17415.pdf)[:](https://chat.openai.com/documents) Dive into the regulations surrounding securitization, a critical financial practice in the capital market.[Rules on Trading in Unlisted Securities - April 13, 2015](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/26/RULES_ON_TRADING_IN_UNLISTED_SECURITIES_17415.pdf): Discover how unlisted securities are traded within the Nigerian capital market.[Rules Relating to the Complaints Management Framework of the Nigerian Capital Market - February 12, 2015](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/27/Rules_on_Complaints_Mgt_Framework_of_NCM_February_12_2015.pdf)[:](https://chat.openai.com/documents) Gain insights into the framework for addressing complaints in the capital market.[Rules on Infrastructure Funds, Foreign Collective Investment Schemes and Sundry Amendments - May 12, 2014](https://sec.gov.ng/documents/28/RULES_ON_INFRASTRUCTURE_FUNDS_and_OTHERS__2014.pdf)[:](https://chat.openai.com/documents) Explore rules related to infrastructure funds, foreign collective investment schemes, and various amendments that shape the market's landscape.These documents collectively define the regulatory landscape that governs Nigeria's capital market, ensuring its integrity, efficiency, and investor confidence. Feel free to access any of these documents to stay informed about the latest market regulations and practices. [SEC Rules For FinTechs Navigate regulatory standards for fintech firms operating in Nigeria\u2019s capital market; uncover li](https://sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/regulation/rules-and-regulations/sec-rules-for-fintechs/) [SEC Rules Related to Bonds Delve into guidelines governing bond issuance and trading; learn about compliance steps, disc](https://sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/regulation/rules-and-regulations/sec-rules-related-to-bonds/)", "content_hash": "4cf059e43fb311938de33850762f11ddf76a3dbcc4fc77234ef17730d1bb107b", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1982", "title": "Insights For Businesses", "url": "https://sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/development/business-insights", "final_url": "https://sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/development/business-insights", "text": "Insights For Businesses From budding entrepreneurs to seasoned business leaders, the SEC's comprehensive insights are tailored to every stage of your business journey. Discover essential guidance for starting, growing, and merging businesses, empowering you to navigate the dynamic capital market with confidence. blocks section [Frequently Asked Questions](https://sec.gov.ng/about/resources/frequently-asked-questions/) [Glossary of Capital Market Terms](https://sec.gov.ng/glossary-of-capital-market-terms/) [Webinars](https://sec.gov.ng/events/webinars/) [Cryptocurrency and Crypto-Assets](https://sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/development/business-insights/sec-policies-on-cryptocurrency-and-crypto-assets/) [Cryptocurrency and Crypto-Assets](https://sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/development/business-insights/sec-policies-on-cryptocurrency-and-crypto-assets/) [Getting Listed in Exchanges](https://sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/development/business-insights/guides-on-getting-listed-in-exchanges/) [Getting Listed in Exchanges](https://sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/development/business-insights/guides-on-getting-listed-in-exchanges/) [On Business Start-Up](https://sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/development/business-insights/on-business-start-up/) [On Business Start-Up](https://sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/development/business-insights/on-business-start-up/) [On Company Registrations](https://sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/development/business-insights/on-company-registrations/) [On Company Registrations](https://sec.gov.ng/our-mandate/development/business-insights/on-company-registrations/)", "content_hash": "b5b038fde845416e2ff168f3b7170dad83ccadef767086f7b2608af80a7421e3", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1983", "title": "Enforcements", "url": "https://sec.gov.ng/enforcements", "final_url": "https://sec.gov.ng/enforcements", "text": "Enforcements Access comprehensive information on enforcement actions taken by the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) to maintain market integrity and protect investors. blocks section [Cos. Facing Enforcement Action](https://sec.gov.ng/enforcements/companies-facing-enforcement-action/) [Cos. Facing Enforcement Action](https://sec.gov.ng/enforcements/companies-facing-enforcement-action/) [Referred Cases](https://sec.gov.ng/enforcements/referred-cases/) [Referred Cases](https://sec.gov.ng/enforcements/referred-cases/) [A.P.C. Matters](https://sec.gov.ng/enforcements/apc-matters/) [A.P.C. Matters](https://sec.gov.ng/enforcements/apc-matters/) [Litigation](https://sec.gov.ng/enforcements/litigation/) [Litigation](https://sec.gov.ng/enforcements/litigation/) [Keep Track of Enforcement Updates](https://sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/) [Keep Track of Enforcement Updates](https://sec.gov.ng/enforcements/keep-track-of-enforcement-updates/) [Information for Harmed Investors](https://sec.gov.ng/enforcements/information-for-harmed-investors/) [Information for Harmed Investors](https://sec.gov.ng/enforcements/information-for-harmed-investors/)", "content_hash": "4c18c889331c040a761d21b2be283b2303bb2cea1e6719d78b3651f432b092b6", "domain": "sec.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1984", "title": "NAFDAC Organisation \u2013 NAFDAC", "url": "https://nafdac.gov.ng/about-nafdac/nafdac-organisation", "final_url": "https://nafdac.gov.ng/about-nafdac/nafdac-organisation", "text": "NAFDAC Organisation Learn about NAFDAC, its mission, history, how it\u2019s organized, and more. The National Agency for Food and Drug Administration and Control (NAFDAC) was established by Decree No. 15 of 1993 as amended by Decree No. 19 of 1999 and now the National Agency for Food and Drug Administration and Control Act Cap N1 Laws of the Federation of Nigeria (LFN) 2004 to regulate and control the manufacture, importation, exportation, distribution, advertisement, sale and use of Food, Drugs, Cosmetics, Medical Devices, Packaged Water, Chemicals and Detergents (collectively known as regulated products). The agency was officially established in October 1992.Vision To be a World Class Regulator that ensures availability of quality and safe Food, Drugs, and other Regulated Products.MissionTo protect and promote the public health by instituting an effective and efficient regulatory system that ensures only the right quality Food, Drugs and other regulated products are manufactured, exported, imported, advertised, distributed, sold, and used.Core ValuesProfessionalism.Resilience.Integrity.Dedication and Commitment.Excellence.FUNCTIONS OF NAFDACThe functions of NAFDAC as provided in the enabling law that establishes it are to:Regulate and control the importation, exportation, manufacture, advertisement, distribution, sale and use of food, drugs, cosmetics, medical devices, bottled water, Chemicals and detergents (Regulated Products);Conduct appropriate tests and ensure compliance with standard specifications designated and approved by the Council for effective control of quality of regulated products and their raw materials as well as their production processes in factories and other establishments;Undertake appropriate investigation into the production premises and raw materials for regulated products and establish relevant quality assurance systems, including certification of the production sites and of the regulated products;Undertake inspection of imported regulated products and establish relevant quality assurance systems, including certification of the production sites.Compile standard specifications regulations and guidelines for the production, importation, exportation, sales, distribution and registration of regulated products;Undertake the registration of foods, drugs, cosmetics, medical devices bottled water, Chemicals and detergents;Control the exportation and issue quality certification of products intended for export;Establish and maintain relevant laboratories or other institutions in strategic areas of Nigeria as may be necessary for the performance of its functions;Pronounce on the quality and safety of Products after appropriate analysis;Take measures to ensure that the use of narcotic drugs and psychotropic substances are limited to only medical and scientific purposes;Grant authorization for the import and export of narcotic drugs and psychotropic substances as well as other controlled substances;Collaborate with National Drug Law Enforcement Agency (NDLEA) in the bid to eradicate drug abuse in Nigeria;Advice Governments, the private sector and other interested bodies regarding the quality, safety and regulatory provisions on regulated products;Issue guidelines, grant approvals and monitor the advertisement of food, drugs, cosmetics, medical devices, bottled water, Chemicals and detergents;Compile and publish relevant data resulting from the performance of the functions of the Agency or from other sources;Sponsor such national and International conferences as may be considered appropriate;Liaise with relevant establishments within and outside Nigeria in pursuance of its functions and;Carry out such activities as are necessary or expedient for the performance of its functions. FundingTo enable the agency meet her mandate, funding of her activities, including this website, is majorly drawn from the Federal Government of Nigeria. Other internally generated funds in user fees and other external sources like donor agencies also provide funds.The NAFDAC\u2019s organization consists of the Director General\u2019s Office and twenty two (22) Directorates executing the functions of the Agency: The Agency also has six (6) [Zonal Offices](https://nafdac.gov.ng/about-nafdac/nafdac-organisation/zonal-offices/) as well as FCT and Lagos State Office, all headed by Directors. There are also 9 units under the DG\u2019s Office which are coordinated by the SA-DG Lagos and SA-DG Abuja, and the PA-DG. [See Organizational Chart](https://nafdac.gov.ng/wp-content/uploads/Files/Resources/NAFDAC-Organogram-Signed_11_2024.pdf) Director General\u2019 Office Leadership of the Agency\u2019s overall functions which include administration and control of regulated products is directly under the Director General, Prof Moji Christianah Adeyeye. There are Units or Programs under the Director General\u2019s Office, namely Public Affairs, NAFDAC Training and Research Institute (NT&RI), Procurement, Internal Audit, One Stop Investment Centre (OSIC), Reforms, Quality Management System (QMS), WHO-Global Benchmarking Program and Health Safety and Environment (HSE) Units. [About the Director General\u2019s Office](https://nafdac.gov.ng/about-nafdac/nafdac-organisation/office-of-the-director-general/) [See Director General's Page](https://nafdac.gov.ng/about-nafdac/director-generals-page/) Navigation [NAFDAC Governing Council](https://nafdac.gov.ng/about-nafdac/nafdac-organisation/nafdac-governing-council/) [Advisory Committees](https://nafdac.gov.ng/about-nafdac/nafdac-organisation/advisory-committees/) [NAFDAC Mandate](https://nafdac.gov.ng/about-nafdac/nafdac-organisation/nafdac-functions/) [Director General's Office](https://nafdac.gov.ng/about-nafdac/nafdac-organisation/office-of-the-director-general/) [NAFDAC Directorates](https://nafdac.gov.ng/about-nafdac/nafdac-organisation/directorates/) [NAFDAC Zonal Offices](https://nafdac.gov.ng/about-nafdac/nafdac-organisation/zonal-offices/) Was this helpful? Yes No Submit Cancel Thanks for your feedback!", "content_hash": "fe4fef988930ff6d72ef681f26842f18e7554b8a16e55af51c99af01a5b2f418", "domain": "nafdac.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1985", "title": "Questions, answered", "url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/faq", "final_url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/faq", "text": "Frequently Asked Questions Support Questions, answered Access frequently asked questions and reliable guidance on NDIC's mandate, operations, and the Deposit Insurance System in Nigeria. All Deposit Insurance Your / Beneficiary Insurance Insurance Deposits Pass-Through Insurance What Is Deposit Insurance? Deposit Insurance is a system established by government to protect depositors against the loss of their insured deposits placed with insured financial institutions in the event that a member institution is unable to meet its obligations to depositors. Deposit insurance ensures that the depositor does not lose all his/her money in the event of a bank failure. It also engenders public confidence in, and promotes the stability of, the banking system by assuring savers of the safety of their funds. Deposit insurance makes bank failure an isolated event, hence it eliminates the danger that unfounded rumours will start a contagious bank run. Why is Deposit Insurance necessary? Deposit-taking financial institutions differ from industrial and commercial enterprises in that they depend mainly on deposits mobilised from the public for their working capital and are highly leveraged. If a financial institution is unable to meet its obligation to depositors due to operational problems or business failure, anxious depositors may cause a run on the bank as well as other healthy institutions. The stability of the financial system and social order in general would also be at risk. Moreover, most depositors have small deposit amounts and therefore cannot cost-effectively collect and analyse information on the financial institutions they do business with. The government has therefore established a deposit insurance mechanism, under which the NDIC is empowered to provide protection for small depositors and contribute to financial and social order. How does deposit insurance maintain financial system stability? Deposit-taking financial institutions play an important role in regulating the supply and demand of capital and promoting economic development. They accept deposits, which are a highly liquid form of debt, yet most of their assets are tied up in long-term illiquid forms. Deposit-taking financial institutions therefore have a hard time realising their assets for cash when their business runs into problems, so depositors may lose confidence, triggering a bank run. The limited liquidity of deposit-taking financial institutions also encourages a perception among depositors that making an early withdrawal is the only way to get their money back. This fear can exacerbate a bank run and also have a chain reaction that leads to runs on other banks as well. Hence, the Deposit Insurance System (DIS) is usually established to prevent this by providing assurance of deposit repayment to the great majority of depositors. In doing so, the system also prevents systemic risk and ensures the stability of the financial system. Who administers the Deposit Insurance System in Nigeria? The NDIC is the agency empowered to administer the DIS in Nigeria, thereby protecting depositors of deposit-taking financial institutions. The NDIC provides incentives for sound risk management in the Nigerian banking system as well as contributes to the stability of the financial system. The NDIC manages five Insurance Funds: Deposit Insurance Fund (DIF) \u2014 for DMBs Special Insured Institutions Fund (SIIF) \u2014 for MFBs and PMBs Non-Interest Deposit Insurance Fund (NIDIF) \u2014 for Non-Interest Banks Non-Interest Special Insured Institutions Fund (NISIF) Payment Service Banks Insurance Fund (PSBIF) Is Deposit Insurance the same as Conventional Insurance? No. Deposit insurance is different from conventional insurance in several respects: Purpose: Deposit insurance is a regulatory tool aimed at ensuring the safety, soundness and stability of a nation's financial system, thereby protecting the macro-economy at large. Conventional insurance is designed only to protect the micro-interest of the policyholder. Structure: Deposit insurance is a tripartite arrangement involving the deposit insurer, the participating institutions and the depositors. Conventional insurance is a bilateral agreement between the insurance company and the insured (policyholder). Premium payment: Under deposit insurance, the participating institution pays the premium while the direct beneficiary is the depositor who pays nothing. In conventional insurance, the beneficiary (the insured) pays the premium. Participation: Best practice dictates that participation in deposit insurance should be compulsory. Participation in conventional insurance is generally voluntary. Coverage: Best practice prescribes that deposit insurance coverage should be limited. Conventional insurance coverage may be full. Who are the Insured Institutions under the Deposit Insurance System in Nigeria? Insured institutions are all deposit-taking financial institutions licensed by the Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN): a) Deposit Money Banks (DMBs) b) Microfinance Banks (MFBs) c) Primary Mortgage Banks (PMBs) d) Non-Interest Banks (NIBs) e) Payment Service Banks (PSBs) Membership is compulsory as provided under the NDIC Act No 33 of 2023. What is Pass-Through-Deposit Insurance (PTDI)? It is an arrangement where the deposit insurer extends deposit insurance coverage to Pool accounts or Trust accounts domiciled in deposit-taking financial institutions and operated by Mobile Money Operators (MMOs). Pool accounts and Trust accounts have many contributors to the funds. Rather than insure the pool account up to the maximum as provided by law, the balance of each contributor in that pool account is insured. The NDIC will provide deposit insurance coverage to subscribers of MMOs using the pass-through deposit insurance concept. What are the Eligibility Criteria for Pass-Through-Deposit Insurance Cover? The conditions for eligibility are as follows: i. The relationship between the MMOs and their subscribers shall be based on a Bare Trust arrangement. ii. MMOs must take Fidelity Bond Insurance. iii. The records of the Trust (pool) account must clearly indicate that the funds belong to individual subscribers and not the agent or custodian. iv. The identities of the subscribers must fulfil all KYC requirements specified by the CBN. v. The interests of the subscribers must be disclosed properly in records maintained by the insured institutions, MMOs and Agents. What is the Maximum Cover for Subscribers under the Pass-Through-Deposit Insurance Scheme? The subscribers of MMOs will be insured up to the maximum coverage level of \u20a65,000,000 (Five Million Naira) per subscriber per DMB, or the applicable coverage level for depositors in line with the NDIC Act. Are MMOs Covered under Pass-Through-Deposit Insurance (PTDI)? No. MMOs are not covered under the pass-through deposit insurance, but their corporate account in the bank is covered up to the maximum insured amount. Need more help? Contact NDIC support for depositor assistance and guidance. [Contact NDIC](https://ndic.gov.ng/contact)", "content_hash": "e45d5091f0438fad64c8655edac00a0917856c61799e15daaf451d499e45ace1", "domain": "ndic.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1986", "title": "Frequently Asked Questions", "url": "https://son.gov.ng/faqs", "final_url": "https://son.gov.ng/faqs", "text": "What is the duration of the registration processes?Six weeks from payment to the issuance of Certificate What is Life Endangering Products?The is a product that can have adverse effect on human life if they are not of good quality e.g Electric cables, LPG Cylinders, Electric bulbs, Engine oil, Tyres etc Toggle TitleToggle Content Do I have to come to SON Lekki office to make enquiries or register my products?We have desk offices in ASPAMDA, Alaba, Computer Village, Ikeja/Aluminium village, Dopemu in Lagos and in every state office of SON nationwide. Is Product Registration Certificate same as Product Certificate?No, it is not the same. Product Certificate is associated with SONCAP Certificate, while Product Registration Certificate is issued after the completion of product registration processes How do I know when my certificate is ready?SON will contact you What is the validity period for the certificate?One Year What are the documents required before you can register your products?\u00bb a. CAC Registration \u00bb b. Trademark Certificate \u00bb c. Letter of Authority to distribute the products in Nigeria \u00bb d. Application Form. \u00bb e. Laboratory Test Result \u00bb f. Evidence of payment (TSA receipt) Are the stamps applicable to petroleum/lubricant industries?Yes, it applies to all finished and packaged petroleum and lubricant products for sales. For import of photocopying papers of five rims/carton. Are stamps put on carton or rim?Stamps shall be affixed on each rims. Fees: N3/stamp Turnaround time: 3 days upon approval of request and payment. How do we handle imported products that have no SONCAP certificate?The test report used to clear the goods at the point of entry will form the basis of issuance of PAM stamps and the importer will be required to submit the test report, packing list and receipt of payment of the requisite penalties for goods that are subject to destination inspection In case stamps are not issued on time due to technicalities, how will SON ensure that there is no delay at the port of entry?Stamps don\u2019t form clearance trail, it is after port clearance that stamps should be affixed. However, application for PAM stamps must be submitted before SON final clearance. Do raw materials require PAM stickers?No, stickers are applied to items that will sell at a retail outlet. In case stamps are not issued on time due to technicalities, how will SON ensure that there is no delay at the port of entry?Stamps don\u2019t form clearance trail, it is after port clearance that stamps should be affixed. However, application for PAM stamps must be submitted before SON final clearance Where will stamps be applied, at the warehouse or port of entry?Affixing of PAM stamps should be after clearance but before offer for sale. The fixing should be at the warehouse. Do bulk imports of fertilizer and cement require PAM stamps?Yes, re-packaged fertilizer and cement by importer into small bags for sale will require the use of MANCAP PAM stamps. What is product Authentication mark (PAM)?This is a mark of quality that is affixed on all products to demonstrate their conformity to approved standards. When is PAM issued?The mark is issued upon application by the manufacturer or importer having paid the application fee. What is the application fee for PAM?The cost of each sticker is N3.00 How is the mark issued?The marks are issued as stickers that are specific to each product. These stickers bear security features plus a QR Code that a smart phone is able to scan and are to be issued in the same quantities commensurate to the production units. How is the mark applied?The mark/sticker is applied on each and every product to ensure traceability and tracking and shall not be defaced in any way to ensure legibility and conspicuousness. They may be applied by hand or using suitable applicators. What happens to products without PAM and are still in the market?Moratorium period will be given to them. What happens to products that do not have the PAM stamps at point of sales after moratorium period?Goods that were imported with valid SONCAP Certificate prior to commencement of the programme can still apply for the PAM stamps. Why are manufacturers who are members of Manufacturers Association of Nigeria issued lower service charge for the importation of some articles?SON and the Manufacturers Association of Nigeria (MAN) have signed a Memorandum of Understanding (MOU) to promote the economic and industrial growth of local industries. Thus, local manufacturers have been given special service charges when processing the SONCAP Import Permit to import raw materials, packaging materials, and production machinery. Why am I being denied SONCAP Import Permit for my commercial imports?The Import Permit is only issued to exempt certain categories of imports of regulated products from offshore certification. Such categories include imported Industrial Machinery, project-specific items, Humanitarian/Charitable items, and other non-commercial products. All other products must be certified as meeting the requirements of application standards; thus, all other products must process PC What next now that I have agreed to conform to the SONCAP scheme? Register and activate FIRS TIN on the national trade portal [trade.gov.ng](http://www.trade.gov.ng) Process the PC through any of the IAF Apply for CBN FormM and attach the PC Process the SC through the chosen IAF Apply for NCS PAAR and attach the SC Process NCS SGD Inform the SON Port and Border Operations to process the clearance of the consignment out of the ports. Do I have to repeat the same process each time I want to import?Yes, as the SONCAP certificate is consignment specific What are the gains of the SONCAP scheme?Some of the gains of the SONCAP program include, but are not limited to, the following: Ensuring that regulated products comply with the NIS or approved international standards prior to export to Nigeria. Providing a level playing field in terms of quality for both Imported and locally manufactured products to prevent unfair trade practices and unfair competition with substandard. Easing trade in Nigeria by preventing delays in the clearance of Imported products at Nigerian Ports and Borders, and all Customs Entry Points and Terminals where consignments are being processed for clearance Easing trade in Nigeria by fast-tracking clearance via pre-shipment document submission, Inspections, and pre-consignment arrival clearance document processing. Preventing the dumping of substandard goods in the Nigerian market and in turn, preventing economic loss to the genuine importers and the nation at large. Forestalling any adverse reaction (such as environmental pollution occasioned by the destruction of substandard products) by regulatory Agencies on the arrival of such goods in Nigeria. Encouraging genuine investors and investment in Nigeria. Promoting MANCAP-certified Made in Nigeria products by ensuring conformity to Standards of imported products. Conserving foreign exchange by regulating the importation of regulated products. I have a valid Import Permit for processing FormM, why is my PAAR Certificate activation request being rejected?PAAR activation can be rejected for a combination of reasons, namely: HS code and number of items approved for the PC differ from that now being declared during PAAR activation The number of items declared during PAAR activation is more than the number approved on the PC The value of the articles declared during PAAR activation is more than the value declared and approved on the PC The required document(s) were not attached, or not in the approved format. FormM Final Invoice Bill Lading NSA EUC Other approvals. Can I change the contact email or phone number associated with my account on SONCAP portal?No, you cannot Must I have a TIN before I can start the SONCAP application process?Yes, you must I want to use my Import Permit for FormM to process PAAR Certificate, but I cannot trace it on the portalThe Import Permit for processing FormM has a validity of 12 months, after which it becomes invalid. This is a control measure as a PFI validity is usually no longer than three months after which the value therein is no longer valid, thus, an IMP validity is to correlate with the PFI validity. For cautionary reasons, every FormM IMP should have been utilized to process CBN FormM within six months after the issuance. I can't access my email what do I do?Before creating an account as an importer on the SONCAP App, it is advisable to register with a valid and accessible email address and phone number. SON cannot assist any client who may have lost access to the registered email. The solution is to recover the registered email address or to create another account using another email address. Can I be exempted from paying SONCAP Import Permit service charges?Exemptions are granted only under special conditions for relief items imported by International NGOs, and charity organisations and for personal articles imported by diplomatic missions. However, the Standards Organisation of Nigeria is obligated to ensure that items being imported are safe for use and fit for purpose. I'm bringing in items donated to charity, do I need SONCAP Certificate?Every article coming to Nigeria, even donated items, must possess evidence of conformity to applicable requirements. This is provided for by using the SONCAP Certificate to ensure that the item of import complies with the Federal Government regulations guiding the importation of products. For donated items, the NGO may apply for SONCAP Import Permit. The application shall be processed in line with the guideline for processing the importation of donated non-commercial articles. Can I change the TIN on my registered account?No, you can\u2019t A certificate issued on a TIN is not transferable Can I change the TIN on an activated certificate?No, you can\u2019t I am importing on behalf on a government organisation do I still pay?Importation made directly by the FG is entitled to concession. However, all importation made using the name of a contractor or private entities shall be processed in line with the procedure for processing SONCAP Import Permit without concession. Is it possible to process consignment clearance without having TIN?No, the TIN is the basis for identifying importers during the importation and clearance of goods in Nigeria Why three working days before approval/rejection?This is the maximum turnaround time approved to process these items in line with the implementation of SON\u2019s ISO 9001:2015 Quality Management System and the Ease of Doing Business in Nigeria. Is SONCAP Import Permit service charge one-off payment, or do I have to pay all over again whenever I importPayment for SONCAP Import Permit is a one-off. What is the lifespan of the certificateThe lifespan of a certificate is a maximum of 12 months after which the payment is deemed utilized and the certificate no longer valid. How do I process an import permit?To process SONCAP Import Permit, an applicant must register as an Importer on the SONCAP Portal through the link [https://soncap.son.gov.ng/](https://soncap.son.gov.ng/) How do I update my TIN details?Through liaison with the Federal Inland Revenue Service (FIRS). The FIRS is responsible for the issuance and management of the TIN. How do I print my import permit or PAAR certificate from the dashboard By browsing through the relevant sections of my experience after logging into the SONCAP portal. The certificate can also be viewed and downloaded after it has been successfully attached to the FormM or PAAR on the trade portal. What is the PC?The product certificate (PC) is the first certificate issued during the SONCAP certification process, which represents the conformities of products to quality requirements. It is used in the opening of the CBN Form M, or other CBN documents for processing importation, to enable importation into Nigeria. The PC is issued to the exporter, as only his details are stated on the certificate. What is the SC?The SONCAP Certificate (SC) is used to process the PAAR for the clearance of goods from any Nigerian port. The SC is issued to an importer, whose details are stated on the SONCAP certificate. What is the meaning of HS code?The Harmonised Commodity Description and Coding System, also known as the Harmonised System (HS) of tariff nomenclature is an internationally standardized system of names and numbers to classify traded products. The Harmonized System is used by the Standards Organisation of Nigeria as a mechanism for the identification, modification, and implementation of regulatory control over all products. The Nigeria Customs Service also uses it to implement duties and taxes on products, and for gathering statistics. Who are the IAFsThe IAFs are the Independent Accredited Firms (IAFs) engaged to conduct offshore Conformity Assessments, on behalf of Standards Organisations of Nigeria, to address the challenges of the incessant influx of substandard and unsafe products into the country. They also issue test reports, Product and SONCAP certificates from accredited laboratories. There are eight (8) Independent Accredited Firms (IAFs) managing the SONCAP offshore process. The names of the IAFs are listed below: Africa Standards and Certifications. Alberk QA Technic. Bureau Veritas. China Certification and Inspection Group. China Hanson Inspection and Certification Co, LTD. China Standards Inspection Co, LTD. COTECNA Verification Services, Nigeria LTD. Intersip Quality Solutions LTD. How long does it take to process SONCAP Product certificate?PCs are generated within 96 Hours considering all time zones. However, PCs can be generated within 24 Hours subject to the following fulfillments: All Qualifying documents and Test Reports are submitted within a reasonable time frame. All products comply with applicable standard requirements. How much does it cost to apply for SONCAP Product certificate? Product Certificate 1 (Unregistered Status) = $500 Product Certificate 2 (Registered Status) = $1000 Product Certificate 3 (Licensed Status) = $2,000 New Product = $350 Certificate amendment fee = $100 Non-conformity Report = $350.00 Additional model to be included in the same certificate, and covered in the same test report, is charged at an additional$10.00 per month of validity per model. PC1 = $60 PC2 = $120 PC3 = $120 How much does it cost to apply for SONCAP certificate?$350.00 per certificate How long does it take to process SONCAP certificate?SCs are generated within 96 Hours considering all time zones. However, SCs can be generated within 24 Hours subject to the following fulfillments: All Qualifying documents and Test Reports are submitted within a reasonable time frame. All products comply with applicable standard requirements. Is it possible to use one SONCAP Certificate to clear multiple consignments?No. SONCAP Certificate is only limited to processing a single PAAR consignment and issued to only one entity Can one apply for SONCAP certificate after the consignment has left the country of origin? What is the penalty of not conforming to the SONCAP scheme prior to my consignment arriving in the country?No. The SONCAP is an offshore and pre-shipment certification scheme, thus, cannot be processed after the consignment has left the country of origin. What is applicable at this stage is the SONCAP Default, which shall be charged at 20% CIF of the consignment or 2 million Naira, whichever is higher What is the validity period of PC and SC? Products Certificate (PC1) \u2013 Unregistered /Unlicensed (Conformity verification) \u2013 valid for six months and for single use. Product Certificate (2) (Registered Status) \u2013 valid for 12 Months (One Year) multiple use. Product Certificate (PC3) (Licensed Status) \u2013 valid for 12 months (one year) multiple use. Is it possible to process SONCAP certificates without a FIRS issues Tax Identification Number?No. One of the key requirements for processing SONCAP and Import Permits is the FIRS Tax Identification Number (TIN). This TIN must be registered for trade on the trade portal before it can be used to process any of SON\u2019s clearance certificate What products are exempted from the SONCAP scheme?The following items are exempted from the SONCAP Offshore certification scheme: Food products. Medicines Medicals, other than equipment and machines. Chemicals, used as raw materials by bonafide manufacturers, to produce food products and medicines (note that some chemicals whose usage also extend to other industrial purposes aside from Food and medicines are regulated by SON) Goods clarified as contraband by the Federal Government of Nigeria. Used products, other than automobiles (refurbished and re-engineered commercial article must process Import Permit) The exemption of some products from the SONCAP Offshore scheme does not translate to some of these articles not being regulated by SON. Importation of some sensitive, high-risk, life-endangering, and associated products, is mandated to be processed through a special license issued by the Director General/Chief Executive (SON). In such circumstances, the importer will be guided on the importation process. What is SONCAP Import Permit?The SONCAP Import permit is issued to exempt certain categories of imports of regulated products from offshore certification (SONCAP). Such categories include raw materials, packaging, and Industrial Machinery imported by Bonafide manufacturers imported. Bonafide manufacturers who intend to import machinery, raw materials, and packaging materials to produce SON-regulated products, are advised to apply to the SONCAP Division SONCAP Import Permit (equivalent to the Product Certificate) to enable them to optimize production, save time and cost, and further participation in the MANCAP scheme. Other articles permitted under SONCAP Import Permit are Project specific items, Humanitarian/Charitable items, and other non-commercial products. Import Permit is issued after the evaluation of the importer, import documents, intent of importation, and permit verification inspection (pre or post-import) by SON. All of the above forms the basis for the approval or denial of an application, under the directive of the Director General. All commercial items and articles for subsequent sale and distribution are to be processed through the SONCAP Product certification route to ensure conformity to standards, fitness for purpose, and safety. How long does it take to obtain SONCAP Import Permit? Issuance of Demand Note \u2013 3 Working Days Activation of Import Permit to NICIS Portal \u2013 3 Working Days Activation of Import Permit for PAAR- 3 Working Days (Note: All Pre-Arrival Assessment Report Applications must be carried out at the time of departure of consignments to Nigeria. Any application for PAAR for goods already at Nigerian Ports and Borders will be termed as default and penalized accordingly. How much does it cost to apply for SONCAP Import Permit?Demand Note values are determined using any combination of the following criteria: 5% FOB subject to a minimum of #550,000.00 Item of Import, HS code, and their use Availability of alternative locally Terms on the SON-MAN MOU for members of the Manufacturers Association of Nigeria MANCAP certification status of the product whose raw material is being imported Special charges as approved by SON\u2019s Management. What is SONCAP?The SONCAP is an offshore and pre-shipment certification scheme to monitor, check and control the importation of regulated products into Nigeria. It comprises a set of conformity assessments and verification procedures applicable to all SON-regulated products imported into Nigeria. The Federal Government of Nigeria introduced the Standards Organisation of Nigeria Conformity Assessment Programme (SONCAP) to address the number of unsafe products entering the country and to eliminate their subsequent risks to public health and safety. The scheme employs four mandatory processes of verification of product quality to meet specification standards such as NIS, International Organisation for Standardisation (ISO), and International Electrotechnical Committee (IEC) among others. The processes are: Product Certificate 1 (otherwise known as PC1 or Unregistered) Product Certificate 2 (otherwise known as PC2 or Registered Status) Product Certificate 3 (otherwise known as PC3 or Licensed Status) SONCAP Certificate (also known as SC) These documents are mandatory for the importation and Customs clearance of SONCAP-regulated goods, as stipulated in the revised Import Guidelines, under the import requirement of the Federal Government of Nigeria. Why do I need SONCAP Certificate for my import to Nigeria?The SONCAP scheme is designed to protect Nigerian consumers from unsafe and substandard imported finished products, to ensure that imported products meet the specifications of acceptable standards while ensuring easier monitoring by responsible agencies. Any regulated products that find their way to Nigeria without the SONCAP certificate are in violation of extant regulations and shall be subject to detention, testing, and possible destruction at the expense of the importer. What is involved in the processing of SONCAP certification?Under the SONCAP regime, imports are required to undergo verification and testing at the country of supply (Exporting country) of any of the approved Independent Accredited Firms (IAFs), and a SONCAP Certificate (SC) is issued to demonstrate that the products have met the applicable standards and regulations, or a Non-Conformity Report (NCR) is issued where the goods do not comply. The conformity assessment elements undertaken in SONCAP include, but are not limited, to physical inspection prior to shipment, sampling, testing, and analysis in accredited laboratories, audit of production processes and systems, and documentary check of conformity with regulations and overall assessment of conformity to standards. Under this program, importers of regulated products must obtain two certificates to process the importation and clearance of the goods. Product Certificate (PC), either unregistered, registered, or licensed SONCAP Certificate (SC) The Product Certificate and the SONCAP Certificate are mandatory for the processing of Form M and Nigerian Customs\u2019 clearance of SON-regulated goods respectively under the import requirements of the Federal Government of Nigeria. What is the validity period of Product-type certificateOne (1) year. The country of destination of the products and the country / customer specification or requirement is also stated on the certificate What is MANCAPMANCAP means Mandatory Conformity Assessment Programme. It is a product certification scheme for locally manufactured products in Nigeria conducted by the Standards Organisation of Nigeria as prescribed in the SON Act 14 of 2015. How do I apply for MANCAP certification of my products?Contact nearest SON State Office for enquiry and obtain the application form What documents do I need to submit with completed application?CAC certificate of incorporation, on-the-spot test report (SON laboratory test report or test report form IPAN accredited laboratory is required if on-the-spot test is not comprehensive), copy of payments for the purchase of MANCAP form and MANCAP certification. How many inspections will be conducted before MANCAP certification?Minimum of two quarterly inspections are required and the product/products must conform with all the requirements of relevant Nigerian Industrial Standard. What do I get at the of MANCAP certification?The MANCAP certificate and logo is issued at the end of the certification. The MANCAP logo has unique identification number and can be affixed on each unit of the products or on the label How long does it take to obtain MANCAP?It takes 60days from the receipt of MANCAP applications at the MANCAP Division office if all the requirements are met How much do I pay for the MANCAP certification?The certification fees and inspection charges are as published and can be obtained at SON State Offices nationwide. For how long is MANCAP certificate valid?Three (3) years After 3years what next?Recertification / Revalidation of the certificate and a new one is issued. What happens within the 3years validity?SON State Office covering the area/state will be conducting inspection of your factory on quarterly basis Can I export my products with MANCAP certificate?Yes. MANCAP certified products can be export to any West African country. What is MANCAP fast track?MANCAP fast track is not a certification but verification exercise to ascertain production and quality assurance capability of companies/factories after which MANCAP logo with number is issued for 6months period. This is to facilitate trade and encourage MSMEs. It is expected that MANCAP certification would be completed within the 6months period. How do I apply for MANCAP fast trackContact the nearest SON State Office to be guided. What is Product-type certificationIt is a certification for products for export based on the destination country or customer requirements How many laboratories does SON have, and where are they located?[Click here to read more](https://son.gov.ng/library-services/) about SON Laboratories Why is SON not at the ports like other agencies?SON is presently close to all Ports of import. SON is operating from outside the port following the directive of the Federal Government that all agencies should operate from outside the port, but should be called upon to participate in the clearance of regulated products. It is the responsibility of consignees or the agent to notify SON of the clearance of SON\u2019s regulated products at all ports of entry, failure to do this will attract stiff penalties. Why is SON not at the ports like other agencies?SON play major roles in the development of SMEs through provision of training, standard specifications and advice of products improvement. SON also have dedicated officer assigned to help in SMEs development and certification of their process. What is the role of SON in the establishment of SME\u2019s?SON play major roles in the development of SMEs through provision of training, standard specifications and advice of products improvement. SON also have dedicated officer assigned to help in SMEs development and certification of their process. Why do we still have sub-standards products in the market?There are still substandard products in the market due to activities of some dishonest individual who smuggled substandard product into the country. Some importers also deliberately request that foreign manufacturer manufacture products below standard specification for profit purpose. SON is working to bring the level of substandard products to manageable level. Is it proper for SON to charge money for her services?YES \u2013 SON enabling Act empower her to charge for services rendered\u2019 For all comparisons, tests or investigations performed by the Organisation under this Act, \u2026.., a fee sufficient in each case to compensate the Organisation for the entire cost of the service rendered shall be charged according to a scale specified in rules made for that purpose by the Council. Why is SON not as popular as NAFDAC?SON is very popular within the country. SON is also actively involved in safe-guarding the life and properties of consumers through various schemes which the importers and manufacturers are familiar with. How can a company be certified?For a company to be certified, it must address all the requirements of the standard being certified to. For QMS, all requirements of ISO 9001:2008 must be addressed, specifically must meet the ISO 9001 Documentation Requirements. What are Management Systems StandardsManagement Systems Standards are sets of policies, processes, and procedures required for planning and execution (production/development/service) in the core business area of an organization to meet customers\u2019 requirements. ISO 9001 2015 is an example of a Quality Management System. How does SON carry out inspection, Can it also inspect government owned organisation? Yes \u2013 SON Act 56 of 1971, 20 of 1984, 18 of 1990 as amended empower her to carry out this function Does SON have standard laboratories for testing products?Yes, [Click here to find out more](https://son.gov.ng/library-services/) If I have NAFDAC registration no. do I still need SON\u2019s registration number?SON Product Registration scheme is mandatory for all SON\u2019s regulated products as mandated by SON Act 56 of 1971, 20 of 1984, 18 of 1990 as amended. This is irrespective of the registration done by NAFDAC. In which of the SON offices can one go for SONCAP related issues?SON\u2019s Headquarter, Abuja, Lagos and other SON Offices nationwide What is MANCAP and SONCAP?MANCAP \u2013 Mandatory Conformity Assessment Programme \u2013 A mandatory programme to which all locally manufactured products in Nigeria must comply with before the product can be allowed to be distributed to the market. SONCAP \u2013 A mandatory off-shore conformity assessment programme to which all regulated importer product must comply with. At what point can a company be eligible for the certification?A company is eligible for certification if it can demonstrate that it has met all the criteria stated in a specific standard. This will be verified through inspection or auditing of the process, system and/or person. What is ISO and NIS certification?ISO means \u2018International Organisation for Standardisation\u2019. The ISO certification is awarded to a process, system, or person has met all the applicable requirement for a specific standard. A typical ISO certification is the ISO 9001:2008 Quality Management System (QMS). The \u2018NIS\u2019 certification is the award given to locally manufactured product with consistently proving record of meeting standard requirements after passing through rigorous analytical process. The awardees of NIS mark are consistently monitored to ensure that they retain their quality level, otherwise, the award is withdrawn. Is SON actually reducing the infiltration of sub-standard products in the market. ?Yes. The mandatory \u2018MANCAP\u2019 and \u2018SONCAP\u2019 schemes ensures that both locally manufactured and imported products comply with the minimum applicable standard. What is the meaning of NIS?NIS stands for \u2018Nigerian Industrial Standard\u2019. This is the prefix given to all standards elaborated as Nigerian standards. It is also included before other adapted or adopted national or international standards. How can one identify products certified by SON?Products certified by SON can be recognized by the certification marks inscribed on them or their packaging. The two certification marks are for \u2018MANCAP\u2019 and \u2018NIS\u2019. Is SON a private or a government organisation?SON is a Federal Government agency. What is SON?SON stands for \u2018Standards Organisation of Nigeria\u2019. [Click here to read more about SON](https://son.gov.ng/about-son/)", "content_hash": "b2198bcb85fb90e12e240f00b1a6503e2a0126ec039e99f63c94376ecbee8908", "domain": "son.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1987", "title": "Mandatory Migration of Soncap and Import Permit Processes to the National Single Window (NSW) Platform", "url": "https://son.gov.ng/2026/03/31/mandatory-migration-of-soncap-and-import-permit-processes-to-the-national-single-window-nsw-platform", "final_url": "https://son.gov.ng/2026/03/31/mandatory-migration-of-soncap-and-import-permit-processes-to-the-national-single-window-nsw-platform", "text": "25th March, 202 PUBLIC NOTICE: MANDATORY MIGRATION OF SONCAP AND IMPORT PERMIT PROCESSES TO THE NATIONAL SINGLE WINDOW (NSW) PLATFORM The Standards Organisation of Nigeria (SON) hereby notifies all importers, port -stakeholders, and the general-public of the new digital framework (instead platform) for trade facilitation. In line with the Federal Government\u2019s directive to streamline port operations and enhance the \u201cEase of Doing Business\u201d via the National Single Window (NSW), the following changes to our certification and permit processes will take effect from March 27, 2026: Effective March 27, 2026, all applications for the following documents must be initiated and processed exclusively on the National Single Window website: Import Permits (Product and Shipment Certificates) SONCAP Defaults Users are directed to use the official link for all submissions: \ud83d\udd17 [https://nsw.gov.ng](https://nsw.gov.ng) For clients utilizing our Internationally Accredited Firms (IAFs) for offshore inspections: Issuance: The generation of SONCAP Product and Shipment Certificates by our accredited partners shall continue to be performed on the SON Portal. Activation: Crucially, the activation of these certificates for the purpose of Form M, PAAR, and cargo clearance must be completed on the National Single Window (NSW) platform. This migration is a critical component of the March 27 Go-Live phase of the NSW, aimed at: Achieving a 24-hour cargo clearance cycle. Centralizing risk management to fast-track compliant traders. Eliminating manual bottlenecks through a \u201cSingle Entry Point\u201d for all regulatory requirements. All stakeholders are advised to ensure their profiles are updated on the NSW platform before the effective date. Please note that SON will no longer process these specific documents through independent channels following the transition. We appreciate your cooperation as we move toward a more transparent, paperless, and efficient trade ecosystem in Nigeria. Signed: Management, Standards Organisation of Nigeria (SON) Safeguarding Nigeria through Standards. /post-content /inner-wrap", "content_hash": "515a77beaf3813d9a5f287237c9793cadd224e57a250e3539d09c2aa6dc30c7b", "domain": "son.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1988", "title": "About", "url": "https://budgetoffice.gov.ng/index.php/about", "final_url": "https://budgetoffice.gov.ng/index.php/about", "text": "Budget Office of the FederationThe Budget Office of the Federation in the Ministry of Budget and Planning was established to provide budget function such as preparation of Executive budget, oversee budget implementation, and budget monitoring. Other functions include the implementation of fiscal policies of the Federal Government of Nigeria to maintain aggregate fiscal discipline, allocate resources in accordance with government priorities, and promote the efficient delivery of services. Specifically, its budget preparation and formulation function entails revenue estimation using current and realistic assumptions on oil production and prices. It also involves estimation of non-oil receipts, and employing realistic macroeconomic inidcators to develop a macroeconomic framework used in at aggregate fiscal estimates for the Medium Term Exependiture Framework. To develop a framework for the annual budget; the Budget Office Specifies fiscal policy objectives, analyse the macroeconomic framework and its associated Medium Term Expenditure Framework, identify and analyse major fiscal risks to derive expenditure estimates. The statement of fiscal policy objectives and an assessment of fiscal sustainability thus provide the framework for the annual budget. Furthermore, BOF is also charged with the responsibility of coordinating the preparation of Medium Term Sector Strategy (MTSS) across the Federal Sector MDAs and ensure that the MTSS significantly drives annual budget estimates with the issuance of Budget Call Circulars (BCC) that clearly links MTEF, MTSS and the annual budget.VisionTo be a world class technology driven budget institution that is a catalyst for equitable mobilization and distribution of the Nation's resources to engender sustainable socio-economic development.MissionTo provide efficient and qualitative budget functions to Nigeria, geared towards promoting fiscal sustainability, transparency and accountability in public finance management for national development in line with International best practices.Core ValuesAccountability, Competence, Courtesy, Efficiency, Probity, Prudence, Responsibility, Transparency.OrganogramThe Budget Office has four operational and two service departments. Each of the department, as listed below, is sub-divided into two divisions for functional purposes. Revenue and Fiscal Policy Expenditure (Social) Expenditure (Economic) Budget Monitoring and Evaluation Human Resources Department; and Finance and Accounts. Besides the core departments, there is the Office of the Director-General (DG Office) that carry out overall coordination of the budget functions and support the departments to achieve their mandates. Quick Links", "content_hash": "6a3054de59417c437c4c43c03694e87fb1af404afc252aa61253ecc7b2496f4c", "domain": "budgetoffice.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00", "language": "en"}]}